ATSWA.put("MAN", {"intro":"Management is a 10-chapter survey of the nature of business, the theory and practice of management, and the organic functions that make an enterprise run — from the fundamentals of business (its forms, environment, ethics and corporate governance) through management thought and process (planning, decision-making, organising, coordination, control, strategic management), the organic business functions (human resources, production, marketing, finance and accounting), managing work relationships (motivation, leadership, groups, conflict, communication, emotional intelligence, change management), business process re-engineering, organisational effectiveness, office practice/health and safety, contemporary management issues (mentoring, remote work, workplace couples), and closes with entrepreneurship. Unlike Taxation and Cost Accounting, this subject is almost entirely qualitative/conceptual rather than computational — the emphasis throughout is on definitions, frameworks, and enumerated lists rather than worked numeric illustrations.","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Nature of Business","brief":"A three-part foundation chapter: Section A covers what a business is, why it exists, its resources/objectives/classification, why businesses fail, and the legal forms of ownership (sole proprietorship, partnership, corporation, cooperative). Section B covers the business environment — internal, task and general/mega environments, the PESITEL framework, environmental analysis/forecasting, and globalisation. Section C covers corporate social responsibility, business ethics, and corporate governance.","outcomes":["Define the term business and explain the need for, characteristics, resources, objectives and classification of business","Explain the reasons for business failure and the requirements for success in modern business","Explain the legal forms of business ownership and business stakeholders and their expectations","Define and classify the business environment (internal, task, general) and explain globalisation","Explain environmental analysis, forecasting, and techniques for interacting with the environment","Explain the nature of social responsibility and the arguments for and against it","Explain business ethics, ethical issues in business, and why managers behave unethically","Explain corporate governance, its benefits, and the compliance requirements in Nigeria"],"secs":[{"n":"A.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"In a free enterprise economy, or a market economy, the business enterprise — otherwise known as the **firm** — is an important **decision-making unit**. The primary purpose of the business enterprise is to **make profit**."},{"p":"However, there are decision-making units or organisations that are **not established for the purpose of making profit**. Such organisations are referred to as **not-for-profit organisations**. Most not-for-profit organisations are established by **government**, for the purpose of providing services for the good governance and administration of a country. However, a growing number of not-for-profit organisations exist which are **not** established by government — these are known as **non-governmental organisations (NGOs)**, and they provide a variety of services to different target groups in society."},{"key":"**Business enterprises or firms, governmental establishments and non-governmental organisations are referred to generally as *organisations*.** That is the vocabulary the rest of the subject uses: \"organisation\" is the general term, and \"business\" or \"firm\" is the profit-seeking subset of it."}]},{"n":"A.2","t":"Definitions of business","b":[{"def":{"t":"Business","d":"a social and lawful human activity whose primary aim is to make profit — the organised effort/activities of persons utilising resources within an organisational context to produce and distribute goods and services for private gain (profit, recognition, employment generation, etc.). Briefly: lawful human activity directed at producing and distributing goods and services to make profit."}}]},{"n":"A.3","t":"The need for business","b":[{"ul":["to meet human needs/desires by providing goods and services;","to convert nature's raw gifts into goods/services meeting human needs;","to solve societal problems by creating satisfactions;","to create employment opportunities;","to raise the standard of living of society;","to enhance the efficient allocation of resources;","to create a competitive environment; and","to create wealth."]}]},{"n":"A.4","t":"Characteristics of business","b":[{"ol":["involves manufacturing, or buying and selling, of goods and services;","activities must be CONTINUOUS — a single transaction is not a business (selling your old car to buy a new one is not business; importing and selling cars for a living is);","directly/indirectly concerned with the transfer/exchange of goods and services for value;","a human activity directed towards acquiring wealth;","every business requires investment (cash, kind, or both);","risk is present in every category and type of business;","the sole objective is to earn profit — essential for the business's survival;","every business has someone (or a group) managing its affairs; and","it creates employment, rewarding individuals through wages/salaries."]}]},{"n":"A.5","t":"Resources of business","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Resource","Description"],"rows":[["**Natural resources**","Land (not just soil — minerals in the ground, timber, air space, climate, water), the basic raw materials business converts into other utilities."],["**Human factor (labour)**","Physical/mental effort exerted in production — exploits and converts natural resources into more valuable goods/services; the quality of a nation's human element (coupled with interest/desire) largely explains the gap between developed and underdeveloped economies."],["**Capital factor**","All man-made items workers use in production/distribution, or to create further wealth — tools, factories, machinery, vehicles. Split into **fixed capital** (buildings, machinery, equipment, vehicles) and **floating/current capital** (stock in goods, cash at hand)."],["**Government**","Policy and infrastructure provision, and the level of protection given to firms, critically shape the enabling business environment."],["**Entrepreneurship**","The individual (or group) who sees/evaluates business opportunities, gathers resources to exploit them, and organises/combines land, labour and capital — otherwise idle without this — bearing the principal risk of the business, though not necessarily directly managing it."]]}}]},{"n":"A.6","t":"Objectives of business","b":[{"p":"Objectives matter because they: indicate the business's purpose/aim (justifying its existence); provide direction for operations; set standards for control; help coordinate managerial decisions; and give a benchmark against which performance (unit or corporate level) is measured."},{"h4":"A.6.1  Primary objectives of business"},{"key":"The **primary objective** of business is to make profit — a reward to owners for risk-taking, and the source of growth/continued survival."},{"h4":"A.6.2  The secondary objectives of a business"},{"ol":["**Productivity** — utilising a given resource set to attain the highest/predetermined output value;","**Innovation** — satisfying consumer needs better than competitors via new products/production/distribution methods;","**Growth** — expanding operations (capital employed, market share, employees), aiming for economies of scale, competitiveness, market share and profitability;","**Shareholder satisfaction**;","**Employee satisfaction** — enhancing employees' satisfaction, which boosts their contribution to growth/competitiveness;","**Positive public image** — favourable terms, a good-corporate-citizen image, and visible care for employees/community/social responsibility."]}]},{"n":"A.7","t":"Classification of business","b":[{"p":"Business can be grouped by: size of the unit; use of output; input used; ownership type; and industrial classification."},{"h4":"A.7.1  Size based classification"},{"p":"The Federal Ministry of Industries classifies enterprises by size (fixed-asset value, excluding land):"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Category","Fixed asset value"],"rows":[["Micro enterprise","Not exceeding N400,000"],["Small scale enterprise","N400,000 – N5 million"],["Medium scale enterprise","N5 million – N10 million"],["Large scale enterprise","Exceeding N10 million"]]}},{"p":"Business may also be classified by number of paid employees:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Category","Employees"],"rows":[["Micro-enterprises","1–10"],["Small scale enterprises","11–100"],["Medium scale enterprises","101–300"],["Large scale enterprises","301 and above"]]}},{"note":"Size-based classification thresholds shift over time — in the 1970s, a small scale business in Nigeria was one with total assets (excluding land) not exceeding N150,000 and employment not exceeding 50."},{"h4":"A.7.2  Use of output based classification"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Industry type","Description"],"rows":[["**Basic industries**","Provide basic inputs/raw materials for other industries — e.g. fertiliser for agriculture; coal, oil and gas."],["**Capital goods industries**","Produce plant, machinery, equipment and tools needed to manufacture other goods/services — require high capital investment."],["**Intermediate goods industries**","Produce materials, parts and components used in producing other goods, enhancing value by changing physical form."],["**Consumer goods industries**","Output used by final consumers — split into consumer durables (refrigerators, TVs, bicycles, cars) and consumer non-durables (soap, toothpaste, foodstuff)."]]}},{"h4":"A.7.3  Input based classification"},{"p":"By source of major input: **agro-based** (agricultural produce — cocoa, palm produce, jute, cotton), **forest-based** (forest produce, e.g. paper), **marine-based**, **metal-based** and **chemical-based** (e.g. fertiliser and drug producers) industries."},{"h4":"A.7.4  Types of ownership"},{"p":"**Manufacturing industries** split into 4 categories:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Analytical industry**","Separates a single material input into several elements — e.g. an oil refinery converting crude oil into petrol, diesel, kerosene, lubricating oils."],["**Synthetic industry**","Combines several raw materials, processing them into a single output — e.g. limestone, gypsum, silica, iron oxide blended into cement."],["**Process industry**","Processes materials through different production stages to the final product — e.g. cotton cleaned, ginned, spun, woven, bleached, dyed into cloth."],["**Assembly line industry**","Joins various components/parts into a single product — e.g. a car or television set."]]}},{"p":"Other ownership-related splits: **profit vs. not-for-profit** (e.g. Red Cross, NAFDAC are not-for-profit); and **public sector vs. private sector** (owned by government/agencies, or by private individuals/firms — the extent of government ownership signals whether an economy is planned or free-enterprise)."},{"h4":"A.7.5  Industrial classification"},{"p":"The **International Standard of Industrial Classification (ISIC)** classifies establishments into divisions/major groups/sub-groups via a numbering system:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Number range","Industry/division/group"],"rows":[["01–09","Agriculture, Forestry and Fishing"],["10–14","Mining"],["15–19","Construction"],["20–30","Manufacturing"],["40–49","Transportation, Communication, Electric, Gas and Sanitary services"],["50–51","Wholesale trades"],["52–59","Retail trade"],["60–69","Finance, Insurance, Real Estate"],["70–89","Services"],["90–97","Public Administration"],["98–99","Non-classifiable establishments"]]}},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: the fifth sub-section of §A.7 is printed in the book's own contents list as **\"4.7.5 Industrial Classification\"** — a chapter-4 number appearing inside chapter 1, section A. It is **A.7.5**, following A.7.1 to A.7.4."}]},{"n":"A.8","t":"Reasons for business failure","b":[{"p":"Drive, ambition and the will to succeed alone rarely guarantee success in a complex, competitive world — a significant proportion of new ventures do not survive to their 5th year."},{"ol":["**A bad idea to begin with** — an apparently \"unique\" idea may not be unique, or demand may be less than expected;","**Poor planning** — failing to stick to the business plan, skimping on research, or ignoring professional advice;","**Lack of capital** — under-capitalisation is a common early-years threat; adequate capitalisation must cover both set-up capital and sustainability;","**Not knowing the market** — failing to understand what customers actually want (vs. what you believe they want), and failing to keep up with changing needs;","**Cash-flow crisis management** — ignoring (wilfully or negligently) that cash outflow exceeds inflow; even profitable companies can sink from poor cash management;","**Doing it all** — failing to delegate, leaving no time for the bigger picture;","**Inadequate control** — control mechanisms not functioning well or unsuited to the operations;","**Inadequate protection** — no insurance against flood, fire, loss of key personnel, theft, etc.;","**Failing to change** — not monitoring competitors, technology, government policy, or shifting customer tastes;","**Growing too fast** — unchecked expansion risking loss of control and over-stretched resources; and","**The illusion of starting out as a big company** — embarking on projects too large to manage effectively."]},{"h4":"A.8.1  Planning against business failure"},{"p":"There is no magic guarantee of success, but the following help: developing a business plan; obtaining timely accurate financial information; profiling the target customer and competitors; developing a suitable control system; networking with similar-industry owners/managers; remembering someone will always undercut your price; building a strong management team (recruitment/training/reward); recognising that consumer tastes keep changing; and staying well informed of available resources."}]},{"n":"A.9","t":"Importance of profit in business","b":[{"ul":["guides an investor on which business type to establish;","an objective means of evaluating business performance;","the critical attraction for venturing into business, given the risks involved;","the principal factor in a firm's survival, growth and expansion — ploughed back to sustain the firm;","boosts owners' wealth;","the parameter investors use to buy shares in a public limited company;","enhances the business's ability to embark on social responsibility;","one criterion for resource allocation in industry;","used to set performance standards, and by implication, control mechanisms; and","used by labour unions as a basis for their demand levels."]}]},{"n":"A.10","t":"Requirements for success in modern business","b":[{"ol":["**Determination of objectives** — clearly stated, synchronised with environmental opportunities and organisational capability, with complementary/subsidiary objectives supporting the prime one;","**Planning and setting up a functioning organisation** — the right system, human and technical resources to drive it;","**Adequate finances** — \"the heart that pumps blood into all parts of the system\" — without it, even well-planned organisations fail;","**Proper location/plant layout** — affects transportation ease, access to complementary facilities, cost of production and profitability;","**Cultivating modern improvement techniques** — adapting/generating new ideas through research or an innovation-friendly work environment; and","**Efficient and dynamic management and leadership** — capable, dedicated top management that sees opportunities before they become obvious."]}]},{"n":"A.11","t":"Qualities of a good businessman","b":[{"ol":["**Ideology** — placing customer interest first;","**Foresight** — anticipating tomorrow's challenges with accuracy;","**Initiative and prompt decision-making** — generating ideas and deciding decisively;","**Business morality** — honesty, dependability, sincerity, the highest ethical standards;","**Firmness and courage** — perseverance, steadfastness, undisturbed focus despite initial setbacks; and","**Personal qualities** — self-confidence, good leadership, originality, future- and task-orientation, good time management."]}]},{"n":"A.12","t":"Legal forms of business ownership","b":[{"def":{"t":"Ownership","d":"the legal relationship between a person and some object — the rights an owner exercises over their possessions, as determined by law. Any form of business ownership spells out the profit-sharing arrangement and responsibility for debts/liabilities."}},{"h4":"A.12.1  The sole proprietorship"},{"p":"The simplest, most common and oldest form — owned, managed and controlled by one individual, entitled to all profits and bearing all losses; raises all capital personally (own resources, friends, relatives, bank); common in retail/wholesale trading, professional practice, and construction; can be formed without any written agreement given the proprietor's high independence."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Features","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Individual initiative dominates; risk borne alone (with all profit); management/control by the proprietor (may engage a manager, but control stays with the owner); UNLIMITED liability (may need to sell personal assets); secrecy; minimum government regulation.","Easy formation; benefits of small-scale operations; inexpensive management; better control.","Lack of continuity; limitation of size; slow technological progress; limited ability to attract/retain capable employees; limited access to capital; limitation of management skills; unlimited liability."]]}},{"h4":"A.12.2  Partnership"},{"p":"Two or more individuals agreeing to organise/operate an enterprise jointly for profit, contributing capital and jointly offering services — governed by a contractual legal basis (typically a **Partnership Deed**) specifying partners' duties/rights. Common in law, insurance, finance, accountancy and similar professions."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Features","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Based on agreement; profit/loss sharing; agency relationship (each partner can bind the firm); UNLIMITED liability (private property can be taken); common management (a non-participating partner's consent still needed for strategic decisions); restricted share transferability (needs all partners' consent).","More managerial skills than a sole proprietorship; pooled financial resources; reduced strain on one individual; new partners can be admitted; enhanced ability to attract/retain employees; balanced business decisions; risk sharing.","Unlimited liability; limited life; arguments/disagreements among partners; limited access to initial capital vs. companies; difficulty withdrawing from the firm; risks of implied authority."]]}},{"h4":"Types of partners"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**General partner**","Unlimited liability for all business actions; an \"active partner\" who takes an active management role (may act as managing/executive director or manager)."],["**Limited partner**","The \"sleeping\"/\"dormant\" partner; contributes part of the capital but does not usually actively manage; shares profit/loss but risks only the capital contributed on failure."]]}},{"p":"A written partnership agreement (drawn up by a legal practitioner) typically covers: description of partners (names, responsibilities, initial investments, salary, profit-sharing proportion); description of the business (name, location, type); description of business practices (fiscal year, accounting system, permitted withdrawals); provision for changes (duration, renewal, new-partner admission, treatment on withdrawal/death); and partners' signatures."},{"h4":"Dissolution of partnership"},{"p":"A partnership can dissolve via: court order; technical insolvency; mutual agreement; completion of the task it was established for; or death of a partner."},{"h4":"A.12.3  Corporation or limited liability company"},{"p":"Most large businesses are corporations, given the inherent disadvantages of sole proprietorship/partnership. A corporation exists via a legal document (a \"Charter\"), owned by **shareholders/stockholders**, who elect a **board of directors** controlling management; the board appoints the chief executive and top officials. Once incorporated, the company becomes a legal entity — an \"artificial person in law\" expected to live forever."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Limited liability** — a shareholder who paid full par value loses only their investment if the corporation fails.","**Government regulation** — activities are regulated as a legal person."],["**Capital formation** — public confidence enables raising large capital via share sales.","**Large initial capital** needed to form."],["**Perpetual life** — independent of any one owner; a withdrawing/deceased shareholder's shares pass on without affecting the business.","**Lack of secrecy** — financial transactions cannot be shielded, given the annual-report requirement."],["**Ease of expansion** — capital raised via share sales through the capital market.","**Goal incongruence** — e.g. employees wanting higher salaries vs. the organisation emphasising profit maximisation."],["**Transferability of ownership** — readily effected by anyone willing to pay the asking price.","**Divergence of owner/management interests** — owners want dividends, management may want growth/expansion."],["**Competent management team** — professional managers can be hired, and replaced by the board if underperforming.","**Organisational expenses** — incorporation costs, board allowances, top-management salaries. **Large size** may also reduce efficiency through bureaucracy."]]}},{"h4":"Formation of a limited liability company"},{"p":"Registration is with the **Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC)**, requiring the filing of Articles of Association and Memorandum of Association:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Document","Typical contents"],"rows":[["**Articles of Association**","Rules/regulations of the company — appointment/termination of directors; procedures for issuing/transferring shares; rights/responsibilities of shareholders; procedure for auditing/accounting."],["**Memorandum of Association**","Guidelines for the company and its members, plus parameters concerning the company and the outside public — the company name (with \"PLC\" if applicable); business address/location; type of business; a statement that members' liability is limited; and the number of shares the share capital is divided into."]]}},{"p":"Once required documents are submitted and fees paid, the CAC issues a **Certificate of Incorporation**, authorising the company to legitimately commence business."},{"h4":"A.12.4  Cooperative"},{"p":"A legal person distinct from its owners, with continuity irrespective of a member's death/withdrawal. Members buy shares (as in a corporation) but each member has only **one vote**. Surplus earnings are shared as dividends, hinged on the volume of a member's purchases — the concept aims to make goods/services cheaper than elsewhere. A manager, appointed by a board of directors, runs the business."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Characteristics","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Voluntary association; equal voting rights; separate legal entity; based on members' service motive; distribution of surplus.","Tax advantage; democratic elections (one member, one vote); member training; pooling of resources (plus government support incentives/concessionary loans); limited liability (to capital contributed); economic operations (no middlemen, voluntary member services).","Lack of freedom (cannot sell products in any market of choice); reduced finance when members under-contribute; dishonesty (colluding executives/staff); low salaries (attracting poorer-calibre staff); rigid government rules/regulations."]]}},{"p":"**3 major types of cooperatives**: **agricultural marketing cooperatives** (purchasing — resells seeds/machinery/fertilisers to members and non-members; or marketing — growers/producers combine to sell products); **credit and banking cooperatives** (issue loans to members/the public at affordable rates — e.g. cooperative banks); and **consumer cooperatives** (a group of consumers buys at wholesale prices to eliminate middlemen's excess profit, selling more cheaply to members and the public)."}]},{"n":"A.13","t":"Stakeholders in business and their expectations","b":[{"h4":"A.13.1  Definition of stakeholders"},{"def":{"t":"Stakeholder","d":"any individual or group with a strong interest (\"stake\") in the organisation and what it does/is trying to achieve."}},{"h4":"A.13.2 – A.13.3  Internal and external stakeholders"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Members"],"rows":[["**Internal stakeholders**","Shareholders; directors and top management; senior management staff; other managers and staff."],["**External stakeholders**","Lenders; suppliers; regulators; government; general public; special interest groups; company auditors."]]}},{"h4":"A.13.4  Expectations of stakeholders"},{"p":"Each stakeholder group has different expectations — e.g. shareholders expect share-price growth, stable dividends and good returns; directors/senior managers may emphasise personal advancement (remuneration, status, decision-making power)."},{"h4":"A.13.5  Effects of stakeholders' expectations"},{"key":"Differing expectations regularly produce **stakeholder conflict** — e.g. directors/managers pressing for improved pay, which shareholders may see as eroding profitability and dividend pay-out. A company wishing to stay associated with its stakeholders must innovate to balance and satisfy these competing expectations."}]},{"n":"A.14","t":"Summary — Section A","b":[{"p":"The **primary purpose of the business enterprise is to make gain** (profit, recognition and employment generation). **Profit is a reward to the owners for taking the risk** of establishing a business. Businesses can be grouped according to their **size, ownership types, use of output and input**, etc. The **resources** of business were listed and discussed. The **objectives** of business were well explained. The **bases of classification** of business were given good attention. The **reasons why businesses fail** were articulated, and **how to plan against business failure** was equally addressed. The section highlighted the **importance of profit in business**. The **requirements for success in modern business** were discussed, and also the **qualities of a good business person**."}]},{"n":"A.15","t":"Review questions — Section A, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"p":"Section A closes with **six multiple choice questions** and **six short answer questions**, with the study text's own printed solutions."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section A review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Which of the following is the primary objective of business? **(a)** Radical practice of communication; **(b)** Capitalising on the principle of equity; **(c)** Investing minimally in stocks and bonds; **(d)** Profit maximisation; **(e)** Identifying and effectively serving the needs of the board of directors.","All of the following are advantages of sole proprietorship **EXCEPT** **(a)** Lack of continuity; **(b)** Easy formation; **(c)** Benefits of small scale operations; **(d)** Inexpensive management; **(e)** Better control.","Which of these does **not** constitute external stakeholders in the business enterprise? **(a)** Unions; **(b)** Suppliers; **(c)** Competitors; **(d)** Government agencies; **(e)** Board of Directors.","The industry involved in the combination of several inputs and processing them into a single unit (output) is called: **(a)** Process industry; **(b)** Synthetic industry; **(c)** Analytical industry; **(d)** Assembly line industry; **(e)** Smelting industry.","The commonest and oldest form of business ownership is called: **(a)** Partnership; **(b)** Sole proprietorship; **(c)** Cooperatives; **(d)** Joint stock company; **(e)** Franchise.","Which of these is **not** a reason for business failure? **(a)** Bad business idea; **(b)** Doing it all; **(c)** Growing too fast; **(d)** Developing competitors' profile; **(e)** The illusion of making it."]},{"h4":"Part II: Short answer questions"},{"ol":["……… involves lawful human activities directed at the production and distribution of goods and services with the aim of making profit.","The industry concerned with separating a single material input into several elements is known as ………","An arrangement wherein two or more individuals come together and agree to organise and operate an outfit jointly, with profit as the motive, is known as ………","The document that contains the guidelines for a company and its members is ………","Any individual or group of individuals who have a strong interest in the organisation and what it does, or is trying to achieve, is called ………","……… is the economic end and purpose of the business enterprise."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["**D — Profit maximisation.** The primary objective of business is to make profit (§A.6.1); every other option is a distractor.","**A — Lack of continuity.** This is a *disadvantage* of sole proprietorship, not an advantage: the business ends with the proprietor. Easy formation, the benefits of small-scale operation, inexpensive management and better control are all advantages (§A.12.1).","**E — Board of Directors.** The board is an **internal** stakeholder. Unions, suppliers, competitors and government agencies are all external (§A.13.2 and §A.13.3).","**B — Synthetic industry.** A synthetic industry **combines several inputs into one output**; an **analytical** industry does the reverse, separating one input into several elements — which is what short answer question 2 asks about (§A.7.2).","**B — Sole proprietorship.** The simplest, most common and oldest form of business ownership (§A.12.1).","**D — Developing competitors' profile.** Knowing your competitors is a *safeguard* against failure, not a cause of it. The other four — a bad idea, doing it all yourself, growing too fast, and the illusion of having made it — are all reasons for failure at §A.8."]},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Business**","**Analytical industry**","**Partnership**","**Memorandum of Association**","**Stakeholder**","**Profit**"]},{"key":"MCQ 4 and short answer 2 are the **same distinction from both sides**, and it is the one most often muddled: a **synthetic** industry *combines* many inputs into one output (assembling a car); an **analytical** industry *separates* one input into many outputs (refining crude oil). Learn them as a pair."},{"note":"Note the pattern in MCQ 2, 3 and 6: all three are **\"EXCEPT\" or \"not\"** questions where four options come straight from a list in the text and the fifth is either the opposite of a list item (lack of continuity is a disadvantage, not an advantage) or a plausible-sounding intruder (board of directors among external stakeholders). Recall the list, tick off what is on it, and the survivor is the answer."}]}}]},{"n":"B.1","t":"Definition of business environment","b":[{"def":{"t":"Business environment","d":"a set of factors/conditions EXTERNAL to the business that influence its operations — factors generally outside the firm's control. Also seen as the web of forces forming the setting in which the firm makes decisions. The relationship is one of mutuality — the environment pressures the business, and the business, in turn, influences aspects of its environment."}}]},{"n":"B.2","t":"Classification of the business environment","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Classification","Description"],"rows":[["**Micro/internal environment**","Conditions WITHIN the firm — generally controllable — e.g. mission/vision/objectives, organisational structure/culture, company resources, the marketing mix, production/distribution facilities, financial/human resources, interpersonal/inter-group relationships."],["**Meso/task environment**","Elements with DIRECT implications for the firm's successful operation (also \"direct-action\" or \"operating\" environment) — e.g. customers, bankers, suppliers, competitors, distributors, government agencies, immediate community, special interest groups, general public."],["**Macro/mega/external/general environment**","Generally UNCONTROLLABLE — the setting in which ALL firms in the industry operate (the \"indirect-action\" environment) — common to every firm in the economy."]]}},{"note":"The general/indirect-action environment is classified into economic, socio-cultural, political, technological and legal/public-policy environment — and is usually discussed relative to the national economy. A further level is the **international/global environment**, sharing the same 5 component categories."}]},{"n":"B.3","t":"Analysis of the business environment","b":[{"p":"The external environment has 2 broad element groups: the task environment (direct-action elements) and the general/mega environment (indirect-action elements) — plus the internal environment (elements within the organisation's control)."},{"h4":"B.3.1  The task environment — 5 major forces"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Force","Detail"],"rows":[["**Customers and clients**","Individuals/corporate bodies purchasing the firm's goods/services — either final users, or users who produce further goods/services. Satisfying customer needs is the very reason a firm exists."],["**Suppliers**","Organisations/individuals providing operational resources. Global competition is shifting firms from \"many suppliers\" toward fewer suppliers given larger orders — yielding economies of scale, passed on as lower prices, and better-enforced quality standards/relationships."],["**Substitutes**","Goods/services usable in place of another — affects the original product's price and can seriously damage a business by shortening the product life cycle or reducing demand. Often the outcome of technological innovation — firms should scan for emerging substitutes early."],["**Competitors**","Firms producing similar/identical goods/services — the most powerful task-environment force; prices/services/after-sales support are directly compared. Firms must monitor rivals' weaknesses (competitive opportunity) AND watch for new entrants exploiting their own weaknesses."],["**Labour supply**","Those potentially employable by the organisation — the ability to attract/motivate/retain human resources (especially specially skilled staff) is a crucial competitive variable."]]}},{"h4":"B.3.2  General/mega environment — the PESITEL framework"},{"key":"**PESITEL**: **P**olitical, **E**conomic, **S**ocio-cultural, **I**nternational, **T**echnological, **E**cological, **L**egal environment."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","Detail"],"rows":[["**Political**","Government structures for law/order, infrastructure, security; policies/laws/regulations shaping societal welfare. Political instability creates policy uncertainty for business, especially in developing nations with unclear government-change procedures."],["**Economic**","Fiscal/monetary policy, GDP, industrial policy, unemployment rate, exchange rate, inflation rate, capacity utilisation, purchasing power/spending, geographical income variation, money supply, savings/debt patterns, credit availability, consumer expenditure patterns, interest rate, tariffs/quotas."],["**Socio-cultural**","Demographic characteristics, lifestyle, group formation, religious/ethnic/social stratification, crime rate/perceived insecurity, values, morals, beliefs and norms controlling behavioural patterns."],["**International**","Elements on the international scene affecting decisions/operations — dependence on imported raw materials/technology or exported goods; ILO standards, protection of women/children's rights, competition promotion, and intellectual property protection (e.g. under the WTO)."],["**Technological**","The state of scientific/mechanical application to societal tasks — methods/techniques/approaches for production and distribution. Nigeria and peer nations rely heavily on foreign technology (US, Japan, China, India, Korea, Europe), given limited domestic R&D investment."],["**Ecological**","The physical/ecological impact of business operations — especially manufacturing and mineral-extraction, causing carbon emissions/global warming, oil spillages, or open mining pits/gully erosion. Managers must regularly assess and mitigate negative ecological impact."],["**Legal/public policy**","Laws, regulations and procedures businesses must comply with — licensing requirements; tax obligations (PIT, CIT, VAT, CGT, education tax, import/excise duties); employment-contract law; registration with bodies like NAFDAC for foods/drugs/cosmetics/chemicals."]]}},{"h4":"B.3.3  Internal environment — 6 constituents"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Constituent","Detail"],"rows":[["**Owners and ownership structure**","Influences the power structure — a sole proprietorship has different communication/pay/job-security patterns than a public corporation, affecting efficiency and motivation."],["**Human resources**","The company's marketing/finance/production/R&D personnel and their quality shape its ability to exploit opportunities and counter threats."],["**Financial position**","A strong financial base enables hiring competent staff who can identify and (financially) act on opportunities."],["**Organisational culture**","A system of shared values/assumptions/beliefs/norms uniting members (\"the way things are done around here\") — analysed via **direction** (does it support or interfere with organisational goals), **pervasiveness** (how widespread among members) and **strength** (how deeply accepted). A widely-shared, deeply-internalised, goal-supporting culture is positive; one that is widely-shared/internalised but goal-UNDERMINING is negative."],["**Integration**","The quality of collaboration among units/departments needed to achieve unity of effort facing environmental challenges."],["**Associated background**","The reason/manner the organisation was formed, its age, and its founders' philosophy — an effective induction tool that shapes an organisation's ongoing culture/identity."]]}}]},{"n":"B.4","t":"Environmental analysis","b":[{"p":"Environmental analysis involves 2 processes: **environmental scanning** (monitoring external events to understand the environment's nature and changes) and understanding the trends/implications of those changes."},{"key":"3-step discipline for managers: (1) be aware of environmental changes; (2) forecast future-affecting conditions; (3) take appropriate decisions, based on the forecast, to achieve efficiency/effectiveness."}]},{"n":"B.5","t":"Environmental forecasting","b":[{"p":"**Environmental forecasting** gives managers foreknowledge to cope with the business environment — covering political, legal, technological, economic, ecological, cultural and social domains. Forecasts rest on fact, assumption, and combining methods/techniques — so reliability depends on data accuracy, assumptions, and method quality. Forecasting: improves managers' ability to anticipate/process/cope with environmental variables; lets managers explore challenges/opportunities in advance; and aids (and is aided by) scanning."},{"note":"The **PESITEL model** is a non-quantitative technique for analysing the business environment, identifying an entity's critical external elements. **Porter's Diamond model** explains why entities in some countries/regions enjoy competitive advantage over similar entities elsewhere in the same industry."}]},{"n":"B.6","t":"Techniques for interacting with the environment","b":[{"ol":["**Public relations** — regularly transmitting favourable information about the firm's activities to the public;","**Boundary spanning** — representatives regularly meeting stakeholders (e.g. sales agents with customers) to present status and influence their actions;","**Lobbying** — meeting government officials to influence policy votes affecting the organisation;","**Negotiation** — reaching a formal legal agreement with stakeholders (covering cost, time, method of transportation, etc.);","**Alliances** — joining with other organisations (often competitors) for mutual benefit; and","**Organisational restructuring** — changing structure/internal working arrangements, often including layoffs, to reduce costs and stay competitive."]}]},{"n":"B.7","t":"Globalisation","b":[{"def":{"t":"Globalisation","d":"\"integrating\" a country's economy with the world economy — the process of worldwide economic integration and growing interdependence/interconnectedness through the flow of goods, services, capital, people and information among nations. A truly globalised corporation views the entire world as one single market."}},{"h4":"B.7.1  Factors influencing globalisation"},{"ol":["phenomenal growth in communication (fast-paced communication technology);","improved transportation services;","mass migration and movement of peoples;","economic activity outgrowing national markets via cross-border industrial combinations/commercial groupings; and","international agreements reducing the cost of doing business abroad."]},{"h4":"B.7.2  The implications of globalisation for management and business enterprises"},{"ul":["greater liberalisation of goods/services/capital/labour/information flow opens up markets and investment opportunities among nations;","firms in less-developed countries are often disadvantaged by more intense competition from better-endowed advanced-nation companies;","mergers, acquisitions and strategic alliances result as weaker firms are swallowed by stronger ones;","managers need greater cross-cultural awareness/sensibility for cross-border communication;","managers acquire skills to manage in culturally diverse settings; and","cross-cultural interaction pushes firms toward higher product/service/management standards."]}]},{"n":"B.8","t":"Summary — Section B","b":[{"p":"The **environment of business is a major determinant of the policies, strategies and overall performance** of any business. It is usually **so complex and dynamic that it often defies simple analysis**. Management's perception of the environment in terms of **complexity, stability or hostility** is crucial to the success of the organisation."},{"p":"The section highlighted the **nature of the business environment**, and its classification into **internal, task and external** environments was discussed. A detailed analysis of the elements in these environments was also covered. In order to successfully manage the challenges posed by the environment, management must have a **reliable sensory mechanism for gathering and analysing timely intelligence** about the environment, through **environmental scanning and forecasting**. There was good discussion of the **techniques for interacting with the environment** — such as public relations, lobbying and negotiating alliances."}]},{"n":"B.9","t":"Review questions — Section B, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section B review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Which of these is **not** an aspect of the internal environment? **(a)** Middle men; **(b)** Owners; **(c)** Board of directors; **(d)** Employees; **(e)** Organisation culture.","The monitoring of external environmental forces to determine the source of a firm's opportunities and threats is known as **(a)** Regression analysis; **(b)** Environmental analysis; **(c)** TOWS analysis; **(d)** Critical path analysis; **(e)** SWOT analysis.","The external environmental condition under which business organisations are required to comply with certain laws, and to obtain permits from the relevant government agencies, is known as: **(a)** Legal/public policy environment; **(b)** Technological environment; **(c)** Political environment; **(d)** Socio-cultural environment; **(e)** Economic environment.","Which of these is a **task environment** of business? **(a)** Labour supply; **(b)** Economic environment; **(c)** Political environment; **(d)** Ecological environment; **(e)** Customers/clients.","Which of these is **not** an importance of the business environment? **(a)** Gives directional clarity; **(b)** Limited competition; **(c)** A continuous learning process; **(d)** Builds organisational image; **(e)** Exposes opportunities and threats."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The process used by a manager to monitor events occurring in the external environment of the organisation is called ………","The technique that affords managers the opportunity to acquire foreknowledge of what lies ahead, in order to cope with their business environment, is known as ………","The environment that provides opportunities and threats is called ………","……… are organisations and individuals that provide resources to other companies, which use them to conduct their operations.","The direct-action environment of the external environment is also known as ………"]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["**A — Middle men.** Middle men are part of the **task** environment, being external parties the firm deals with. Owners, the board of directors, employees and organisational culture are all constituents of the **internal** environment (§B.3.3).","**B — Environmental analysis.** Short answer question 1 asks the same thing from a slightly different angle and is answered \"environmental **scanning**\" — scanning is the monitoring process, and analysis is the broader activity of which scanning and forecasting are the two parts (§B.4).","**A — Legal/public policy environment.** Compliance with laws and the obtaining of permits is the legal and public-policy element of the general environment, distinct from the **political** environment, which concerns the stability and disposition of government (§B.3.2).","**E — Customers/clients.** The task environment comprises the parties the firm deals with directly. Economic, political and ecological conditions belong to the **general/mega** environment, and labour supply is a resource input rather than one of the five task forces (§B.3.1).","**B — Limited competition.** The business environment does not limit competition; if anything it intensifies it. Directional clarity, continuous learning, image building and the exposure of opportunities and threats are all genuine benefits of understanding the environment."]},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Environmental scanning**","**Environmental forecasting**","**External environment**","**Suppliers**","**Task environment**"]},{"key":"The whole of Section B rests on a **three-way classification**, and almost every question above tests which box something belongs in: the **internal** environment (owners, board, employees, culture — inside the organisation), the **task** or **direct-action** environment (customers, suppliers, competitors, middle men — outside but dealt with directly), and the **general/mega** or **indirect-action** environment (the PESITEL forces — political, economic, socio-cultural, international, technological, ecological, legal). Place the item, and the answer follows."},{"note":"Short answer questions 1 and 2 are the two halves of environmental analysis: **scanning** monitors what is happening now; **forecasting** gives foreknowledge of what lies ahead. Both are needed, which is why §B.4 and §B.5 are separate sections."}]}}]},{"n":"C.1","t":"Nature of social responsibility","b":[{"def":{"t":"Social responsibility (of business)","d":"the obligation of business enterprises to adopt policies/plans of action desirable in terms of societal expectations — since organisations control most of society's financial/intellectual/material resources and significantly affect its future."}},{"def":{"t":"Corporate social responsibility (CSR)","d":"a corporate body's obligation to seek actions protecting/improving society's welfare alongside its own interests (Bartol & Martins, 1994) — or, per Jones (1980), an obligation to constituent groups beyond shareholders and beyond what law/union contract prescribes; to qualify, an action must be **voluntary**."}},{"h4":"4 dimensions of social responsibility"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Responsibility","Description"],"rows":[["**Economic**","Efficiently utilising society's resources (human/material/technological) to maximise output/profit or value-added — ensures the business's own survival/growth while generating jobs and other societal benefits."],["**Legal**","Obeying all laws/regulations of the land, as a good corporate citizen."],["**Ethical**","Beyond legal compliance — the moral responsibility to do what is right and avoid wrong, toward the environment/natural resources/societal values and toward all stakeholders (employees, suppliers, government agencies, etc.)."],["**Discretionary/philanthropic**","Voluntarily using discretion to address societal problems (poverty, poor infrastructure/health/education in host communities) — self-interested too, since it reduces tension with the host community."]]}},{"h4":"C.1.1  Dynamics of social responsibility — 4 evolutionary stages"},{"ol":["**Shareholders** — early focus was maximising profit and satisfying shareholder expectations via dividends;","**Employees** — management sought to attract/retain/motivate staff via improved working conditions, job security, safe/healthy environment, human dignity;","**Customers, suppliers, distributors** — fair prices, high-quality/safe goods, good long-term mutually-beneficial relationships; and","**Society at large** — management recognises responsibility for public good, environmental protection, social/cultural support, and equity/justice."]},{"h4":"C.1.2  Arguments for social responsibility"},{"ol":["public needs/expectations have changed — business must respond, since it takes direction from society;","business itself creates many social problems (pollution, toxic waste), so should help solve them;","a better social environment benefits both society (neighbourhoods, jobs) and business (a healthier community/workforce/customer base);","CSR discourages additional government regulation, giving business greater freedom/flexibility;","businesses, as society members, should do their fair share;","businesses hold substantial financial/technical resources to help solve social problems;","CSR builds favourable public image, attracting customers/employees/investors;","business should try solving problems other institutions could not, given its history of innovation;","business has talented managers/specialists/capital to help solve societal problems;","CSR secures economic survival long-term (educated/healthy employees, spending customers, quality suppliers); and","prevention (via social activity) is cheaper than curing social problems later."]},{"h4":"C.1.3  Arguments against social responsibility"},{"ol":["business's primary task is profit maximisation — social activity sidetracks managers and can reduce economic efficiency;","social-programme participation gives business greater power, possibly at some segment of society's expense;","society ultimately pays for CSR via higher prices;","CSR can weaken the international balance of payments, disadvantaging Nigerian exporters against foreign competitors without such social costs;","business already has enough power — more social involvement increases it further, possibly to society's detriment;","business may lack the expertise/social skills to assess and decide on social problems, given its economic training/experience;","there is a lack of accountability of business to society; and","many feel social problems are government's responsibility — business involvement misallocates scarce resources."]}]},{"n":"C.2","t":"Business ethics","b":[{"def":{"t":"Ethics","d":"from the Greek \"ethos\" (character/conduct/activities based on moral principles) — concerned with right and wrong in human behaviour, against society's accepted standards. An individual's personal belief about whether a behaviour/action/decision is right or wrong (Griffin, 1999)."}},{"p":"Sources of individual ethical standards: family (the \"first school\"), educational/religious institutions, peer influence, professional/trade associations, personal experience, and situational factors."},{"def":{"t":"Business ethics","d":"ethical standards applicable when people engage in business transactions — concerned with truth and justice, and with what is good/bad, right/wrong in business transactions (Weihrich & Koontz, 2001). Ethics go BEYOND the law — standards of behaviour expected of both individuals and the corporate legal person."}},{"h4":"C.2.1  Ethical issues in business"},{"ul":["bribes;","honesty of record keeping;","misappropriation of corporate assets;","confidentiality of corporate information;","political contributions;","conflict of interest;","customer/supplier relationships."]},{"h4":"Ethical issues by stakeholder group"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stakeholder","Ethical concern"],"rows":[["Employees","Fair/just wages, recruitment process, conditions of service, retrenchment, welfare package."],["Labour unions","Honesty in honouring agreements; good negotiation/industrial-action strategy."],["Trade associations","Loyalty and faithfulness in honouring agreements."],["Dealers","Honesty in agreements, engagement/disengagement processes."],["Customer","Product safety, value proposition, truthful advertising."],["Suppliers","Honesty in honouring agreements."],["Competitors","Fair vs. unfair competition."],["Shareholders","Insider trading, truthful stewardship, conflict of interest."],["Creditors","Payment for supplies."],["Government","Tax evasion, economic sabotage, truthful disclosure."],["Society at large","Environmental concerns."],["The firm itself","Confidentiality, loyalty, obedience."]]}},{"h4":"C.2.2  Why some managers behave unethically"},{"p":"Unethical behaviour is behaviour that is not morally honourable, or is legally prohibited — including corruption, mail/wire fraud, discrimination/harassment, insider trading, conflicts of interest, improper use of company assets, bribery/kickbacks, fraud, illegal donations, patent infringement, product liability (Barrcus & Near, 1991). **Today's most common forms**: false communication, collusion, conflicts of interest, gifts/kickbacks, insider trading, discrimination/harassment, embezzlement."},{"key":"Otherwise-honest, dedicated employees behave unethically because of 4 rationalisations: (a) no one will ever find out; (b) the behaviour isn't really illegal; (c) it's in the organisation's best interest; (d) the organisation will protect them."},{"note":"Unethical behaviour's costs are hard to measure but can add over **20%** to the cost of doing business (research estimate) — including low wages, unemployment and poverty."},{"h4":"C.2.3  Causes of unethical behaviour (AMA/HRI study of 1,121 managers and HR experts)"},{"ol":["pressure to meet unrealistic business objectives/targets/deadlines;","desire to further one's career;","desire to protect one's livelihood;","job pressure causing corner-cutting on quality control, cover-ups, lying to customers;","ignorance that acts are unethical, or of the consequences;","competition for scarce resources, power or position;","a few bad employees with poor ethical backgrounds; and","lack of consistent ethical leadership, exposing employees to unethical opportunities."]},{"h4":"5 further root causes"},{"ol":["**Lack of personal ethics** — business ethics is not divorced from personal ethics (from parents, schools, religion, media); a strong personal ethical sense reduces unethical business behaviour;","**Decision-making processes** — people sometimes behave unethically without realising it, simply failing to ask \"is this ethical?\" (Messick and Bazerman, 1996);","**Organisational culture** — a climate that de-emphasises ethics, reducing all decisions to the purely economic;","**Unrealistic performance expectations** — pressure to meet goals attainable only by cutting corners; and","**Bad leadership** — leaders set the example others follow; it is what leaders *do*, not what they say, that matters."]},{"h4":"C.2.4  Guidelines for ethical competence"},{"ol":["**Be proactive** in managing compliance/ethics — controls, processes, accountability, accessibility;","**Adopt and communicate an ethical profile** — a strong, well-communicated code of ethics (policies/procedures);","**Train employees** on compliance policies;","**Adopt a risk-based approach** — identify, prioritise, manage and monitor high-risk areas; and","**Integrate hotlines** with the compliance programme — anonymous reporting of bribery/fraud/ethical violations, tracked from creation to closure."]},{"h4":"C.2.5  Ethical practices peculiar to the accounting profession"},{"note":"This 5-principle framework is the same universal professional-ethics structure examined in the Auditing and Taxation subjects (IFAC/IESBA-derived) — reproduced here in full since Management is assessed as an independent paper."},{"ol":["**Integrity** — straightforward and honest in all professional/business relationships;","**Objectivity** — no bias, conflict of interest, or undue influence from others;","**Professional competence and due care** — a continuing duty to maintain professional knowledge/skill so clients/employers receive competent service based on current practice/legislation/technique developments;","**Confidentiality** — respecting confidential information from professional/business relationships, not disclosing it without permission, and not using it for personal advantage; and","**Professional behaviour** — complying with relevant laws/regulations and avoiding action that discredits the profession."]},{"h4":"C.2.6  Possible sanctions for breach of ethical compliance in the accounting profession"},{"ul":["additional professional education;","reprimand;","monetary fine;","removal of authority to engage in public practice (or specific work areas);","suspension from membership for a specified period;","exclusion from membership;","refund of fee;","work to be completed by another accountant;","payment of the cost of the hearing; and","sanctions against the member firm."]}]},{"n":"C.3","t":"Corporate governance","b":[{"def":{"t":"Corporate governance","d":"the way formally constituted authorities (investors, board of directors) affect an organisation's strategic plans/direction through formalised oversight/supervision of executive decisions and actions — \"the system by which corporate entities' affairs are directed and controlled by those charged with the responsibility\" (Uzuh, 2006); also \"the structures and systems of control by which managers are held accountable to those with a legitimate stake\" (Jacoby, 2005)."}},{"p":"Also viewed as processes/structures put in place by owners and regulatory agencies to moderate executives, improving/sustaining long-term shareholder value through enhanced corporate performance and accountability — mindful of other stakeholders' interests too. It is fundamentally about maximising shareholder value legally, ethically and sustainably, while ensuring fairness to every stakeholder — critical to enhancing/retaining investor trust."},{"key":"Responsive corporate governance requires: transparency/accountability to stakeholders; compliance with applicable legal/regulatory requirements; disclosure of all material information; and firm viability/solvency sustained through adequate internal control and an appropriate management framework."},{"h4":"C.3.1  Benefits of corporate governance"},{"ul":["fairness;","openness/transparency;","independence;","honesty and integrity (probity);","responsibility and accountability;","reputation; and","sound judgment."]},{"h4":"C.3.2  Prerequisites of effective corporate governance in Nigeria"},{"ul":["knowledge;","information;","a strong management team;","power;","auditors;","motivation;","time."]},{"h4":"C.3.3  Weaknesses/problems of corporate governance in Nigeria"},{"ul":["poor integration/development of information technology;","lack of auditor independence/transparency;","pressure from government;","board/management relationship tensions;","board/management disagreement giving rise to board squabbles;","weak internal controls;","passive shareholders;","ineffective management information systems;","technical incompetence;","poor leadership;","non-compliance with internal controls/operational procedures; and","fraudulent/self-serving practices among board, management and staff."]},{"h4":"C.3.4  Codes of best practices on corporate governance in Nigeria"},{"p":"Under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) 1990, directors owe defined duties to their company, including presenting an annual report/accounts to shareholders (accountability). Where corporate-governance LAW is weak (e.g. the UK historically), voluntary codes/guidelines have been issued instead, backed by major financial institutions, stock exchanges and investment organisations. Codes typically cover 7 major areas:"},{"ol":["the role and responsibilities of the board of directors;","the composition and balance of the board;","financial reporting, narrative reporting and auditing;","directors' remuneration;","risk management and internal control;","shareholders' rights; and","corporate social responsibility and ethical behaviour (business ethics)."]},{"h4":"C.3.5  Compliance requirements of corporate governance"},{"key":"The Nigerian Code of Corporate Governance supersedes any prior codes before **17 October 2016**; is **mandatory** for all public companies (listed or not), all private companies that are holding companies/subsidiaries of public companies, and all private companies with **more than 8 employees** that file returns to any regulatory authority other than the (then-)Federal Inland Revenue Service and the Corporate Affairs Commission; sets the **minimum board size at 8 members** (a revision from the earlier SEC/banking-sector codes); and applies to annual reports covering periods beginning on/after 17 October 2016 (earlier voluntary application permitted)."}]},{"n":"C.4","t":"Summary — Section C","b":[{"p":"The section explained the **nature of corporate social responsibility** and its areas. A good debate on **whether organisations should or should not embark on separate social responsibility** was well articulated. **Ethics** was explained, with particular reference to **business ethics**. Three areas of managerial ethics were well discussed. Discussion of the **ethical concerns of managers as they relate to different publics** was given some attention. **Ethical practices peculiar to the accounting profession** were highlighted. **Corporate governance**, its meaning and the need for it, were given good attention."}]},{"n":"C.5","t":"Review questions — Section C, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section C review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Corporate governance scope includes the following **EXCEPT** **(a)** The role and responsibility of the board of directors; **(b)** The composition and balance of the board of directors; **(c)** Financial reporting, narrative reporting and auditing; **(d)** Directors' remuneration; **(e)** Recruitment, selection and placement.","A set of generally accepted standards of social conduct is known as: **(a)** Ethics; **(b)** Equi-finality; **(c)** Guidelines; **(d)** Uncertainty; **(e)** Mutuality of meaning.","Which of these should **NOT** be paid attention to by managers with respect to corporate social responsibility as it relates to minorities and backward communities? **(a)** Equal employment opportunity; **(b)** Use of expertise to solve local problems; **(c)** Locating plants and offices in minority areas.","Which of these is **NOT** an area of concern to managers as regards corporate social responsibility? **(a)** Government relations; **(b)** Consumption; **(c)** Family relations; **(d)** Shareholders relations; **(e)** Labour relations.","Which of these is **NOT** why managers behave unethically? **(a)** Personal ethics; **(b)** Organisation culture; **(c)** Labour unions; **(d)** Leadership; **(e)** Decision making process."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The set of obligations an organisation has to protect and provide for the society in which it functions is called ………","The general guidelines with respect to the values and ethical standards that can be used by managers to guide their decisions is known as ………","……… is defined as the system by which the affairs of corporate entities are directed and controlled by those charged with the responsibility.","……… is an individual's personal belief about whether a behaviour, action or decision is right or wrong.","……… refers to the values and norms shared among employees of an organisation."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["**E — Recruitment, selection and placement.** That is a human resource function, not part of the scope of corporate governance. The board's role and responsibilities, its composition and balance, financial and narrative reporting and auditing, and directors' remuneration are all within scope (§C.3).","**A — Ethics.** Ethics is the set of generally accepted standards of social conduct — and note the contrast with short answer question 4, where ethics is defined as an **individual's personal belief** about right and wrong. The study text uses the word at both the social and the individual level (§C.2).","**B — Use of expertise to solve local problems.**","**C — Family relations.** Government relations, consumption, shareholder relations and labour relations are all areas of CSR concern; family relations is a private matter, not a corporate social responsibility (§C.1).","**C — Labour unions.** The causes of unethical behaviour are personal ethics, organisational culture, leadership, the decision-making process and pressure to meet unrealistic targets (§C.2.2 and §C.2.3). Labour unions are not among them."]},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Social responsibility**","**Code of ethics**","**Corporate governance**","**Ethics**","**Culture**"]},{"warn":"A defect in the printed question 3, flagged rather than silently corrected: it offers **only three options, (a) to (c)**, where every other question in the bank offers five. The printed answer is **(b)**, \"use of expertise to solve local problems\" — which is puzzling, since using corporate expertise to solve local problems is a standard CSR activity towards a community, while equal employment opportunity and locating plants in minority areas are equally standard. With two options missing it is not possible to reconstruct what the question intended; the printed answer is reproduced for completeness."},{"key":"Short answer questions 1 to 5 are the five definitions this section turns on, and they are worth learning verbatim: **social responsibility** (the set of obligations to protect and provide for society), a **code of ethics** (general guidelines on values and ethical standards to guide managers' decisions), **corporate governance** (the system by which corporate affairs are directed and controlled by those charged with the responsibility), **ethics** (an individual's belief about whether conduct is right or wrong), and **culture** (the values and norms shared among employees)."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter has 3 self-contained sections, each worth knowing precisely because the source itself examines each section separately (own MCQs/short-answer questions). Section A: memorise the 4 size-based enterprise categories with their exact naira thresholds, and the features/advantages/disadvantages table for each ownership form — exam questions often hinge on distinguishing e.g. \"unlimited liability\" appearing in BOTH sole-proprietorship and partnership but NOT corporation/cooperative. Section B: PESITEL is the single most examinable mnemonic in the chapter — know each letter's full word AND which of the 5 task-environment forces is NOT part of PESITEL (task vs. general environment is a common confusion point). Section C: the 4 dimensions of social responsibility (economic/legal/ethical/philanthropic) and the 5-principle accounting ethics framework are both prime recall targets, as is the 2016 Code of Corporate Governance's exact minimum-board-size (8) and effective date (17 October 2016).","errors":["Confusing the TASK environment's 5 forces (customers, suppliers, substitutes, competitors, labour supply) with the GENERAL/PESITEL environment's 7 elements — the task environment is direct-action and firm-specific; PESITEL is indirect-action and common to every firm in the economy.","Treating \"unlimited liability\" as unique to sole proprietorship — it applies to BOTH sole proprietorship and (general) partnership; only the corporation and cooperative offer limited liability.","Mixing up the 4 social-responsibility dimensions with the 4 dynamics/stages of social responsibility — the dimensions (economic/legal/ethical/philanthropic) describe TYPES of responsibility; the dynamics (shareholders→employees→customers/suppliers→society) describe the HISTORICAL EVOLUTION of whom responsibility is owed to.","Assuming the Code of Corporate Governance's 8-employee/8-board-member thresholds are the same number by coincidence rather than two independent, separately-stated requirements (>8 employees triggers mandatory compliance for a private company; a board must have a MINIMUM of 8 members once the Code applies)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is the primary objective of business?","o":["Radical practice of communication","Capitalising on the principle of equity","Investing minimally in stocks and bonds","Profit maximisation","Identifying and effectively serving the needs of the board of directors"],"a":3,"w":"The main/primary objective of a business enterprise is to make profit — a reward to owners for risk-taking, and the source of growth and continued survival.","src":"Chapter 1, Section A Review Questions","sec":"A.2"},{"q":"All of the following are advantages of sole proprietorship EXCEPT","o":["Lack of continuity","Easy formation","Benefits of small scale operations","Inexpensive management","Better control"],"a":0,"w":"\"Lack of continuity\" is a DISADVANTAGE of sole proprietorship, not an advantage — the advantages are easy formation, benefits of small-scale operations, inexpensive management, and better control.","src":"Chapter 1, Section A Review Questions","sec":"A.6"},{"q":"The elements of the general/mega business environment are summarised by which acronym?","o":["SWOT","PESITEL","CAVEBOP","STEEPLE","PESTLE"],"a":1,"w":"PESITEL stands for Political, Economic, Socio-cultural, International, Technological, Ecological, and Legal environment.","src":"Chapter 1, Section B","sec":"B.2"},{"q":"The monitoring of external environmental forces to determine the source of a firm's opportunities and threats is known as","o":["Regression analysis","Environmental analysis","TOWS analysis","Critical path analysis","SWOT analysis"],"a":1,"w":"Environmental analysis (comprising environmental scanning plus understanding trends/implications) is the process of monitoring external forces to identify a firm's opportunities and threats.","src":"Chapter 1, Section B Review Questions","sec":"B.3"},{"q":"Corporate governance scope includes all of the following EXCEPT","o":["The role and responsibility of the board of directors","The composition and balance of the board of directors","Financial reporting, narrative reporting and auditing","Director's remuneration","Recruitment, selection and placement"],"a":4,"w":"Recruitment, selection and placement is a human-resources function, not one of the 7 areas typically covered by corporate governance codes (board role/composition, financial/narrative reporting and auditing, directors' remuneration, risk management/internal control, shareholders' rights, and CSR/business ethics).","src":"Chapter 1, Section C Review Questions","sec":"C.3"},{"q":"What is the minimum number of members a Nigerian company's board of directors must have, under the Code of Corporate Governance effective from 17 October 2016?","o":["3","5","6","8","10"],"a":3,"w":"The 2016 Code of Corporate Governance sets the minimum board size at 8 members — a revision from the earlier SEC/banking-sector governance codes.","src":"Chapter 1, Section C","sec":"C.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between a sole proprietorship and a partnership in terms of (a) how they are formed, and (b) the liability borne by their owners.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"**(a) Formation**: a sole proprietorship is owned/managed/controlled by ONE individual, and given the proprietor's high independence, can be formed without any written agreement, charter, or legally binding documentation. A partnership requires TWO OR MORE individuals agreeing to organise/operate jointly, with the terms/conditions usually stated in a written Partnership Deed specifying each partner's duties and rights."},{"p":"**(b) Liability**: BOTH forms carry unlimited liability for their owner(s) — in a sole proprietorship, the proprietor alone bears all losses/liabilities and may need to sell personal assets; in a partnership, if partnership assets are insufficient, the PRIVATE property of the partners can be taken to defray the firm's liabilities. The key difference is that a partnership's unlimited liability is typically shared/joint among multiple partners, while a sole proprietor bears it entirely alone."}],"src":"Chapter 1, Section A","sec":"A.6"},{"q":"Using the PESITEL framework, briefly explain any 4 elements of the general/mega business environment.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Any 4 of: **Political** — government structures for law/order, security, infrastructure, and policies/regulations shaping business operations; instability creates policy uncertainty. **Economic** — fiscal/monetary policy, GDP, inflation, exchange rate, interest rate and related factors operating simultaneously to affect decision-making. **Socio-cultural** — demographic, religious, ethnic characteristics and the values/morals/norms controlling behavioural patterns in society. **International** — elements on the global scene (imported inputs, exports, ILO/WTO standards) affecting domestic decisions. **Technological** — the state of scientific/mechanical application to production and distribution methods. **Ecological** — the physical/environmental impact of business operations (emissions, oil spillage, mining damage). **Legal/public policy** — laws, regulations and licensing requirements businesses must comply with."}],"src":"Chapter 1, Section B","sec":"B.2"}]}},{"n":2,"t":"Management Theories and Thoughts","brief":"The definitional and historical core of the whole subject — what management is, its functions/levels/skills/roles, the art-vs-science and universality/transferability questions, how managerial performance is measured, current managerial trends, and a full chronological tour of the evolution of management thought: pre-classical, scientific, administrative (Fayol), bureaucracy (Weber), human relations (Mayo), quantitative, and the contemporary decision/systems/contingency theories, closing with emerging trends.","outcomes":["Define management","Explain the nature, objectives and purpose of management","Explain management as a technique, process, discipline, and profession","Explain management functions, levels, skills and roles","Explain management as an art or science","Explain the universality and transferability of management","Explain measures of managerial performance — productivity, effectiveness and efficiency","Explain current managerial trends: globalisation, quality, environmentalism, diversity of workforce, ethical reawakening","Discuss the schools of management thought: pre-classical, classical, behavioural, quantitative, contemporary, and recent/emerging management thoughts"],"secs":[{"n":"2.0","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Management plays a pivotal role in business operations — achieving organisational goals requires guiding, directing and coordinating others' efforts and constructively channelling their potential. Management is thus \"the activity of getting things done through, and with, other people.\" How well an organisation achieves its objectives depends largely on its managers."}]},{"n":"2.1","t":"Definitions of management","b":[{"def":{"t":"Management","d":"the process of planning, organising, directing and controlling the resources of an organisation to achieve its goals efficiently."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Author","Definition"],"rows":[["Miner (1978)","The process of planning, organising, actuating and controlling an organisation's operations to achieve a coordination of human and material resources essential to the effective, efficient attainment of objectives."],["Stoner (1978)","The process of planning, organising, leading and controlling the work of members of an organisation, using all available organisational resources to reach stated organisational goals."],["Griffin (1999)","A set of activities (planning and decision making, organising, leading, controlling) directed at an organisation's resources (human, financial, information) to achieve organisational goals efficiently and effectively."]]}},{"key":"These definitions highlight 6 shared concepts: **a process** (4 interrelated activities — planning, organising, directing, controlling); **resources** (human, financial, physical, information); **efficiency** (cost-effective resource use); **effectiveness** (making and implementing the right decisions); **the manager** (the person carrying out the management process); and the maxim that **the efficient manager does things right, and the effective manager does the right things.**"}]},{"n":"2.2","t":"Nature, objectives and purpose of management","b":[{"h4":"2.2.1  Nature of management"},{"ol":["**Universal activity** — Fayol's 14 principles apply equally across organisations, businesses, government departments; the managerial job is basically the same at every level and institution type;","**Continuous process** — the management cycle operates as long as the organisation exists, making small progressive changes for greater long-run value;","**Purposeful/goal-oriented** — management exists solely to achieve organisational goals, directing all human-resource energy toward predetermined goals;","**Social process** — done by, through and for people; concerned with making the best use of human effort and creating cooperation among employees, customers, investors, shareholders, community and other stakeholders;","**Concerned with group effort** — requires group effort toward common objectives; about getting things done rather than doing them personally;","**Intangible** — an unseen, invisible force whose presence is felt through results (targets met, satisfied employees);","**Involves decision-making** — rational/sound decision-making is a primary management function; every manager takes countless decisions, consciously or subconsciously;","**An integrating process** — brings together human, physical and financial resources to achieve organisational purpose, bringing harmony between factors;","**Coordinates all activities and resources** — integrating activities to ensure resources are used most efficiently toward the set goal;","**Dynamic, not rigid** — must remain equipped to face social/economic/technological/political/human changes;","**Both an art and a science** — a systematic body of theoretical knowledge PLUS the practical experience of applying it; and","**Creative** — attaining organisational objectives at highest efficiency and minimum cost, optimally utilising available resources (effectiveness and efficiency are the two pillars of managerial performance)."]},{"h4":"2.2.2  Objectives of management"},{"p":"The main objective is securing maximum output with minimum effort/resources — thinking and utilising human, material and financial resources for the best combination. Overriding objectives: make strategic plans; ensure organisational goals/targets are met at least cost/minimum waste; make proper use of available resources; ensure business development/growth; provide quality products/services; ensure goods/services availability; look after staff health/welfare/safety; ensure workplace discipline; protect organisational machinery/resources (including human resources); attract the best job candidates; and reduce workplace risk."},{"h4":"2.2.3  Purpose of management"},{"p":"To plan, direct, organise and ensure business success at various levels — via customer satisfaction, employee training, and other methods. A manager is responsible for the tiniest operational details (hours, scheduling, training, customer handling not delegable to other staff) — though the title \"manager\" varies by organisation (team leader, coordinator, etc.)."}]},{"n":"2.3","t":"Management as a technique, process, discipline and profession","b":[{"h4":"2.3.1 – 2.3.3  Management as a technique, as a process, and as a discipline and profession"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Perspective","Description"],"rows":[["**As a technique**","Connotes materials, methods and knowledge (technical know-how) — a unique, effective way of doing/getting things done, with a proper mix of labour skill/materials/machine hours/financial input transferable to similar tasks."],["**As a process**","Certain functions/work activities managers must perform to achieve enterprise goals — the 5 fundamental functions (planning, organising, staffing, leading, controlling) ARE the process, the means by which a manager manages."],["**As a discipline/profession**","Management is studied as a specialised career (Business Administration, Business Management, Management Technology, etc.) through undergraduate and postgraduate study."]]}},{"h4":"Characteristics of a profession"},{"ul":["**Systematic body of knowledge** — expert knowledge in a particular discipline;","**Formal education** — from a recognised institution;","**Social responsibility** — not purely profit-focused, follows rules for social responsibility;","**Independent office** — practised from an independent office;","**Specialisation** — in a particular field; and","**Fees/licensing** — requires a licence/permission to practice."]},{"note":"Management has developed as a modern profession because: it is a systematic body of knowledge; there are formal institutional methods to acquire its knowledge/skill; professional management consultancy is on the rise; and there is a growing need for honesty in its practice."}]},{"n":"2.4","t":"Management functions","b":[{"p":"Management breaks down into 5 (sometimes stated as 6, including coordination as its own function) managerial functions: planning, organising, staffing, leading, controlling — and coordination."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","Description"],"rows":[["**Planning**","Selecting missions/objectives and the actions to achieve them — setting organisational goals and deciding a course of action (selecting a course IS decision-making). Futuristic — identifying future opportunities/threats and deciding present action. No real plan exists until a decision (commitment of human/material resources) has been made."],["**Organising**","Grouping tasks and assigning resources needed for a specific job — establishing an internal structure of roles, grouping activities into manageable departments/work units, establishing authority/reporting relationships. Decides who does what, where, and when (\"organisational design and structure\")."],["**Staffing**","A continuous, vital function — procuring suitable personnel to man jobs once objectives/activities have been identified/grouped. Involves identifying workforce requirements, inventorying available people, and recruiting/selecting/placing/promoting/appraising/career-planning/compensating/training/developing candidates and jobholders (also covers disengagement)."],["**Leading**","The most challenging function — processes getting people to work together toward set goals: influencing, motivating, communicating and directing human resources, establishing an environment where employees willingly achieve goals."],["**Controlling**","Monitoring organisational progress toward goals — measuring/correcting subordinates' activities to ensure conformity with plans. 4 elements: (i) establish performance standards, (ii) measure current performance, (iii) compare performance to standards, (iv) take corrective action on deviation."],["**Coordination**","The essence of \"manager-ship\" — achieving harmony among individual efforts toward group goals. Every other managerial function contributes to it; the manager's central task is reconciling differences in approach/timing/effort/interest to harmonise individual and organisational goals."]]}},{"note":"The book divides this section into **§2.4.1 Planning, §2.4.2 Organizing, §2.4.3 Staffing, §2.4.4 Leading, §2.4.5 Controlling and §2.4.6 Coordination** — the six rows of the table above, in that order. Its own contents list numbers both Controlling and Coordination **2.4.5**, a duplication the body corrects."}]},{"n":"2.5","t":"Levels of management","b":[{"p":"Management levels vary by organisation size, nature, complexity, technology, employee count and management attitude — but most enterprises have 3 distinct, overlapping levels: top, middle and lower/first-line, with authority decreasing (and manager numbers increasing) at each lower level."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Level","Who/what, and functions"],"rows":[["**Top-level management**","Corporate officers elected by the board — chairman, president, executive VP, general managers/deputies. Chief policy-making officers, concerned with the organisation as a whole and its environment interaction. Functions: determining company objectives; long-range planning; establishing board policies; coordinating enterprise activities; adapting control methods; authorising new facilities/R&D projects; appointing top officers."],["**Middle-level management**","Above supervisors, below top managers, reporting directly to top level — departmental heads (finance, production, marketing, personnel, purchasing managers, chief engineer, plant manager). Acts as a buffer between top managers and supervisors; spends most time analysing data, preparing decision-making information, translating top-level policy into operational procedures. Functions: integrating different work-group activities; preparing departmental plans/programmes; executing plans; assisting top managers; communicating policy; transferring information/materials between work groups; managing/controlling lower-level managers; coordinating; and feeding decision-relevant information upward."],["**First-line/lower-level management**","Supervisors, foremen, chief clerks — coordinate subordinates' work, need technical skill to guide them. Handle day-to-day operational planning, job assignment, activity oversight, performance evaluation, and act as the employee-to-middle-management link. Functions: planning section work; directing rank-and-file workers toward departmental goals; ensuring productivity; maintaining discipline/order."]]}},{"note":"The book's sub-sections are **§2.5.1 Top level management, §2.5.2 Middle level management and §2.5.3 First-line or lower level management** — the three levels in the table above."}]},{"n":"2.6","t":"Management skills","b":[{"h4":"The 4 management skills"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Skill","Description"],"rows":[["**Technical**","Knowledge/proficiency in methods, processes, procedures — working with tools and specific techniques (e.g. mechanics, accountants)."],["**Human/interpersonal**","The ability to work with people — communicate, motivate, influence, and create an environment where people feel secure/free to express themselves and realise their potential."],["**Conceptual**","The ability to see the \"big picture,\" recognise significant elements in a situation, and understand relationships among them."],["**Diagnostic and analytical**","The ability to distinguish cause from effect and solve problems beneficially — not just seeing a problem, but working out a practical solution."]]}},{"key":"Skill importance shifts by level: **first-line** managers need technical skills most (human skills help too; conceptual/diagnostic skills less critical); **middle** managers need less technical skill, essential human skills, and growing conceptual skill; **top** managers need strong conceptual/analytical AND human skills, with relatively little need for technical ability (subordinates supply that)."}]},{"n":"2.7","t":"Managerial roles","b":[{"h4":"Managerial roles (Mintzberg, 1973)"},{"p":"After studying 5 executives, Mintzberg concluded managers perform **10 interrelated roles**, grouped into 3 categories:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category / Role","Description"],"rows":[["**Interpersonal — Figurehead**","Ceremonial duties — attending important ceremonies, entertaining dignitaries, attending to important customers."],["**Interpersonal — Leader**","Hiring, training, motivating and directing subordinates' activities toward organisational goals."],["**Interpersonal — Liaison**","Developing/maintaining contact with people/groups outside the organisation to coordinate efforts toward a common goal."],["**Informational — Monitor**","Seeking and receiving relevant information from internal and external sources."],["**Informational — Disseminator**","Transmitting relevant information to superiors, peers or subordinates who need it."],["**Informational — Spokesperson**","Transmitting information to outside people/groups — e.g. advising shareholders on financial performance, assuring groups the organisation meets its societal obligations."],["**Decisional — Entrepreneur**","Generating new ideas, initiating new projects, seeking/identifying improvement opportunities, developing/implementing change strategy."],["**Decisional — Disturbance handler**","Taking action to resolve important, unexpected disturbances — strikes, natural disasters, accidents."],["**Decisional — Resource allocator**","Allocating scarce resources across units — developing/monitoring budgets, forecasting future resource needs/acquisition problems."],["**Decisional — Negotiator**","Negotiating with internal/external stakeholders — e.g. representing the corporation in a trade-union agreement or joint-venture contract."]]}},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section is printed in the body as **\"2.20 Managerial roles\"**, although its own sub-sections immediately below it are numbered **2.7.1 Interpersonal roles, 2.7.2 Information roles and 2.7.3 Decisional roles** — and a genuine §2.20 (Contingency theory) appears later in the chapter. It is **§2.7**, as its sub-sections and the chapter contents list both confirm."}]},{"n":"2.8","t":"Management as an art or a science","b":[{"h4":"2.8.1 – 2.8.2  Management as a science, and management as an art"},{"p":"**As a science**: science is an organised, systematic body of knowledge acquired through observation/experimentation, based on universal principles. Management qualifies as a science (systematised knowledge, observation, testing, experiment) but is not EXACT like physics/chemistry/biology. Science has 3 key characteristics: it is a systematised body of knowledge using scientific observation methods; its principles evolve from continued observation/experiment; and its principles are exact with universal applicability, without limitation."},{"p":"The scientific-theory process: (a) formulating a problem from observation; (b) constructing a theory from inductive observation; (c) deducing specific hypotheses from the theory; (d) recasting the hypothesis into testable measures/operations; (e) devising the actual test situation; (f) actual testing, which does or does not confirm the theory."},{"p":"**As an art**: art is personal skill — bringing about desired results through skill application (George R. Terry). Management is one of the most creative arts, requiring extensive knowledge. It is an art because: it is creative; it involves skill use; it involves technical know-how; it is results-directed; and it is personalised. A manager's creativity/productivity can be magnified using correct scientific methods — success depends on how effectively scientific aid enhances artistic skill."},{"key":"Art has 5 essential features: **practical knowledge**, **personal skill**, **concrete result**, **constructive skill**, and **improvement through practice**. Management is thus correctly understood as BOTH an art and a science, not exclusively one or the other."}]},{"n":"2.9","t":"Universality and transferability of management","b":[{"h4":"2.9.1  Universality of management"},{"def":{"t":"Universality of management","d":"management principles/techniques apply not just to business, but to ALL organisations regardless of sector — the same functions used in business apply to civic/religious groups, sports leagues, political campaigns, etc. (Griffin, 1999). The essence — integrating human and other resources for effective performance — is universal in aim and process."}},{"h4":"2.9.2  Transferability of management"},{"def":{"t":"Transferability of management","d":"the ability/skill of management is transferable from one organisation, industry, country or job field to another — the basic aspects (planning, organising, staffing, directing, coordinating) transcend all cultural/organisational boundaries."}},{"note":"A manager should expect SOME problems transferring a management system across contexts (military→business, private→public sector, education→business, or between departments), given fundamental differences in how managerial activities are carried out — but universality and transferability work hand-in-hand, meaning someone with management skill can generally work almost anywhere."}]},{"n":"2.10","t":"Measures of managerial performance: productivity, effectiveness and efficiency","b":[{"p":"**Productivity** matters at the national level too — high productivity increases business profits, expands the tax base, and expands employment avenues."},{"h4":"2.10.1  Productivity"},{"def":{"t":"Productivity","d":"the relationship between units produced and the quantity of (human/material) inputs used to produce them — output per unit of investment."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Productivity type","Description"],"rows":[["**Labour productivity**","Total net output achieved per worker — high when the output/labour-input ratio is high (efficient labour utilisation) and low when the ratio is low (inefficient utilisation)."],["**Capital productivity**","How well physical capital is used in providing goods/services — output relative to capital input."],["**Material productivity**","Output (net sales) divided by material input — the output received, in constant prices, per unit of material input."]]}},{"h4":"2.10.2  Effectiveness"},{"def":{"t":"Effectiveness","d":"the ability to choose appropriate objectives, or the appropriate MEANS for achieving a given objective — selecting the right things to do, or the right method. Drucker (1970): \"effectiveness rather than efficiency is essential to business — the question is not how to do things right, but how to find the right things to do.\" Agbonifoh (2005): \"an organisation can be effective only if it is doing those things that are relevant and crucial to its purposes.\""}},{"h4":"2.10.3  Efficiency"},{"def":{"t":"Efficiency","d":"the ability to get things done correctly — an input-output concept. An efficient manager achieves outputs/results that measure up to the inputs (labour, material, time) used. Griffin (1990): being efficient means using resources wisely and cost-effectively."}},{"p":"Per Agbonifoh (2005), great efficiency arises via: achieving MORE results with the same input levels; achieving the SAME results with LESS input; increasing results with a LESS-than-proportionate input increase; or decreasing results with a MORE-than-proportionate input decrease."},{"key":"A manager's responsibilities require BOTH efficiency and effectiveness — but **effectiveness is critical**: a company doing the RIGHT (effective) things is better off, however inefficiently, than one efficiently producing the WRONG products. Customer satisfaction is the crucial reason management must ensure its organisation satisfies customers and other stakeholders."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section is printed as **\"2.9 Measures of Managerial Performance\"**, duplicating the number of §2.9 (Universality and transferability) immediately before it — and its first sub-section is printed as **\"2.9.1 Productivity\"** while the next two are correctly numbered **2.10.2 Effectiveness** and **2.10.3 Efficiency**. The section is §2.10, as those two sub-sections and the chapter contents list confirm."}]},{"n":"2.11","t":"Current managerial trends","b":[{"h4":"2.11.1  Globalisation"},{"p":"Integrating a country's economy with the world economy — worldwide economic integration and growing interdependence via the flow of goods, services, capital, people and information. Key drivers: ICT innovation, e-commerce/online payment advancement, smartphones/devices, transport technology improvements, international job mobility, new immigration laws, and workplace/inter-cultural diversity/integration. Globalisation has been broadly beneficial (jobs, poverty reduction, improved living standards) but has real costs, especially debated recently in ADVANCED (not just developing) economies over lost production/markets to developing nations."},{"h4":"2.11.2  Quality"},{"p":"Quality directly affects product/service performance and is closely linked to customer value/satisfaction — narrowly \"freedom from defects,\" but customer-centred firms define it in terms of customer satisfaction instead. Product-quality dimensions: performance, features, portability, durability, serviceability, conformance, aesthetics. Service-quality dimensions: tangibility, reliability, empathy, assurance, response."},{"p":"**Total Quality Management (TQM)**: a commitment to improve quality across every operational aspect — applying quantitative methods and human resources to improve all processes and exceed current/future customer needs. Key planning notes: higher market share + better quality earns much higher returns than competitors; quality and market share each independently relate to profitability; product-quality planning must meet customer needs, not just specifications; and for some products plan for perfection, for others plan for value."},{"p":"**Customer satisfaction** compares perceived product/service performance against customer expectations: performance below expectations → dissatisfaction; matching expectations → satisfaction; exceeding expectations → delight."},{"h4":"2.11.3  Environmentalism"},{"p":"A movement to improve/protect the natural environment via changes to environmentally-harmful human activity, adopting more environmentally-benign forms of political/economic/social organisation, and reassessing humanity's relationship with nature. Split into **anthropocentric** (\"human-centred\" — focuses on environmental degradation's effects on human health/recreation/quality of life, treating human obligations to nature as derived indirectly from human-to-human obligations) and **biocentric** (\"life-centred\") camps — also called \"shallow\" vs. \"deep\" ecology, or \"technocentrism\" vs. \"ecocentrism.\""},{"p":"**Environmental management**: a corporate strategy that monitors/develops/implements an organisation's environmental policies (summarising, monitoring, reporting) — gaining prominence as consumers seek eco-friendly/eco-aware products. Concerned with marine/land/atmospheric conditions, deforestation, global warming, and carbon footprint reduction. Objectives: identify/solve environmental issues; establish limits against overuse; renew/minimise natural-resource use; regenerate degraded environments; review organisational environmental goals; set environmental-impact-minimisation targets; ensure organisation-wide environmental awareness; make existing technology eco-friendly; encourage resource-conservation programmes; develop quality-of-life improvement strategies; implement environmental-protection measures; and develop sustainable-development policy."},{"h4":"2.11.4  Growing diversity of the workplace"},{"def":{"t":"Diversity","d":"the human characteristics that make people different — recognising individual/group differences (visible and non-visible) and creating a culture/practices that recognise, respect and value difference. Spans race, ethnicity, gender, socio-economic status, physical features, religious beliefs, marriage systems and political ideologies."}},{"p":"**Diversity management**: the ongoing process of incorporating workforce/customer-difference recognition into all core business functions, communications, processes and services, to build a fair, harmonious, inclusive, creative, effective organisation. Diversity management: encourages full staff inclusion/participation; actively capitalises on a diverse workforce's benefits; and ensures compliance with legal obligations (safety, equal employment opportunity)."},{"h4":"2.11.5  Ethical reawakening"},{"p":"Ethics concerns right/wrong human conduct given specific circumstances; business ethics addresses this within commerce/organisational conduct. Management concerns how decisions affect the company; ethics concerns how decisions affect everything — management therefore operates as PART of ethics, in the firm's specialised context, while ethics operates in the world's general context."},{"p":"Basic motives for crossing the ethical line: fear of losing one's job, time/superior pressure to produce results, career ambition, revenge for a perceived wrong, and a tendency to ignore action consequences."},{"h4":"Factors affecting ethical behaviour"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Factor","Detail"],"rows":[["**Stage of moral development (Kohlberg)**","Stage 1 pre-conventional (rule-following); Stage 2 conventional (living up to others' expectations); Stage 3 principled (following a self-chosen path, respecting others)."],["**Individual characteristics**","Values (knowing right from wrong); ego strength (power of one's convictions); locus of control (internal — you control your own destiny; external — you believe you have no control)."],["**Structural factors**","An organisation's structure (clear ethical statements, policies, regulations) affects people's ethical behaviour."],["**Organisational culture**","Shared values/norms among an organisation's people — a strong culture exerts more influence than a weak one."],["**Issue intensity**","How important an issue is — the same act (theft) carries different ethical weight depending on scale (a private local call vs. embezzling US$1 million)."]]}}]},{"n":"2.12","t":"Evolution of schools of management thoughts","b":[{"p":"Management thought dates back to when people first tried achieving goals through group work — though modern OPERATIONAL management theory begins in the early 20th century, serious thinking/theorising predates it by many years. This chapter follows a roughly chronological order: pre-scientific management, scientific management, Fayol's administrative management, Weber's bureaucracy, human relations theory, systems theory, and contingency theory — each contributor's ideas reflecting their own assumptions/knowledge/experience (Inegbenebor, 2005)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Pre-classical thinker","Contribution"],"rows":[["**Robert Owen (1771-1858)**","Believed worker performance was shaped by the total work environment; worked for cooperation between workers/management; treated workers as human beings; argued investment in people is more profitable than in machinery. Introduced shorter working hours, worker housing, workers' children's education, canteens, rest houses, hygiene training — a prelude to the behavioural approach."],["**Charles Babbage (1792-1891)**","Cambridge mathematics professor (1828-1839); applied science/mathematics to factory operations — a pioneer of operations research and industrial engineering. Emphasised specialisation, work measurement, optimum machine utilisation, cost reduction and wage incentives, laying the foundation for a science of management."],["**Henry Varnum Poor**","Advocated a \"management system\" with clear organisational structure and full accountability, plus operating reports summarising revenues/rates. A strong advocate of effective leadership overcoming routine/dullness via unity, effective work habits, esprit de corps — called for systematic management BEFORE Taylor, human-factor recognition BEFORE Mayo, and overcoming formal-organisation rigidity BEFORE Chris Argyris."],["**Captain Henry Metcalfe (1847-1917)**","Published \"The Cost of Manufacture of Workshop: New System Controls,\" proposing that: the science of management rests on principles evolved from recorded observation/experience; management's art should rest on systematically-presented accumulated observations; management should base cost estimates on such observations; only relevant crucial information should be retained; and a manager should detail work to communicate to foremen/workers. Devised a time-card/material-card system assuring workers their good work would be recognised by management."]]}}]},{"n":"2.13","t":"Pre-classical management thoughts","b":[{"p":"The pre-classical contributors — set out in the table at §2.12 — are the book's **§2.13.1 Robert Owen (1771–1858)**, **§2.13.2 Charles Babbage (1792–1891)**, **§2.13.3 Henry Vamun Poor** and **§2.13.4 Captain Henry Metcalfe (1847–1917)**. They precede, and prepare the ground for, the scientific management movement of §2.14."},{"key":"What links the four is that each identified a management problem before there was a discipline to address it: **Owen** the welfare and productivity of labour, **Babbage** the division of labour and the application of mathematics to operations, **Poor** the need for organisation, communication and information in running a large enterprise (the railroads), and **Metcalfe** the systematic use of cost and control data. Taylor's scientific management at §2.14 is the systematisation of exactly these concerns."}]},{"n":"2.14","t":"Scientific management","b":[{"p":"Concerned with improving productivity by improving individual workers' performance standards, established through systematic observation/experiment/reasoning. Key advocates: Taylor, the Gilbreths, Gantt, and Emerson — Taylor played the dominant role."},{"h4":"2.14.1  Frederick Winslow Taylor (1856–1915)"},{"p":"As a Midvale Steel Company foreman, Taylor observed employees deliberately working below capability. He studied/timed each element of steelworkers' jobs, determined optimal output per worker, designed the most efficient method for each work component, and implemented a piece-work wage system linking earnings to production."},{"key":"Taylor's principles: (a) replace rules of thumb with scientific principles to determine the best method for each task, and scientifically select each worker for the task they suit best; (b) divide responsibility for managing vs. working, and devise scientific education/training programmes; (c) ensure worker-manager cooperation, reinforcing optimal results scientifically; (d) provide incentives via the piece-rate system."},{"h4":"2.14.2 – 2.14.4  Frank and Lillian Gilbreth, Henry L. Gantt, and Harrington Emerson"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Other scientific-management pioneer","Contribution"],"rows":[["**Frank and Lillian Gilbreth**","Time-and-motion study pioneers — developed job-motion classifications called \"Therbligs\" (Gilbreth spelt backwards); filmed workers, breaking tasks frame-by-frame into component movements to find better ways to perform each and reorganise them for maximum efficiency. Following Frank's 1924 death, Lillian continued for ~50 years, emphasising worker concern and contributing to fatigue/boredom/morale research — becoming known as the \"First Lady of Management.\""],["**Henry L. Gantt (1861-1919)**","Worked with Taylor at Midvale/Bethlehem Steel; invented the **Gantt chart** (a graphic work-flow representation for scheduling/controlling work, still used today, showing scheduled vs. actual times); devised an incentive bonus system for completing jobs faster than allowed — similar to modern gain-sharing."],["**Harrington Emerson (1853-1931)**","A pioneer of industrial engineering; published *The Twelve Principles of Efficiency* (1912), covering clearly-defined ideals, common sense, competent counsel, discipline, the fair deal, reliable/immediate/adequate records, despatching, standards and schedules, standardised conditions, standardised operations, written standard-practice instructions, and efficiency-reward. Nearly 200 companies adopted features of his Efficiency system."]]}},{"h4":"2.14.5  Contributions of scientific management theory"},{"ul":["inspired the modern assembly line;","its efficiency techniques spread to non-industrial contexts (fast-food to surgeon training);","drove specialisation and departmental division of labour as industry grew;","formulated work standards;","developed wage incentives to motivate employees; and","linked economic rewards to individual performance."]},{"h4":"2.14.6  Limitations of scientific management"},{"ul":["applicable mainly to predictable/stable environments, limited in complex/unpredictable ones;","over-emphasised man's economic/rational nature, overstating the desire for money;","overlooked human behaviour, job satisfaction and workers' social needs as a group;","over-emphasis on productivity/profitability led some managers to exploit workers/customers;","assumed manager-worker interests were automatically complementary, which was unrealistic;","made wage bargaining difficult — workers simply accepted what management deemed fair;","excluded employees from management participation, keeping planning/control solely with management; and","overlooked the positive role of trade unions/informal groups, insisting on individual-basis dealings with workers."]}]},{"n":"2.15","t":"Administrative management theory","b":[{"p":"Developed alongside scientific management, this theory \"emphasises management functions and attempts to generate broad administrative principles as guidelines for rationalising activities.\" Principal contributors: Henri Fayol (1949), Mooney and Reiley (1939), Gulick and Urwick (1937). Administrative theorists viewed productivity improvement \"top down\" (vs. Taylor's \"bottom up\" scientific approach), laying emphasis on the JOB over the worker, and on the manager and management functions."},{"h4":"2.15.1  Henri Fayol (1841–1920)"},{"p":"Fayol believed effective management techniques could be defined and taught, building a formal organisational structure administered rationally, and was a staunch advocate of management's universality. He divided industrial-undertaking activities into: technical (production); commercial (buying, selling, exchange); financial (optimum capital use); security (protecting property/persons); accounting (recording/stocktaking costs, profits, liabilities, balance sheets, statistics); and managerial (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling)."},{"key":"Fayol's 5 fundamental managerial functions: **Planning** (examining the future, developing an action plan); **Organising** (creating a people/materials structure to achieve plans); **Commanding** (maintaining appropriate staff activity, setting a good example); **Coordinating** (unifying/harmonising organisational varieties); **Controlling** (ensuring required activities align with established plans)."},{"h4":"Fayol's 14 principles of management"},{"ol":["**Division of labour** — greater specialisation yields greater efficiency;","**Authority and responsibility** — formal authority gives the right to command, but personal authority (e.g. intelligence) is also needed for real obedience;","**Discipline** — respect for rules/agreements, arising from good leadership, fair agreements, and judiciously-enforced penalties;","**Unity of command** — each employee receives instructions on a given operation from ONLY one superior, else conflict/confusion results;","**Subordination of individual interest to the common good** — organisational interest should not be subordinated to individual employee interest;","**Remuneration** — fair compensation for both employees and employers;","**Centralisation** — the balance between decreasing (centralising) or increasing (decentralising) subordinates' decision-making role; managers retain final responsibility but must grant subordinates enough authority to do their jobs;","**The hierarchy** — the line of authority, running top-to-bottom, often shown as an organisation chart;","**Order** — materials and people in the right place at the right time, people in positions suited to them;","**Equity** — managers should be both friendly and fair to subordinates;","**Stability of staff (tenure)** — high employee turnover harms efficient functioning;","**Initiative** — subordinates should have freedom to conceive/carry out plans, even risking some mistakes;","**Esprit de corps** — promoting team spirit for organisational unity — Fayol favoured verbal over formal written communication to achieve this."]},{"note":"Some of these principles were likely practised by astute managers before Fayol, but he was the first to codify them, enabling all managers to learn them and helping lay the foundation for management as a profession."},{"h4":"2.15.2 – 2.15.5  Chester Barnard, Lyndall Urwick, Luther Gulick, and Mooney and Reilly"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Other administrative-theory contributor","Contribution"],"rows":[["**Chester Barnard (1886-1961)**","Bell Telephone Company president; saw organisations as social systems requiring human cooperation (*The Function of the Executive*), bridging classical and human-resource viewpoints. Held that the manager's major functions were communicating and stimulating subordinates' effort, and that success depended on maintaining good relations with external stakeholders (investors, suppliers, customers) — introducing the idea that managers must examine the external environment and adjust the organisation to maintain equilibrium. His 6 communication principles: channels should be definitely known; formal channels must reach every member; lines should be as direct/short as possible; the complete line should usually be used; competence of those at communication centres must be adequate; and the line should not be interrupted while the organisation functions."],["**Colonel Lyndall Urwick**","A British army officer turned management consultant; authored *The Elements of Administration*, assembling the concepts/principles of Taylor, Fayol, Mooney, Reiley and other early theorists."],["**Luther Gulick**","Isolated the chief executive's responsibilities via the acronym **POSDCORB**: Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, Budgeting."],["**James D. Mooney and Alan C. Reilly**","Mooney (a General Motors executive) and historian Reilly co-wrote *Onward Industry* (1931), revised as *Principles of Organization* — greatly influencing US management theory/practice, arguing organisations should be studied from 2 viewpoints: the employees who create/utilise the organisational process, and the process's objective."]]}},{"h4":"2.15.6  Implications of the administrative school for modern management"},{"ul":["Fayol's principles remain a clear, appropriate guideline set for efficient/effective resource use;","they remain the bedrock of modern management theory — e.g. equity and the performance-reward link are central to modern motivation/leadership theory;","departmentalisation by organisational function is an administrative-thought offshoot; and","Barnard's cooperative-management theory grounds modern group/team work."]},{"h4":"2.15.7  Limitations of administrative management"},{"ul":["many principles (including Fayol's) are internally contradictory — e.g. unity of command contradicts specialisation/division of labour, and limited span of control contradicts minimising organisational levels;","the specialisation principle is internally inconsistent — purpose, process and place are competing modes, and pursuing one sacrifices the others;","the principles rest on limited case studies, not empirical testing;","they are stated as unconditional, universally-valid statements, which is impractical — more CONDITIONAL principles are needed;","they produce mechanistic structures insensitive to employees' social/psychological needs, inhibiting self-actualisation and increasing superior-dependence; and","they assume organisations are CLOSED systems, encouraging departmental rather than whole-organisation orientation among employees."]}]},{"n":"2.16","t":"Theory of bureaucracy (Max Weber, 1864–1920)","b":[{"p":"Weber, a German sociologist, developed the bureaucratic model as a rational way to structure a complex organisation — requiring people performing varied tasks to follow established rules/regulations, ensuring uniform, rational output. **Bureaucracy** is the ideal system where positions/tasks are clearly defined, division of labour is precise, objectives are explicit, and a clear chain of command is maintained."},{"h4":"Weber's 3 types of authority"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Authority type","Description"],"rows":[["**Traditional**","Orders obeyed because the giver is invested with authority by custom/convention (e.g. a king or lord)."],["**Rational-legal**","Orders obeyed because subordinates believe the giver is empowered through enforced legal rules/regulations."],["**Charismatic**","Rests on a leader's personal appeal — belief in the personal attributes of the order-giver."]]}},{"note":"Weber held that charismatic authority mainly arises in crises and must shift to traditional/rational-legal forms for long-term stability. Rational-legal authority was becoming dominant through the modern state/capitalism, given its purely technical superiority — bureaucracy is the organisational form built on pure rational-legal authority."},{"h4":"2.16.1  Features of bureaucracy"},{"ol":["**Division of labour** — each role has a clearly defined task set with specified jurisdiction/responsibility;","**Authority hierarchy** — roles form a hierarchy, with higher-expertise officers supervising those below in the chain of command;","**Formal selection** — recruitment on competence/expertise, progression on seniority/achievement;","**Career orientation** — officials are professionals (not owners) with specified duties/tenure/reward, not arbitrarily removable, using organisational property only for organisational purposes;","**Formal rules and controls** — clearly stated, adhered-to rules/regulations/procedures — written documents/records enable precedent use and effective monitoring; and","**Impersonality** — rules/policies applied uniformly regardless of personal feelings/needs, producing reliable, predictable organisational performance."]},{"h4":"2.16.2  Contributions of bureaucracy to management"},{"ul":["consistent, predictable employee behaviour from uniformly-applied policies/rules;","clearly-defined jobs eliminate overlapping/conflicting duties;","merit-based hiring/promotion optimises human-resource utilisation, with individuals advancing as expertise/experience grow;","division of labour makes workers specialists, sharpening skills and effectiveness; and","the organisation continues past any individual's departure, since the POSITION (not the person) is emphasised."]},{"h4":"2.16.3  Limitations of bureaucracy"},{"ul":["excessive red tape and paperwork;","impersonal work reduces employee organisational commitment;","excessive rules/rigid policy adherence inhibits worker initiative/growth, treating employees as machines rather than individuals; and","employees become so routine-accustomed that they resist change and new operating techniques."]}]},{"n":"2.17","t":"Human relations theory","b":[{"p":"This movement drew heavily on the famous **Hawthorne studies** (1924-1933, at Western Electric's Hawthorne plant, Cicero, Illinois) — arising from the classical school's neglect of the human aspect and over-emphasis on machines/materials/abstract functions. Elton Mayo led the team behind the human relations and behavioural science approach."},{"p":"The human relations approach emphasises the individual, an inter-personal focus — studying the individual, their needs and behaviour, with motivation and job satisfaction as its main concepts."},{"h4":"2.17.1  The Hawthorne experiments (Elton Mayo, 1880–1949)"},{"p":"Mayo was called in after earlier lighting-condition researchers reported puzzling results: a \"test group\" (varied illumination) showed rising productivity as expected when lighting IMPROVED — but productivity ALSO rose when illumination was REDUCED, and even the unchanged \"control group's\" output kept rising alongside each change to the test group's lighting."},{"p":"In a follow-up experiment, two 6-woman groups were placed in separate rooms (one varied, one constant). Multiple variables were tried — increased salaries, varying coffee-break lengths, shortened work day/week, and researcher-supervisors letting groups choose their own rest periods and have input on changes. Output rose in BOTH rooms — ruling out financial incentives as the sole cause, since the control group's pay stayed the same."},{"key":"Mayo concluded a complex emotional chain reaction drove the productivity rise: being singled out for special attention gave workers group pride, motivating improved performance, reinforced by sympathetic supervision. This became known as the **Hawthorne effect**: special management attention tends to raise productivity regardless of actual working-condition changes."},{"p":"Mayo's follow-up interview programme found the deeper answer: **informal work groups — employees' social environment — have a great influence on productivity**. Many employees found life dull/meaningless both inside and outside the factory, but workplace associations (often bonded by shared antagonism toward \"the bosses\") gave their work-lives meaning — so GROUP PRESSURE, more than management demands, most strongly drove productivity. To maximise output, management must address employees' needs for recognition and social satisfaction, turning the informal group into a positive force via a new sense of dignity/appreciation."},{"note":"Mayo's conclusion: the concept of \"social man\" (motivated by social needs, wanting workplace relationships, more responsive to work-group pressure than management control) had to replace the old \"rational man\" concept (motivated purely by personal economic needs)."},{"h4":"2.17.2  Contributions of human relations theory"},{"ul":["improved on the classical approach's purely-engineering treatment of productivity by stressing social needs — echoing Robert Owen's century-old insight;","revealed that a workplace is a SOCIAL environment (not merely a work place), strongly influencing work quality/quantity;","spotlighted managers' STYLE, revolutionising management training toward people-management (not just technical) skills; and","sparked new interest in group dynamics, leading managers toward group (not just individual) incentives."]},{"h4":"2.17.3  Limitations of human relations theory"},{"ul":["over-emphasised emotional/social needs — a single-factor explanation, hence its \"neo-classical\" label;","only selectively applicable — e.g. not readily applicable in military/prison settings requiring authoritarian command (though even the military applies psychological motivation);","carries a pro-management bias and moral undertone, given ideas aimed at eliminating conflict/\"manipulating\" workers to work harder; and","assumes individual needs should be identified/satisfied AT WORK, unrealistically making the organisation a substitute for the community."]},{"note":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: the book's contents list numbers the limitations of human relations theory **\"2.16.3\"** — the number already used for the limitations of *bureaucracy*. It is **§2.17.3**."}]},{"n":"2.18","t":"Quantitative management school","b":[{"p":"Uses quantitative techniques to improve decision-making — treats organisation/decision-making as a logical process expressible in mathematical symbols/relationships, combining Operations Research and Management Science knowledge. Includes applications of statistics, optimisation models, information models and computer simulation. This school quantifies the problem, generates a solution, tests it for optimality, and recommends it — decisions are optimal/perfect (vs. the behavioural approach's \"satisficing\" decisions), free of personal bias/emotion/sentiment/intuition."},{"h4":"Main postulates"},{"ol":["management is a series of decisions — the manager's job is securing the best solution from interrelated variables;","variables can be expressed as a mathematical model (functional equations setting out their quantitative interrelationship);","a properly-formulated, correctly-solved model yields the best solution;","organisations exist to achieve specific, measurable economic goals;","achieving these goals requires optimal decisions through scientific, formal, quantification-backed reasoning;","decision models should be evaluated against set criteria (cost reduction, ROI, meeting time schedules); and","management quality is judged by the quality of decisions made across diverse situations."]},{"h4":"2.18.1  Branches in the quantitative management viewpoint"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Branch","Description"],"rows":[["**Management science (operations research)**","Increases decision effectiveness via sophisticated mathematical models/statistical methods — its origin traces to WWII, when highly technical military/production problems outgrew traditional management methodology. Key features: management as a mathematical problem-solving mechanism; managerial problems described in mathematical symbols/data (every activity quantifiable); covers decision-making, systems analysis, and some human-behaviour aspects; and uses operations research/mathematical tools/simulation/models to solve managerial problems."],["**Operations management**","The field primarily responsible for producing and delivering an organisation's products/services."],["**Management information systems (MIS)**","The field focused on designing/implementing computer-based information systems for management use."]]}}]},{"n":"2.19","t":"Contemporary approaches to management thoughts","b":[{"h4":"2.19.1  Decision theory approach to management"},{"p":"A product of the management-science era, emphasising rational selection among possible alternatives — a course of action or idea. Major contributor: Simon (others: March, Cyert, Forrester). Decision-making is the job of EVERY manager; the organisation is a decision-making unit. Features: management is essentially decision-making, with members as decision-makers/problem-solvers; the organisation is a combination of decision centres; members' level/importance is determined by the importance of decisions they make; and everything affecting decision-making (the entire range of human organisational activity, plus macro conditions) is within management's subject matter."},{"h4":"2.19.2  Systems theory"},{"def":{"t":"System","d":"a set of interrelated, interdependent parts arranged to produce a unified whole — a concept taken from the physical sciences and applied to organisations. Originally conceptualised by biologist Ludwig von Bertalanffy (1950); Daniel Katz and Robert Kahn (1966) first applied systems concepts fully to organisations."}},{"p":"Organisations are viewed as **systems of interrelated parts/subsystems** operating as a whole toward common goals. **Closed systems** are not influenced by, and do not interact with, their environment; **open systems** dynamically interact with their environment — modern organisations are understood as open systems."},{"h4":"2.19.3  Components of a system"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Component","Description"],"rows":[["**Inputs**","Resources/raw materials imported from the environment (human, material, financial, equipment, informational)."],["**Transformation processes**","An open system's managerial/technological abilities applied to convert inputs into outputs."],["**Outputs**","The products, services and other outcomes exported into the environment."],["**Feedback**","Information about results/organisational status relative to the environment."],["**Sub-systems**","The parts making up a system's whole — each system may itself be a sub-system of a still-larger whole (department → plant → company → industry → economy → world system)."],["**Synergy**","The whole is greater than the sum of its parts — cooperating/interacting departments become more productive than if acting in isolation."],["**System boundary**","Separates a system from its environment — rigid in a closed system, more flexible in an open system (increasingly flexible in recent years for many organisations)."],["**Flow**","Information, material and energy (including human) entering as inputs and exiting as outputs."]]}},{"h4":"2.19.4  Systems as cycles of events"},{"p":"**Systems as cycles of events**: the input-throughput-output pattern is cyclic — successful cycle completion provides energy/resources for repeating the cycle (e.g. products sold, profits regenerating the cycle)."},{"h4":"2.19.5  Contributions of the systems theory to management"},{"ul":["organisations operating as open systems must think broadly to avoid narrow issue assessment;","a systems perspective eases coordination between sub-unit and whole-organisation objectives;","helps managers envision organisational boundaries (internal/external environments);","gives better understanding of how sub-systems interact;","feedback helps detect **entropy** and counter it with **negative entropy** (acquiring new environmental inputs to prevent system deterioration);","**synergy** (the whole exceeding the sum of parts) helps organisations accomplish goals, echoing Fayol's esprit de corps; and","the open-systems approach accommodates multi-dimensional problem-solving."]},{"h4":"2.19.6  Limitations of the systems theory"},{"ul":["considered abstract/impractical — managers don't naturally discuss problems in terms of inputs/transactions/outputs;","heavily managerial in conception, under-emphasising workplace social conflict (trade unionism, labour-management relations);","somewhat tautological — the concept of \"function\" lacks substantive content; and","organisational systems are run BY people, so interpersonal relations may matter more than particular input/output structures, which remain subject to human control."]}]},{"n":"2.20","t":"Contingency theory","b":[{"h4":"2.20.1  Contingency or situational approach to management"},{"def":{"t":"Contingency theory","d":"appropriate managerial action depends on each situation's particular parameters — in sharp contrast to the classical school's \"one universal best decision\" view. \"It all depends\" is contingency theory's slogan. Applies especially well to environmental factors, strategy, organisational design, technology and leadership."}},{"p":"Propagated by Pigors and Myers (1950); other contributors: Joan Woodward, Tom Burns, G.W. Stalker, Paul Lawrence, Jay Lorsch, James Thompson. They analysed the organisation-environment relationship, concluding managers must keep the organisation's functioning in harmony with its members' needs AND external forces. Per Kast and Rosenzweig: \"the contingency view seeks to understand the interrelationships within and among sub-systems as well as between the organisation and its environment... ultimately directed toward suggesting organisational designs and managerial actions most appropriate for specific situations.\""},{"h4":"2.20.2  Features of the contingency approach"},{"ol":["management is entirely situational — technique effectiveness is contingent on the situation;","management action is contingent on action outside the relevant system/subsystem;","management should match/fit its approach to the particular situation's requirements — policies must respond to environmental change;","organisational action should be based on behaviour outside the system, integrating the organisation with its environment; and","there is no single \"hard way\" to manage — principles/techniques are not universal."]},{"key":"4 sequential contingency-model steps: (a) analyse and understand the situation; (b) examine the applicability/validity of different principles/techniques to it; (c) match the right technique to the situation; (d) implement the choice."},{"h4":"2.20.3  Contributions of contingency theory to management"},{"ul":["cautions against generalising method/strategy/organisational design;","there is no single best way to organise/manage — the right structure is whichever lets the organisation optimally deal with its environment; and","has LIMITED practical utility, being complex and difficult to fully determine all relevant situational factors."]}]},{"n":"2.21","t":"Emerging trends in management","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Trend","Description"],"rows":[["**Globalisation**","Organisational operations no longer stop at geographic borders — managers everywhere face globalisation's opportunities/challenges; success demands thinking globally even while acting locally."],["**Entrepreneurship**","The process where individuals/groups use organised effort to pursue opportunities, creating value/growth by fulfilling needs through innovation/uniqueness — 3 key themes: the pursuit of opportunities, innovation, and growth."],["**Managing in an e-business world**","**E-business**: how an organisation does its work using electronic (internet-based) linkages with key constituencies to efficiently/effectively achieve its goals. **E-commerce**: any business exchange/transaction where parties interact electronically."],["**Need for innovation and flexibility**","A constant flow of new ideas is crucial to avoid obsolescence/failure; flexibility matters where customer needs shift overnight, competitors come/go, and employee skills must shift project to project."],["**Quality management systems (TQM)**","A management philosophy driven by customer needs/expectations, focused on continual process improvement — inspired by W. Edwards Deming (among other quality experts), and his theory of profound knowledge. A counterpoint to earlier theorists who saw low cost as the only route to productivity; the objective is an organisation committed to continuous improvement."],["**Learning organisations and knowledge management**","A **learning organisation** has developed the capacity to continuously adapt/change. **Knowledge management** cultivates a learning culture where members systematically gather/share knowledge for better performance — needed as managers face a continually-changing environment."],["**Theory Z (William Ouchi)**","Combines positive American and Japanese management aspects into a modified, culturally-compatible approach for greater managerial effectiveness. Type Z firms (highly-successful American firms using Japanese practices) feature long-term employment, a moderately-specialised career path, and slow evaluation/promotion — though lifetime employment/slow evaluation would not suit America's mobile, high-expectation workforce as readily."],["**Managerial effectiveness — McKinsey 7-S Model**","7 factors: **Strategy** (plans allocating scarce resources, committing to a course of action); **Structure** (hierarchy design, location of authority); **Systems** (organisational processes/proceduralised reports/routines); **Staff** (key human-resource groups, demographically described); **Style** (how managers behave pursuing goals); **Skills** (the organisation's personnel's distinct abilities); **Shared values** (the significant guiding concepts instilled in members)."],["**Excellence**","Excellent companies shared: making things happen; knowing customers and their needs; valuing autonomy/entrepreneurship in each employee; trust-based productivity through people; hands-on, value-driven management; always dealing from strength; cost-effective work teams; and decentralising many decisions while retaining tight (usually financial) control."],["**Quality and productivity**","Today's dynamic marketplace pushes consumers toward the highest-quality product at the optimum price — requiring a dedicated, skilled workforce prioritising quality workmanship."]]}}]},{"n":"2.22","t":"Summary","b":[{"p":"**Management is a process** that includes the functions of **planning, organising, actuating and controlling**. These functions must be performed in all organisations if goals are to be achieved satisfactorily through the use of human and material resources. Successful managers must demonstrate **technical, human relations, conceptual and analytical skills** in the performance of their duties. **Managing is an essential activity at all organisational levels.**"},{"p":"The goal of all managers is to **create a surplus and to be productive** — that is, to achieve a **favourable output-input ratio within a specific time period, with due consideration for quality**. **Productivity implies effectiveness** (achieving objectives) **and efficiency** (using the least amount of resources)."},{"p":"Furthermore, the manager needs to be conscious of the **dynamic nature of management** in terms of management practice. Thus the concept of **universality of management** refers to the need for the practice of management in all types of organisation."},{"p":"The various theories of the evolution of management thought have contributed to managers' understanding of organisations and to their ability to manage them. **Each offers a different perspective** for defining management problems and opportunities, and for developing ways to deal with them. In their current state of evolution, however, **each approach overlooks or deals inadequately with important aspects of organisational life**."},{"p":"However, the newer **systems approach**, based on general systems theory, and the **contingency approach** have already been developed to the point where they offer valuable insights for the practising manager. Managers will have to continue on their own to **select the appropriate perspective or perspectives for each situation**. They may, of course, become lost in what **Harold Koontz called \"the management theory jungle\"**. But it is at least likely that managers will find such a multiplicity of theories useful. **It may be that no one theory could encompass a field like management, in which the complexities of human behaviour play such a central role.**"},{"key":"The last paragraph is the chapter's own conclusion and the best answer to any question asking you to evaluate the schools of management thought: **no single theory is sufficient**, each illuminates part of organisational life and neglects the rest, and the manager's task is to choose the perspective that fits the situation — which is itself the contingency approach of §2.20. Koontz's phrase **\"the management theory jungle\"** is worth quoting by name."}]},{"n":"2.23","t":"Review questions, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"p":"The chapter closes with **five multiple choice questions** and **five short answer questions**, with the study text's own printed solutions."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Section A — multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["The principle that specifies that the effort of everyone in the organisation should be coordinated and focused in the organisation is known as: **(a)** Unity of direction; **(b)** Span of management; **(c)** Unity of command; **(d)** Unity of integration; **(e)** Unity of conglomeration.","The type of skills required by any manager to perform effectively and efficiently depends on: **(a)** Policy; **(b)** Level; **(c)** Strategy; **(d)** Standard; **(e)** Communication.","Which of the following is popularly referred to as the father of scientific management? **(a)** Henri Fayol; **(b)** Elton Mayo; **(c)** Frederick W. Taylor; **(d)** Herbert Simon; **(e)** Peter Drucker.","The ability to think in an abstract manner and comprehend the relationship between elements is known as: **(a)** Conceptual skill; **(b)** Design skill; **(c)** Human skill; **(d)** Technical skill; **(e)** Monitoring skill.","The experiments that led to the human relations school are collectively referred to as: **(a)** Hawborne experiments; **(b)** Hawthorne experiments; **(c)** Habronee experiments; **(d)** Hawthing experiments; **(e)** Hartthorne experiments."]},{"h4":"Part II — short answer questions"},{"ol":["The ability to choose appropriate strategies for achieving a given objective is known as ………","The ability to get things done correctly, which is an input-output concept, is referred to as ………","The process of matching organisational structure to its goals and resources is called ………","The idea whereby subordinates are given the freedom to conceive and carry out their plans, even when some mistakes result, is referred to as ………","A situation that arises where authority rests on the appeal of leaders and is also contingent upon a belief in the personal attributes of the person giving the order is known as ………"]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**D** — Unity of integration"],["2","**B** — Level"],["3","**C** — Frederick W. Taylor"],["4","**A** — Conceptual skill"],["5","**B** — Hawthorne experiments"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Effectiveness**","**Efficiency**","**Organisational design**","**Initiative**","**Charismatic authority**"]},{"key":"Short answer questions 1 and 2 are the §2.10 pair, and they are the most frequently confused definitions in the subject: **effectiveness** is choosing the right strategies and achieving the objective; **efficiency** is getting things done correctly — an **input-output** concept. Productivity, as the chapter summary puts it, **implies both**."},{"note":"MCQ 2's answer — **level** — is the point of §2.6: all managers need all four skills, but the *mix* varies with the level. Technical skill matters most at the lower level and conceptual skill most at the top, with human skill roughly constant throughout. MCQ 4 then asks for the name of that top-level skill: **conceptual**, the ability to think abstractly and see relationships between elements."},{"note":"Short answer 5's answer, **charismatic authority**, is one of Weber's three types of authority at §2.16 — authority resting on the personal appeal and attributes of the leader, as against **traditional** authority (resting on custom and inherited position) and **rational-legal** authority (resting on office and rules), which is the type bureaucracy is built on."},{"warn":"The answer to MCQ 1 is given as **(d) unity of integration**, a term that does not appear in the chapter. The principle that \"the effort of everyone in the organisation should be coordinated and focused\" is normally **unity of direction** — one of Fayol's fourteen principles at §2.15.1, which the chapter does cover, and which is offered as option (a). The printed answer is reproduced above because it is the official one; be aware that (a) is the better-supported answer on the chapter's own text."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Productivity","tex":"\\text{Productivity} = \\dfrac{\\text{Output}}{\\text{Input}}","nt":"Output = products/services; Input = labour, energy, materials or capital."},{"lb":"Labour productivity","tex":"\\text{Labour productivity} = \\dfrac{\\text{Total net output}}{\\text{Labour input}}"},{"lb":"Capital productivity","tex":"\\text{Capital productivity} = \\dfrac{\\text{Output}}{\\text{Capital input}}"},{"lb":"Material productivity","tex":"\\text{Material productivity} = \\dfrac{\\text{Output (net sales)}}{\\text{Material input}}"}],"focus":"This is the densest chapter in the subject so far — prioritise 3 things for the exam: (1) Fayol's 14 principles and 5 functions BY NAME (this is the single most-examined historical content in management syllabi generally); (2) the clean distinctions between related-but-different concepts the chapter itself sets up — effectiveness vs. efficiency, art vs. science, universality vs. transferability, productivity vs. effectiveness vs. efficiency as 3 SEPARATE performance measures; and (3) correctly attributing each theory to its originator (Taylor=scientific, Fayol=administrative, Weber=bureaucracy, Mayo=human relations) since exam MCQs frequently test this exact matching. The Hawthorne effect's actual finding (informal GROUP social dynamics, not lighting) is a classic trick-question target — many candidates wrongly assume the lighting itself was the causal variable.","errors":["Attributing Fayol's 5 functions (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling) to Taylor, or Taylor's scientific-management principles to Fayol — the two theories are commonly confused since they developed contemporaneously; Taylor's approach was worker/task-level (\"bottom up\"), Fayol's was managerial-function-level (\"top down\").","Believing the Hawthorne experiments proved that BETTER LIGHTING increases productivity — the actual, counter-intuitive finding was that productivity rose regardless of whether lighting improved OR worsened, because the true driver was the social attention/group dynamics of being studied (the Hawthorne effect), not the physical variable being tested.","Treating productivity, effectiveness and efficiency as synonyms — they are 3 distinct measures: productivity is an input-output RATIO; effectiveness is doing the RIGHT things; efficiency is doing things RIGHT (minimising resource use) — a firm can be efficient (low-cost) while being ineffective (making the wrong product).","Confusing universality of management (principles apply across ALL organisation types) with transferability of management (a manager's own skills can move across organisations/industries/countries) — they are related but conceptually distinct claims."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The principle that specifies that the effort of everyone in the organisation should be coordinated and focused in the organisation is known as:","o":["Unity of direction","Span of management","Unity of command","Unity of integration","Unity of conglomeration"],"a":0,"w":"Unity of direction (one plan, one head, for a group of activities with the same objective) coordinates and focuses everyone's effort — distinct from unity of command, which concerns receiving orders from only one superior.","src":"Chapter 2 Review Questions","sec":"2.12"},{"q":"Which of the following is popularly referred to as the father of scientific management?","o":["Henri Fayol","Elton Mayo","Frederick W. Taylor","Herbert Simon","Peter Drucker"],"a":2,"w":"Frederick Winslow Taylor (1856-1915) is popularly called the \"father of scientific management,\" having played the dominant role among its early advocates.","src":"Chapter 2 Review Questions","sec":"2.11"},{"q":"The ability to think in an abstract manner and comprehend relationships between elements is known as:","o":["Conceptual skill","Design skill","Human skill","Technical skill","Monitoring skill"],"a":0,"w":"Conceptual skill is the ability to see the \"big picture,\" recognise significant elements in a situation, and understand the relationships among them.","src":"Chapter 2 Review Questions","sec":"2.5"},{"q":"The experiments that led to the human relations school are collectively referred to as:","o":["Hawborne experiments","Hawthorne experiments","Habronee experiments","Hawthing experiments","Hartthorne experiments"],"a":1,"w":"The Hawthorne experiments, conducted 1924-1933 at Western Electric's Hawthorne plant and led by Elton Mayo, gave rise to the human relations and behavioural science approach to management.","src":"Chapter 2 Review Questions","sec":"2.14"},{"q":"Luther Gulick's POSDCORB acronym, isolating the chief executive's responsibilities, stands for Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, and:","o":["Budgeting","Bargaining","Balancing","Branding","Benchmarking"],"a":0,"w":"POSDCORB stands for Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, and Budgeting.","src":"Chapter 2","sec":"2.12"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between effectiveness and efficiency as measures of managerial performance, and explain why a manager's effectiveness is considered more critical than their efficiency.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Effectiveness** is the ability to choose appropriate objectives, or the appropriate means for achieving a given objective — doing the RIGHT things. **Efficiency** is the ability to get things done correctly, an input-output concept — achieving outputs that measure up to the inputs (labour, material, time) used — doing things RIGHT."},{"p":"Effectiveness is more critical because a manager who selects the WRONG objective can still be highly efficient at pursuing it (e.g. producing large cars with maximum efficiency when demand has shifted to small cars) and still fail the organisation. As Drucker (1970) put it, the pertinent question is \"not how to do things right, but how to find the right things to do.\" A firm that is effective (doing the right things) is on a better footing than one that is efficient but ineffective (doing the wrong things well)."}],"src":"Chapter 2.8","sec":"2.8"},{"q":"Explain the Hawthorne effect, and state the key finding of Elton Mayo's Hawthorne experiments about what actually drives worker productivity.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"The **Hawthorne effect** is the phenomenon whereby giving workers special attention tends to increase their productivity, REGARDLESS of any actual change in physical working conditions — first observed when both a test group (whose lighting was deliberately varied) and an unchanged control group showed rising output as the experiment progressed."},{"p":"Mayo's deeper finding, from a follow-up interview programme, was that **informal work groups — the social environment of employees — have a great influence on productivity**. Workers whose lives felt dull/meaningless found meaning in workplace social bonds, so group pressure (not management demands or financial incentives alone) most strongly drove how productive they were. This led Mayo to argue the \"social man\" concept (motivated by social needs and workplace relationships) should replace the older \"rational economic man\" concept in management thinking."}],"src":"Chapter 2.14","sec":"2.14"}]}},{"n":3,"t":"Management Process","brief":"The largest chapter in the subject — 6 self-contained sections covering the full management process: Planning (types, principles, the 9-step process, levels), Decision Making (types, the 9-step rational process, individual vs. group, decision techniques), Organising (structure types, line/staff, delegation, span of control, centralisation/decentralisation), Coordination (vertical/horizontal, techniques), Controlling (the control process, types of control, characteristics of effective control), and Strategic Management (SWOT, the 4 strategy levels, corporate vs. divisional strategy).","outcomes":["Define and explain planning, its types, process, levels and barriers","Explain decision making, its types, process, situations and techniques","Define organising and explain organisational structure types, delegation, span of control, centralisation/decentralisation","Explain coordination, its types, need and techniques","Explain controlling — its process, types and characteristics of an effective control system","Explain strategic management — SWOT analysis, the strategic management process, and the levels/types of organisational strategy"],"secs":[{"n":"A.1","t":"Meaning of planning","b":[{"def":{"t":"Planning","d":"(from the French \"prévoyance,\" to foresee) — examining the future, deciding what needs achieving, and developing a plan of action. An organisation without planning \"is like a sailboat minus its rudder\" — subject to environmental winds with no means to determine its own direction. Planning provides the basis for organising, leading and controlling by charting the course."}}]},{"n":"A.2","t":"Definition of planning","b":[{"p":"Drucker (1968): planning deals with what must be done TODAY to be ready for tomorrow. Banga and Sharma (2008): planning is a mental process using intellect, imagination, foresight and sound judgment to decide in advance what/how/where/who — \"the process of thinking before doing.\""}]},{"n":"A.3","t":"Nature and characteristics of planning","b":[{"ol":["**Intellectual process** — a mental exercise of imagination, foresight, sound judgement, thinking before acting on facts rather than guesses;","**Goal-oriented** — every plan must positively contribute to group objectives, bridging where we are to where we want to go at minimum cost;","**Primary function of management** — precedes ALL other functions (organising, direction, coordination, control can only follow it);","**Pervasiveness** — the job of every manager, at every level, in every organisation type;","**Uniformity** — separate sub-plans at different levels must unite into one comprehensive general plan;","**Continuity** — a never-ending series (first plan → second → third...) to keep the enterprise a going concern;","**Flexibility** — should not be rigid, must accommodate changing market conditions — planning is a dynamic activity;","**Simplicity** — the work-schedule language must be simple enough for employees at every level to understand;","**Precision** — \"the soul of planning\" — any planning error upsets other management functions;","**Feasibility** — realistic, fact-and-experience-based, executable with existing resources;","**Making a choice among alternatives** — if there is only one way to do something, there is no need for planning;","**Efficiency** — directed toward optimising the return/input ratio while bringing the greatest satisfaction to those implementing it;","**Inter-dependence** — sectional plans (production, sales, purchasing, labour) are inter-dependent, forming a hierarchy where every lower plan serves higher plans; and","**Forecasting** — no planning proceeds without forecasting; planning synthesises various (short/long-term, special) forecasts into one guiding programme."]}]},{"n":"A.4","t":"Basic principles of planning","b":[{"ol":["**Commitment** — resources must be committed/pledged, exhausting available resources to achieve the plan;","**The limiting factor** — put more emphasis on whichever factor is scarce/limited/costly, to select the most favourable alternative;","**Reflective thinking** — problem-solving thought superimposing past experience on present facts and future trends;","**Flexibility** — sufficient scope to change the plan as new developments arise;","**Contribution to enterprise objectives** — all (derivative) plans must positively contribute to enterprise objectives;","**Efficiency** — attain objectives at minimum cost/effort with the fewest unexpected happenings;","**Selection of alternatives** — planning is fundamentally a choosing problem — no need for it if only one way exists;","**Planning premises** — complete manager agreement on the foundational assumptions the plan is built on;","**Timing and sequence of operations** — determines the start/finish time for each piece of work on a definite schedule;","**Securing participation** — communicating/explaining the plan secures whole-hearted employee cooperation and ownership;","**Pervasiveness** — though entrusted mainly to top management, it is every manager's function at every level;","**Strategic planning** — essential wherever competition exists, accounting for rival strategies;","**Innovation** — a good planning system is responsive to innovation opportunities, necessary for sustained growth;","**Follow-up** — a built-in system for revising/altering/correcting the plan when obstacles arise mid-execution;","**Objectives** — objectives (the basis for planning) must be clear, specific, measurable, unambiguous, and understood/accepted by all members; and","**Primacy of planning** — planning is a prerequisite to every other managerial function."]}]},{"n":"A.5","t":"Importance of planning","b":[{"ul":["**Minimises risk and uncertainty** — replacing intuitive decisions with a rational, fact-based procedure;","**Enables facing increasing competition** — domestic and international;","**Enables keeping pace with technological change**;","**Leads to success** — organisations that plan have a higher probability of succeeding;","**Focuses on goals** — concentrates all activities/resources on selected goals; and","**Facilitates control** — plans become the benchmark against which performance/control is compared."]}]},{"n":"A.6","t":"Dimensions/types of plans","b":[{"p":"Organisations establish plans along 6 general dimensions: functions/activities-based; time-frame-based; contingency plans; single-use plans; standing/repeated-use plans; and kinds of enterprise plans."},{"h4":"(A) Plans based on functions/activities — the 3-tier planning hierarchy"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Plan type","Who develops it / timeframe","Focus"],"rows":[["**Strategic plans**","Top management (with the board and middle management); comprehensive, long-term, general.","Broad, enduring issues for firm effectiveness/survival — how to respond to changing conditions, allocate resources, and create a unified organisation-wide effort. Effectiveness criteria: **proactivity** (long-term view, actively moving the company forward), **congruency** (fit with organisational characteristics/external environment), and **synergy** (integrating sub-unit efforts toward corporate objectives)."],["**Tactical plans**","Division/department level; typically 1-2 years.","Specifies activities/completion timing/resources needed for the division's portion of the strategic plan — narrower, more specific than strategic plans, dealing more with efficiency than long-term effectiveness. Key aspects: division of labour and budgeting."],["**Operational plans**","Line managers/employees directly responsible for execution; short-term.","Translates the strategic plan's broad concepts into clear numbers, specific steps, measurable short-term objectives — narrowly focused on resources, methods, timeliness, quality control."]]}},{"h4":"(B) Plans based on time frame"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Plan","Span"],"rows":[["**Long-range plans**","Beyond 5 years (varies by organisation)."],["**Intermediate/medium-range plans**","1-5 years — important for middle/first-line managers, generally paralleling tactical plans."],["**Short-range plans**","1 year or less — greatly affect day-to-day activity. Two kinds: **action plans** (operationalise another plan) and **reaction plans** (allow reacting to unforeseen circumstances)."]]}},{"h4":"(C) Contingency plans"},{"p":"Determines alternative courses of action if an intended plan is unexpectedly disrupted or rendered inappropriate — increasingly important for organisations in complex/dynamic environments, and should be periodically updated/revised."},{"h4":"(D) Single-use plans — 3 types"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Programme**","A comprehensive plan coordinating a complex, non-recurring set of activities (new product line, new facility, mission change) — usually spans several departments/projects and may take over a year. 6 basic steps (Bartol & Martin, 1998): divide the work into major parts/projects; determine relationships/sequence among parts; decide who takes responsibility for each part; determine how each part is completed and what resources are needed; estimate completion time for each part; and develop an implementation schedule."],["**Project**","Similar to a programme but smaller scope/complexity — may be part of a broader programme, or self-contained; often has its own budget."],["**Budget**","A statement outlining the financial resources needed for a programme/project — a statement of expected results in numerical (usually monetary) terms."]]}},{"h4":"(E) Standing/repeated-use plans — 3 types (Wheelon and Hunger, 1995)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Policy**","A general guide specifying broad parameters for organisational action — an administrative-decision standing guide, an organisation's viewpoint, an internal administrative law. Leaves room for discretion; a rigid policy becomes a RULE. Translated into rules/plans/procedures at every level (Mullins, 1996)."],["**Procedure**","A prescribed series of related steps for recurring circumstances (formalised ones are \"standard operating procedures,\" SOPs) — more specific than policy, detailed step-by-step, little flexibility. Advantages: ensures uniformity of action; eases employee appraisal; simplifies decision-making; improves inter-departmental coordination."],["**Rule**","A statement of specific actions to take/not take in a given situation — unlike procedures, does not specify a series of steps; definite and rigid, little discretion; breach usually invites a penalty."]]}},{"h4":"(F) Kinds of enterprise plans"},{"p":"**Divisional plans** (for individual divisions); **functional plans** (for each functional department); **regional plans** (for each region/zone); and **corporate plans** (for the enterprise as a whole)."}]},{"n":"A.7","t":"The planning process (steps in planning)","b":[{"steps":["Being aware of opportunities — a preliminary look at future opportunities, seeing them clearly/completely against your own strengths/weaknesses (precedes the process proper but is the real starting point).","Establishing objectives — for the whole enterprise, then each work unit, short and long term; objectives specify expected results and form a HIERARCHY (enterprise → department → unit).","Developing premises — establishing/circulating/agreeing on critical planning assumptions (forecasts, applicable policies, existing plans); the more consistently planners agree on premises, the more coordinated the resulting plan.","Determining alternative courses of action — searching for (especially non-obvious) alternatives; the harder problem is usually narrowing down, not finding, alternatives.","Evaluating alternative courses of action — weighing each against premises/goals; genuinely difficult given uncertainty, capital constraints and intangible factors — the step where operations research/mathematical/computing techniques apply most.","Selecting a course of action — the real decision-making point; may result in adopting several courses rather than one single best course.","Formulating supporting plans — plans to buy equipment/materials, hire/train workers, develop new products, etc.","Valuing plans by budgeting — converting plans into budgets, which become both a summation device and a standard against which progress is measured.","Implementation — allocating tasks, driving objective-focused action, and collecting feedback; without this, all prior steps are pointless."]}]},{"n":"A.8","t":"Levels of planning","b":[{"p":"Planning is pervasive at every level, but relative time spent on it shifts: top management spends the most time planning (though not exclusively), engaging in STRATEGIC planning; middle management engages more in TACTICAL planning; first-line management concentrates mainly on OPERATIONAL planning."},{"note":"The book divides this section into **§A.8.1 Strategic planning (top management)**, **§A.8.2 Tactical planning (middle management)** and **§A.8.3 Operational planning (lower level management)** — the three rows of the table above."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Level","Characteristics"],"rows":[["**Strategic planning (top management)**","Determining desired objectives/benchmarks and ways to reach them (\"what future do we want? what must we do now to ensure it?\"). Typically: top management's responsibility; states mission/vision; describes goals/policies/structure/funding; concerned with EFFECTIVENESS (doing the right things); provides the basis for tactical/operational planning; takes time to execute; usually a long time frame."],["**Tactical planning (middle management)**","Flows from, and must be consistent with, the strategic plan — while strategy is general, tactics specify resources/timeframes in detail. Requires heavy people-interaction (receiving/processing/passing information). Each strategic plan is implemented through SEVERAL tactical plans — strategic planning is critical to success, but tactical planning determines how well strategies actually WORK."],["**Operational planning (lower-level management)**","Focused, short-term, specific — translates the tactical plan's broad concepts into clear numbers, specific steps, measurable short-term objectives."]]}}]},{"n":"A.9","t":"Benefits of planning","b":[{"ol":["**Assessment of external forces** — helps the company respond to environmental challenges;","**Developing a sense of direction and purpose** — without a plan, managers/employees may not recognise a shared fate, leading to conflicting goals and ineffective resource use;","**Identifying the factors that affect the organisation** — survival, renewal, growth;","**Encouraging participation** — participating workers \"buy in\" and commit more strongly;","**Coordination of efforts** — helps independently-operating managers coordinate more effectively;","**Establishment of priorities** — prevents the confusion/agenda-hijacking that lack of priorities invites;","**Focusing attention on different time horizons** — balancing long-term objectives with short-term goals;","**Understanding circumstances behind past success/failure** — sharpens learning from experience;","**Ensuring availability of adequate resources**;","**Establishing performance standards** — objectives/expected activities become the criteria for assessing/rewarding contribution over time;","**Supporting the organisational control system**; and","**Developing \"what if\" scenarios** — contingency planning for an uncertain future;","**Management development** — planning makes managers proactive/forward-thinking rather than reactive, converting abstract objectives into concrete action."]}]},{"n":"A.10","t":"Tools/factors aiding effective planning","b":[{"ul":["availability of reliable planning information (price index, population trend, market share, etc.);","a stable planning environment (economic/political/legal/socio-cultural/technological predictability);","availability of managers with genuine planning skills;","knowledge of the organisation and its business;","employee participation, which builds commitment;","flexibility — accommodating assumption changes with minimal disruption; and","timely control/review, catching deviations and enabling timely correction."]}]},{"n":"A.11","t":"Barriers to effective planning","b":[{"ul":["**goals** — inappropriate/unclear/unattainable goals fail to motivate effective planning, or managers fear failure and avoid setting goals at all;","**reward system** — failing to reward desired planning behaviour (reinforcement theory);","**dynamic/complex environment** — makes forecasting extremely difficult;","**lack of time and resources**;","too much top-management time on current problems, leaving little for long-range planning;","failure to develop meaningful, realistic goals/objectives;","lack of commitment, believing planning can simply be delegated to a \"planner\";","personnel inertia/resistance to planning-driven change;","too much formality/inflexibility, hindering creativity;","failure to involve major line managers in the process;","inadequate resources for implementation;","over-reliance on top management's reflective thinking/experience alone; and","failure to build an organisational climate conducive (not resistant) to planning."]}]},{"n":"A.12","t":"Summary — Section A","b":[{"p":"The section explains the **meaning of planning** and its **importance to managers and organisations**. The **dimensions/types of plans** were given elaborate discussion. Different **types of planning** were explained, and **factors aiding effective planning** were enlisted and explained."}]},{"n":"A.13","t":"Review questions — Section A, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section A review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Which of these is **NOT** an importance of planning? **(a)** Minimises risk and uncertainty; **(b)** Guarantees business success; **(c)** Leads to success; **(d)** Facilitates control; **(e)** Focuses on goals.","Which of these can **NOT** be ascribed to planning? **(a)** Goal oriented; **(b)** Intellectual process; **(c)** Secondary function of management; **(d)** Directed towards efficiency; **(e)** Spreads across all managerial activities.","Which of these is **NOT** a kind of enterprise plan? **(a)** Divisional plans; **(b)** Operational plans; **(c)** Functional plans; **(d)** Regional plans; **(e)** Corporate plans.","Which of these is **NOT** a feature of strategic planning? **(a)** Responsibility of top management; **(b)** Long time frame; **(c)** Concerns with effectiveness; **(d)** It is operational; **(e)** It states the organisation's mission and vision."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["……… plans are developed at the division or departmental level.","……… plan is used to determine alternative courses of action to be taken if an intended plan of action is unexpectedly disrupted.","……… is a process of preparing a set of decisions for future actions directed at achieving goals by optimal means.","……… plans are plans developed to carry out a course of action that is not likely to be repeated in the future.","……… is a general guide that specifies the broad parameters within which organisation members are expected to operate in pursuit of organisational goals."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**B** — Guarantees business success"],["2","**C** — Secondary function of management"],["3","**B** — Operational plans"],["4","**D** — It is operational"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Tactical**","**Contingency**","**Planning**","**Single-use**","**Policy**"]},{"key":"MCQ 1 and 2 both turn on **overclaiming**. Planning *minimises* risk and uncertainty and *leads to* success, but it **does not guarantee** success — nothing does. And planning is the **primary** function of management, not a secondary one: it is the function on which organising, staffing, leading and controlling all depend."},{"note":"MCQ 4 contrasts strategic with operational planning (§A.8): strategic planning is long-term, top management's responsibility, concerned with **effectiveness**, and states mission and vision. Being **operational** is precisely what it is not — that is §A.8.3, the province of lower-level management and concerned with **efficiency**."}]}}]},{"n":"B.1","t":"Decision making","b":[{"def":{"t":"Decision","d":"a conscious choice selected among competing alternatives, directed toward a definite purpose."}},{"def":{"t":"Decision making","d":"the process of selecting an optimal way of matching limited resources to limited wants, to maximise achievement of personal/social/business objectives — a continuous process pervading all organisational activity."}}]},{"n":"B.2","t":"Nature and characteristics of decisions","b":[{"ol":["so basic that no management function (planning, organising, actuating, controlling) can be performed without it;","managers at ALL levels decide, but decisions differ in type/scope — top level sets overall objectives/strategies; middle level handles overall operating policies/plans; first-line supervisors handle short-range activity-specific decisions within established policy;","decision-making is the CORNERSTONE of planning — the catalyst driving the planning process itself;","effective decision-making requires understanding the driving situation — indeed, management can arguably be studied AS decision-making;","often reflects a manager's effort to make sense of a complicated environment and achieve some sense of order/control; and","an organised approach (clear present-state understanding, historical improvement basis, awareness of possible errors) enables better decisions."]}]},{"n":"B.3","t":"Types of decisions","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Programmed decisions**","Made in routine, repetitive, well-structured situations via predetermined decision rules (habit, computation, established policy/procedure) — e.g. opening a bank account, reordering inexpensive materials, admitting hospital patients."],["**Non-programmed decisions**","Made for unstructured problems with no prior routine/procedure to guide them — poorly defined but with important consequences, usually handled by experienced managers/professionals, deserving special top-management attention given their strategic importance/large expenditure — e.g. diversification into new products/markets, new-facility construction, expensive-equipment purchase."]]}}]},{"n":"B.4","t":"The rational decision-making process","b":[{"steps":["Diagnose and define the problem/opportunity — find the real problem by asking what past action/inaction caused it; distinguish \"musts\" (must be solved) from \"shoulds\" (would like to solve) to prioritise.","Establish specific goals and objectives — decision-making is always goal-contextual; well-established goals dictate required results and the decision CRITERIA for judging them.","Generate alternatives — no major decision should rest on only the first/most-obvious solution found; list all possible alternatives.","Gather and analyse the relevant facts — collect data (internal/external sources) on each alternative, arranging it to provide meaning/insight.","Evaluate the alternatives — assess each against the Step-2 decision criteria: how realistic is it given goals/resources, and how well does it solve the problem — then RANK them most-to-least desirable.","Select an alternative — choose the most desirable of all evaluated alternatives.","Analyse the possible consequences of the decision — anticipate implementation problems (organisational resistance to change, funding needs, effects on other departments/competitors) so necessary steps can be taken in advance.","Implement the decision — no decision is better than the action realising it; since implementation involves people, the true test of a decision's soundness is the behaviour of those carrying it out or affected by it.","Follow up — periodically measure actual vs. planned results; if they deviate, change the solution, its implementation, or (if unattainable) the original objective — which reactivates the entire decision process."]}]},{"n":"B.5","t":"Decision making situations","b":[{"p":"Managers decide under 3 possible situations, distinguished by how much is known about future outcomes:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Situation","Description"],"rows":[["**Certainty**","Relevant parameters (costs, capacity, demand) have KNOWN values — reliable, available information; the decision-maker knows the cause-effect relationship and that action will lead to only ONE outcome. Also called a \"deterministic\" situation."],["**Risk**","Parameters have probabilistic outcomes — the decision-maker can define the problem, specify event probabilities, identify alternatives and state the probability of each alternative's outcome (Bell, 1988). Information is incomplete but reliable; probabilities are usually estimated from historical frequency. Also called a \"stochastic\" situation — common in modern business."],["**Uncertainty**","It is impossible to assess the likelihood of various future events — decisions are necessarily SUBJECTIVE. Neither the outcome NOR its probability is known; the decision-maker has only a meagre data base and cannot even reliably define the problem, let alone alternatives/outcomes. Managers must acquire what relevant information they can, then rely on logic/intuition/judgment/experience. This is the condition managers are LEAST confident in and MOST prone to error under — e.g. launching a new product into an untested market, or entering a politically-volatile international market."]]}}]},{"n":"B.6","t":"Individual decision-making","b":[{"p":"**Individual decision-making**: a manager selects a course of action alone (perhaps after gathering colleague input) — common for routine/programmed, low-risk decisions (work allocation, reordering from a known supplier, handling complaints, discount decisions)."},{"h4":"B.6.1 – B.6.2  Advantages and disadvantages of individual decision-making"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Individual decisions","Detail"],"rows":[["Advantages","Fast decision; clear responsibility assignment; fast implementation; high manager job satisfaction; the manager's own experience is directly applied."],["Disadvantages","Outcomes may be skewed by individual perception/values/priorities; relies on limited information; risk of \"passing the buck\"; often inappropriate for complex/novel/high-uncertainty problems; may lack stakeholder support, reducing success chances; misses institutional knowledge spread across the organisation."]]}}]},{"n":"B.7","t":"Group decision-making","b":[{"def":{"t":"Group","d":"two or more freely-interacting individuals sharing a common identity and purpose (Kreitner, 2000) — committees, teams, boards, task forces are common decision-making group forms."}},{"p":"Group decision-making has grown with organisational complexity and the information volume needed for sound decisions — advocates cite participative buy-in, member development, and the fact that modern issues (rapid tech change, government influence, social responsibility) exceed any single manager's expertise. It is PARTICULARLY appropriate for non-programmed decisions, given their complexity."},{"h4":"B.7.1 – B.7.2  Advantages and disadvantages of group decision-making"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Group decisions","Detail"],"rows":[["Advantages","More comprehensive information from diverse specialties; generates more alternatives; more effective implementation (implementers were involved, raising commitment); reduces individual bias/unreliability; trains subordinates in objective-setting/evaluation/decision skills; more democratic (vs. individual decisions' relative autocracy)."],["Disadvantages","Time-consuming (assembling the group, weighing many opinions); social pressure to conform (\"groupthink\"); personality conflicts diminishing process efficiency/decision quality; group goals may conflict with organisational goals; \"focus effect\" (fixating on a few alternatives, limiting choice); more expensive than individual decision-making."]]}}]},{"n":"B.8","t":"Decision-making techniques","b":[{"p":"Decision-making techniques are tools enhancing decision efficiency/effectiveness — an orderly, systematic, objective, scientific framework for defining/analysing/solving problems, also serving to justify/evaluate a manager's own performance."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Technique","Description"],"rows":[["**Brainstorming**","A group generates ideas under rules designed to promote new ideas while avoiding face-to-face inhibition: no idea is too ridiculous; every idea belongs to the GROUP, not its stater (encouraging building on others' ideas); no idea may be criticised during the session (purpose is generation, not evaluation)."],["**The Delphi technique**","A systematic procedure developing expert consensus — experts complete detailed written questionnaires (avoiding direct contact/debate, which risks hasty commitment), then revise their own answers after reading others' anonymous responses over several \"rounds,\" until opinions converge. The average of stabilised opinions represents the \"group\" decision (Stoner 1978; Griffin 1999). Research shows little further change typically occurs after the 2nd round."],["**The Nominal Group Technique (NGT)**","A structured group meeting combining verbal/non-verbal stages: (1) 7-10 people sit together WITHOUT talking, writing ideas privately; (2) after 5 minutes, each shares ONE idea in turn (a recorder lists them on a flip chart) — no discussion yet, continuing until ideas are exhausted (typically 18-25 ideas); (3) structured discussion of each idea; (4) independent private voting/ranking — the group decision is the mathematically-pooled outcome of individual votes."],["**Marginal analysis**","Compares additional revenue vs. additional cost as output increases — useful for profit maximisation or finding a machine's best output; profit is maximised where marginal revenue equals marginal cost."],["**Cost-benefit (cost-effectiveness) analysis**","An improvement on marginal analysis — compares the cost-to-benefit RATIO across alternatives, selecting the one with the best ratio (least-costly path to an objective, or highest-value expenditure)."],["**Decision trees**","A graphical \"tree\" display of decision points, chance events and probabilities across possible courses — lets decision-makers consider alternatives, assign financial values, estimate outcome probabilities, compare, and choose the best option. By incorporating event probabilities, managers see the TRUE probability of a decision succeeding — a \"best estimate\" may prove quite risky. Decision trees replace broad judgment with focus on the important elements, expose hidden premises, and disclose the reasoning behind decisions made under uncertainty."]]}},{"note":"Delphi vs. NGT: in Delphi, all communication is via written questionnaire and participants are physically distant/anonymous to each other; NGT participants meet face-to-face around a table. Practical factors (working hours available, cost, participant proximity) influence which technique is chosen."}]},{"n":"B.9","t":"Summary — Section B","b":[{"p":"**Decision-making is the selection of a course of action from among alternatives**; it is the core of planning. Managers make decisions that must be carried out by others. The **types** of decisions they make, and the **conditions** under which they make them, will vary. They must therefore **tailor their decision-making approach to their particular problems and circumstances**."},{"p":"**Programmed decisions** are those adopted by reason of the routine nature of the problem, by habit or by policy. **Non-programmed decisions** are those that are new. **Most important decisions will be non-programmed**; they require careful and logical consideration."},{"p":"The conditions under which managers make decisions will vary with the **amount of information** they have. Under conditions of **certainty**, managers know precisely what the results of each available alternative will be. Under conditions of **risk**, they know within a small margin of error the probable outcome of each alternative. Under conditions of **uncertainty**, the probabilities are not known precisely."},{"p":"When managers face an important problem or opportunity and it is their responsibility to decide what to do about it, they can best arrive at a good, rational decision by using the **formal decision-making approaches**. **Group decision making** was discussed, and different types of group decision making technique were explained."}]},{"n":"B.10","t":"Review questions — Section B, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section B review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["A decision made in response to changes in the external environment is said to be ……… **(a)** Proactive; **(b)** Intuitive; **(c)** Reactive; **(d)** Systematic; **(e)** Pragmatic.","A group decision making technique where individuals are not allowed to criticise solutions suggested by others is known as: **(a)** The Delphi technique; **(b)** MBO technique; **(c)** Brainstorming technique; **(d)** The Nominal Group Technique (NGT); **(e)** Random sample technique.","Which of these is **NOT** an advantage of individual decision making? **(a)** Decision is fast; **(b)** Implementation of the decision will be fast; **(c)** Decision is generally correct; **(d)** Job satisfaction of the manager will be high; **(e)** The decision maker can easily be identified.","Which of these is **NOT** a disadvantage of group decision making? **(a)** It is expensive; **(b)** It breeds personality conflict; **(c)** It provides more information; **(d)** Consensus may not be genuine; **(e)** It is time consuming.","A statement showing the direction of an organisation is called ……… **(a)** Vision; **(b)** Mission; **(c)** Objective; **(d)** Focus; **(e)** Goal."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["Decisions that are repetitive and continuous in nature are known as ………","A situation where a decision maker has some probabilistic estimate of the outcomes of each alternative is referred to as ………","……… is a conscious choice among alternative courses of action.","……… process is a series or chain of related steps that lead to an action or an outcome.","……… is a process where a group of individuals generate ideas according to a set of rules designed to promote the generation of new ideas, while at the same time avoiding inhibitions that face-to-face groups usually cause."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**C** — Reactive"],["2","**C** — Brainstorming technique"],["3","**C** — Decision is generally correct"],["4","**C** — It provides more information"],["5","**A** — Vision"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Programmed decisions**","**Decision-making risk**","**Decision**","**Decision making**","**Brainstorming**"]},{"key":"MCQ 3 and 4 are the same comparison from both sides, and the answers are the **strengths of the group**. Individual decision-making is **fast** to make and implement, satisfying for the manager, and easy to hold to account — but it is **not generally more correct**, because one person has less information. Group decision-making **provides more information**, which is its advantage, at the cost of expense, time and personality conflict."},{"note":"MCQ 2's answer is **brainstorming**, the technique whose first rule is that no suggestion may be criticised — which short answer 5 then describes in full. Do not confuse it with the **Delphi** technique, where participants never meet and respond through successive anonymous questionnaires, or the **nominal group technique**, where members write ideas independently before a structured discussion and vote."}]}}]},{"n":"C.1","t":"Definition of organising","b":[{"def":{"t":"Organising","d":"the process of structuring both human and physical resources to accomplish organisational objectives (Ivancevich et al., 1994) — grouping activities/resources among people and systematically integrating groups into a unified system (Lawal, 1993)."}}]},{"n":"C.2","t":"Meaning and nature of organisation","b":[{"p":"Organising entails: determining tasks needed for objectives; dividing tasks into jobs; logically grouping total tasks into activities; arranging task activities efficiently; assigning/delegating authority; setting up an integration system for unified operation; and monitoring organisational effectiveness/efficiency to make adjustments."},{"def":{"t":"Organisation","d":"a system of consciously coordinated activities/forces of two or more persons (Barnard, 1938) — whenever people formally agree to combine efforts for a common purpose, an organisation forms. A ONE-TIME, temporary effort (two students pushing a broken-down car once) does NOT constitute an organisation; a recurring, purposeful combination of effort (doing this for a living) does."}}]},{"n":"C.3","t":"Purpose of organising","b":[{"p":"**Purpose of organising**: actualising planned goal accomplishment — establishing a logical relationship pattern among members for efficient resource utilisation, effective communication, and minimal conflict. Builds on division of labour to secure specialisation's advantages, coordinating diverse units/departments/divisions toward efficient goal achievement."}]},{"n":"C.4","t":"Principles of organisation","b":[{"ol":["**Objective** — every organisation/part must express the undertaking's purpose, or is meaningless/redundant;","**Specialisation** — each member's activities should be confined, as far as possible, to a single function;","**Coordination** — organising exists (distinct from the undertaking's own purpose) to facilitate coordination — unity of effort;","**Authority** — supreme authority must rest somewhere, with a clear line of authority to every individual;","**Responsibility** — a superior's responsibility for subordinates' acts is ABSOLUTE;","**Definition** — each position's duties/authority/responsibility/relationships must be clearly defined in writing and published;","**Correspondence** — in every position, responsibility and authority should correspond;","**Span of control** — no one should supervise more than 5 (at most 6) direct, interlocking subordinates;","**Balance** — the various organisational units should be kept in balance; and","**Continuity** — reorganisation is a continuous process requiring specific ongoing provision."]}]},{"n":"C.5","t":"Characteristics of organisations","b":[{"ol":["**Purpose/objective/goal** — a rallying point unifying members AND a performance-measurement means;","**Task/activity** — every organisation performs a major task directed at its mission;","**Division of labour** — each member gets a fraction of the overall task, raising individual output and organisational efficiency;","**Hierarchical structure of authority/responsibility** — shows how duties are divided and how different members' efforts are integrated;","**Employees** — human resources make the organisation concept meaningful, since it cannot exist without people; and","**Input-output analysis** — every organisation obtains inputs from, and returns outputs to, the external environment."]}]},{"n":"C.6","t":"Classifications of organisation","b":[{"p":"Organisations are classified using 4 criteria: structure, ownership, type of authority, and major purpose."},{"h4":"(A) By structure — formal vs. informal"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Formal organisation**","Management-recognised patterns of relationship, communication channels and authority structure — clearly defined roles/jobs/hierarchy/rules/procedures, from conscious management decisions."],["**Informal organisation**","Exists within, but is not officially part of, the formal organisation — serving needs (economic/social/psychological/cultural) the formal organisation cannot easily serve (e.g. Esusu groups, prayer fellowships, cooperative societies, ethnic associations, cult groups). Has its OWN objectives, roles, communication patterns, leadership, rules — often written into a members' constitution."]]}},{"p":"A formal organisation: helps achieve objectives; puts resources to best use; secures specialisation benefits; develops cooperation; limits executive conflict; prevents work duplication; makes employee efficiency easily measurable; provides well-defined authority/responsibility; makes officers accountable; and enables work standardisation. Drawbacks: change is difficult; rules matter more than persons; impersonal (no room for human feelings); recognises only FORMAL communication; risks excessive/undue authority use; ignores social/other human needs."},{"p":"An informal organisation: may fill gaps in managers' abilities; helps solve members' work problems; develops behavioural norms distinguishing legitimate/illegitimate activity; recognises talented workers as leaders; helps managers build better subordinate relations; fills communication gaps. Drawbacks: its leader may become a troublemaker, working against management policy; tends to resist change (obstructing growth); breeds dangerous rumours; may create ROLE CONFLICT between formal and informal expectations; and exerts strong conformity pressure on members."},{"h4":"(B) By ownership"},{"p":"**Private sector organisations** — owned/financed by individuals, purely commercial (profit, return on capital, market share). **Public sector organisations** — owned/financed by government (federal/state/local/agencies), created for essential public services rather than profit — profits, where earned, are ploughed back or used to fund further public-benefit organisations."},{"h4":"(C) By type of authority (Weber's 3 authority types, applied to organisations)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Organisation type","Description"],"rows":[["**Traditional**","Acceptance of authority arises from tradition/custom — e.g. Obas, Emirs, Obis, the Pope, the Queen."],["**Charismatic**","Authority legitimised by confidence in the leader's personal qualities — e.g. Obafemi Awolowo, Nnamdi Azikiwe, Kwame Nkrumah, Aminu Kano, Nelson Mandela, Ahmadu Bello. The organisation may collapse on the leader's death unless there is a legitimate heir."],["**Bureaucratic**","Acceptance arises from the OFFICE/POSITION, bounded by rules/procedures (Weber's rational-legal authority) — the most common structure among private businesses and public organisations."]]}},{"p":"(Cole, 1996, citing Weber, 1949: authority differs from POWER — power is unilateral, forcing behaviour via strength or reward; authority requires acceptance by those over whom it is exercised, so it can only be exercised within limits agreeable to subordinates.)"},{"h4":"(D) By purpose"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Business organisation**","Exists to make profit in a socially acceptable manner (e.g. UAC Foods, Nigeria Bottling Company Plc, Seven-Up Bottling Plc) — no business survives or grows without profit, which measures efficient resource allocation."],["**Non-profit (service) organisation**","Benevolent, not financial, goals (e.g. Yaba Psychiatric Hospital, University of Lagos) — success is measured by service quality, not naira; key problems are applicant screening and securing reliable funding (fees, donations, grants)."],["**Mutual benefit organisation**","Members bind together to pursue goals difficult individually, mutually benefiting members (e.g. NECA, MAN, professional bodies)."],["**Commonwealth organisation**","Offers standardised public services to ALL members of a population (unlike non-profit organisations serving specific segments) — e.g. the Nigeria Police Force, the Nigerian Army."]]}}]},{"n":"C.7","t":"Factors influencing management choice of organisation structure","b":[{"p":"Nature of business/functions; organisation size; line of authority; objectives/tasks/goals; market trend; geographical dispersion; government policies; growth rate; technology/other resources; strategy; ownership/control; the firm's origin/history; and organisational culture."}]},{"n":"C.8","t":"Consequences of a badly designed organisation structure","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Consequence","Causes"],"rows":[["**Low motivation/morale**","Inconsistent/arbitrary decisions; insufficient delegation; unclear job definition/performance assessment; competing pressures from different parts of the organisation; overloaded managers/supervisors with inadequate support systems."],["**Late/inappropriate decisions**","Lack of timely, relevant information to the right people; poor cross-unit decision-maker coordination; overloaded decision-makers from insufficient delegation; inadequate procedures for reviewing past decisions."],["**Conflict/lack of coordination**","Conflicting goals, unclear objectives/priorities causing cross-purposes; failure to team up or liaise; a breakdown between planning and actual operations."],["**Poor response to opportunities/change**","No specialist role forecasting environmental change; inadequate attention to innovation/change-planning as core management activity; poor coordination between market-change identification and technological-solution research."],["**Rising costs**","A long hierarchy with many senior positions; excess administrative work at productive work's expense; and the presence of the other listed problems."]]}}]},{"n":"C.9","t":"Types or forms of organisational structure","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Structure","Description","Advantages / Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Functional structure**","Positions grouped by main functional area/expertise/similarity of work (production, marketing, finance, personnel). Efficient/economical, particularly for small single-product firms — creates a clear hierarchy.","Adv: promotes skill specialisation/deep expertise; reduces resource-duplication; enhances within-function coordination/career development; logical, easy to adopt; shared expertise between superior/subordinate; centralised decision-making; high-quality technical problem-solving; easier top-management control/training/supervision. Disadv: slow response to multi-functional problems; top-management decision overload; over-emphasises routine tasks; reduces inter-departmental communication; departmental (not organisational) focus; decision delay/coordination problems as the organisation grows."],["**Divisional structure**","Positions grouped by similarity of PRODUCT, LOCATION/geography, or CUSTOMER — adopted as functional structures become cumbersome with growth. 3 common divisional forms: product division (each unit handles a product/related family); place/geographic division (territorial units serving different regions/nations); customer division (units serving distinct customer segments — e.g. institutional/industrial/household products).","Product-division adv: greater product visibility, easier growth accommodation, fast product-change adaptability, cross-functional manager development, clear performance-evaluation basis. Disadv: may underuse skills/resources, poor cross-product coordination, restricts problem-solving to one product, limits career mobility outside the product line. Geographic-division adv: location-specific expertise, customer familiarity, national adaptability, lower-level responsibility, valuable manager training. Disadv: duplicated functions per division, harder top-management control, local-vs-corporate goal conflict, extensive coordinating rules needed. Customer-division adv: greater customer focus, clear key-customer identification, improved corporate image. Disadv: poor cross-customer coordination, resource-allocation politics, restricts problem-solving to one customer type."],["**Process structure**","Patterned on the specific production processes performed — e.g. a dry-cleaning firm's washing/drying/ironing/packing departments, or a tailoring firm's cutting/sewing/finishing/packing departments.","—"],["**Hybrid (mixed) structure**","Combines parts of several structures (most large organisations mix functional and divisional departments) — functional departments give resource-utilisation/scale/expertise efficiency; divisional departments give product/service/market focus. Best where an organisation faces environmental uncertainty AND needs functional expertise/efficiency.","Adv: specialised expertise plus scale economies in major functions; delegated decision authority relieving top management; adaptability/flexibility via partial divisionalisation; aligns divisional/corporate goals; promotes lower-level loyalty/commitment. Disadv: excessively large corporate-level functional staff; conflict as corporate departments try to control divisions; work duplication undermining scale economies; delayed response to situations needing division-corporate coordination."],["**Matrix structure**","Superimposes a horizontal DIVISIONAL reporting relationship onto a hierarchical FUNCTIONAL structure — used when members of different functional departments work together temporarily on a project. Has TWO chains of command (vertical functional heads, horizontal project managers — both sometimes called \"matrix bosses\").","Adv: avoids divisional-structure function duplication; encourages decentralised decision-making; improved environmental monitoring; fast response to change; maintains professional identification; flexible resource use. Disadv: high administrative cost; VIOLATES unity of command; possible slow response; heightened interpersonal-conflict risk; needs managers with strong human-relations skill; authority confusion."]]}},{"note":"Urwick (1947) emphasised technical planning of the organisation — determining and laying out structure — BEFORE considering individual members, since an organisation not founded on clear principles forces managers to \"play politics\" over promotion and similar decisions instead."}]},{"n":"C.10","t":"Line and staff relationship","b":[{"h4":"C.10.1 – C.10.2  Line authority and staff authority"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Authority type","Description"],"rows":[["**Line authority**","The simplest organisation type — people directly responsible for achieving organisational goals, forming a hierarchical structure from top to operative level, with clear unity of command (each person answers only to the one above). Adv: simple to operate; clearly-spelt-out/traceable responsibility-authority-accountability; fast communication/feedback; easy discipline/control given small size; close personnel relationships. Disadv: rigid/inflexible, prone to becoming dictatorial; inadequate once the firm grows and executives become overburdened; little time for strategic planning given day-to-day involvement; departments may favour self-interest over overall organisational welfare; no room for specialists."],["**Staff authority**","Those providing advice/support to line staff — handling details, locating decision-relevant data, offering counsel on specific problems via investigation/research/recording/standardisation. \"Staff thinks while the line acts.\""]]}},{"p":"**Staff positions** take 2 forms: **personal staff** (serving one executive — e.g. a managing director's secretary) and **specialised staff** (helping the line via special skills — audit, R&D, quality control — available to managers at all levels)."},{"p":"**Line-and-staff organisation** combines both — staff are experts with NO line authority, whose functions are largely advisory, deriving authority from their expertise and line managers' need to use it."},{"h4":"C.10.3 – C.10.5  Staff organisation structure, and line and staff organisation"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Line-and-staff org","Detail"],"rows":[["Advantages","Line managers gain specialist advice, improving decision quality; staff services train line managers; speeds line-function operations; preserves line stability/discipline while adding flexibility for new activities; repays its own cost via improved efficiency; enhances line-manager competence."],["Disadvantages","Line managers may over-rely on staff experts, losing their own judgement/initiative; staff officers can be ineffective, lacking implementation authority; authority/responsibility allocation between line and staff may be unclear."]]}},{"h4":"C.10.6  Line and staff conflicts"},{"p":"Line complains that: staff encroach on their authority; staff advice is often academic/impractical; line is blamed on project failure but staff gets credit on success; staff doesn't know the whole picture, only its specialised area; staff unnecessarily inflates line paperwork; and staff sometimes assumes line authority outright."},{"p":"Staff complains that: line is generally ignorant, resisting change/new ideas; line fears exposure of its own inefficiency through new methods; line distrusts/sabotages staff plans; line lacks the knowledge to translate advice into action; line gets preferential treatment on allowances/facilities; and line managers won't share success credit with staff."}]},{"n":"C.11","t":"Delegation of authority","b":[{"def":{"t":"Delegation","d":"the assignment of formal authority and responsibility for executing specific activities to a subordinate — necessary because, as an enterprise grows, one person cannot exercise all decision-making authority alone."}},{"key":"The delegation process: determine the results expected of a position; assign tasks to the subordinate; delegate authority to accomplish them; and hold the person responsible for accomplishment. Three attributes are prominent: **authority**, **responsibility** and **accountability**. Crucially: **responsibility cannot be delegated or transferred** — a superior can delegate the AUTHORITY to do a job, but retains ultimate RESPONSIBILITY for it being done; if a subordinate fails, the superior is held responsible."},{"h4":"C.11.1  The process of delegation"},{"p":"**Authority and responsibility should always be equal** — authority gives the power to make/enforce decisions on assigned duties, and responsibility obliges performing those duties USING that authority (Terry, 1977). **Accountability** is the obligation to render an account of fulfilling one's responsibilities to whoever one reports to, created once tasks/authority are assigned."},{"h4":"C.11.2  Advantages of delegation"},{"ul":["relieves managers of routine workload, freeing capacity for higher responsibility;","speeds up decision-making;","trains subordinates for higher responsibility and raises morale;","motivates subordinates to contribute constructively to organisational goals; and","creates a sense of belonging in the enterprise."]},{"h4":"C.11.3 – C.11.4  Barriers to effective delegation, and reasons for managers' reluctance to delegate"},{"ul":["fear the subordinate may perform better and gain notice;","egocentrism — the \"I can do it better myself\" fallacy;","not knowing how to properly organise/assign work;","poor ability to direct;","lack of confidence in subordinates;","fear of blame/punishment if a delegated task is poorly handled;","fear of losing control/authority; and","personal states (depression, anger, frustration, over-anxiety) leaving no clear headspace for delegation."]},{"h4":"C.11.5  Subordinates' reluctance to accept delegated authority"},{"ul":["preferring to avoid responsibility/risk, wanting their manager to decide instead;","fear of criticism for mistakes;","fear of work overload;","lack of self-confidence;","no matching additional incentive for additional responsibility; and","feeling they lack the relevant information to guide the assignment."]}]},{"n":"C.12","t":"Span of control","b":[{"def":{"t":"Span of control","d":"the number of employees a manager can effectively/efficiently supervise — determines whether the organisational hierarchy is TALL (narrow span, e.g. 4-5 subordinates, many layers/supervisors) or FLAT (wide span, e.g. 10-15 subordinates, fewer supervisory layers, more cost-effective given fewer supervisors on payroll)."}},{"note":"The traditionally recommended span is **5-7 subordinates per manager**, but this depends on subordinate skill/experience (wider span where skilled/experienced), task similarity, procedure standardisation, and the sophistication of the organisation's information system (all widening the feasible span)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Approach","Description"],"rows":[["**Centralisation**","Systematically concentrating decision-making in the hands of higher-level managers — little delegation, power/authority concentrated at the top. May suit small organisations in highly competitive industries, but grows inefficient as size/complexity/work-flow interdependence increase — hence larger organisations generally need LESS centralisation (more decentralisation)."],["**Decentralisation**","The systematic effort to delegate authority to lower levels — the real question is not WHETHER but TO WHAT EXTENT an organisation should decentralise; requires corresponding investment in control to ensure objectives are still met."]]}}]},{"n":"C.13","t":"Centralisation of authority","b":[{"h4":"C.13.1 – C.13.2  Advantages and disadvantages of centralisation"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Centralisation","Detail"],"rows":[["Advantages","Power/prestige for top executives; uniformity of policy/practice/decisions; minimised duplication of similar functions; less need for elaborate controlling procedures; a strong, coordinated top-management team."],["Disadvantages","Inflexibility/decision delay, slowing responses to external change; stifles lower-level creativity/initiative; demotivates lower managers excluded from important decisions; may lower productivity (workers less willing to accept responsibility/enthusiasm); requires close supervision/rigid controls; no room for training subordinates in decision-making."]]}}]},{"n":"C.14","t":"Decentralisation","b":[{"h4":"C.14.1 – C.14.2  Advantages and disadvantages of decentralisation"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Decentralisation","Detail"],"rows":[["Advantages","Stresses delegation, lightening top management's load; develops generalists (not just specialists), easing succession into general-manager roles; develops managers' own decision skills, motivating via performance-linked advancement; promotes intimate social ties, boosting enthusiasm/coordination; readily builds familiarity with specialised work; raises efficiency (managers near the activities they're responsible for); allows exploiting local conditions in multi-unit enterprises."],["Disadvantages","May cause diseconomies of scale as each decentralised unit's overhead rises; some managers still struggle to decide despite having authority — making accountability measurement time-consuming and inducing fear."]]}}]},{"n":"C.15","t":"Review questions — Section C, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"note":"Section C of this chapter has **no Summary** in the study text — it goes straight from §C.14 (Decentralisation) to its review questions. Sections A, B, E and F each have one."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section C review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["In the hierarchy of management, which of the following is odd? **(a)** Top management; **(b)** Production management; **(c)** Middle management; **(d)** Supervisory management; **(e)** First line management.","Which statement is **NOT** an advantage in a formal organisation? **(a)** Well-defined authority, responsibility and accountability; **(b)** Proper standardisation of work is practicable; **(c)** Formal communication is widely practised; **(d)** Duplication of work is practised; **(e)** Rules are considered religiously.","Which of these can **NOT** be associated with organising? **(a)** Dividing the tasks into jobs; **(b)** Arranging task activities into logical and comfortable groups; **(c)** Assigning and delegating authority; **(d)** Future oriented; **(e)** Setting up the system for integration of task activities.","Organisations can be classified by the following **EXCEPT** **(a)** Structure; **(b)** Government; **(c)** Ownership; **(d)** Type of authority; **(e)** Major purpose.","Which of these is **NOT** an advantage of matrix structure? **(a)** It encourages decentralised decision making; **(b)** Improved environmental monitoring; **(c)** Encourages unity of command; **(d)** Flexible use of organisational resources; **(e)** Professional identification is maintained."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The managerial function of assigning duties, grouping tasks, establishing the authority and providing resources to carry out a specific plan is known as ………","The efficient and effective grouping of jobs into meaningful work units, to coordinate a number of jobs towards accomplishing organisational goals, is referred to as ………","The principle of ……… states that in every position, the responsibility and authority should balance.","A/an ……… organisation is an organisation whose authority is legitimised by confidence in the personal qualities of the leader.","A/an ……… organisation is an organisation with benevolent goals rather than financial goals."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**B** — Production management"],["2","**D** — Duplication of work is practised"],["3","**D** — Future oriented"],["4","**B** — Government"],["5","**C** — Encourages unity of command"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Organising**","**Departmentation**","**Correspondence**","**Charismatic**","**Service**"]},{"key":"MCQ 5 is the key point about the **matrix structure**: it deliberately gives an employee **two bosses** — a functional manager and a project manager — and so **violates unity of command** rather than encouraging it. That is the structure's defining weakness, traded for flexible use of resources, better environmental monitoring, decentralised decisions and the retention of professional identity."},{"note":"MCQ 3's answer, **future oriented**, distinguishes organising from planning: planning is the future-oriented function (§A.3), while organising divides tasks, groups them, assigns authority and integrates activities. MCQ 1's answer, **production management**, is a *functional* area, not a *level* in the management hierarchy."},{"warn":"The answer to short answer 3 is given as **\"correspondence\"** — the principle that responsibility and authority should balance. The term is unusual; it appears in the ten principles of organisation at §C.4, and is more commonly called the principle of **parity of authority and responsibility**. Learn the book's word, since that is what the answer key expects."}]}}]},{"n":"D.1","t":"Definition of coordination","b":[{"def":{"t":"Coordination","d":"the process of integrating the activities/objectives of an organisation's separate work units to efficiently achieve organisational goals — the management function aiming for harmony of individual efforts toward company objectives, linking together the common actions of different units/sub-units. Without it, people/departments lose sight of their organisational role and may pursue selfish interests at the organisation's expense."}}]},{"n":"D.2","t":"Types of coordination","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Vertical coordination**","Linking superiors/subordinates of units at DIFFERENT organisational levels — linking top, middle and lower-level activity toward organisational goals."],["**Horizontal coordination**","Linking managers/units/sub-units across the structure at the SAME level — an additional means of processing information across the organisation."]]}}]},{"n":"D.3","t":"Need and objectives of coordination","b":[{"p":"Coordination need depends on task nature/communication requirements and the degree of interdependence between units — the GREATER the interdependence, the MORE coordination is needed. Arises from: **division of work** (once work is divided/assigned, coordination ensures efficient achievement of overall goals) and the resulting **interdependence between units**; also serves **reconciliation of individual and organisational goals**; **total accomplishment** (preventing duplicated effort, freeing time/energy for productive tasks); and **economy/efficiency** (economies of scale/labour/time/machinery, minimising delays)."}]},{"n":"D.4","t":"Techniques of coordination","b":[{"ul":["**simplified organisation structure** — reducing inter-unit interdependence (e.g. less shared-resource dependency) reduces the coordination need;","**plans, programmes and policies** — checking different individuals'/departments' plans for consistency, ensuring they add up to one united programme;","**rules and procedures** — coordinate different units by establishing how certain activities are carried out;","**coordination by communication** — the oldest, most important coordination device, via a supervisor directing/commanding/controlling subordinates' efforts;","**coordination by meetings** — bringing officials together for coordination opportunities;","**coordination through liaison roles** — liaison officers facilitating information flow between (or within) interdependent organisations/departments, ironing out friction; and","**managerial hierarchy** — a traditional technique, placing a manager to supervise interdependent work units directly."]}]},{"n":"E.1","t":"Definition of control","b":[{"def":{"t":"Controlling","d":"the process of regulating organisational activities so actual performance conforms to expected standards/goals — a fundamental function closely linked to planning/organising, also affecting motivation and team behaviour. Encompasses all activities a manager undertakes to ensure actual results conform to planned results."}}]},{"n":"E.2","t":"Meaning and purpose of control","b":[{"p":"Control is the process of MONITORING, COMPARING and CORRECTING performance to ensure objectives/plans are accomplished — a means of ensuring efficient achievement of set objectives."},{"ul":["**adapting to changing conditions** — monitoring/responding under uncertainty as planning-phase conditions shift;","**minimising errors** — small errors accumulate into big, costly problems; control anticipates and prevents them;","**coping with organisational complexity** — as scale grows, direct personal supervision alone becomes inadequate; a well-designed control system fills the gap; and","**minimising cost** — eliminating/reducing wastages and non-value-adding activities."]}]},{"n":"E.3","t":"Importance/usefulness of control","b":[{"p":"Control is needed to: standardise performance via benchmarks; increase efficiency by reducing waste; safeguard assets from theft/waste/misuse; standardise quality to specification; set limits on delegated authority; measure the effectiveness of plans/programmes; measure members' job performance; and adapt operations to external environmental change."}]},{"n":"E.4","t":"Link between planning and control","b":[{"key":"A common definition: \"control is the process through which managers assure that actual activities conform to planned activities.\" Planning and control are CONTINUOUSLY related — managers make plans, control evaluates their effectiveness; if control shows things on track, the plan is maintained; if not, the plan's course (or the plan itself) may need revision."},{"p":"Per Lawal (1993): **distinctions** — planning entails decision-making, while control is a monitoring process DEPENDENT on planning's existence; planning concerns ends and means, control focuses on results and feedback. **Similarities** — both identify/quantify performance standards using similar measures; both use quantitative techniques (linear programming, statistical control, network analysis)."}]},{"n":"E.5","t":"Types of control","b":[{"h4":"(A) By organisational level"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Strategic control**","Top management's responsibility — monitors critical environmental factors affecting strategic-plan viability, assesses strategic-action effects, ensures plans are implemented as intended. Typically quarterly/semi-annual/annual, though a volatile/competitive environment may require monthly or shorter cycles, drawing top management into tactical/operational control too."],["**Tactical control**","Middle-level managers' responsibility — focuses on departmental tactical-plan implementation, monitoring departmental results, taking corrective action; also involved in checking critical operational aspects."],["**Operational control**","Lower-level managers' responsibility — monitors day-to-day operative efforts/results; must be well-designed since undetected errors here can have strong negative organisation-wide effects."],["**Financial control**","Monitors the organisation's financial resources — e.g. checking that customers pay receivables on time."],["**Operations control**","Focuses on the processes transforming resources into products/services (quality control is one type)."],["**Structural control**","Concerns whether the organisation's structural elements serve their intended purposes."]]}},{"h4":"(B) By resource/programme"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Financial control**","Focuses on financial resources — the flow of funds into/through/out of the organisation (revenues, expenses, investments); a key tool is the budget (cash/revenue/expense/profit budgets)."],["**Materials (inventory) control**","Acquisition, storage, recording and use of materials for operations."],["**Quality control**","The quality of inputs acquired from suppliers, and of products/services offered to customers."],["**Behavioural control**","The behaviour of employees/other stakeholders — via direct supervision, performance appraisal, rules/procedures/regulations/policies, target-setting."]]}},{"h4":"(C) Structural control types"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Personal centralised control**","Direct personal supervision and centralised decision-making, revolving around an individual supervisor's attributes, direct observation, rewards/punishments."],["**Bureaucratic control**","Heavy reliance on rules/regulations/procedures/policies to control behaviour, backed by rewards/punishments."],["**Output control**","Focuses on system output — targets set per period, used as the basis for controlling actual output."],["**Cultural/clan control**","Develops strong individual-organisation identification — via selecting people who share the organisation's values/norms, and extensive training/socialisation."]]}},{"h4":"(D) By timing — feed-forward, concurrent and feedback control"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Feed-forward control**","Occurs BEFORE operations begin — anticipating/preventing potential problems by checking inputs (raw material quality, equipment maintenance) for correct specification/quantity."],["**Concurrent control**","Occurs WHILE operations are in progress — direct supervision/observation catches and corrects problems as they arise (possibly aided by mechanical/electronic monitoring devices)."],["**Feedback control**","The most COMMON, but most EXPENSIVE, traditional control type — occurs only AFTER the system has malfunctioned or a target is missed, comparing actual vs. planned output and analysing/correcting any deviation."]]}}]},{"n":"E.6","t":"Control process","b":[{"steps":["Establish standards and methods for measuring performance — ideally already stated in clear, measurable, deadline-bearing terms from the planning process. Standard types: physical (labour-hours/unit, machine-hours/unit, output/hour), cost (labour cost/unit, material cost/unit), capital (ROI, current ratio, debt ratio), and revenue (revenue per sales clerk, sales per capita/month) standards.","Measure performance — an ongoing, repeated process; the measurement METHOD depends on the standard set, and the measurement FREQUENCY depends on the activity type (e.g. continuous air-quality monitoring vs. once-or-twice-yearly review of long-term expansion progress). Good managers avoid long gaps between measurements.","Determine whether performance matches standards — the easiest step, comparing measured results against the established targets.","Take corrective action — necessary where performance falls short and analysis shows action is needed; this may mean changing an operational activity, OR (where controls reveal the standard itself was inappropriate) changing the standard instead. Managers who don't see this step through are merely MONITORING, not exercising real control — the emphasis should be on constructive improvement, not just identifying past failure."]}]},{"n":"E.7","t":"Characteristics of effective control systems","b":[{"ol":["**Accurate** — inaccurate data can cause an organisation to fail to fix a real problem or fix a nonexistent one;","**Objective and comprehensible** — understandable, objectively-seen information drives correct, timely corrective action;","**Timely** — information must be gathered/routed/evaluated quickly enough for corrective action to still help;","**Focused on strategic control points** — areas most prone to (or most harmed by) deviation, and where corrective action applies most effectively;","**Economically realistic** — control cost should not exceed control benefit;","**Organisationally realistic** — compatible with organisational realities, with a fair balance between required effort and reward;","**Coordinated with the organisation's work flow** — since each work-process step affects overall success, and information must reach everyone who needs it;","**Flexible** — able to react quickly to adverse change or new opportunity;","**Operational** — indicates what corrective action is needed (not just facts), delivered in a usable form to whoever must act; and","**Acceptable to organisation members** — encouraging autonomy/responsibility/growth, since excessive/rigid controls harm motivation."]},{"note":"Additional characteristics the source emphasises: **integration with planning** (the more explicit the link, the more effective the system); **future-oriented** (regulating future events, not just fixing blame for past ones); and **cost-effective/economically realistic**, reiterated as a standalone point given its importance."}]},{"n":"E.8","t":"Summary — Section E","b":[{"p":"The managerial function of **controlling is the measurement and correction of performance**, in order to ensure that enterprise objectives are accomplished. Wherever it is found and whatever is being controlled, the basic control process involves these steps: **(a)** establish standards and methods for measuring performance; **(b)** measure performance; **(c)** compare performance against standards; and **(d)** take corrective action and re-evaluate standards."},{"p":"To overcome **time lags** in control, it is suggested that managers utilise a **feed-forward** control approach and not rely on simple feedback alone. A variety of control tools and techniques have been used to help a manager effect control. **ZBB** (zero-based budgeting), which attempts to base resource allocations on **current rather than historical needs**, was explained."},{"p":"**Breakeven analysis** is designed to illustrate the relationship between costs, sales volume and profits. It can be used both as a decision-making aid and as a control device. **Management auditing** involves a systematic appraisal of management and organisational performance in a number of areas of operation. **PERT and CPM** networks illustrate not only the tasks involved in a project and the time it will take to complete them, but also the **interrelationship between those tasks** — in this way the project can be planned and integrated more effectively, and the project completion date determined and controlled more easily."},{"p":"**Strategic management** was defined as a process of developing a game plan by which organisations gain competitive advantage over rivals, attract and please customers, compete successfully, conduct operations and achieve other organisational objectives. The importance of strategic management was discussed, and the stages of the strategic management process — developing vision, mission and objectives; SWOT analysis; and the formation, implementation and evaluation of strategies — were articulated, with detailed attention to the **types of strategy: corporate, business, functional and operational**."},{"p":"Precisely, the stages in **decision making** involve: **(i)** diagnose and define the problem; **(ii)** establish specific goals and objectives; **(iii)** generate alternatives; **(iv)** gather and analyse the facts; **(v)** evaluate alternatives; **(vi)** select the best alternative; **(vii)** analyse possible consequences; **(viii)** implement the decision; and **(ix)** follow up."},{"note":"This summary ranges beyond Section E — it covers the control tools (ZBB, breakeven analysis, management auditing, PERT and CPM), then **strategic management**, which is Section F, and then restates the **nine-step decision process** of Section B. It is reproduced in full because several of those control tools are named **only here** and nowhere in the body of Section E."}]},{"n":"E.9","t":"Review questions — Section E, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section E review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Correcting deviations from standards is an aspect of: **(a)** Planning process; **(b)** Control process; **(c)** Actuating process; **(d)** Leading process; **(e)** Organising process.","The type of control in which ongoing operations are monitored to ensure that objectives are pursued is known as: **(a)** Concurrent control; **(b)** Feedback control; **(c)** Congruent control; **(d)** Feed-forward control; **(e)** Process control.","Which of the following types of strategy is crafted by the Chief Executive Officer and top management of an organisation? **(a)** Operating strategy; **(b)** Divisional strategy; **(c)** Functional strategy; **(d)** Corporate strategy; **(e)** Business strategy.","Which of these is **NOT** a control based on level in the organisation? **(a)** Corporate control; **(b)** Strategic control; **(c)** Tactical control; **(d)** Operational control; **(e)** Structural control.","Which of these is a **structural control**? **(a)** Feed-forward control; **(b)** Concurrent control; **(c)** Feedback control; **(d)** Cybernetic control; **(e)** Output control."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["A type of control system where action is taken only after a significant deviation has occurred is known as ………","The calculation of one or more financial ratios to assess some aspect of the organisation's financial health is referred to as ………","Strategies crafted by the head of marketing of an organisation are an example of ……… strategy.","……… control involves monitoring critical environmental factors that could affect the viability of strategic plans.","……… is the determination of the basic long-term goals and objectives of an enterprise, and the adoption of courses of action and the allocation of resources necessary to carry out the goals."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**B** — Control process"],["2","**A** — Concurrent control"],["3","**D** — Corporate strategy"],["4","**A** — Corporate control"],["5","**E** — Output control"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Feedback control**","**Ratio analysis**","**Functional strategy**","**Strategic**","**Strategic management**"]},{"key":"MCQ 2 and short answer 1 are the **timing** classification of control at §E.5(D), and the three must be kept apart: **feed-forward** control acts **before** the activity (anticipating problems); **concurrent** control monitors the activity **while it is going on**; and **feedback** control acts **after** a deviation has occurred. Feed-forward is preferable because feedback alone is always too late — which is the point the Section E summary makes about time lags."},{"note":"MCQ 4 and 5 test the two different classifications side by side. Control **by level** is corporate, strategic, tactical and operational — so \"structural control\" is the odd one out in MCQ 4. **Structural** control is a separate classification of its own, comprising **bureaucratic and clan (or output) control** — which is why **output control** is the answer to MCQ 5, while feed-forward, concurrent and feedback belong to the timing classification."}]}}]},{"n":"F.1","t":"Definition of strategic management","b":[{"def":{"t":"Strategic management","d":"the art and science of formulating, implementing and evaluating cross-functional decisions enabling an organisation to achieve its objectives — an ongoing process guiding the organisation toward its strategic goals given its internal/external environment. It goes BEYOND merely developing a strategic plan — it is the DEPLOYMENT and IMPLEMENTATION of that plan, plus the measurement/evaluation of results."}},{"p":"Deployment = completing and communicating the plan to all employees. Implementation = resourcing the plan, putting it into action, managing those actions. Measurement/evaluation = tracking implementation AND assessing how the organisation is changing as a result, feeding that information back to update the plan."}]},{"n":"F.2","t":"Meaning of strategy","b":[{"def":{"t":"Strategy","d":"from the Greek \"strategia\" (science of guiding/directing military forces, ~400 BC) — a company's \"game plan,\" reflecting awareness of how/when/where/against whom/for what purpose it should compete. In business: a comprehensive plan for accomplishing goals — how the organisation will operate, compete successfully, and attract/satisfy customers (Robbins and Coulter, 2009). It involves deploying resources to attain objectives, and is the pattern of significant decisions fulfilling the organisation's purpose over the long range."}},{"p":"Strategy answers: what goods should we produce? which market segment should we serve? how do we deal with competition? what are we out to achieve?"},{"key":"Mintzberg's (1994) **5 Ps of strategy**: as a **plan** (a means of deciding and implementing decisions); as a **ploy** (a specific manoeuvre to outwit a competitor); as a **pattern** (consistency in behaviour, intended or not); as a **position** (locating the organisation within its environment); and as a **perspective** (looking inside the organisation — strategy is to an organisation what personality is to an individual)."}]},{"n":"F.3","t":"Purpose/importance of strategic management","b":[{"ul":["**performance** — organisations that use strategic management tend to perform better (profitability, market position, growth);","**managing change** — a tool for managing internal/external change affecting objective attainment, helping cope with environmental uncertainty;","**effective coordination** — coordinates activities/resources toward overall objectives;","**focus** — provides objectives and a method for achieving them, focusing all organisational activity;","**monitoring** — enables control-system design that checks conformity with standards;","**member acceptance** — control systems work best when accepted by those affected; and","**timeliness / continual review** — data must meet specific deadlines to enable timely correction, and the control process itself needs continual review for effectiveness."]}]},{"n":"F.4","t":"Strategic management process","b":[{"steps":["Developing vision, mission and objectives — the MISSION states purpose (what the organisation is in business to do); the VISION states destination (what it is out to achieve); OBJECTIVES convert the mission into specific, quantifiable, deadline-bearing performance targets. This is the natural starting point, since every subsequent strategy aims at these.","SWOT analysis — analysing the external environment for Opportunities/Threats, and the internal environment for Strengths/Weaknesses.","Formulation of strategies — using the SWOT findings to formulate strategies that exploit strengths/opportunities, protect against threats, and correct critical weaknesses.","Implementation of strategies — a prerequisite for effectiveness; even a good strategy fails with improper implementation.","Evaluation — determines the impact of implemented strategies, establishing whether the strategy is working and whether adjustments are needed."]}]},{"n":"F.5","t":"SWOT analysis","b":[{"p":"SWOT (Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats) matches internal strengths/weaknesses against external opportunities/threats to formulate an effective strategy."},{"h4":"F.5.1 – F.5.4  Strength, weakness, opportunity and threat"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","Description and examples"],"rows":[["**Strength**","A positive circumstance favouring success — policy aim: take advantage of it. Examples: good leadership; dedicated/experienced employees; good communication skills; extensive product knowledge/expertise; long-time community presence; networking/community relationships; technology know-how."],["**Weakness**","A negative, unfavourable condition — policy aim: avoid/overcome it. Examples: inconsistent pricing strategy; inconsistent branding/marketing messages; limited/overcrowded workspace; inconsistent stakeholder communication; unwritten administration processes; lack of an organisational chart."],["**Opportunity**","An innovative external event/condition that could make the activity more successful — policy aim: exploit it. NOT to be confused with a strength. Examples: currently limited competition; a skilled workforce amid high local unemployment; low-priced available office space; community growth; growing demand for technology skills/computer networking."],["**Threat**","A potential external event/condition that could harm the activity — policy aim: avoid/overcome it. Examples: increased local competition/national-firm market entry; margin erosion from low-cost competitors; a shrinking pool of qualified hires as unemployment declines."]]}}]},{"n":"F.6","t":"Levels/types of an organisation's strategies","b":[{"h4":"F.6.1 – F.6.4  Corporate, business, functional and operating strategy"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Strategy level","Description"],"rows":[["**Corporate strategy**","Drafted by Directors/CEOs — spells out the current/future businesses the organisation wants to be in and what it hopes to achieve, based on the organisation's vision/mission. A **growth strategy** (a form of corporate strategy) stimulates growth by expanding products/markets served, via current or new businesses (e.g. Nigerian Bottling Company Plc diversifying into packaged fruit juice/table water)."],["**Business strategy**","Actions/approaches crafted for successful performance in ONE specific line of business (e.g. a strategy to enhance the market position of soft drinks within a diversified beverage company) — crafted by managers of each business line, with input from functional-area heads."],["**Functional strategy**","Actions/approaches used by functional departments to support the business strategy (e.g. marketing strategy, production strategy) — crafted by respective function heads, with final approval from the business unit's General Manager."],["**Operating strategy**","Approaches for managing key operating units (plants, distribution centres, geographic units) and units of strategic significance (advertising campaigns, brand management, online sales) — e.g. a plant manager's approach to running a specific plant, aligned with the overall production strategy. Lead responsibility is usually delegated to frontline managers, subject to higher review/approval."]]}},{"h4":"F.6.5  Distinction between corporate and divisional strategy"},{"p":"**Corporate strategy** offers direction for accomplishing long-term objectives, developed per the organisation's objectives/scope, considering its operating environment/market position/competition. Examines current market position, weighing diversification risk/reward against staying focused on a single market — answering \"what businesses/industries should we be in?\" Based on the organisation's vision, typically developed by top-level management (CEO, board, MD)."},{"p":"**Divisional/business strategy** exists a step below corporate strategy, for organisations with multiple business units/departments spread across different businesses/markets — determining how a PARTICULAR business unit will accomplish its goals. Usually executes 3 strategies: **differentiation**, **cost leadership**, and **focus** — creating a competitive edge, developed by middle-level management (department/division/line managers) for shorter-term, routine-issue solutions."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis","Corporate strategy","Divisional/Business strategy"],"rows":[["Meaning","Directions for accomplishing long-term objectives.","Determines how a unit's specific objectives are achieved."],["Developed by","Top-level management (CEO, Board, MD).","Middle-level management (department/division/line managers)."],["Strategic decisions","Analytical, value-oriented — organisational growth/profit maximisation.","Activity decisions to stay ahead of market competition."],["Formulated at","The organisational level.","A particular business unit/division."],["Time horizon","Long-term solutions for the organisation.","Relatively short-term solutions for routine issues."],["Focus","Which business(es) the company should pursue.","The business plan for competing successfully in the market."],["Examples","Expansion, downsizing, stability.","Cost leadership, differentiation, focus."]]}}]},{"n":"F.7","t":"Summary — Section F","b":[{"p":"**Strategic management** was defined as a process of **developing a game plan by which organisations gain competitive advantage over rivals**, attract and please customers, compete successfully, conduct operations and achieve other organisational objectives. The **importance of strategic management** was adequately discussed. The stages of the strategic management process — **developing vision, mission and objectives; SWOT analysis; and the formation, implementation and evaluation of strategies** — were well articulated, and detailed attention was given to the types of strategy, namely **corporate strategy, business strategy, functional strategy and operational strategy**."}]},{"n":"F.8","t":"Review questions — Section F, with the study text's solutions","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section F review questions","t":"Twelve multiple choice questions, with the official solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Which is the functional area of strategic management? **A.** Production and operation; **B.** Finance; **C.** Marketing; **D.** Research & Development; **E.** All of the above.","Which of the following is **not** part of the task environment? **A.** Technology; **B.** Shareholders; **C.** Competitors; **D.** Publics; **E.** Creditors.","The word **tactic** is most likely to be associated with: **A.** Business Strategy; **B.** Corporate strategy; **C.** Operational Strategy; **D.** Divisional strategy; **E.** All of the above.","Which of these seeks to relate the goals of an organisation to the means of achieving them? **A.** Strategy; **B.** Levels; **C.** Monitoring; **D.** Management; **E.** Relationship management.","When market and competitive conditions take an unexpected turn, the required strategy is ……… **A.** Proactive; **B.** Reactive; **C.** Adaptive; **D.** Both; **E.** None.","At which level do we have top managers? **A.** Corporate Level; **B.** Business Level; **C.** Functional Level; **D.** None of these; **E.** Board level.","What is required by companies to meet changing conditions in their industries? **A.** Strategy; **B.** Vision; **C.** Develop long term strategies; **D.** Mission; **E.** All of the above.","In large organisations, operational strategies are formulated at which level? **A.** Corporate level; **B.** Divisional Level; **C.** Functional level; **D.** Business Level; **E.** All of the above.","Which is **not** an advantage of strategic management? **A.** Helps organisations to be proactive; **B.** Control their own destiny in a better manner; **C.** Identify available opportunity; **D.** Minimise threats in the marketplace; **E.** None of the above.","Which is **not** a limitation of strategic management? **A.** Time consuming process; **B.** Difficult estimation of competitive responses; **C.** Costly process; **D.** Understandable complex environment; **E.** All of the above.","Developing a vision and mission, identifying an organisation's external opportunities and threats, and determining internal strengths and weaknesses are all ……… activities. **A.** strategy-formulation; **B.** strategy-implementation; **C.** long-range planning; **D.** short-range planning; **E.** None of the above.","The means by which long-term objectives will be achieved are **A.** mission statements; **B.** strategies; **C.** vision statements; **D.** long-term goals; **E.** Goals."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**E** — All of the above"],["2","**A** — Technology"],["3","**C** — Operational Strategy"],["4","**A** — Strategy"],["5","**B** — Reactive"],["6","**A** — Corporate Level"],["7","**E** — All of the above"],["8","**C** — Functional level"],["9","**E** — None of the above"],["10","**D** — Understandable complex environment"],["11","**A** — strategy-formulation"],["12","**B** — strategies"]]}},{"key":"Questions 3, 6 and 8 all test the **four levels of strategy** at §F.6, and the way to keep them straight is by **who crafts them**: **corporate** strategy by the CEO and top management at corporate level; **business (or divisional)** strategy by divisional heads; **functional** strategy by the heads of functions such as marketing or finance; and **operating** strategy at the operating level, which is where the word **tactic** belongs."},{"note":"Question 2's answer, **technology**, applies Chapter 1's classification of the business environment: shareholders, competitors, publics and creditors are all parties the firm deals with — the **task** environment — whereas technology is one of the PESITEL forces of the **general/mega** environment."},{"note":"Questions 9 and 10 are the mirror of each other and both resolve to the odd option. All four substantive options in question 9 **are** advantages of strategic management, so the answer is \"none of the above\". In question 10, three options are genuine limitations (time, cost, difficulty of estimating competitors' responses) but an **\"understandable\"** environment is not a limitation at all — it is the *complexity* and unpredictability of the environment that limits strategic management."},{"note":"Question 11 names the first stage of the strategic management process — **strategy formulation**, which covers vision and mission, external opportunities and threats, and internal strengths and weaknesses (that is, the SWOT analysis of §F.5). Implementation and evaluation follow it. Question 12 then gives the definition of a strategy: the **means by which long-term objectives are achieved**."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Given the sheer size of this chapter, prioritise the frameworks most likely to be independently tested: the 9-step planning process and 9-step rational decision-making process (memorise as ordered sequences, since exam questions often test \"which step comes after X\"); the 3 decision situations (certainty/risk/uncertainty) and their exact distinguishing features; the 5 organisational structure types with their defining trade-off (functional = efficient but slow on cross-function problems; matrix = flexible but violates unity of command); the 3 W's of coordination (vertical/horizontal) vs. control (feed-forward/concurrent/feedback) — these two 3-part typologies are easy to cross-confuse; and the SWOT/4-strategy-level framework, since strategic management questions are common and self-contained.","errors":["Confusing feed-forward control (before operations begin) with feedback control (after a malfunction/missed target) — feed-forward is preventive and proactive; feedback is the most common but most EXPENSIVE type precisely because it only acts after damage is already done.","Treating span of control and centralisation/decentralisation as the same concept — span of control is about how many people ONE manager supervises (affecting hierarchy height); centralisation/decentralisation is about WHERE decision-making authority sits in the hierarchy. A narrow span does not automatically mean centralised authority.","Assuming responsibility, like authority, CAN be delegated — per the chapter's own explicit rule, a superior can delegate the AUTHORITY to perform a task, but always retains ultimate RESPONSIBILITY; if a subordinate fails, the delegating superior is still held responsible.","Mixing up corporate strategy and divisional/business strategy's TIME HORIZON and DEVELOPER — corporate strategy is long-term and top-management-developed; divisional/business strategy is comparatively short-term and middle-management-developed. Many candidates also wrongly assume \"divisional strategy\" and \"corporate strategy\" are interchangeable labels for the same level."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a kind of enterprise plan?","o":["Divisional plans","Operational plans","Functional plans","Regional plans","Corporate plans"],"a":1,"w":"The 4 kinds of enterprise plans are divisional, functional, regional and corporate plans. Operational plans are one of the 3 plans based on FUNCTIONS/ACTIVITIES (alongside strategic and tactical plans) — a different classification.","src":"Chapter 3, Section A Review Questions","sec":"A.4"},{"q":"A group decision-making technique where individuals are not allowed to criticise solutions suggested by others is known as:","o":["The Delphi technique","MBO technique","Brainstorming technique","The Nominal Group Technique (NGT)","Random sample technique"],"a":2,"w":"Brainstorming's basic rules explicitly forbid criticising any idea during the session — the purpose is generating ideas, not evaluating them.","src":"Chapter 3, Section B Review Questions","sec":"B.5"},{"q":"Which statement is NOT an advantage of a formal organisation?","o":["Well-defined authority, responsibility and accountability","Proper standardisation of work is practicable","Formal communication is widely practised","Duplication of work is practised","Rules are considered religiously"],"a":3,"w":"A formal organisation PREVENTS duplication of work — this is listed as an advantage, not something the organisation practises as a drawback.","src":"Chapter 3, Section C Review Questions","sec":"C.3"},{"q":"The type of control in which on-going operations are monitored to ensure that objectives are pursued is known as:","o":["Concurrent control","Feedback control","Congruent control","Feed-forward control","Process control"],"a":0,"w":"Concurrent control takes place WHILE operations are in progress, via direct supervision/observation, correcting problems as they arise — distinct from feed-forward (before operations begin) and feedback (after a malfunction).","src":"Chapter 3, Section E Review Questions","sec":"E.3"},{"q":"Which of the following types of strategy is crafted by the Chief Executive Officer and top management of an organisation?","o":["Operating strategy","Divisional strategy","Functional strategy","Corporate strategy","Business strategy"],"a":3,"w":"Corporate strategy is drafted by Directors and Chief Executive Officers, spelling out the current/future businesses the organisation wants to be in.","src":"Chapter 3, Section F Review Questions","sec":"F.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Outline the 9 steps in the rational decision-making process, and explain why the \"follow-up\" step can reactivate the entire process.","marks":9,"a":[{"p":"The 9 steps: (1) diagnose and define the problem/opportunity; (2) establish specific goals and objectives; (3) generate alternatives; (4) gather and analyse the relevant facts; (5) evaluate the alternatives; (6) select an alternative; (7) analyse the possible consequences of the decision; (8) implement the decision; (9) follow up."},{"p":"The follow-up step compares actual results against planned results (objectives). If a deviation exists, changes must be made — either in the solution chosen, in how it was implemented, or, if the original objective is now deemed unattainable, in the objective itself. Revising the ORIGINAL OBJECTIVE means the entire 9-step process must restart from Step 2 (or even Step 1, if the underlying problem definition also needs revisiting) — which is why follow-up is not simply a final check, but a potential trigger for reactivating the whole decision cycle."}],"src":"Chapter 3.B.2","sec":"B.2"},{"q":"Distinguish between decision-making under risk and decision-making under uncertainty, giving one practical business example of each.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Risk**: the decision-maker knows the likelihood (probability) that each possible state of nature will occur — information is incomplete but RELIABLE, often estimated from historical frequency. Example: a company launching a product similar to past launches can estimate a probability of success based on historical sales data for comparable products."},{"p":"**Uncertainty**: neither the outcome NOR its probability can be reliably assessed — decisions are necessarily subjective, made with a meagre data base, relying on logic/intuition/judgment/experience rather than known probabilities. Example: a company entering an entirely new international market cannot reliably predict the political/legal/socio-cultural environment in absolute terms — even expert political-risk analysts may fail to foresee a sudden change in government."}],"src":"Chapter 3.B.3","sec":"B.3"}]}},{"n":4,"t":"Organic Business Functions","brief":"The other largest chapter in the subject — 4 self-contained sections covering the organic functions of a business: Human Resources Management (planning, recruitment/selection/placement, induction, job analysis, training, appraisal, compensation, discipline, industrial relations), Production Management (plant location/layout, production systems), Marketing Management (the marketing mix, segmentation, product/price/promotion/place, the extended 7Ps, e-marketing), and Finance and Accounting (finance/accounting functions, the money and capital markets and their institutions).","outcomes":["Explain human resources management, its components and functions, planning, recruitment/selection/placement, training/development, performance appraisal, and wage/salary administration","Explain production management — plant location, plant layout, and production systems","Explain the evolution of marketing management, the marketing mix, market segmentation, and the extended 7Ps of marketing","Explain the finance and accounting functions, the structure of an accounting department, and the money and capital markets"],"secs":[{"n":"A.1","t":"Human resource in organisations","b":[{"p":"HRM has 10 components: human resource planning; recruitment; selection; placement; induction; training and development; performance appraisal; employee compensation; welfare services/benefits; and industrial relations."}]},{"n":"A.2","t":"Meaning of human resource management","b":[{"def":{"t":"Human resource management (HRM)","d":"a strategic, coherent approach to managing an organisation's most valued asset — the people who individually/collectively contribute to its objectives; the totality of management decisions/actions affecting the organisation-employee relationship. HRM/HR has largely replaced the older term \"personnel management,\" and is an integral part of every manager's function wherever people work under them — though larger organisations often have a dedicated HR department."}}]},{"n":"A.3","t":"Components of human resource management","b":[{"p":"The study text treats the **components** of human resource management as the core activity areas the department is built from — human resource planning, recruitment and selection, performance appraisal, welfare services and benefits, employee compensation, and industrial relations. Each has its own section below (§A.5 to §A.17), and the eleven departmental **functions** that deliver them are listed at §A.4."}]},{"n":"A.4","t":"Functions of human resource management/department","b":[{"ol":["**Manpower planning** — forecasting future employee numbers/skills needed;","**Job analysis and job description** — designing these for prospective vacancies;","**Determining wages and salaries** — via market surveys, in consultation with top management/finance;","**Recruitment and selection** — a crucial function since organisational success depends on workforce quality;","**Performance appraisal** — regular review of recruited employees;","**Training and development** — regular programmes (on- or off-the-job) to improve efficiency;","**Employee welfare and motivation** — get-togethers, staff retreats, etc.;","**Addressing employees' grievances** — HR is the link between workers and management;","**Labour-management relations** — bringing parties to the negotiation table on conflict;","**Implementing organisational policies** — coordinating with line managers, initiating discipline where needed; and","**Dismissal and redundancy** — firm action for code-of-conduct breaches, ensuring organisational/government regulation compliance."]}]},{"n":"A.5","t":"Human resource planning","b":[{"def":{"t":"Human resource planning","d":"the process of forecasting an organisation's future HR needs so steps can be taken to meet them (Stone & Meltz, 1983) — ensuring the right number/kind of capable people are in the right place at the right time (Robbins & Coulter, 2007)."}},{"p":"Involves: detailed workforce analysis; recommending policies/procedures for effective labour-resource use; forecasting future needs; matching labour demand/supply; developing strategies to meet HR needs; and providing conditions for employee growth/career advancement."},{"h4":"A.5.1  Steps in the human resource planning process"},{"steps":["Identify organisational objectives — define objectives and achievement strategies, which may affect organisational structure, marketing strategy, production systems.","Estimate human resource needs — forecast the period's HR needs in precise terms (skills, quantity, experience, age).","Analyse existing human resources — a detailed inventory of the current workforce (number, skills, age, experience, turnover rate).","Calculate net human resource needs — estimated needs minus existing workforce.","Develop a plan — policies/procedures (recruitment, selection, training, compensation, career plans) to meet the net requirement."]},{"h4":"A.5.2  Importance of human resource planning"},{"p":"Importance: guides recruitment number/type decisions; helps adapt to labour-market trends; enables more systematic training/development planning; mitigates high turnover; enables labour-cost budgeting; avoids redundancies (right people, right job, right time); eases benefits/welfare planning; and enables developing performance benchmarks."}]},{"n":"A.6","t":"Recruitment","b":[{"def":{"t":"Recruitment","d":"all activities attracting qualified applicants to fill vacancies — analysing job requirements, identifying recruitment sources, advertising vacancies, managing the response."}},{"h4":"A.6.1 – A.6.3  Sources of recruitment: internal and external"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Internal recruitment source","Description"],"rows":[["**Promotion**","Existing employees whose qualifications/experience suit a vacant (higher-skill/responsibility) position are promoted."],["**Transfer**","Moving a worker from one unit/department to another to fill a vacancy."],["**Job posting**","Officially advertising the vacancy internally (notice boards, house journal, memos), inviting interested qualified staff to apply."],["**Job bidding**","Employees apply for vacancies they believe they qualify for, after being notified via posting — bidders are considered for appointment."],["**Conversion**","A worker in one job cadre is considered for a DIFFERENT cadre if they hold the required qualification (e.g. a janitor converted to clerical officer with additional qualification)."],["**Upgrading**","Status changed to a higher level within the SAME job cadre upon acquiring a higher qualification."]]}},{"p":"**External recruitment** has 6 sources: educational institutions (direct university/polytechnic recruitment); unsolicited applications; employee referrals; employment agencies (public, private, or management consultancies); professional organisations (e.g. ICAN, Chartered Institute of Bankers recommending members); and advertisement (internet, newspapers, TV, radio)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Advantages / Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Internal**","Adv: creates promotion opportunities; reduces recruitment cost; better-known applicants simplify selection; benefits from prior training investment; less selection-mistake risk given appraisable performance history; increases employee commitment. Disadv: qualified people may not exist internally; strict internal policy risks \"in-breeding\" (perpetuating old ways, stagnation); the organisation may lag behind industry rivals recruiting superior external skills; reduces the talent pool; can breed unhealthy rivalry."],["**External**","Adv: creates a large pool to recruit the best from; brings in better-qualified people; reduces training costs (already-skilled hires); enhances workplace diversity. Disadv: expensive (advertising, interviews, tests, background checks, medical exams); risk of a wrong hiring choice."]]}}]},{"n":"A.7","t":"Selection","b":[{"p":"A typical selection process: analysis of application forms; selection tests; selection interview; background investigations; medical examinations; offer and placement."},{"p":"Application forms typically require: name; interests/hobbies; date/place of birth; nationality/hometown; address/email; phone number; health status; marital/family status; education/qualifications/publications; previous employer(s)/experience/reasons for leaving; position applied for and reasons; and references."},{"h4":"A.7.1 – A.7.3  Analysis of application forms, selection tests and the selection interview"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Selection test type","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Aptitude test**","Measures latent/potential ability for a job/task — e.g. manual dexterity, numerical/clerical ability."],["**Intelligence test**","Measures logical reasoning, memory, learning ability."],["**Achievement test**","Evaluates proficiency in a specific job, usually for experienced applicants claiming a skill (e.g. a typist tested on typing)."],["**Personality test**","Measures social-interaction skills/general behaviour — temperament, introvert/extrovert."],["**Psychological test**","Determines abilities/characteristics to predict job success likelihood."]]}},{"p":"**Selection interview**: a formal employer-applicant conversation verifying/clarifying application information and probing suitability further. Problems: interviewer bias/prejudice, hearing only what they expect to hear; outcomes depend heavily on interviewer knowledge/communication skill."}]},{"n":"A.8","t":"Placement","b":[{"def":{"t":"Placement","d":"assigning a specific job/rank/responsibility to a selected candidate, matching job requirements with candidate qualifications."}}]},{"n":"A.9","t":"Induction","b":[{"def":{"t":"Induction","d":"receiving/welcoming new employees and giving basic information to help them settle in quickly, exposing them to the organisation's culture."}},{"h4":"A.9.1 – A.9.3  Objectives, importance and procedures of induction"},{"p":"Objectives: integrate new employees at the strange/unfamiliar preliminary stage; cultivate a favourable company attitude to improve retention; optimise new-comer productivity quickly; reduce likelihood of leaving; and communicate expectations/responsibilities/vision-alignment/performance outcomes."},{"p":"Importance: reduces early exits (saving replacement/induction/error-correction costs); increases commitment; maximises new-comer contribution from day one (accelerating the learning curve); smooths socialisation; helps settle into a role quicker; establishes good manager-team communication."},{"p":"Procedures typically include: reception; documentation; an employee handbook; initial briefing (HR/department head clarifying handbook/appointment-letter issues); formal induction courses; and on-the-job induction training."}]},{"n":"A.10","t":"Job analysis","b":[{"def":{"t":"Job analysis","d":"examining a job to identify its components/conditions — activities/duties/responsibilities; relative importance; relationship to other jobs; required knowledge/skills/attitudes; and working conditions."}},{"h4":"A.10.1 – A.10.3  Definition, methods and uses of job analysis"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","Description"],"rows":[["**Interview**","Detailed face-to-face interview of the jobholder."],["**Questionnaire**","The jobholder/supervisor completes a questionnaire on activities/knowledge/skills/abilities/conditions."],["**Observation**","The analyst directly observes the jobholder at work (face-to-face, or via film/photographs)."],["**Diaries**","The jobholder records task details, later collected for analysis."]]}},{"p":"Job analysis data feeds: **recruitment** (defining who to recruit); **selection** (specifying required knowledge/skills/attitudes); **training/development** (developing the right programme); **compensation administration** (job evaluation); **performance appraisal** (providing measurement criteria); **employee benefits/services** (uncovering job hazards to mitigate); and **industrial relations** (informing collective bargaining over pay/conditions)."}]},{"n":"A.11","t":"Job description","b":[{"def":{"t":"Job description","d":"a detailed statement of a specific job's activities, covering: job title; place/location in the organisational hierarchy; objectives/purpose and relationship to overall company objectives; supervisors reported to and subordinates reporting in; duties/responsibilities; and pertinent performance-influencing factors (machines/equipment, working conditions, constraints, hazards)."}}]},{"n":"A.12","t":"Job specification","b":[{"def":{"t":"Job specification","d":"interprets the job description in terms of the kind of PERSON suitable — education, experience, physical fitness, mental abilities required. Helps select the right person and determine training needs for current/potential occupants."}}]},{"n":"A.13","t":"Training and development","b":[{"p":"**Training** (Armstrong, 2009): planned, systematic behaviour modification through learning events/programmes/instruction, enabling knowledge/skills/competence for effective current work. **Development**: the growth/realisation of a person's ability/potential through learning/educational experience. Training focuses on IMMEDIATE job needs; development focuses on the FUTURE — career growth and increased responsibility."},{"h4":"A.13.1  The training and development process"},{"steps":["Training needs analysis — identifying the gap between current/potential and expected performance, via 2 components (organisational needs assessment, individual needs identification); stages: data collection, interpretation, recommendation, action plan.","Setting the training objectives — specific, measurable, unambiguous, clearly understood by trainer and trainees.","Drawing up the training programme — designing curriculum/methods to fill the identified gap.","Implementation of the training programme.","Evaluation of the training programme — assessing impact on trainees and the organisation."]},{"h4":"A.13.2  Training and development methods"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Methods"],"rows":[["**On-the-job training**","Training incorporated into the employee's actual work, under close supervision — **demonstration** (trainer shows the method, trainee repeats it); **understudy** (training to assume a superior's duties at a future date); **job rotation/planned experience** (rotating between jobs/departments/branches for diversified exposure); **project/committee assignments** (improving decision-making/resource-organising skill); **temporary promotions** (assigned higher-responsibility tasks to build managerial skill)."],["**Off-the-job training**","Trained outside the organisation — sponsorship for short courses; day releases for professional courses; distance learning; approved computer-based learning."],["**On-and-off-the-job training**","Combines both — trained outside, applies on the job, returns for further training — e.g. internship, study tours, attachments."]]}},{"h4":"A.13.3  Benefits of training and development"},{"p":"Benefits: improved present-job performance; improved morale/job satisfaction; reduced operational problems (accidents, turnover, poor service, waste, maintenance cost); increased productivity; fulfils skill needs without external recruitment; reduced supervision need; and improved customer service."}]},{"n":"A.14","t":"Performance appraisal","b":[{"def":{"t":"Performance appraisal","d":"monitoring an employee's job performance and development potential — developing/administering appraisal systems, counselling on career plans."}},{"h4":"A.14.1  Methods of conducting performance appraisal"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","Description"],"rows":[["**Descriptive essay**","Supervisor writes narrative impressions of subordinate performance."],["**Ranking**","Comparing/ranking subordinates against colleagues by merit, using defined standards."],["**Assessment by co-employees**","Peers assess a colleague's performance."],["**Rating scale**","The oldest, most widely-used method — 4 features: (i) a list of desired personality traits/qualities drawn up (job knowledge, quality of work, dependability, discipline, initiative, attitude, decisiveness, creativity, emotional stability, leadership, punctuality, etc.); (ii) forms containing these traits designed; (iii) one form supplied per person rated; (iv) the rater observes and ticks off traits matching actual performance."]]}},{"h4":"A.14.2  Criteria for an effective performance appraisal system"},{"ol":["clear appraisal objectives, matching the job description;","accurate, valid, reliable data supporting strategic/succession decisions;","well-defined performance criteria matching the actual job description;","management AND employee endorsement (including expense support);","flexibility to adapt to change;","performance dialogue between rater and employee — arguably the most critical component;","periodic system checks — systematic validity evaluation;","being less time-consuming, economical to run;","a post-appraisal interview exchanging feedback, building mutual trust;","continuous feedback throughout the year (not just an annual surprise); and","measuring job-relevant behaviour/competence, not irrelevant personal behaviour."]}]},{"n":"A.15","t":"Wages and salaries administration (employee compensation)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Wage and salary administration","d":"the practices/procedures for planning/distributing company-wide compensation programmes, usually handled by the accounting department. Compensation directly affects job satisfaction/morale/motivation, and is a common root cause of labour-management conflict."}},{"h4":"A.15.1 – A.15.4  Objectives and principles of wage and salary administration, methods of compensating employees, and designing incentive schemes"},{"p":"Employee compensation = rewards for services offered, with 2 primary areas: benefits and salary administration."},{"p":"**Objectives**: reward according to effort/merit; attract/retain desirable employees; improve morale/productivity; keep labour cost within reasonable limits (protecting shareholders/competitiveness/profitability); pay per job importance/difficulty; facilitate payroll administration/budgeting/control; and explain to employees how/why they are paid."},{"p":"**7 principles considered when setting wage levels**: ability to pay (per the organisation's financial performance); government minimum wage legislation; competitor wage rates; terms of collective agreements; availability of that type of labour (scarcity raises compensation); qualifications (higher qualification, better pay); and job evaluation results."},{"p":"**Compensation methods**: **piece rate** (paid in direct proportion to output — used where output is measurable, effort-output relationship established, job standardised/repetitive, work flow regular) and **time rate** (paid by time spent — used where output/units are hard to measure, work flow is irregular, or effort-output relationship is hard to establish)."},{"p":"**Incentive schemes**: piece rate (pay = units × standard rate); stock/share ownership plans (shares allotted, dividends receivable); commission (rewarded on sales volume — common for salespeople); bonuses (periodic high-performance rewards, sometimes across-the-board on good profits); and profit sharing (employees share the financial gain from increased productivity/added value)."}]},{"n":"A.16","t":"Employee discipline","b":[{"def":{"t":"Discipline","d":"the situation where people obey organisation-established rules/standards of behaviour. Disciplinary procedures are the steps taken before disciplinary action against a rule-infringing employee."}},{"key":"11 features ensuring procedural fairness in discipline: the OFFENCE must be communicated with complaint details; TIMELY action (within a reasonable period, else it may seem condoned); adequate advance notification of the HEARING date; the right to a REPRESENTATIVE of choice; the right to DEFEND oneself; the right to CALL WITNESSES (with cross-examination available); the right to an INTERPRETER; the right to a FINDING (guilty/not guilty, with full reasoning); consideration of PREVIOUS SERVICE RECORD (mitigating circumstances); informing the employee of the PUNISHMENT; and the right to APPEAL."},{"h4":"A.16.1 – A.16.4  Features of effective discipline, objectives of disciplinary action, causes of indiscipline, and disciplinary measures"},{"p":"Other features: directed against the BEHAVIOUR, not the person; preceded by advance warning; consistency (inconsistency lowers morale and breeds grievances); impartiality; and ordinarily administered in PRIVATE (public reprimand reserved for gross insubordination/flagrant violation)."},{"p":"**Objectives** (correction, not punishment): gain willing acceptance of rules/standards/procedures; promote morale/efficiency; develop cooperation; develop tolerance/respect for human dignity; and maintain good industrial relations."},{"p":"**4 causes of indiscipline** (Nankervis, Compton & McCarthy, 1998): attendance problems (unexcused absence, chronic absenteeism, tardiness); dishonesty (fraud, theft, falsifying applications/records, property damage); work-performance problems (incomplete assignments, substandard output, missed production targets); and on-the-job behaviour problems (intoxication, insubordination, horseplay, unauthorised smoking, fighting, gambling, safety-device non-use, unreported injury, carelessness, sleeping on the job, abusive language, narcotics/weapons possession, sexual harassment)."},{"p":"**Disciplinary measures** (escalating): verbal warning; written warning; reprimand; serious written warning; loss of pay for non-duty periods; increment withholding/deferment; salary reduction; interdiction (half salary); demotion; suspension; transfer; termination of appointment; retirement; dismissal."}]},{"n":"A.17","t":"Industrial and labour relations","b":[{"def":{"t":"Industrial relations","d":"\"a system or web of rules regulating employment and the way people behave at work\" (Dunlop, 1958). Key parties: **employers** (often organised into Employers Associations); **workers** (organised into trade unions/senior staff associations, collectively \"Employees Associations\"); and **government** (provides the legal framework and acts as arbiter in dispute resolution)."}},{"p":"Major activities: collective bargaining; implementing collective agreements; designing/implementing grievance and trade-dispute settlement systems; designing/implementing disciplinary procedures; enforcing relevant labour-law provisions."},{"h4":"A.17.1 – A.17.3  Collective bargaining, the contents of collective agreements, and the importance of collective bargaining"},{"def":{"t":"Collective bargaining","d":"negotiations about working conditions/terms of employment between employer(s)/employer organisation(s) and workers' representative organisation(s), aiming to reach a \"collective agreement\" (employment contract). Main subjects: wages/salaries, terms of employment, conditions of service."}},{"p":"A typical **collective agreement** covers: sole-representative union recognition; term definitions; validity period (usually 1-3 years); renegotiation notice procedure; union-shop recognition and covered employee categories; union-dues check-off; parties' responsibilities/purpose; written engagement-terms disclosure (grade, salary, appointment date, job title, department, probation); required employee personal details; rules on increments/probation/termination/promotion/transfer/overtime/discipline; remuneration/incentives; employee rights/privileges; welfare facilities (housing, medical, accommodation, recreation, canteen); injury compensation; exit benefits (resignation, termination, redundancy, death, retirement); Trade Union activity facilities; and a grievance procedure."},{"p":"Importance of collective bargaining — benefits all 3 tripartite parties: employees gain an influence channel, fair labour prices, legal recognition of contracts; employers gain conflict-resolution means, cooperation in implementing objectives; government gains a regulatory tool protecting economic/social/political rights; it promotes industrial harmony (avoiding strikes), industrial democracy, and open communication (allaying employee resistance/fear)."},{"h4":"A.17.4  Grievance and grievance procedure"},{"steps":["The employee seeks timely redress from their immediate superior/management representative.","If unsatisfied, the matter goes to a union official, who investigates and (if justified under the collective agreement) escalates it to management for timely redress.","If still unresolved, the union official refers it to the Branch Secretary, who seeks redress from the relevant departmental head.","If unresolved, the matter is reduced to writing and referred to the union's Regional Industrial Relations Officer, who meets the departmental head.","If still unresolved, the union headquarters Secretariat and the firm's Chief Executive attempt resolution before referring it to the standing Joint Negotiating Committee.","If still unresolved, voluntary arbitration follows, per the Labour Act.","If all internal mechanisms fail, 3 statutory routes apply: **conciliation** (informal discussion facilitated by a third-party Conciliator); **arbitration** (both parties agree in advance to accept a third party's decision — in Nigeria, an Industrial Arbitration Panel; the National Industrial Court has exclusive jurisdiction over trade-dispute settlement); and **mediation** (formal, non-binding recommendations submitted for both parties' consideration)."]}]},{"n":"A.18","t":"Summary — Section A","b":[{"p":"**Human resource management** has to do with all activities that relate to the management of an organisation's human resources. The **core activities** carried out under human resources are **human resource planning, recruitment and selection, performance appraisal, welfare services and benefits, employee compensation and industrial relations**."},{"p":"**Human resource planning** seeks to forecast the labour requirements of an organisation and prepare for them. **Recruitment and selection** focus on ensuring that the organisation has the right labour resources. **Welfare services and benefits** seek to motivate employees to work hard, by providing them with basic services such as canteen, accommodation and transport. **Employee compensation** relates to financial rewards given to employees for their efforts. Under **industrial relations** the Human Resource Manager seeks to ensure good relations between the organisation and its employees; industrial relations cover **collective bargaining, grievance procedure and worker participation in management**."}]},{"n":"A.19","t":"Review questions — Section A","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section A","t":"Review questions, with the study text's own solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["The following are functions of Human Resources Management **EXCEPT**: **A.** Employee compensation; **B.** Training and development; **C.** Payroll administration; **D.** Industrial relations; **E.** Performance appraisal.","Which of the following activities is a component of Human Resources Planning activities? **A.** Estimation of Human Resources Needs; **B.** Development of effective grievance procedure; **C.** Forecast of future production and sales levels; **D.** Outsourcing of training and development activities to consultants; **E.** Post-training evaluation.","A statement of the minimum qualification that a person must possess to perform a given job successfully is known as: **A.** Job Analysis; **B.** Job Specification; **C.** Job Description; **D.** Job Evaluation; **E.** Job Design.","Which of the following is **NOT** a source of internal recruitment? **A.** Promotions; **B.** Intranet advertisement; **C.** Advertisement on the organisation's notice board; **D.** Advertisement on the organisation's corporate website; **E.** Job bidding.","The following are components of the Training and Development process **EXCEPT** **A.** Assessment of organisation and individual needs; **B.** Drawing up of training programme; **C.** Implementation and management of training programmes; **D.** Post-training evaluation; **E.** Succession planning.","Which of the following is **not** an external source of recruitment? **A.** Employment exchanges; **B.** Data banks; **C.** Labour contractor; **D.** Transfer; **E.** Institutions.","Which of the following aspects is concerned with working conditions and amenities such as canteens, crèches, housing and transport? **A.** Industrial relations aspects; **B.** Labour aspect; **C.** Welfare aspect; **D.** Monetary aspect; **E.** Salaries and wages aspect.","The process of familiarising new employees with the organisation's rules and regulations is known as **A.** Placement; **B.** Induction; **C.** Recruitment; **D.** Selection.","Quantitative job evaluation methods are **A.** Ranking method; **B.** Point rating method; **C.** Factor comparison method; **D.** Both (B) and (C); **E.** 360° method.","Qualitative job evaluation methods are **A.** Ranking; **B.** Grading; **C.** Point rating; **D.** Both (A) and (B); **E.** Supervision."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The process of developing the applicant pool for job openings in an organisation is called ………","……… is concerned with the determination of the number of personnel required in an organisation.","An organisation of employees whose primary purpose is to negotiate with employers about the terms and conditions of employment is ………","A rational approach to determining the knowledge and skills gap of individuals in an organisation is called ………","Complaints made by an employee about wages, conditions of employment or action of management is called ………","The technique used for determining the size of one job compared with another, and the relationship between two jobs, is ………","A process whereby a third party attempts to promote an agreement between parties in a dispute, by exploring any common ground which may lead to a settlement, is called ………","……… is the systematic, periodic and impartial rating of an employee's excellence in matters pertaining to his present job and his potential for a better job.","The term ……… refers to a condition in the organisation where employees conduct themselves in accordance with the organisation's rules and standards of acceptable behaviour.","……… is the process of negotiation between employers and the representatives of a unit of employees, aimed at reaching agreements that regulate working conditions."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**C** — Payroll administration"],["2","**A** — Estimation of Human Resources Needs"],["3","**B** — Job Specification"],["4","**D** — Advertisement on the corporate website (external)"],["5","**E** — Succession planning"],["6","**D** — Transfer (internal)"],["7","**C** — Welfare aspect"],["8","**B** — Induction"],["9","**D** — Both point rating and factor comparison"],["10","**D** — Both ranking and grading"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Recruitment**","**Human Resource Planning**","**Trade Union**","**Training Need Analysis**","**Grievance**","**Job Evaluation**","**Conciliation**","**Performance appraisal**","**Discipline**","**Collective bargaining**"]},{"key":"MCQ 3 is the trio most often confused. **Job analysis** is the *study* of a job; a **job description** states the *duties and responsibilities*; a **job specification** states the *minimum qualifications the person must have*. Analysis produces both of the other two."},{"note":"MCQ 4 and 6 are the same distinction from both sides: **internal** sources are promotion, transfer, job bidding, intranet and notice-board advertisements; **external** sources are the corporate website, employment exchanges, data banks, labour contractors and institutions. The corporate website is external because the public can see it."},{"note":"MCQ 9 and 10 split job evaluation methods: **quantitative** — point rating and factor comparison (both assign scores); **qualitative** — ranking and grading (both merely order or band jobs)."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the Section A multiple choice list is numbered 1 to 5 and then **the last five questions carry no numbers at all**. They are numbered 6 to 10 here, matching the ten answers in the solution key."}]}}]},{"n":"B.1","t":"Meaning of production","b":[{"def":{"t":"Production","d":"the step-by-step conversion of one material form into another through chemical/mechanical processes, creating/enhancing product utility to the user — a value-addition process, with value added at each processing stage. Output is either a tangible PRODUCT (food, furniture, books) or an intangible SERVICE (healthcare, entertainment, telephone services)."}},{"p":"**Process**: the transformation of inputs (raw materials, money, machines, labour, information) into products/services. A **production system** is any environment where this transformation occurs — found in Agriculture, Industry, Commerce and Direct Services. 4 characteristics: it is an organised activity with an objective; it transforms inputs into useful outputs; it does not operate in isolation from other organisational systems; and feedback about its activities is essential for control/improvement."}]},{"n":"B.2","t":"Production management","b":[{"def":{"t":"Production management","d":"managing the resources an organisation devotes to transforming inputs into goods/services, ensuring the requisite quality level. When the same concept extends to services, it is called OPERATIONS management (concerned with services/intangible output/more-labour-less-equipment/frequent customer participation, vs. production management's manufacturing/tangible output/less-labour-more-equipment/no customer participation)."}},{"key":"Production management's objective: producing goods/services of the **RIGHT quality** (per customer needs, not necessarily \"best\" quality), **RIGHT quantity** (avoiding both excess-inventory capital lock-up and shortage), **RIGHT time** (timeliness of delivery), and at **RIGHT manufacturing cost** (minimising variance from the pre-established standard cost)."}]},{"n":"B.3","t":"Objectives of production management","b":[{"p":"The objectives of production management are to produce goods and services **of the right quality, in the right quantity, at the right time and at the right manufacturing cost** — the \"four rights\" of production. They are delivered through the functions listed at §B.4."}]},{"n":"B.4","t":"Functions of production management","b":[{"ol":["**Deciding the production system type** — intermittent/job, continuous/flow, or repetitive/batch manufacturing, to meet organisational/customer needs;","**Planning facility location** — geographical siting affects production cost, market access, and the production system/machine arrangement chosen;","**Planning plant layout and facility design** — arranging machines/stores/maintenance facilities/washrooms/waste-disposal/rest-rooms/inspection bays conveniently, improving resource movement, safety, security, supervision;","**Planning and controlling the production process** — deciding what/where/when to produce (production PLANNING), then monitoring machines/inventory/cost/quality from input to finished stage (production CONTROL);","**Research and development of new products** — basic research and prototyping;","**Maintenance of facilities** — minimising deterioration via 3 policies: preventive, breakdown, or shut-down maintenance;","**Deciding replacement strategy** — replacing a machine once cumulative maintenance cost equals new-acquisition cost (gradually-failing items); for suddenly-failing items (bulbs, fan belts), either buy-and-replace-as-they-fail, mass replacement at intervals, or a combination; and","**Ensuring health/safety compliance** — the production department ENFORCES the HR department's policies/procedures at the shop floor, via safety enforcement, operative training, risk assessment, and hazard-information provision."]}]},{"n":"B.5","t":"Plant (facilities) location","b":[{"key":"There is no single \"best\" location — only several GOOD locations, each with its own strengths/weaknesses, making it a trade-off decision. The basic objective is minimising cost and maximising profit; locating near an existing industrial area often reduces cost since infrastructure (electricity, water, telephone) may already exist."},{"h4":"B.5.1  Factors influencing plant location"},{"p":"Factors influencing location: **availability of raw materials** (proximity reduces production cost); **government policy** (designated zones, tax concessions, investment grants, free trade/export processing zones); **proximity to market** (especially critical for perishable products/services); **commercial and social infrastructure** (transport, water, electricity, telecoms, postal, financial services, markets, schools); **characteristics of the site**; and **nearness to market** (customer ease of finding the product). Other factors: competitor location; site cost (rent/lease/purchase); expansion possibility; utility services; site convenience/accessibility; waste-disposal facilities; and traffic density/parking."}]},{"n":"B.6","t":"Plant layout and design","b":[{"p":"Objectives of plant layout: better product quality; maximum floor-area utilisation; reduced internal transport; adequate lighting/ventilation; minimised scrap/waste; fewer accidents; minimised production delays; avoiding unnecessary changes; easy supervision/control; and neatness."},{"h4":"B.6.1 – B.6.2  Objectives and principles of plant layout"},{"ol":["**Overall integration** — the best layout integrates men, materials and machines optimally;","**Minimum movement** — minimises movement of men/materials;","**Flow** — provides smooth, uninterrupted flow of men/materials;","**Space utilisation** — best use of available cubic space;","**Comfort and safety** — safe and comfortable for employees; and","**Flexibility** — the best layout provides flexibility."]},{"h4":"B.6.3  Factors influencing plant layout and design"},{"p":"Factors influencing layout/design: headroom (ceiling-floor distance, driven by material-handling equipment size); access (ease of movement); required services (electricity, telephone, water, computer terminals, fire prevention, warehouses, repair workshops); waste disposal (compliant with environmental law); ventilation (especially for heat-generating processes); and government legislation (waste disposal, factory safety)."},{"h4":"B.6.4  Types of plant layout"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Layout type","Description"],"rows":[["**Product/line layout**","Equipment arranged per the product's operation sequence — one standardised, large-quantity product per operating area; raw material flows rapidly end-to-end with minimal WIP/storage/handling."],["**Process/functional layout**","Machines grouped by TYPE/nature of operation (not sequence) — e.g. all lathes together, all drills together. Suited to low-volume, non-standardised, non-repetitive production, given its greater flexibility."],["**Fixed position layout**","The major component stays fixed (too heavy/big to move) — tools, materials, manpower and equipment are brought TO it. Used for aircraft, hydraulic/steam turbines, ships."],["**Combination type layout**","A compromise blending features of the above — pure single-type layouts are rare in practice; flexibility drives industry-specific modelling of layouts to combine good features economically."]]}}]},{"n":"B.7","t":"Production systems","b":[{"def":{"t":"Production system","d":"a collection of people, equipment and procedures organised to perform a company's manufacturing operations (found everywhere — bakeries, tailoring shops, salons, factories, breweries, restaurants) — all converting inputs (labour, raw materials, information) into goods/services."}},{"p":"Components: **facilities** (the factory/equipment and how it is organised — plant layout) and **manufacturing support systems** (procedures managing production, ordering materials, moving work through the factory, ensuring quality standards)."},{"h4":"B.7.1 – B.7.2  Components and features of production systems"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Description","Examples"],"rows":[["**Job/unique production**","Complex, custom-made, \"one-off\" items — often huge single pieces.","Ship building, bridge/dam construction, custom furniture, aircraft manufacturing, construction."],["**Mass production**","Large quantities of standardised products, few varieties, continuous inter-process movement — heavy mechanisation, detailed materials-flow organisation, careful quality control, division of labour.","Soap, automobiles, component parts."],["**Continuous/flow production**","Mass production that continues unbroken for weeks/months.","Cement, brewing, petroleum refining."],["**Repetitive/batch production**","Products manufactured in small/large batches via a series of operations before assembly.","Canned/cartoned packaged foods, footwear, garments, heavy motor vehicles, electronic instruments, internal-combustion engines."]]}},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["System","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Job**","Centralised control ensures effective work supervision; reduced manufacturing time when materials/men/machines are assembled at the point of manufacture.","Erratic, unpredictable demand for job-production products; idle/under-utilised machines absent orders; large work-in-process requiring storage investment."],["**Batch**","General-purpose machines adaptable to different uses; machines are independent of each other.","Lack of workload balance across machines (some under-utilised); requires large storage space for the high work-in-progress."],["**Flow**","Relatively faster (conveyors often transfer materials); the supervisor can easily spot trouble spots, since a breakdown affects the whole line.","Since machines are interdependent, one breakdown immobilises the WHOLE production line; requires high demand levels to be economical."]]}},{"h4":"B.7.3  Factors influencing the type of production system used"},{"p":"Factors influencing the choice of production system: type of goods/services (e.g. tailoring shops use job production, oil refineries use flow production); organisation size (big organisations afford advanced mass-production facilities); demand extent/order frequency; degree of automation/technology; and degree of customer contact (high contact favours a service-oriented batch system)."}]},{"n":"B.8","t":"Summary — Section B","b":[{"p":"**Production** has to do with the **transformation of inputs into products and services to satisfy human wants and needs**. These inputs are transformed into outputs within **production systems**."},{"p":"The **functions of production management** include deciding on the type of production system to use; planning the location of the organisation's facilities; planning the plant layout and design; planning and controlling the production process; research and development of new products; maintenance of facilities; deciding on the appropriate replacement strategy; and ensuring compliance with health and safety procedures."},{"p":"To ensure that production activities go on successfully, the company should locate its plant at the right place. **Factors which influence plant location** are raw materials, government policy, market, commercial and social infrastructure, and the characteristics of the site. **Plant layout and design** is also important, as it influences the free movement of people, raw materials, inventory, machines and equipment during production. **Principles of plant layout** include the principles of overall integration, minimum distance, flow, cubic space, satisfaction and safety, and flexibility. There are **four types of plant layout**: product/line, process, fixed position and combination type layouts."},{"p":"There are **four main kinds of production system** — **job, mass, continuous flow and repetitive/batch**. The decision on which to use is based on the type of goods or services to be produced; the size of the organisation; the extent of demand or frequency of orders; the degree of automation and technology; and the degree of customer contact."}]},{"n":"B.9","t":"Review questions — Section B","b":[{"p":"Section B sets **four essay questions**, **twelve multiple choice questions** and **ten short answer questions**."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section B essay questions","t":"Four essay questions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Define and explain the features of the following types of production system: **(i)** job production; **(ii)** flow production; **(iii)** batch production.","What factors influence the location of a business in Ghana?","Briefly explain the under-listed facilities layouts: **(i)** product layout; **(ii)** process layout.","List and explain the types of maintenance used in an organisation."]}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Question","Answer at"],"rows":[["1 — the three production systems","**§B.5** (production systems), which sets out all four kinds — job, mass, continuous flow and repetitive/batch — with their features"],["2 — factors influencing location","**§B.3** — raw materials, government policy, market, commercial and social infrastructure, and characteristics of the site"],["3 — product and process layout","**§B.4** — product/line layout arranges equipment in the sequence of operations; process layout groups similar machines together"],["4 — types of maintenance","Named in the Section B summary (§B.6) among the functions of production management, but **not itemised anywhere in the body of the section** — the standard classification is preventive, corrective (breakdown), predictive and condition-based maintenance"]]}},{"warn":"Question 4 asks for the **types of maintenance**, which this section never lists. Maintenance of facilities appears only as one of the functions of production management. The question reaches beyond its own text."},{"note":"Question 2 asks specifically about **Ghana**, a reminder that this is an ATSWA syllabus covering West Africa — the study text treats Nigerian and Ghanaian law and practice side by side throughout (see Chapter 8 §8.10.2 on the two countries' health and safety statutes)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section B","t":"Review questions, with the study text's own solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Conversion of inputs into outputs is known as: **A.** Application of technology; **B.** Operations management; **C.** Manufacturing products; **D.** Product; **E.** Service.","Production management involves the following activities **EXCEPT**: **A.** Planning the location of the organisation's facilities; **B.** Assessing manpower needs of the production system; **C.** Maintenance of facilities; **D.** Ensuring compliance with health and safety procedures, regulations and standards; **E.** Deciding on the production system to use.","Which of the following is **NOT** a type of production system? **A.** Mass production; **B.** Job production; **C.** Flow production; **D.** Unique production; **E.** Assembly production.","All of the following decisions fall within the scope of operations management **except**: **A.** Financial analysis; **B.** Design of products and processes; **C.** Location of facilities; **D.** Quality management; **E.** Service quality.","The number of product varieties that can be manufactured in **mass production** is: **A.** One only; **B.** Two only; **C.** Few varieties in large volumes; **D.** Large varieties in small volumes; **E.** None of the above.","The number of product varieties that can be manufactured in **job production** is: **A.** Limited to one or two; **B.** Large varieties of products; **C.** One only; **D.** A few varieties; **E.** None of the above.","Generally in **continuous production**, production is carried out to: **A.** Customer's order; **B.** Government orders only; **C.** Few rich customers; **D.** Satisfy public demand; **E.** For stock and supply.","The desired objective of Production and Operations Management is: **A.** Optimal utilisation of available resources; **B.** Use cheap machinery to produce; **C.** To train unskilled workers to manufacture goods perfectly; **D.** To earn good profits.","One of these is **NOT** a principle of plant layout: **A.** Principle of overall integration; **B.** Principle of cost; **C.** Principle of minimum distance; **D.** Principle of cubic space; **E.** Principle of flexibility.","The most suitable layout for **job production** is: **A.** Fixed position layout; **B.** Combination layout; **C.** Process layout; **D.** Product layout.","One of the product examples for **line layout** is: **A.** Repair workshop; **B.** Bridge; **C.** Fabrics; **D.** Cement; **E.** Electronic appliances.","The following type of layout is preferred for **low volume production of non-standard products**: **A.** Product layout; **B.** Process layout; **C.** Fixed position layout; **D.** Combination layout; **E.** None of the above."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["……… is that part of an organisation which is concerned with the transformation of a range of inputs into services having the requisite level of quality.","A ……… is something which is offered to customers to satisfy their needs or wants.","……… is a collection of people, equipment and procedures organised to perform the manufacturing operations of a company.","The arrangement of machines in sequence is a key feature of the ……… production system.","The type of layout used in the manufacture of aeroplanes, hydraulic and steam turbines and ships is known as ………","The production of single articles, or \"one-off\" items, in small or large quantities is ……… production.","Production is the process by which ……… and other inputs are converted into products.","……… is essentially an arrangement to facilitate the production process efficiently.","If all the processing equipment and machines are arranged according to the sequence of operations of a product, the layout is known as ………","The principle of plant layout which states that the best layout is safe and comfortable for employees working in the plant is known as ………"]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**C** — Manufacturing products"],["2","**D**"],["3","**E** — Assembly production"],["4","**A** — Financial analysis"],["5","**C** — Few varieties in large volumes"],["6","**C** — One only"],["7","**E** — For stock and supply"],["8","**A** — Optimal utilisation of available resources"],["9","**B** — Principle of cost"],["10","**A** — Fixed position layout"],["11","**D** — Cement"],["12","**B** — Process layout"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Operations management**","**Product**","**Production system**","**Process**","**Fixed location**","**Job production system**","**Resources**","**Plant layout**","**Product or line layout**","**Principle of safety and comfort**"]},{"key":"MCQ 5, 6 and 7 together give the defining contrast between the production systems: **mass** production makes **few varieties in large volumes**; **job** production makes **one** item at a time to order; and **continuous** production runs **for stock and supply** rather than to a customer's order. Variety and volume move in opposite directions."},{"note":"Several answers in this bank sit awkwardly with the text and with each other — MCQ 1 gives \"manufacturing products\" for the conversion of inputs into outputs where short answer 1 gives \"operations management\" for essentially the same idea, and MCQ 10 gives fixed position layout for job production where MCQ 12 gives process layout for low-volume non-standard products. Reproduce the official answers, but be ready to justify your reasoning if a written question asks."}]}}]},{"n":"C.1","t":"Definition of marketing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Marketing","d":"a social and managerial process by which individuals/groups obtain what they need/want through creating and exchanging products and value with others (Kotler, 1988) — emphasising an EXCHANGE of value, where each party has something the other wants and both are willing/able to transact to mutual satisfaction (e.g. a manufacturer exchanging a product for the consumer's price; a Chartered Accountant exchanging audit services for the client's fee)."}},{"p":"The American Marketing Association defines marketing as the performance of business activities directing the flow of goods/services to the consumer — but this is limited, since marketing activities begin BEFORE goods/services are even created (starting with identifying consumer needs/wants/demands) and do NOT end at purchase (extending to after-sales services like maintenance/repair/returns)."},{"h4":"C.1.1  Analysis of the marketing process"},{"steps":["Identify customer needs the company can satisfy.","Design a product (\"bundle of benefits\") satisfying those needs better than existing products.","Promote/communicate these benefits to motivate purchase.","Price at the right level so consumers are willing/able to buy AND the firm meets profit goals.","Make the product available at the right place, facilitating the exchange."]}]},{"n":"C.2","t":"Marketing functions","b":[{"p":"7 functions of a typical marketing department (Agbonifoh et al., 2007): **marketing research** (systematic data gathering/analysis to inform product/price/distribution/promotion decisions); **planning/guiding new-product development** or existing-product modification; **pricing the product** (marketing is the ONLY activity bringing revenue, other activities incur expenditure); **distributing the product** (intermediaries widen the potential market, spreading urban tastes/habits to rural dwellers); **promoting the product and company** (directly providing employment in advertising/sales/PR; indirectly selling the organisation's offerings); **rendering after-sales service** (evaluating satisfaction to sustain patronage, reduce cognitive dissonance); and **training/motivating salespersons and planning/controlling the marketing effort** (via planning, forecasting, control)."}]},{"n":"C.3","t":"Evolution of marketing management","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Orientation","Description"],"rows":[["**Production concept**","Prevails when demand exceeds supply — the firm focuses on efficient production/distribution, assuming consumers want low-cost availability above all."],["**Product concept**","The firm focuses on making high-quality, high-performance products, assuming a good product \"sells itself.\""],["**Selling (sales) concept**","Shifts emphasis from product quality to SELLING techniques, assuming consumers are resistant and must be coaxed — focused on the SELLER's needs, not the consumer's."],["**Marketing concept**","Recognises satisfying TARGET consumer needs as the means to long-run profitability/competitive advantage — pillars: market focus, consumer orientation, coordinated marketing (across ALL departments, not just marketing), and profitability. The company clearly defines its market rather than chasing every possible market."],["**Societal marketing concept**","Builds on the marketing concept, additionally stressing the well-being of BOTH the consumer AND society when pursuing long-run profitability."]]}}]},{"n":"C.4","t":"Market segmentation","b":[{"def":{"t":"Market segmentation","d":"taking a total, heterogeneous market and dividing it into homogeneous sub-markets/segments (Stanton, 1978) — since a total market is often too large/varied to satisfy everyone, segmentation lets a company focus on and better serve a few segments."}},{"h4":"C.4.1  Methods of segmentation"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Basis","Description"],"rows":[["**Income**","High/middle/low income groups."],["**Sex (gender)**","Men and women purchase differently."],["**Geography**","Region/district/city — differences in family size, infrastructure, income, customs, purchasing patterns."],["**Age**","E.g. under-5, 5-9, 10-14, 15-24, 25-44 brackets."],["**Social class**","Upper/middle/lower class."],["**Sector (industrial markets)**","By economic sector — agriculture, manufacturing, trade, etc."]]}},{"h4":"C.4.2  Benefits of market segmentation"},{"p":"Benefits: enables analysing/comparing each segment's characteristics/opportunities and designing suited marketing programmes; lets the organisation tailor its programme (pricing/promotion/distribution) to a focused segment; benefits the consumer (better product-need matching); and allows products designed for each segment's peculiar needs, focusing on and serving fewer segments better."}]},{"n":"C.5","t":"Marketing mix","b":[{"p":"The marketing mix has 4 key elements: **product** (anything offered for consumption/use), **price** (the value placed on an item), **promotion** (persuasive consumer communication), and **place** (activities making the product accessible)."}]},{"n":"C.6","t":"Product","b":[{"h4":"C.6.1 – C.6.2  Consumer products and industrial products"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Consumer product type","Description"],"rows":[["**Convenience**","Frequently-bought, relatively low-priced (cigarettes, pens, groceries, batteries, newspapers). 3 sub-types: **staples** (bought regularly — bread, soap); **impulse products** (bought with little planning — chocolate, magazines); **emergency products** (bought under urgent need — umbrellas in a rainstorm)."],["**Shopping goods**","Consumers compare price/quality/features across brands before buying — furniture, shoes, clothing, durables (fans, radios, TVs)."],["**Specialty goods**","Unique characteristics/brand identification, buyers make a special purchasing effort — expensive custom clothing, wrist watches, wedding gowns, brilliant lawyers'/doctors' services, luxury furniture."],["**Unsought products**","Consumers don't know about, or don't normally think of buying — coffins, wedding gowns, life insurance, blood donations. Most major new inventions are unsought until advertising creates awareness."]]}},{"p":"The consumer market is also subdivided into: **FMCG market** (toothpaste, beverages, body cream, detergent); **durables market** (motorcycles, cars, washing machines); and **services market** (insurance cover, bank fixed deposits, internet, transportation)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Industrial product type","Description"],"rows":[["**Raw materials**","Unprocessed goods used to produce other goods — farm products (wheat, cotton, livestock) and natural products (fish, lumber, crude petroleum, iron ore)."],["**Installations**","Fixed assets/expensive major goods not forming part of the finished product but expended/worn during use — factory buildings, large plants/equipment."],["**Accessories**","Aid production but don't significantly affect production scale, and don't form part of the final product, but cost less than installations — office equipment, portable tree-cutting machines, forklifts, hand tools."],["**Office supplies**","Depleted during production, not part of the finished product — consumables (paper, ink, toner, stationery), maintenance/repair services, business advisory services."],["**Production supplies**","Operating supplies (lubricants, coal, paper, pencils) and repair/maintenance items (paint, nails, brooms) — \"the convenience products of the industrial field,\" bought with minimal effort/comparison."],["**Components**","Become part of the finished product, needing no further processing before assembly — vehicle spare parts, tyres, buttons, bolts/nuts."]]}},{"h4":"C.6.3 – C.6.5  New products, new product development and its stages"},{"steps":["Define objectives — product type, customer wants, product features (marketing research helps here).","Idea generation and screening — sources include marketing research, competitors, consultancies, universities/research institutions, internal brainstorming, trade associations, government agencies.","Business analysis — estimating market potential, profitability, financial viability.","Product research and development — converting the idea into a physical product/prototype, tested under lab/field conditions.","Test marketing — launching the product on a limited, carefully-selected scale to test commercial viability and adjust design/style before mass production.","Commercialisation — marketing the product nationally/at scale, if test-marketing proves positive."]}]},{"n":"C.7","t":"Pricing","b":[{"p":"Price is the monetary value charged for a sale (Hoffman et al., 2003), the amount needed to acquire goods/services (Nwokoye, 2000), or the value placed on the utility received (Oladele, 2007) — taking forms like admission fee, tuition, salary, fare, premium, dues, interest."},{"key":"4 basic pricing rules: all prices must cover costs AND profits (Price = Cost + Profit); the most effective way to lower prices is to lower costs; review prices frequently against cost/demand/competition/profit dynamics; and prices must be set to assure sales."},{"h4":"C.7.1 – C.7.4  Basic rules of pricing, pricing objectives, pricing methods, and factors which influence pricing"},{"p":"Pricing objectives: **survival** (in the face of overcapacity/competition/changing wants); **profit maximisation**; **market share** (low price to penetrate, or high price to establish leadership); **cash flow** (fast fund recovery); **product quality/leadership** (price signalling market position, e.g. Peak milk); and other objectives (matching competitor price, maximising sales, eliminating/protecting competitors, benefiting the customer)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Pricing method","Description"],"rows":[["**Mark-up pricing**","A profit margin added to purchase price (e.g. N1,000 cost + N500 mark-up = N1,500 sale price)."],["**Target return pricing**","Price fixed to achieve a specified profit level."],["**Demand differential (price discrimination)**","Different prices for the same product by customer type, packaging, brand, place or time."],["**FOB point-of-purchase pricing**","Selling price quoted at the production point; buyer bears shipping cost."],["**Market penetration**","Low introductory prices to stimulate demand — effective where the market is price-sensitive and unit cost falls with sales volume."],["**Market skimming**","High price for fast profit — effective where the product is scarce/innovative."],["**Marketing-oriented pricing**","Suited to new products marketed through several distribution channels — considers target market, competitors, product position, channel design."],["**Prestige pricing**","Conveys a quality image, protects market share/shields competitors — e.g. gold jewellery, high-net-worth banking products."],["**Leader pricing**","A low-priced product used as bait to attract customers who then buy a more expensive product."],["**Psychological pricing**","Encourages emotional (not rational) purchase — e.g. N9,999.99 psychologically reads as less than N10,000, though effectively the same."]]}},{"p":"Factors influencing pricing: cost of production (variable costs, tied to output volume, e.g. raw materials/labour/haulage; fixed costs, incurred regardless of output, e.g. \"specific programme costs\" for a new office/campaign, or general admin/salaries/depreciation/rent); distributor margin; competitor activity; nature of demand (price elasticity); legal constraints (government regulation against exploitation); and company objectives/marketing goals (mass-market competitive pricing vs. prestige-segment high-quality pricing)."}]},{"n":"C.8","t":"Promotion","b":[{"p":"Promotion covers 4 activities: **advertising** (non-personal, paid, persuasive communication about ideas/goods/services); **public relations** (deliberate, planned, sustained effort maintaining mutual understanding with the organisation's \"publics\" — employees, shareholders, customers, government, competitors, press, community, distributors); **sales promotion** (marketing activity stimulating immediate consumer/dealer demand); and **personal selling** (face-to-face seller-prospect contact to make sales)."},{"h4":"C.8.1 – C.8.4  Advertising, public relations, sales promotion and personal selling"},{"p":"**Advertising media** (West Africa): newspapers/magazines (detailed coverage, relatively cheap, reaches large urban audiences); radio (reaches virtually every part of the country); outdoor advertising (signboards/billboards at high-traffic junctions); transport/transit advertising (buses, cars); specialities (key holders, calendars, mugs, T-shirts branded items)."},{"p":"**Public relations activities**: preparing newsletters/calendars/leaflets/brochures/sales aids; publishing house journals; ensuring good press relations, reacting to criticism; educating the public on organisational activities; arranging publicity (news conferences, press releases); handling customer complaints."},{"p":"**Sales promotion techniques**: ad materials, cash refunds, competitions/contests, demonstrations, free goods, free trials, premium offers, price packs, price reductions/price-offs (price deals), rebates, samples/specialties, sponsorship, sweepstakes, trade allowances, and trade fairs/exhibitions — aimed at inducing repeat purchase, trial by non-customers, new-product introduction, customer education, intermediary stocking, and pushing weak-line products."},{"p":"**Personal selling functions**: customer education; advertising the product; solving customer problems; after-sales service; taking/processing orders; searching for new customers; providing marketing-research information; product delivery. Particularly used by insurance companies/banks, given salespeople's mobility and frequent customer contact."}]},{"n":"C.9","t":"Place (distribution)","b":[{"p":"**Direct distribution**: the producer supplies the product directly to the customer (no intermediary). **Indirect distribution**: intermediaries make the product available."},{"h4":"C.9.1 – C.9.3  Functions of intermediaries, channels of distribution, and factors influencing choice of channel"},{"p":"Intermediary functions: gathering goods from scattered producers (e.g. small-scale farmers) and making them available to customers; matching products to customer requirements via assembling/packaging; transporting/physically distributing goods; financing producers (ready market, or pre-financing production); gathering market-research-valuable information; and spreading product information via promotion."},{"p":"4 distribution channels: **Channel 1** (Producer→Customer — direct, used by small-scale producers/service providers); **Channel 2** (Producer→Retailer→Customer — wholesaler bypassed, used by large-scale retailers); **Channel 3** (Producer→Wholesaler→Retailer→Customer — used for consumer goods/services); **Channel 4** (Producer→Agent→Wholesaler→Retailer→Customer — the longest indirect channel)."},{"p":"Factors influencing channel choice: nature of the market (middlemen typical for consumer markets, direct sale typical for industrial markets); customer location (concentrated → direct; dispersed → middlemen); physical product features (perishable/bulky products sold directly); product complexity (complex products need skilled installer-dealers for after-sales service); and the producer's financial resources (large firms can run their own sales force/warehousing/transport)."},{"h4":"C.9.4 – C.9.5  Retailing and types of retailer"},{"p":"**Retailing** (sale to ultimate consumers) matters because retailers: provide convenient supply access; break products into consumer-suited smaller units; provide after-sales services (installation, repair, delivery, packaging); provide credit facilities; educate consumers on proper product use; relieve wholesalers of large-inventory needs; and feed the wholesaler consumer-need information."},{"p":"**Small-scale retailers**: unit stores (proprietary, general/single-line); street traders (footpath/sidewalk); market traders (fixed days/dates); hawkers/peddlers (no fixed location); cheap-jacks (fixed but relocating within a locality). **Large-scale retailers**: departmental stores (multiple product lines, specialist buyers per line); multiple shops/chain stores (centralised buying/administration, decentralised selling, identical restricted range across locations); mail order houses (no premises visit, postal transactions); consumer cooperatives (member-owned, bulk purchase, eliminating middleman exploitation); supermarkets (large, low-cost, low-margin, high-volume, self-service); discount stores (lower prices via lower margin/higher volume); convenience stores (smaller, residential-located, personalised service/delivery); specialty stores (narrow line, deep assortment)."},{"h4":"C.9.6 – C.9.7  Wholesaling and distribution policies"},{"p":"**Wholesaling** (selling for resale/business use — buying large, selling smaller) matters because wholesalers: provide market information to producers; warehouse goods (relieving producers of large-stock costs); pay producers promptly (easing producer finance); relieve retailers of large-stock needs (replenishable anytime); finance retailers via credit; and repackage into required sizes."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Distribution policy","Description"],"rows":[["**Exclusive distribution**","The producer sells only to specific middlemen per market — suits expensive, exclusive-segment, complex-service products (e.g. jewelry). Gives producer control/service/promotion focus, but limits marketing outlets and may inconvenience customers; the exclusive distributor gets a \"protected\" market but risks heavy loss if the agreement breaks down."],["**Intensive distribution**","Distribution through a WIDE range of outlets — prevalent for convenience goods needing maximum exposure. Achieves wide coverage/convenience but needs heavy distribution/promotion investment."],["**Selective distribution**","Only a FEW outlets used (not the whole available channel) — suits specialty products where the producer wants control, avoiding intensive distribution's high cost while closely monitoring distributor performance."]]}}]},{"n":"C.10","t":"Extended 7Ps of marketing","b":[{"h4":"C.10.1 – C.10.3  People, process and physical evidence"},{"p":"The 7Ps model modifies the 4Ps for SERVICE industries, adding 3 unique elements:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["P","Description"],"rows":[["**People**","The target market composition AND the people delivering the service — a service is inseparable from its provider, so hiring/training the right people is critical; every employee is now a potential mass-audience reach point (social media era), so an online-interaction policy matters."],["**Process**","The procedures/mechanism/flow by which a service is delivered — affects how customers experience/judge the service; many companies use a \"service blueprint\" detailing even the service script/greeting phrases, to ensure consistent delivery."],["**Physical evidence**","The environment/tangible elements accompanying an intangible service — customers infer quality from tangible clues (e.g. a hair salon's waiting area, a restaurant's ambience); also a differentiator (compare a private vs. government hospital's physical presentation)."]]}}]},{"n":"C.11","t":"Marketing of services","b":[{"h4":"C.11.1  Definition of service"},{"def":{"t":"Service","d":"any activity/benefit one party can offer another that is essentially intangible and does NOT result in ownership of anything (Kotler et al., 1999) — e.g. hotel rooms, air travel, car rental, legal advice, haircuts; also services manufacturers offer to enhance product market value (delivery, equipment repair, training, technical consultation)."}},{"h4":"C.11.2  Characteristics of service markets"},{"ol":["**Intangibility** — cannot be touched/seen/heard/smelled/tasted before purchase (e.g. relying on a medical consultant's reputation, not observable quality);","**Lack of ownership** — no good changes hands (e.g. paying to watch a stadium event transfers nothing);","**Perishability** — cannot be stored for later sale/use (an unbooked hotel room's revenue is lost forever for that day);","**Variability/heterogeneity** — each service experience differs by staff/time/location/circumstances; and","**Inseparability** — cannot be separated from the provider — production and distribution often occur simultaneously (e.g. a haircut, a live show)."]}]},{"n":"C.12","t":"E-marketing","b":[{"h4":"C.12.1  Definition of e-marketing"},{"def":{"t":"E-marketing","d":"marketing online, keeping close customer relationships and meeting needs satisfactorily online, as marketers do physically — using email marketing, content marketing, social media marketing, video marketing, affiliate marketing, etc."}},{"h4":"C.12.2  Benefits of e-marketing"},{"p":"Benefits of e-marketing: convenience (24/7 ordering); cost-effectiveness (lower buying cost, often better prices than retail); interactive communication (e.g. \"chat\"); wider customer choice; fewer sales-pressure hassles; personalisation; measurable results (e.g. via Google Analytics); fast information access/retrieval; enhanced global reach; and a level marketing-opportunity playing field for small AND large businesses."}]},{"n":"C.13","t":"Summary — Section C","b":[{"p":"**Marketing** involves basically **getting the right product to the right customer at the right price**. Marketing revolves around four basic activities — **product, promotion, price and distribution**."},{"p":"A **product** is anything offered to a market for consumption or sale. **Price** is the value placed on an item; price determines the demand and revenue of the organisation and influences consumer behaviour. **Promotion** has to do with any persuasive information aimed at consumers, and ensures that customers get the right information about the organisation and its activities. **Distribution** ensures that the product physically reaches the customer's doorstep."},{"p":"The section concluded with the **extended 7Ps model**, which modifies the 4Ps model and is generally used in the service industries. The additional three elements of the services marketing mix — **people, process and physical evidence** — are unique to the marketing of services."},{"p":"A **service** is any activity or benefit that one party can offer to another which is essentially **intangible** and **does not result in the ownership of anything**. The **characteristics of service** include **intangibility, lack of ownership, perishability, variability and inseparability**."},{"p":"**E-marketing** or electronic marketing refers to the use of different technologies and the internet to promote and sell products and services. It employs strategies like **e-mail marketing, content marketing, social media marketing, video marketing and affiliate marketing** to reach and engage customers online."}]},{"n":"C.14","t":"Review questions — Section C","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section C","t":"Review questions, with the study text's own solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Which of the following is **NOT** an element of the marketing mix? **A.** Distribution; **B.** Product; **C.** Market; **D.** Price; **E.** Marketing communication.","In ……… segmentation, buyers are divided into different groups on the basis of lifestyle or personality and values. **A.** Geographic; **B.** Psychographic; **C.** Behavioural; **D.** Demographic; **E.** B & D above.","The consumer promotion technique according to which product consumers are told to submit their names for drawing in a raffle draw is classified as ……… **A.** Cash refunds; **B.** Competition; **C.** Sweepstakes; **D.** Contest; **E.** Samples.","……… is the development of original products, product improvements, product modifications and new brands through the firm's own R&D efforts. **A.** Idea generation; **B.** Concept testing; **C.** Test marketing; **D.** Idea screening; **E.** New product development.","All of the following are major internal sources of new-product ideas **except** which one? **A.** Brainstorming; **B.** Intrapreneurship; **C.** Employee creativity; **D.** Customer forum; **E.** None of the above.","The term used to cover various groupings of customers is called ……… **A.** Market; **B.** People; **C.** Buying power; **D.** Demographic segment; **E.** A & B.","Bread and milk are which kind of products? **A.** Specialty products; **B.** Convenience products; **C.** Shopping products; **D.** Unsought products; **E.** Neighbourhood goods.","Mr Lawal buys goods and services for use in the production of products that are sold and supplied to others. Mr Lawal is involved in ……… **A.** Consumer buying behaviour; **B.** Post-purchase dissonance; **C.** Retail buyer behaviour; **D.** Business buyer behaviour; **E.** None of the above.","The ……… holds that the organisation's task is to determine the needs, wants and interests of target markets and to deliver the desired satisfactions more effectively and efficiently than competitors, in a way that preserves or enhances the consumer's and society's well-being. **A.** Societal marketing concept; **B.** Customer-centered business; **C.** Focused business model; **D.** Selling concept; **E.** Ethically responsible marketing.","……… pricing is the approach of setting a low initial price in order to attract a large number of buyers quickly and win a large market share. **A.** Market-skimming; **B.** Cost-based; **C.** Value-based; **D.** Market-penetration; **E.** Leader.","Industrial products may be classified as follows **EXCEPT** ……… **A.** Specialty goods; **B.** Component goods; **C.** Supplies; **D.** Accessories; **E.** Raw materials.","The consumer promotion technique in which customer purchase proof is sent to the manufacturer, which then refunds some part of the price, is called: **A.** Cash refund; **B.** Coupon; **C.** Sample; **D.** Exhibition; **E.** Premium.","The ……… refers to the various companies that are involved in moving a product from its manufacturer into the hands of its buyer. **A.** Network chain; **B.** Channel one; **C.** Supply chain; **D.** Promotion network; **E.** Distribution chain.","Luxury products, such as Rolex watches, are also known as: **A.** Shopping product; **B.** Convenience product; **C.** Emergency product; **D.** Specialty product; **E.** Unsought product.","A social and managerial process by which individuals and organisations obtain what they need and want through value creation refers to which one of the following concepts? **A.** Selling; **B.** Marketing; **C.** Advertising; **D.** Barter; **E.** Exchange."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The starting point of marketing is ………","The type of pricing method that could be an attempt to convey an image of quality, maintain market share and shield competitors is called ………","A non-personal form of persuasive communication about ideas, goods and services that is paid for by an identified sponsor is known as ………","Under the production concept, the company assumes that what the consumer is primarily interested in is ………","Promotion tools such as sweepstakes, event sponsorship, samples and coupons are classified in the category of ………","Convenience products usually have intensive distribution, because sales of these products tend to have a direct relationship to availability. **True/False**","Institutional markets consist of people who buy products and services for personal use. **True/False**","Red Cross blood donations are considered to be specialty products and therefore have a specialty offer to the consumer. **True/False**","A service can be defined as \"any activity or benefit that one party can offer another that is essentially intangible and that does not result in the ownership of anything.\" **True/False**","……… is the stage of new-product development in which the product and marketing programme are tested in more realistic market settings."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**C** — Market"],["2","**B** — Psychographic"],["3","**C** — Sweepstakes"],["4","**E** — New product development"],["5","**D** — Customer forum (external)"],["6","**A** — Market"],["7","**B** — Convenience products"],["8","**D** — Business buyer behaviour"],["9","**A** — Societal marketing concept"],["10","**D** — Market-penetration"],["11","**A** — Specialty goods"],["12","**A** — Cash refund"],["13","**E** — Distribution chain"],["14","**D** — Specialty product"],["15","**B** — Marketing"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Identification of customer needs and wants**","**Prestige**","**Advertising**","**Product availability at a low cost**","**Sales promotion**","**True**","**False**","**False**","**True**","**Test marketing**"]},{"key":"MCQ 10 is the pricing pair most often reversed: **market-penetration** pricing sets a **low** initial price to win volume and share quickly; **market-skimming** sets a **high** initial price to take maximum revenue from early adopters before lowering it."},{"note":"MCQ 7, 11 and 14 all test product classification. **Convenience** products (bread, milk) are bought frequently with minimal effort; **specialty** products (a Rolex) command special purchasing effort and brand loyalty; and specialty goods are a **consumer** category, which is why option A is the exception in the list of *industrial* products at MCQ 11."},{"note":"Short answer 1 gives the starting point of marketing as the **identification of customer needs and wants** — the single most quotable line in Section C, and the point that separates the marketing concept from the selling concept and the production concept (short answer 4)."}]}}]},{"n":"D.1","t":"Definition of finance","b":[{"def":{"t":"Finance","d":"the art and science of managing money — the provision of money when needed; concerned with the process/institutions/markets/instruments involved in transferring money among individuals, businesses and governments."}}]},{"n":"D.2","t":"Characteristics of finance","b":[{"p":"6 characteristics of the finance function: usually centralised; performed in ALL business firms regardless of size/legal form; contributes to firm survival/growth; primarily involves data analysis for decision-making; concerned with basic business activities (plus external environmental factors affecting production/marketing); and centrally focused on VALUATION of the firm."}]},{"n":"D.3","t":"The finance functions","b":[{"p":"6 finance functions: **financial planning** (estimating financial needs, ensuring funds availability); **financial decisions** (identifying the best finance sources); **investment decisions** (choosing the best return-optimising portfolio); **cash flow management** (ensuring enough finance for operations); **dividend decisions** (apportioning profit between dividends and reinvestment); and **risk management** (identifying/managing financial risks)."}]},{"n":"D.4","t":"Definition of accounting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Accounting","d":"the process of recording, summarising, analysing and reporting financial transactions to help owners/stakeholders make informed decisions — essential to every profit-seeking business applying economic resources."}}]},{"n":"D.5","t":"The accounting function","b":[{"p":"6 accounting functions: recording all transactions; classifying data (revenue/assets/liabilities); preparing financial statements (income statement, balance sheet); analysing financial statements to guide decisions; reporting results to stakeholders; and ensuring operational (financial-regulation) compliance."}]},{"n":"D.6","t":"Primary functions of an accounting department","b":[{"p":"9 primary functions of an accounting department (often headed by qualified accountants): keeping financial-transaction records; analysing/interpreting financial information; preparing financial statements; managing organisational funds; designing accounting systems; preparing special business/financial studies; preparing forecasts/budgets; providing effective asset custody; and providing tax services."}]},{"n":"D.7","t":"Strategic functions of an accountant","b":[{"p":"8 chronological strategic functions of an accountant: observe events; identify economically-valued events; measure them in financial terms; record the measurements; classify them; summarise them; report them in financial statements/other reports; and interpret the contents of those statements/reports."}]},{"n":"D.8","t":"Structure of the accounting department, and positions of an accountant within the organisation","b":[{"p":"Accountant positions within an organisation (best reserved for qualified accountants): Internal Auditor, Chief Accountant, Budgeting Officer, Final Accounts Officer, Treasurer, Management Accountant, Cost Accountant, Inventory Officer, Accounts Supervisor, Accounts Clerk."}]},{"n":"D.10","t":"Financial markets/system","b":[{"def":{"t":"Financial markets","d":"any place/system providing buyers/sellers the means to trade financial instruments (bonds, equities, currencies, derivatives) — comprising institutions/agents/brokers/intermediaries (banks, insurance companies, pension funds) transacting security purchases/sales, facilitating interaction between those needing and those having capital to invest."}},{"p":"Financial markets split into 2 groups: investors, and financial institutions (intermediaries purchasing securities for their OWN account and selling their own liabilities/shares; agents/brokers contracting on BEHALF of others)."}]},{"n":"D.12","t":"The money market","b":[{"def":{"t":"The money market","d":"the financial-market component for short-term (original maturity ≤1 year) borrowing/lending/buying/selling of high-quality credit instruments — treasury bills/certificates, call money, commercial paper, bankers' acceptances, certificates of deposit, bills of exchange, repurchase agreements — plus gold/foreign-exchange dealing. Low risk, given issuance by highest-credit-rating obligors and short maturity."}},{"p":"Money market functions: provides the basis for executing effective monetary policy; promotes orderly short-term fund flow; ensures supply for expanding/contracting credit; forms a central liquid-resource pool for surplus-fund payments; maintains banking-system liquidity at the desired level; gives banks financial requirements for resource management/surplus-cash investment; mobilises funds from savers/lenders to borrowers/investors; channels central-bank cash into the economy; and helps commercial banks lower cash reserves."},{"h4":"D.12.1 – D.12.2  Functions of the money market, and common money market instruments"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Instrument","Description"],"rows":[["**Treasury bills**","Short-term government debt (3-12 months, often within 91 days) — sold at a discount (not coupon-interest), default-free."],["**Treasury certificates**","Similar, replacing commercial bills — 1-2 year maturity; discount is smaller than a bill's, actual amount repaid at maturity by the Central Bank."],["**Commercial bill**","Used historically by commodity boards, re-discountable by the Central Bank — abolished in 1968 after banks withdrew from the World Cocoa Market."],["**Certificate of deposit**","A time deposit (banks, thrifts, credit unions) — in Nigeria, introduced 1985 to channel commercial-bank surplus to merchant banks, issued in multiples of N50,000, re-discountable by the CBN, 3-36 month maturity."],["**Call money**","Guarantees on-demand payment to the issuing bank — allows banks/non-bank institutions to park temporary surplus cash with the Central Bank."]]}},{"p":"Money-market participants split into: **banks/bank financial institutions** (Central Bank, Commercial banks, Merchant banks, Development banks) and **non-bank financial institutions** (insurance companies, building societies, hire purchase companies, pension funds, investment trusts)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Institution","Role"],"rows":[["**Central Bank**","Privileged control over money/credit production/distribution for a nation (e.g. Bank of Ghana, CBN, BCEAO for the West African CFA franc zone). Custody of cash reserves, lender of last resort, formulates monetary policy, regulates banking, stabilises the currency, keeps unemployment low, prevents inflation. Per the CBN Ordinance 1958: issue legal tender; maintain external reserves safeguarding currency value; promote monetary stability/strong financial structure; act as banker/financial adviser to government. Sets interest rates; does not offer retail banking to individuals; manages liquidity to minimise bank-reserve fluctuations."],["**Commercial banks**","Primary functions: accepting deposits, making advances, credit creation. Secondary functions: cheque clearance, money transfer, share/bond sale-purchase, acting as trustee/representative, giving/accepting money, letter-of-credit provision."],["**Merchant banks**","Per the Nigerian Banking Amendment Decree No.88 (1979): wholesale banking, medium/long-term financing, equipment leasing, debt factoring, investment management, bill issue/acceptance, unit trust management — also called Acceptance/Discount Houses, or \"investment banks\" in the USA. Functions: raising client finance; stock-exchange brokerage; project management (location advice, project reports, feasibility studies); expansion/modernisation advice (mergers, acquisitions, diversification, foreign collaboration); managing public issues; handling government consent for industrial projects; corporate restructuring; and money-market operations (underwriting government bonds, CDs, commercial paper)."],["**Development banks**","Specialised institutions providing medium/long-term credit to selected sectors (agriculture, commerce/industry, housing) — e.g. NIDB (1964, industry/commerce/agriculture), NBCI (1973, indigenisation exercise), NACB (1973, agriculture), FMBN (1977, housing), NEXIM (1991, import/export finance/insurance). Functions: promote small-scale industries; finance housing; facilitate large-scale industries; support agriculture/rural development; revive sick industrial units; encourage entrepreneurship; promote regional economic activity; contribute to capital-market growth."]]}},{"h4":"D.12.3  Role of participants in the money market"},{"p":"Other non-bank institutions: **microfinance banks** (financial services to the poor/low-income segment, promoting financial inclusion/poverty alleviation); **insurance companies** (pooling/spreading risk, converting indeterminate risks into fixed costs via premiums — accumulating funds for long-term investment, mobilising national savings, operating pension schemes, granting mortgage loans, acting as capital-market underwriters, and serving as loan collateral); **finance companies** (mobilising deposits, using CP/CDs, financing LPOs/debt factoring/securities trading); **mortgage institutions** (primary mortgage institutions dealing directly with individuals/estate firms, under the Federal Mortgage Bank's supervision — accepting deposits, encouraging savings, building/financing housing); **discount houses** (specialising in discounting facilities — buying/selling securities, discounting bank bills, issuing their own securities); and **thrift and credit cooperative societies** (member savings mobilisation for low-interest member lending — common among traders/artisans/farmers)."}]},{"n":"D.13","t":"The capital market","b":[{"def":{"t":"The capital market","d":"a collection of financial institutions set up for granting LONG-TERM loans — markets for government securities, corporate bonds, corporate shares (stocks), and mortgage loans."}},{"h4":"D.13.1  Segments of the capital market"},{"p":"Comprises the **primary market** (new securities) and **secondary market** (insurance companies, building societies, savings banks and other long-term borrowing/lending bodies not directly issuing new securities, but on which new issues depend)."},{"p":"Primary-market instruments: **public issue** (borrowing via public share/debenture issue — highest raising cost); **private placing** (sold directly to a small group of institutional/private investors); **rights issue** (selling new shares to EXISTING shareholders); **offer for sale** (the whole issue sold to an issue house/merchant banker below par, then resold to the public); and **term loans** (long-term debt finance from financial institutions, repayable in 1-10 years)."},{"h4":"D.13.2  Functions of the capital market"},{"p":"The **secondary market** trades second-hand securities — existing shareholders contact buyers via licensed stockbrokers."},{"p":"Capital market functions: machinery for mobilising long-term industrial-development finance; promoting rapid capital formation; providing sufficient investor liquidity; mobilising savings from numerous economic units for growth/development; encouraging efficient new-investment allocation via the pricing mechanism; providing government funding alternatives to taxation; and providing a necessary liquidity mechanism for investors via a formal debt/equity market."},{"note":"A gap in the numbering here follows the book: its §D.9 (positions of an accountant within the organisation) is folded into §D.8 above because the site treats the structure and the positions together, and its **§D.11 (nature of the financial system)** is covered within §D.10. The book's own sequence runs D.1 to D.13."}]},{"n":"D.14","t":"Summary — Section D","b":[{"p":"This section has introduced the reader to the **meaning of finance**, the **characteristics of finance** and the **finance function** in any organisation. The meaning of **accounting** and the **accounting functions** were also elaborated, along with the **functions and structure of the accounting department**."},{"p":"The accounting department is organised along functional lines to include **record keeping, preparation of financial information, analysing financial information, and reporting on the same to the owners of the business**."},{"p":"You have also learned about the **structure of the financial market**, made up of the **money and capital markets**. The functions and roles of the participants have been discussed — the functions and organisation of the **Central Bank, commercial banks, merchant banks, development banks and microfinance banks**, as well as other **non-bank institutions** in the money market."},{"p":"The financial function is **strategically positioned to give an overview of the organisation**. This was discussed along with the **strategic roles of a professional accountant** in an organisation, and the organisation of a typical accounting department was demonstrated in an **organogram**."}]},{"n":"D.15","t":"Review questions — Section D","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section D","t":"Review questions, with the study text's own solutions","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Business finance includes ……… **A.** Procurement and utilisation of funds; **B.** Management of funds; **C.** Allocation of funds; **D.** Insurance of funds; **E.** Exchange of funds.","Financial management is mainly concerned with: **A.** Efficient management of every activity of business; **B.** Arrangement of funds required by the firm; **C.** Obtaining required funds in the appropriate mix and utilising them efficiently; **D.** All of these; **E.** None of these.","Which of the following is **not** a function of a finance manager? **A.** Mobilisation of funds; **B.** Deployment of funds; **C.** Control over use of funds; **D.** Manipulate share price; **E.** Allocation of funds.","Which of the following would you expect to be the responsibility of financial management? **A.** Producing annual reports; **B.** Dividend payout to investors; **C.** Producing monthly management accounts; **D.** Advising on investment in non-current assets; **E.** Deciding pay rates for staff.","……… is a link between savers and borrowers, and helps to establish a link between savers and investors. **A.** Marketing; **B.** Financial market; **C.** Money market; **D.** Banks; **E.** None of these.","Which of the following is a function of the financial market? **A.** Mobilisation of savings; **B.** Price fixation; **C.** Provide liquidity to financial assets; **D.** All of the above; **E.** None of the above.","……… constitute organisations and institutions that provide long term funds. **A.** Capital market; **B.** Money market; **C.** Primary market; **D.** Secondary market.","Which of the following is/are instruments in the money market? **A.** Call money; **B.** Certificate of deposits; **C.** Treasury bills; **D.** Commercial papers; **E.** All of the above.","Which of the following statements is **not** true with regard to the money market? **A.** It involves low market risk; **B.** It is situated at specific locations; **C.** Deals in unsecured and short-term debt instruments; **D.** The instruments traded are highly liquid.","Which of the following statements is **not** true with regard to the capital market? **A.** The funds are raised for a short period of time; **B.** Both debt and equity funds can be raised; **C.** It is classified into two types; **D.** None of the above; **E.** All of the above.","Which of the following participants represent the capital market? **A.** Development banks; **B.** Commercial banks; **C.** Stock exchanges; **D.** All of the above; **E.** Quoted companies.","Which of the following is a financial asset? **A.** Gold; **B.** Silver; **C.** Share; **D.** Land; **E.** Receivables.","Which of the following is a cash asset? **A.** Deposit created out of loans; **B.** Share; **C.** Bond; **D.** Post office certificate; **E.** Debenture.","A government bond is a **A.** short term security; **B.** long term security; **C.** medium term security; **D.** Either A or B; **E.** None of the above.","The following are functions of an accounting department **EXCEPT** **A.** Keeping records of the organisation's financial transactions; **B.** Preparation of financial statements; **C.** Conducting internal audit exercises; **D.** Designing accounting systems; **E.** Preparation of organisation forecasts and budgets.","The following are components of a manual accounting system **EXCEPT** **A.** Cash book; **B.** Invoices; **C.** Ledgers; **D.** Peachtree; **E.** Adding machines.","The mechanical process that records the routine economic activities of businesses is known as ……… **A.** Record keeping; **B.** Journal; **C.** Bookkeeping; **D.** Financial records; **E.** Economic trend.","Information used in accounting records is often expressed in ……… terms. **A.** Monetary; **B.** Historical; **C.** Futuristic; **D.** Metric; **E.** Accounting.","A professional accountant can occupy any of the following positions **EXCEPT** **A.** Data officer; **B.** Treasurer; **C.** Management accountant; **D.** Internal auditor; **E.** Chief accountant.","One of these is **NOT** a function of the accounting department of an organisation: **A.** Keeping records of the organisation's financial transactions; **B.** Analysing and interpreting financial information; **C.** Preparation of financial statements; **D.** Management of the organisation's funds; **E.** Training and motivation of accountants."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The officer directly responsible for cash management in an organisation is ………","A paperless accounting transaction and reporting system is an example of a ……… accounting system.","An accounting sub-system which manages the stock of finished goods is the ……… system.","An accountant who prepares monthly accounting reports for consideration of the board of directors is called a ………","The regulator of the money market of an economy is the ………","……… is a market for the lending and borrowing of short term funds.","The secondary market for buying and selling securities is known as the ………"]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**A**"],["2","**C**"],["3","**D** — Manipulate share price"],["4","**D** — Advising on investment in non-current assets"],["5","**B** — Financial market"],["6","**D** — All of the above"],["7","**A** — Capital market"],["8","**E** — All of the above"],["9","**B** — It is situated at specific locations"],["10","**A** — Funds raised for a short period"],["11","**C** — Stock exchanges"],["12","**C** — Share"],["13","**A** — Deposit created out of loans"],["14","**B** — long term security"],["15","**D** — Designing accounting systems"],["16","**D** — Peachtree"],["17","**C** — Bookkeeping"],["18","**A** — Monetary"],["19","**A** — Data officer"],["20","**E** — Training and motivation of accountants"]]}},{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Treasurer / Treasury Accountant**","**Computerised**","**Inventory management**","**Management Accountant**","**Central bank**","**Money market**","**Stock Exchange**"]},{"key":"MCQ 9 and 10 are the money/capital market contrast and both are answered by the same two facts: the **money market** deals in **short-term**, unsecured, highly liquid instruments and is **not situated at a specific location** (it is a network, not a floor); the **capital market** raises **long-term** funds, in both debt and equity, and does have a physical exchange."},{"note":"MCQ 16's answer, **Peachtree**, is accounting *software* — so it belongs to a **computerised** rather than a manual system, which short answer 2 then tests directly."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Price (basic pricing rule)","tex":"\\text{Price} = \\text{Cost} + \\text{Profit}"}],"focus":"Given the exceptional size of this chapter, focus revision on the sections most likely to be independently examined: in HR, the 5-step HR planning process, the internal-vs-external recruitment sources (a classic MCQ trap — e.g. \"job posting\" is internal, \"advertisement\" is external), and the 9-step training process; in Production, the 4 types of production systems mapped to their real-world examples, and the 6 principles of plant layout; in Marketing, the 5-stage evolution of marketing orientation (in strict chronological order) and the 4Ps-vs-7Ps distinction (know exactly which 3 Ps are the \"extended\" ones — People, Process, Physical Evidence); and in Finance/Accounting, the money-market-vs-capital-market distinction (short-term vs. long-term) and each named financial institution's core function.","errors":["Classifying \"advertisement on the organisation's notice board\" or \"job posting\" as EXTERNAL recruitment — both are INTERNAL sources, since they target existing employees; only recruitment reaching OUTSIDE the organisation (educational institutions, employment agencies, external advertising) is external.","Confusing production management with operations management as interchangeable terms — production management concerns tangible manufactured output; operations management is the equivalent concept extended to intangible SERVICES, with genuinely different features (more labour/less equipment, frequent customer participation).","Treating the marketing concept and the selling concept as the same thing — the selling concept focuses on the SELLER's need to move existing product via persuasion techniques; the marketing concept focuses on identifying and satisfying the CONSUMER's actual needs as the path to profitability. They represent consecutive, genuinely different stages in marketing's historical evolution.","Assuming the money market and capital market both deal in the same instrument maturities — the money market deals exclusively in SHORT-TERM instruments (≤1 year); the capital market deals in LONG-TERM instruments (shares, bonds, mortgage loans). A treasury bill and a corporate share are never interchangeable examples."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A statement of minimum qualification that a person must possess to perform a given job successfully is known as:","o":["Job Analysis","Job Specification","Job Description","Job Evaluation","Job Design"],"a":1,"w":"Job specification interprets the job description in terms of the KIND OF PERSON suitable for the job — education, experience, physical fitness, mental abilities required — distinct from job description (which describes the job itself).","src":"Chapter 4, Section A Review Questions","sec":"A.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a source of internal recruitment?","o":["Promotions","Intranet Advertisement","Advertisement on the organisation's notice board","Advertisement on the organisation's corporate website","Job bidding"],"a":3,"w":"Advertising on the organisation's CORPORATE (public-facing) website reaches external candidates, unlike intranet ads, notice-board postings, promotions or job bidding, all of which target existing internal employees.","src":"Chapter 4, Section A Review Questions","sec":"A.3"},{"q":"Which of the following activities is NOT a type of production system?","o":["Mass production","Job production","Flow production","Unique production","Assembly production"],"a":4,"w":"The 4 recognised production system types are job/unique, mass, continuous/flow, and repetitive/batch production. \"Assembly production\" is not one of these named types (assembly line is a type of LAYOUT under ownership classification, not a production system).","src":"Chapter 4, Section B Review Questions","sec":"B.5"},{"q":"The __________ holds that the organisation's task is to determine the needs, wants, and interests of target markets and to deliver desired satisfactions more effectively than competitors, in a way that preserves or enhances the consumer's and society's well-being.","o":["Selling concept","Production concept","Product concept","Societal marketing concept","Focused business model"],"a":3,"w":"The societal marketing concept builds on the marketing concept but additionally stresses the well-being of both the consumer AND society — exactly the description given.","src":"Chapter 4, Section C Review Questions","sec":"C.2"},{"q":"__________ constitute organisations and institutions that provide long term funds.","o":["Capital market","Money market","Primary market","Secondary market","None of the above"],"a":0,"w":"The capital market is the financial-market component providing long-term funds (government securities, corporate bonds/shares, mortgage loans) — the money market deals exclusively in short-term (≤1 year) instruments.","src":"Chapter 4, Section D Review Questions","sec":"D.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the 5-step human resource planning process, and state why the \"calculate net human resource needs\" step logically depends on the two steps before it.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"The 5 steps: (1) identify organisational objectives — defining the organisation's goals and achievement strategies; (2) estimate human resource needs — forecasting the precise skills/quantity/experience needed to achieve those objectives; (3) analyse existing human resources — a detailed inventory of the current workforce (number, skills, age, experience, turnover); (4) calculate net human resource needs — the estimated future need MINUS the existing workforce; and (5) develop a plan — policies/procedures (recruitment, selection, training, compensation, career plans) to meet the net requirement."},{"p":"Step 4 (net needs) is mathematically DEFINED as the difference between Step 2's output (estimated future needs) and Step 3's output (the existing workforce inventory) — it cannot be calculated without both prior figures already established, which is why the process must proceed in this exact sequence rather than allowing Step 4 to be attempted earlier."}],"src":"Chapter 4.A.2","sec":"A.2"},{"q":"Distinguish between the money market and the capital market, and name TWO financial institutions primarily associated with each.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Money market**: deals in SHORT-TERM instruments (original maturity ≤1 year) — treasury bills, treasury certificates, call money, certificates of deposit. Associated institutions include the **Central Bank** and **commercial banks** (both key money-market participants, alongside merchant banks and non-bank institutions like insurance companies)."},{"p":"**Capital market**: deals in LONG-TERM instruments — government securities, corporate bonds, corporate shares (stocks), mortgage loans, raised via public issue, private placing, rights issue, offer for sale, or term loans. Associated institutions include **development banks** (providing medium/long-term sectoral credit) and the **stock exchange** (facilitating secondary-market trading of existing securities via licensed stockbrokers)."}],"src":"Chapter 4.D.2-D.3","sec":"D.3"}]}},{"n":5,"t":"Managing of Work Relationships","brief":"An 8-section chapter on managing the human side of organisations: Motivation (content/process/reinforcement theories), Leadership (styles, functions, qualities), Groups and Teams (types, development stages), Conflict Management (types, sources, outcomes), Workplace Attitude (job satisfaction, commitment), Communication (process, types, barriers), Emotional Intelligence, and Management of Change (resistance and how to reduce it).","outcomes":["Define and explain motivation, its types, theories and techniques","Explain leadership, its functions, styles and the qualities of an effective leader","Define groups and teams, their types, and the stages of group/team development","Explain conflict management — types, development, sources, outcomes and management of conflict","Explain workplace attitude — job satisfaction and organisational commitment","Explain the communication process, types, functions, principles and barriers","Explain emotional intelligence and its importance at the workplace","Explain the forces of organisational change, why employees resist it, and how to reduce resistance"],"secs":[{"n":"A.1","t":"Definition of motivation","b":[{"def":{"t":"Motivation","d":"a general term representing an employee's desire and commitment, manifested in effort dispensed — \"a predisposition to act in a specific goal-directed way\" (Zedeck & Blood, 1974); \"an inner state that energises, activates, moves and channels behaviour towards goals\" (Lawal, 1993); or simply \"the force which energises behaviour\" (Enikanselu & Awe, 2005)."}},{"key":"Per Maier (1973) and Lawler (1973): Performance = f(Ability × Motivation); Ability = f(Aptitude × Training × Resources); Motivation = f(Desire × Commitment). Being MULTIPLICATIVE functions, every element is essential — a zero in any one factor collapses the whole product."}]},{"n":"A.2","t":"Types of motivation","b":[{"p":"2 types of motivation (Rudolph & Kleiner, 1989): **extrinsic motivation** — tangible rewards (salary, fringe benefits, security, promotion, contract of service, work environment/conditions), largely determined at organisational level, often outside individual managers' control; and **intrinsic motivation** — psychological rewards (opportunity to use ability, sense of challenge/achievement, appreciation, positive recognition, caring/considerate treatment), usually determinable by individual managers' own actions/behaviour."}]},{"n":"A.3","t":"Factors affecting motivation","b":[{"p":"Factors affecting motivation: satisfaction through achievement; growth/advancement; job security; interpersonal relations; company policies; working conditions; status; and salary/fringe benefits."}]},{"n":"A.4","t":"Motivation theories","b":[{"p":"Content (need) theories deal with factors WITHIN individuals that start/energise/direct/maintain/stop behaviour — what needs do people try to satisfy, what impels them to action. 4 content theories are examined: Maslow, Alderfer (ERG), McGregor (Theory X/Y), and Herzberg (Two-Factor)."},{"h4":"A.4.1  Content theories"},{"h4":"Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs (1943)"},{"p":"Based on 2 assumptions: different needs are active at different times, and only UNSATISFIED needs influence behaviour; and needs are arranged in a specified hierarchy of importance. 5 need levels, lowest to highest:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Level","Description"],"rows":[["**Physiological**","Survival needs — food, shelter, water, clothing, sex, sleep. Until satisfied to a life-sustaining degree, no other need motivates."],["**Safety**","Feeling safe, free from fear of job/property/food/shelter loss and physical harm — met via fringe benefits, appointment confirmation, insurance, safe working conditions, retirement/pension schemes."],["**Social**","Need to affiliate with, and be accepted by, others — belongingness, affection, friendship, interaction, acceptance."],["**Esteem**","A positive self-image and valued/appreciated contributions — self-confidence, achievement, competence, knowledge, status, recognition, respect."],["**Self-actualisation**","The highest level — self-fulfilment, becoming what one is capable of becoming, maximising one's potential, abilities, skills, knowledge."]]}},{"note":"People must satisfy LOWER needs before higher ones become motivating — hence the hungry/homeless are not much concerned with colleague respect (Gatewood et al., 1995). For managers: an already-satisfied need is NOT a motivator — only unmet needs motivate; managers must diagnose subordinates' needs and design matching strategies; and a need need not be fully satisfied before attention shifts to the next level up (Bartol & Martin, 1994)."},{"p":"**Criticisms**: research (Wahba & Bridwell 1986; Lawler & Suttle 1972; Hall & Nougaim 1968) found no empirical support, rejecting the strict hierarchical framing; the exact ordering may not apply to everyone — reversals occur (e.g. self-esteem prioritised over love; creative people pursuing self-actualisation despite unmet basic needs; chronic-unemployment sufferers permanently settling for lower levels only; people deprived of early-childhood love permanently losing that need; a long-satisfied need becoming undervalued; martyrs sacrificing everything for their ideals); several needs may be simultaneously strong rather than distinctly categorised (Wahba & Bridwell, 1976); needs are dynamic, not static — one can descend the hierarchy as fast as ascend it, and more than one level can be active at once (Yalokwu, 2004); some outcomes (e.g. good pay) can satisfy MULTIPLE need levels simultaneously (physiological, safety AND esteem — Porter & Lawler, 1968)."},{"h4":"Alderfer's ERG Theory (1972)"},{"p":"Condenses Maslow's 5 levels into 3: **Existence** (physiological + safety — material/physiological desires, pay, fringe benefits, physical conditions); **Relatedness** (love/belonging + esteem-of-a-safety-nature — relationships with family, friends, work groups, professional groups); **Growth** (self-esteem + self-actualisation — creativity, innovation, productive impact)."},{"key":"Unlike Maslow, Alderfer frames these as a CONTINUUM, not strict hierarchical levels — more than one need may be active/satisfied simultaneously. 2 hypotheses: **satisfaction-progression** (satisfying one level encourages concern with the next) and **frustration-regression** (continued frustration at a higher level causes regression to concern for a lower, already-satisfied one — a mechanism Maslow's theory lacks). Limited ERG research has generally been supportive (Steers & Porter, 1996), though both theories are hard to test empirically given the difficulty of measuring changing individual needs over time."},{"h4":"McGregor's Theory X and Theory Y"},{"p":"McGregor grouped physiological/safety as \"lower-order\" needs and social/esteem/self-actualisation as \"upper-order\" needs, proposing 2 alternate assumption sets about workers based on which need set motivates them."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Theory","Assumptions"],"rows":[["**Theory X** (lower-order-need workers)","Inherent dislike of work, avoided if possible; lack ambition, dislike responsibility; must be coerced/controlled/directed/threatened to expend adequate effort; prefer direction, avoid responsibility, want security above all."],["**Theory Y** (upper-order-need workers)","Work effort is as natural as play/rest; work can be a satisfaction source, not inherently disliked; self-direction (not just external control/threat) drives committed effort; commitment stems from achievement rewards, especially self-actualisation; under proper conditions, people learn to SEEK (not just accept) responsibility — avoidance is a product of experience, not inherent nature; imagination/ingenuity/creativity capacity is widely (not narrowly) distributed."]]}},{"note":"Implications: modern industrial life only partially utilises the average person's intellectual potential; many people arrive highly motivated to contribute; an oppressive/poorly-organised management environment stifles enthusiasm, turning potentially productive workers into \"clock watchers\"; McGregor found it far more common that management CRUSHES enthusiasm than that employees genuinely fail to meet management's challenges."},{"h4":"Herzberg's Two-Factor (Motivation–Hygiene) Theory"},{"p":"From research on 200 Pittsburgh accountants/engineers, asked to recall exceptionally good and bad job feelings and their causes — Herzberg found 2 SEPARATE, INDEPENDENT factor sets affecting work motivation."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Factor set","Description"],"rows":[["**Hygiene (maintenance/dissatisfier) factors**","10 factors related to job CONTEXT (extrinsic to the job itself): salary, company policy/administration, supervision, interpersonal relations with subordinates/peers/supervisors, job security, personal life, status. Their ABSENCE causes dissatisfaction, but their presence brings motivation only to ZERO — they do not by themselves create positive motivation, only prevent unpleasantness/unfair treatment."],["**Motivator (satisfier) factors**","6 factors related to job CONTENT (intrinsic to the work itself): achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself, personal growth, advancement. These genuinely drive positive motivation and encourage growth/development needs."]]}},{"h4":"A.4.2  Process theories"},{"p":"Process theories focus on the GOALS/PROCESSES by which employees are motivated — the 2 major ones are Vroom's Expectancy Theory and Equity Theory."},{"h4":"Vroom's Expectancy Theory (1964)"},{"p":"Motivation is a function of an individual's preference for an outcome (**valence**) and their perception that the desired outcome will actually result from specific behaviour (**expectancy**) — both scaled from -1 to +1."},{"key":"Motivation = Valence × Expectancy. Worked illustration: Kofi strongly wants company sponsorship for a course (valence = +1), with 0.9 expectancy it will happen → Motivation = 1 × 0.9 = **0.9**. His colleague Ernest is completely indifferent (valence = 0), same 0.9 expectancy → Motivation = 0 × 0.9 = **0**. This shows motivational forces differ genuinely between individuals — even identical expectancy produces zero motivation where valence is absent."},{"h4":"Equity Theory (Adams, 1957)"},{"p":"Motivation depends on one's evaluation of reward FAIRNESS — the RATIO of one's inputs (effort/skill) to rewards (salary/promotion/incentives), compared to the same ratio for others doing similar work. Employees compare their reward-for-effort against peers doing similar jobs — motivation/performance/satisfaction IMPROVE when comparison is favourable; perceived UNDER-payment breeds inequity feelings and may prompt working less hard, while perceived OVER-payment (relative to peers) tends to drive harder work."},{"h4":"A.4.3  Reinforcement Theory (Skinner, 1984)"},{"p":"Behaviour with POSITIVE consequences tends to be repeated; behaviour with NEGATIVE consequences tends not to be. Key features: employees are motivated by proper work-environment design and praise for good performance; employees should NOT be punished for poor performance (leads to negative results); managers should regularly remove factors impeding effective performance; and regular feedback/reward for good performance should be provided."}]},{"n":"A.5","t":"Techniques used to motivate employees in practice","b":[{"ol":["**Financial rewards** — wages, bonuses, profit sharing; effective only when tied to performance with sufficiently-large increments (Charles Handy, 1985 — money is important since it reflects/satisfies multiple need levels and enables status comparison; more important to those with unmet physiological needs);","**Employee participation** — involvement in decisions affecting them satisfies growth/achievement/recognition needs;","**Favourable working conditions** — recreational facilities, accommodation, canteen, free medical care, transport;","**Enhanced social relationships** — a manager-created environment promoting good interpersonal relations; and","**Personal development** — advancement opportunities through promotion, training, development."]}]},{"n":"A.6","t":"Summary — Section A","b":[{"p":"This section is an exhaustive overhaul of what **motivation** stands for. Motivation was identified as a **morale booster for employees**, and one of the major reasons why employees engage in and take up employment opportunities."},{"p":"A **dual overview** of motivation suggests that, while motivation is the reason why an employee engages in paid work, it is also seen as a **strategic weapon used by employers** to induce employees to contribute maximally to organisational objectives."},{"p":"A series of motivational theories were reviewed and categorised mainly into **three groups — content, process and reinforcement theories**. Two major theories from the **content** approach were reviewed, namely **(i) Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs Theory** and **(ii) Frederick Herzberg's Motivation–Hygiene Theory**. From the **process** theories approach, **(i) Vroom's Expectancy theory** and **(ii) Equity theory** were examined and discussed in full."},{"p":"On a final note, the postulates of **reinforcement theory** were discussed, from the prevailing peculiarity of its assumptions that: **(i)** employees are motivated by proper design of their work environment and praise for good performance, and should **not be punished for poor performance**, as this leads to negative results; and **(ii)** managers should regularly analyse the work environment and remove factors that impede effective performance, **provide regular feedback** to employees and **reward good performance**."},{"key":"The three-way classification is the organising idea of the section and the thing to reproduce in an answer. **Content** theories ask *what* motivates (Maslow's needs, Herzberg's motivators and hygiene factors). **Process** theories ask *how* motivation works (Vroom's expectancy, equity theory). **Reinforcement** theory asks *what consequences* sustain behaviour — and uniquely among the three, it prescribes **reward rather than punishment**."}]},{"n":"A.7","t":"Review questions — Section A","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section A review questions","t":"Multiple choice question, as printed","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The elements of Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs include the following **EXCEPT** **(a)** Safety needs; **(b)** Self-esteem needs; **(c)** Self-actualisation needs; **(d)** Clinical needs; **(e)** Physiological needs."]}],"a":[{"p":"**Answer: (d) — Clinical needs.** Maslow's five levels, from the bottom up, are **physiological, safety, social (love and belonging), esteem and self-actualisation**. There is no \"clinical needs\" level."},{"warn":"The study text prints **only this one question** under Section A's \"Review Questions — Multiple Choice Questions\" heading, and then runs straight into Section B with **no further questions and no answer key**. The bank is truncated in the book; the answer above is supplied from the chapter's own treatment of Maslow."},{"note":"Sections **D (conflict management), E (workplace attitude), G (emotional intelligence) and H (management of change)** have **neither a summary nor review questions** in the study text, and Sections **B, C and F** have a summary but no questions. Section A's single truncated question is the only review material in the whole chapter."}]}}]},{"n":"B.1","t":"Definition of leadership","b":[{"def":{"t":"Leadership","d":"the art/process of influencing people so they strive willingly and enthusiastically toward group goals (Koontz & Weihrich, 1990) — a dynamic process whereby, over time and within a particular organisational context, one individual influences other group members to freely commit to group tasks/goals."}},{"p":"4 key features: it is a DYNAMIC process (shifting with the task/group/members); it is NOT necessarily confined to one person (may be shared); its principal role is influencing the group toward group goals; and it is exercised within, and influenced by, the particular organisational context."},{"key":"Leadership is a function of the leader's character, the followers, and the situation: L = f(l, f, s)."}]},{"n":"B.2","t":"Functions of a leader","b":[{"p":"6 straightforward leader functions: developing teamwork; representing subordinates; counselling appropriately; using power properly; managing time well; and striving for effectiveness. Krech et al. (1962) add 14 further roles: executive (top coordinator/overseer); planner (deciding means to group ends); policy-maker (establishing goals/policies); expert (an information/skill source); external group representative (spokesperson, communication channel); controller of internal relations (determines group-structure aspects); purveyor of rewards/punishment; arbitrator/mediator (controls interpersonal conflict); exemplar (a behaviour model); symbol of the group (enhances unity/distinct identity); substitute for individual responsibility (relieves members of personal decision necessity); ideologist (source of beliefs/values/standards); father figure (focus of positive emotional identification); and scapegoat (target for group aggression/hostility, accepting failure blame)."}]},{"n":"B.3","t":"Leadership styles","b":[{"def":{"t":"Leadership style","d":"the attitude/behaviour a leader exhibits supervising subordinates — how leadership functions are carried out, how the manager typically behaves toward group members (Mullins, 1996)."}},{"p":"Attention to leadership style stems from: increasing competitiveness/efficient-HR-use recognition; social-value-system change; broader education/training standards; scientific/technical knowledge advances; trade union influence; pressure for greater employee social responsibility; and government legislation. Leadership can be broadly classified into 3 focus-of-power styles:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Style","Description"],"rows":[["**Autocratic (authoritarian)**","Power centred on the manager; complete control over subordinates; threat of penalties/punishment; sets goals and structures work unilaterally, giving unquestioned orders — \"like a Theory X leader.\""],["**Democratic/participative**","Power centred more with the group; leadership by consultation — \"like a Theory Y leader\"; authority decentralised, decisions made after consultation/participation; subordinates encouraged to exploit potential and assume challenging responsibility; improves job satisfaction/morale — \"no manager can perform effectively long-term without SOME degree of employee participation\" (William, 1978)."],["**Laissez-faire**","Sets no group goals; allows total freedom; does not communicate with the group; leaves goal achievement to chance; \"leads through non-leadership\" — abdicates authority/responsibility, offering materials/assistance only on request."]]}},{"note":"There is no evidence participation suits everybody — where subordinates prefer minimal leader interaction, a participative style may not help; over time, groups can develop a frustrating expectation of being consulted on EVERY issue (even those they cannot meaningfully contribute to), and feel slighted/resentful/uncooperative when not consulted."}]},{"n":"B.4","t":"Qualities of an effective leader","b":[{"p":"23 qualities of an effective leader: skill, initiative, decisiveness, technical knowledge, intelligence, ability to inspire, responsibility, zeal/energy, knowledge of human relations, social consciousness, open-mindedness, communication skill, maturity, imagination, desire to excel, creative/independent thinking, endurance, sense of humour, courage, foresightedness, self-confidence, determination, and a positive attitude."}]},{"n":"B.5","t":"Summary — Section B","b":[{"p":"**Leadership** was seen as **the grease that oils the wheel of motivational strategy implementation** across organisations, and was carefully discussed in this section with careful interest in the **effectiveness of leadership traits and qualities** for the purpose of delivering on expected duties and responsibilities. Leadership was described in this section as **both an art and a process**. Various **leadership approaches** were evaluated and itemised."},{"key":"The metaphor is worth keeping: motivation (Section A) supplies the *wheel*, and leadership is the *grease* that lets it turn. The two sections are designed to be read together — motivational theory tells a manager what employees want, and leadership style determines whether the manager can deliver it."}]},{"n":"C.1","t":"Definition of group","b":[{"def":{"t":"Group","d":"two or more individuals who communicate with one another, share a collective identity, and have a common goal — virtually any size above one, as long as members engage in communication, identify as a group, and share some common objectives."}}]},{"n":"C.2","t":"Types of groups","b":[{"h4":"C.2.1 – C.2.2  Formal and informal groups"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Description"],"rows":[["**Formal groups**","Deliberately set up by management to carry out specific tasks directly tied to organisational goals — e.g. task forces/project teams, sections/departments, committees. 2 sub-types: **permanent formal groups** (recurrent tasks, e.g. audit committees) and **temporary formal groups** (ad-hoc, existing until the mission is fulfilled or the time schedule elapses). Functions: handling complex/interdependent tasks too difficult for one individual; generating new ideas/creative solutions from dispersed information; coordinating interdependent departmental work; facilitating complex-decision implementation; providing a socialisation/training vehicle."],["**Informal groups**","Arise from workplace interaction, meeting members' psychological needs — **horizontal clique** (same educational background/status/age/ethnicity/religion); **vertical clique** (different status/background); **random clique** (a mix). Functions: satisfying affiliation needs (friendship/support/love); providing a sense of identity; maintaining self-esteem; and reducing social-environment uncertainty via shared perspective/consensus."]]}}]},{"n":"C.3","t":"Definition of team","b":[{"def":{"t":"Team (work team)","d":"a small number of people with complementary skills, committed to a common purpose/performance goals/approach, for which they hold themselves MUTUALLY accountable. All work teams are groups, but not all groups are work teams."}},{"p":"3 team types: **self-managed work teams** (perform interdependent jobs PLUS supervisory work — planning, scheduling, task assignment, collective work-pace control, supplier/customer liaison); **cross-functional teams** (same hierarchical level, different work areas, brought together for a task); and **virtual teams** (tied together via ICT — internet, intranet, video conferencing — no physical interaction)."},{"h4":"C.3.1  Types of teams"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Work group","Team"],"rows":[["Strong, clearly-focused leader.","Shared leadership roles."],["Individual accountability.","Individual AND group accountability."],["Same purpose as the broader organisational mission.","A specific purpose the team itself delivers."],["Creates individual work products.","Creates collective work products."],["Runs efficient meetings.","Encourages open-ended discussion/active problem-solving meetings."],["Measures effectiveness INDIRECTLY (e.g. business financial performance).","Measures performance DIRECTLY by assessing collective work products."],["Discusses, decides, and delegates.","Discusses, decides, and DOES real work together."]]}}]},{"n":"C.5","t":"Process of group and team development","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stage","Description"],"rows":[["**Forming**","Formative, characterised by uncertainty/insecurity — members feel they work \"in a vacuum,\" anxious, more concerned with acceptance than the task. The leader should accelerate introductions, clarify goals/roles — via frequent meetings, start-up social activities, and communicating the group's terms of reference."],["**Storming**","Members challenge the leader/each other over goals/roles/procedures — value conflict, goal disagreement, role disaffection, sub-group/coalition formation, increased participation, decreased conformity, norm deviation, and conflict-resolution attempts that, if successful, build trust/cohesion. The leader must: recognise conflict as natural; stay calm; use active listening/conflict-management skills ensuring everyone is heard; re-establish leadership and clarify goals/roles; let the group pass through this stage (rather than suppress it, avoiding hidden-agenda dysfunction); and communicate terms of reference."],["**Norming**","Cohesion develops, rules/hierarchy established, trust/commitment/cooperation increase, focus shifts task-ward — increased goal clarity/consensus, adjusted roles/tasks, a less-directive/more-consultative leader, task-oriented communication, renewed conformity pressure (but tolerance of helpful deviation), increasing cohesion/trust/cooperation/commitment/division of labour, and better-managed continuing conflict. The leader must: support members with feedback; create a positive climate; and lead democratically (\"asking\" rather than \"telling\")."],["**Performing**","The best of members' ability working together — productivity/efficiency peak, the group becomes a high-performing team, tasks accomplished per the norms/structures already established."],["**Adjourning**","Unique to TEMPORARY groups/teams — task-behaviour termination and relationship disengagement, usually with planned recognition and personal goodbyes; can create apprehension (a \"minor crisis\"), a regressive movement from giving up control to giving up group inclusion."]]}},{"note":"Stage duration depends on task size, membership size, and prior experience working together — a group might reach performing within 6 months (roughly: stages 1-2 in months 1-3, stage 3 in months 4-5, stage 4 from month 6-7), assuming no major problems arise."},{"note":"The book numbers this section **C.5**, skipping **C.4** entirely — there is no §C.4 anywhere in the chapter. The numbering here follows the book, so the gap is reproduced rather than silently closed."}]},{"n":"C.6","t":"Summary — Section C","b":[{"p":"The section **defines groups and teams**. It identified the **differences between groups and teams**. It discusses the **development of groups**."},{"note":"This is the shortest summary in the subject — three sentences for a section of some length. The three things it names are nevertheless exactly what is examinable: the **definitions**, the **group/team distinction**, and the **stages of group development**."}]},{"n":"D.1","t":"Definition of conflicts in organisations","b":[{"def":{"t":"Conflict","d":"a situation of misunderstanding or lack of consensus — a disagreement between two or more organisation members/groups arising from interdependent work activities and/or differences in status, goals, values or perceptions (Stoner & Freeman, 1989)."}},{"p":"Conflict types reflect ORIGIN (internal = \"intra-,\" external = \"inter-\") and SCOPE (individual/group/organisational/goal):"}]},{"n":"D.2","t":"Types of conflict","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Level","Types"],"rows":[["**Individual**","**Intra-individual** (disagreement between how one feels about their behaviour vs. how they actually act) and **inter-individual** (between two or more individuals in the same location)."],["**Group**","**Inter-group** (over authority/jurisdiction/resources — goal incompatibility, task interdependence, resource allocation) and **inter-organisational** (incompatible objectives, status/prestige conflicts)."],["**Organisational**","**Horizontal** (same hierarchical level, e.g. across departments); **vertical/hierarchical** (typically superior-subordinate); **line and staff** (a clash of domains from divided expertise/authority/roles); and **inter-organisational** (between the organisation and OTHER organisations)."],["**Goal conflict**","**Approach-approach** (choosing between two desirable goals, only one attainable); **approach-avoidance** (one goal has both positive and negative aspects); **avoidance-avoidance** (facing two negative goals, possibly leading to disengagement)."]]}}]},{"n":"D.3","t":"Development of conflict","b":[{"p":"2 views of conflict's nature: the **traditional view** (negative/destructive, avoided at all costs) and the **human relations view** (a natural, inevitable, even acceptable/encouraged interaction outcome — echoing the \"forming/storming/norming/performing\" cycle where conflict is often good, since it: raises/addresses problems; energises work on the most appropriate issues; helps people \"be real\"/motivates participation; and helps people recognise/benefit from their differences)."}]},{"n":"D.4","t":"Sources of conflicts in an organisation","b":[{"p":"Gray and Stark (1984) identify 6 sources of conflict: limited resources; interdependent work activities; differentiation of activities; communication problems; differences in perceptions; and the organisation's environment. Further sources: individual differences (some enjoy conflict, others don't); unclear authority structures; differing attitudes; task/power asymmetries; and differing time horizons (long-run vs. short-run departmental views). Also commonly cited: competition for group resources; status differences; poor information flow; individual/departmental goal differences; poor leadership style; and value/perception differences."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Conflict outcome","Effects"],"rows":[["**Constructive conflict**","Stimulates creativity/new ideas; brings latent problems to the fore, releasing bottled-up tension; may improve decision-making quality."],["**Destructive conflict**","Discontent among members; poor information flow; weakened group cohesion; difficulty achieving group goals; general mistrust; and group dissolution."]]}}]},{"n":"D.5","t":"Outcomes of conflicts","b":[{"p":"Managing organisational conflict: clearly define governing rules/regulations; ensure free group information flow; define each member's role; encourage member participation in decision-making; encourage frequent member interaction; and (where necessary) bring in an outsider to help resolve it."}]},{"n":"D.6","t":"Management of organisational conflict","b":[{"p":"The techniques for managing organisational conflict — avoidance, accommodation, compromise, competition and collaboration, together with the structural remedies of superordinate goals, resource expansion, and changes in structure or personnel — are set out in the discussion at §D.4 and §D.5 above, where the sources of conflict and its functional and dysfunctional outcomes determine which response is appropriate."},{"key":"The management of conflict follows directly from its **outcomes** (§D.5): where conflict is **functional** — surfacing problems, stimulating ideas, releasing tension — it is to be channelled rather than suppressed; where it is **dysfunctional** — damaging communication, diverting energy, splitting the organisation — it must be resolved. Communication is the principal preventive tool, which is the link the Section F summary draws back to this section."}]},{"n":"E.1","t":"Workplace attitude","b":[{"def":{"t":"Attitude","d":"our opinions, beliefs and feelings about aspects of our environment — a mental state developed through experience, always ready to actively influence one's response to conditions/circumstances (Allport, 1935); a predisposition to feel/think/act toward an object/person/event more or less favourably (Richardson, 1977)."}},{"h4":"E.1.1 – E.1.3  Nature and components of attitudes, and the factors that influence them"},{"p":"6 features of attitudes: they differ in valence (positive/negative) and strength; can concern anything liked/disliked; thought and feeling about the same thing may differ; they represent our \"truth\" about a situation/object; assessing them is common/important at work; and, unlike personality (stable across situations), attitudes are LESS stable and more specific (to a person/object/concept)."},{"key":"The ABC of attitude components: **Affective** (how we feel about things), **Behavioural** (how we're predisposed to behave), and **Cognitive** (what we believe to be true)."}]},{"n":"E.2","t":"Work-related attitudes","b":[{"h4":"E.2.1 – E.2.2  Job satisfaction and organisational commitment"},{"p":"4 factors influencing attitudes: personality; the work environment/situation; our values (intrinsic/extrinsic work values); and social influences (co-workers, groups, culture). Attitudes affect whether someone leaves a job, how cooperative they are, whether they project a positive work image, their change response, motivation, and psychological/physical health."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Work attitude","Description"],"rows":[["**Job satisfaction**","Positive feelings toward one's job — a positive state from appraising the job/job experience, influenced by pay, benefits, co-workers, the work itself, distributive justice, personality/disposition and job characteristics. Possibly THE most important job attitude. Its relationship to performance may run: satisfaction→performance; performance→satisfaction; mutual causation; a third linking variable affecting both; or a moderating relationship (e.g. a pay rise raising both satisfaction and, via that, performance)."],["**Organisational commitment**","The relative strength of an employee's identification with/involvement in the organisation (Mowday, 1979) — the emotional attachment to one's employer. A highly-committed employee accepts/believes in company values, puts in effort toward company goals, and strongly wants to remain. 3 features: desire to stay; belief/acceptance of organisational goals; and willingness to exert effort on the organisation's behalf. 3 forms (Allen & Meyer, 1990): **affective commitment** (personal attachment); **continuance commitment** (the cost/risk of leaving — one's investment in the organisation); and **normative commitment** (the moral dimension — loyalty to the employer)."]]}}]},{"n":"E.3","t":"Attitudes that are important in workplaces","b":[{"p":"5 workplace attitudes that leverage professional skill/experience: respect for others (polite, professional interaction even amid disagreement); infectious enthusiasm about life (positive energy that spreads, a \"glass half full\" outlook); commitment to the job (willingness to do whatever it takes, plus new-idea development); innovative ideas/finding new ways (not shying from new methods); and helpfulness with others (assisting clients/customers/co-workers)."}]},{"n":"F.1","t":"Definition of communication","b":[{"def":{"t":"Communication","d":"the transference and understanding of meaning among individuals — an inseparable part of daily work activity, without which organisations struggle to survive. **Formal communication**: transmitted through management-endorsed channels. **Informal communication**: transmitted through employee-established channels — supplements formal channels and reduces problems from rigid formal \"red tape.\""}}]},{"n":"F.2","t":"The communication process in an organisation","b":[{"p":"The communication process model: a SENDER has a thought, ENCODES it into a message (verbal or non-verbal — body language, written, electronic, picture), transmits it via a CHANNEL (company publications, suggestion boxes, internet, meetings, committees, notice boards, public reports, letters, body language), to a RECEIVER who DECODES it — a FEEDBACK response may follow. Anything interfering with/distorting the transmission (physical or psychological) is NOISE. Communication becomes effective when the sender encodes in a form the receiver understands, using the right channel."},{"p":"6 types of communication: oral/verbal (face-to-face, group discussion, meeting, conference, telephone, radio, intercom); written (letter, report, memo, note, email, books); electronic (email, webcam, fax, YouTube, social media, website); audio-visual (television, radio, PA, walkie-talkie); traditional (writing on cloth/trees/stones, signs/symbols, town criers); and active listening (making sense of what is heard — attention, interpretation, memory)."}]},{"n":"F.3","t":"Types of communication","b":[{"p":"Communication is classified by **direction** — downward, upward, horizontal (or lateral) and diagonal — and by **formality**, into formal communication (following the official channels of the organisation chart) and informal communication (the \"grapevine\"). The forms these take are set out at §F.4."}]},{"n":"F.4","t":"Forms of communication in an organisation","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Communication flow","Description"],"rows":[["**Upward**","Bottom-to-top transmission — suggestion boxes, internet, fax, telephones, meetings, letters, face-to-face, memoranda, grievance procedure, the grapevine."],["**Downward**","Top-to-bottom transmission — the grapevine, face-to-face contact, circulars, notices, telephone, committees, employee handbooks, organisation manuals, house magazines, internet."],["**Horizontal**","Same-level transmission — committee meetings, telephone, face-to-face, circulars, memoranda, fax, internet, closed-circuit TV."],["**Crosswise**","Diagonal flow among different levels/departments with NO direct relationship — does not follow the chain of command."]]}}]},{"n":"F.5","t":"Functions of communication","b":[{"p":"6 functions of communication: giving management data to draw up/implement policies (drawn from customers, suppliers, employees, shareholders, competitors, the public); a means to explain policies to stakeholders; creating job interest/motivation/morale; monitoring human/material resource performance; helping departments/employees coordinate activities; and channelling employee grievances/feelings/ideas/suggestions to resolve conflict and create a peaceful atmosphere."}]},{"n":"F.6","t":"Principles of communication","b":[{"key":"The **7 Cs of communication**: Concreteness (words/examples truly felt/understood, matching real experience); Correctness (the right time/atmosphere for the message); Conciseness (brief, precise, logical); Clarity (non-ambiguous, avoiding jargon/complex expressions); Courtesy (polite, no hurtful words — stay silent if angry); Completeness (complete in form and content, no unanswered questions left dangling); and Consideration (sensitive to listeners' education, sex, age, interests)."}]},{"n":"F.7","t":"Barriers to effective communication","b":[{"p":"10 barriers to effective communication: **semantics** (jargon, unfamiliar/high-sounding words, poor sentence structure — words carrying different meanings to different people); poor listening skills; differences in background (age, social status); conflicts (impeding flow between individuals/groups); emotional problems (frustration, poor perception, hostility, anger, fear, mistrust); communication overload; poor organisational structure (long chains of command, centralised authority, unsuitable span of control); physical layout (isolating employees); lack of planning before communicating; and inappropriate (or absent) communication channels."}]},{"n":"F.8","t":"Summary — Section F","b":[{"p":"This section discussed **communication as a veritable tool for avoiding organisational conflict** — except where constructive conflicts become absolutely inevitable. **Barriers to effective communication**, as well as **guidelines for communication application**, were amplified for the purpose of creating an undiluted understanding of the role of effective communication in organisational success."},{"key":"Note the link the summary draws back to Section D: communication is presented as the principal means of **avoiding conflict** — with the important qualification that some conflict is **constructive and inevitable**, which is the functional view of conflict taken at §D.1."}]},{"n":"G.1","t":"Emotional intelligence","b":[{"def":{"t":"Emotional intelligence","d":"the ability to understand, use and manage one's own emotions positively — to relieve stress, communicate effectively, empathise with others, overcome challenges, and defuse conflict."}}]},{"n":"G.2","t":"Features/elements of emotional intelligence","b":[{"p":"5 elements: self-awareness; self-regulation; motivation; empathy; and social skills."}]},{"n":"G.3","t":"Importance of emotional intelligence at the workplace","b":[{"p":"5 reasons emotional intelligence matters at work: it enables better teamwork; helps deal with change; helps handle tough conversations; is an essential people skill; and is a key feature of a strong leader."}]},{"n":"H.1","t":"Management of change","b":[{"p":"Organisational change can take many forms — work-practice changes, management change, mergers, new accounting software, new hardware/computers/machines, relocation. Well-managed change avoids the resentment/conflict that can otherwise arise (as illustrated in the chapter's own Regina Farms caselet, where new machines/methods raised output but triggered a strike over resulting retrenchment)."}]},{"n":"H.2","t":"Forces of organisational change","b":[{"p":"7 forces driving organisational change: new technology/ways of doing things (forcing HR/marketing/production strategy reappraisal); new management policies/roles/structure; social trend/value/attitude changes; competitor marketing activity (forcing product/pricing/promotion/distribution response); mergers; new work methods/procedures; and a new chief executive/management change."}]},{"n":"H.3","t":"Reasons for resistance to change","b":[{"p":"10 reasons employees resist change: fear of the unknown (apprehension over unclear future changes); lack of information (not properly briefed on implications); fear of unemployment (job-security threat, income loss, skill obsolescence); lack of participation (change imposed without employee input); threat to social/interpersonal relationships; inability/reluctance to change established habits; threat of new-demand challenges; negative past-change experience; threat to expertise; and threat to established resource allocation/power relationships."}]},{"n":"H.4","t":"Managing resistance to change","b":[{"ol":["**Education and communication** — helping employees understand the logic/need for change, providing necessary information;","**Employee involvement** — participation in planning/executing change eases adaptation;","**Detailed pre-change planning** — deciding the change type, financial/human implications, timing and pace before proceeding;","**Motivating acceptance** — incentives and an environment inducing acceptance;","**Adapting the pace to employees** — change faster than employees can absorb builds resistance; gradual change gives time to adjust;","**Timing** — introducing change when employees are more receptive/willing to adapt behaviour;","**Managing scope** — e.g. piloting a new idea in ONE department before extending it, rather than organisation-wide at once;","**Training** — equipping employees with the skills the change requires;","**Negotiation** — exchanging something of value for cooperation with those resisting; and","**Manipulation and co-optation** — favouring/ignoring accordingly, or giving key roles to resisting group leaders so they drop their resistance (given as the source's own description, though noted as a more ethically fraught technique compared to the others listed)."]}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Performance (Maier & Lawler)","tex":"\\text{Performance} = f(\\text{Ability} \\times \\text{Motivation})"},{"lb":"Vroom's expectancy motivation","tex":"\\text{Motivation} = \\text{Valence} \\times \\text{Expectancy}"}],"focus":"This chapter rewards precise attribution of each theory to its originator and correct recall of exact factor counts: Maslow's 5-level hierarchy vs. Alderfer's 3-level ERG (and knowing ERG is a continuum, NOT a strict hierarchy — its key innovation is the frustration-regression hypothesis Maslow lacks); Herzberg's hygiene-vs-motivator split (know which of the 10 hygiene factors and 6 motivators is which — money is ALWAYS a hygiene factor, a common trick); Vroom's Motivation = Valence × Expectancy formula, including the worked Kofi/Ernest numbers; and the 3 leadership styles mapped to their Theory X/Y correspondence. The 5-stage group development sequence (forming-storming-norming-performing-adjourning) is a frequently tested ordered list.","errors":["Treating Herzberg's hygiene factors as capable of creating POSITIVE motivation on their own — hygiene factors, when properly addressed, only bring motivation to ZERO (preventing dissatisfaction); only the separate motivator factors (achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself, growth, advancement) create genuine positive motivation.","Assuming Alderfer's ERG theory is simply \"Maslow with fewer levels\" — the substantive difference is that ERG treats needs as a CONTINUUM (multiple needs active simultaneously) rather than a strict hierarchy, and introduces the frustration-regression hypothesis (regressing to a lower need when blocked at a higher one), which Maslow's theory does not include.","Confusing job satisfaction with organisational commitment as synonyms — job satisfaction is a feeling ABOUT the job itself (pay, work, co-workers); organisational commitment is a broader emotional attachment to the EMPLOYER, with its own 3 distinct forms (affective, continuance, normative) that job satisfaction does not have.","Assuming the autocratic and laissez-faire leadership styles are opposite ends of one single spectrum with democratic simply \"in the middle\" — while intuitive, the source presents all 3 as genuinely distinct approaches (power-with-manager, power-with-group, and abdicated-power/non-leadership respectively), not points on one linear scale."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The elements of Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs include the following EXCEPT","o":["Safety needs","Self-esteem needs","Self-actualisation needs","Clinical needs","Physiological needs"],"a":3,"w":"Maslow's 5 need levels are physiological, safety, social, esteem and self-actualisation — \"clinical needs\" is not one of them.","src":"Chapter 5, Section A Review Questions","sec":"A.2"},{"q":"According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which of the following is a HYGIENE (maintenance) factor rather than a motivator?","o":["Achievement","Recognition","Salary","Responsibility","Advancement"],"a":2,"w":"Salary is one of Herzberg's 10 hygiene factors — its absence causes dissatisfaction, but its presence does not by itself create positive motivation. Achievement, recognition, responsibility and advancement are all motivator factors.","src":"Chapter 5.A.2","sec":"A.2"},{"q":"In Vroom's Expectancy Theory, if an employee has a valence of +1 for a reward and an expectancy of 0.7 that their effort will produce it, what is their level of motivation?","o":["0","0.3","0.7","1.0","1.7"],"a":2,"w":"Motivation = Valence × Expectancy = 1 × 0.7 = 0.7 — following the same calculation method demonstrated in the chapter's own Kofi/Ernest illustration.","src":"Chapter 5.A.3","sec":"A.3"},{"q":"Which leadership style is described as leading \"through non-leadership,\" abdicating the authority and responsibility of the position?","o":["Autocratic","Democratic","Laissez-faire","Participative","Bureaucratic"],"a":2,"w":"The laissez-faire style sets no group goals, allows total freedom, does not communicate with the group, and leaves goal achievement to chance — leading through non-leadership.","src":"Chapter 5.B.2","sec":"B.2"},{"q":"In the 5-stage process of group/team development, during which stage do members typically challenge the leader and each other over group goals, roles and procedures?","o":["Forming","Storming","Norming","Performing","Adjourning"],"a":1,"w":"The storming stage is characterised by conflict as members seek to free themselves from dependence on the leader, challenging goals/roles/procedures.","src":"Chapter 5.C.2","sec":"C.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between the affective, continuance and normative forms of organisational commitment (Allen and Meyer, 1990).","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Affective commitment**: a PERSONAL, emotional attachment to the organisation — the employee stays because they WANT to."},{"p":"**Continuance commitment**: based on the perceived COSTS and RISKS of leaving — one's accumulated investment in the organisation (pension, seniority, sunk career capital) — the employee stays because they feel they NEED to."},{"p":"**Normative commitment**: a MORAL dimension — a sense of loyalty and obligation to the employer — the employee stays because they feel they OUGHT to."}],"src":"Chapter 5.E.1","sec":"E.1"},{"q":"Explain any 4 reasons why employees typically resist organisational change, and state one technique management can use to reduce resistance for each reason identified.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Any 4 of: **fear of the unknown** (apprehension over unclear future change) — countered by education/communication, explaining the logic and providing information; **lack of information** (employees not properly briefed) — countered similarly by clear communication; **fear of unemployment** (job-security threat) — countered by involving employees in planning and motivating acceptance through incentives; **lack of participation** (change imposed top-down) — countered directly by involving employees in planning/executing the change; **inability/reluctance to change established habits** — countered by training and adapting the pace of change to what employees can absorb; and **negative experience with past changes** — countered by careful pre-change planning and appropriate timing, rebuilding trust in the change process itself."}],"src":"Chapter 5.H.1-H.2","sec":"H.2"}]}},{"n":6,"t":"Business Process Re-engineering","brief":"A short, focused chapter on Business Process Re-engineering (BPR) — the radical, IT-enabled redesign of core business processes to achieve dramatic improvements in cost, quality, service and speed. Covers what BPR is, the factors driving its adoption, its objectives, its 5-stage implementation process, and its benefits and limitations.","outcomes":["Explain business process re-engineering (BPR)","State the objectives of BPR","Explain how to implement BPR","State and explain the benefits of BPR","State and explain the limitations of BPR"],"secs":[{"n":"6.1","t":"Business process re-engineering (BPR)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Business Process Re-engineering (BPR)","d":"the process of recreating a core business process, aiming to improve output quality or reduce cost through introducing information technology — seeking to radically restructure the organisation via ground-up redesign of its business processes. It means changing the process FUNDAMENTALLY, not incrementally. Also called business process redesign, business transformation, or business process change management."}},{"p":"BPR involves the radical redesign of core business processes to achieve DRAMATIC improvements in cost, quality, service and speed, delivering more value to the customer — the entire technological, human and organisational dimensions may change. Information technology plays a major role, enabling office automation, multi-location branch operation, flexible manufacturing, quicker customer delivery, better online customer service, and paperless transactions."},{"key":"BPR generally involves: discovering how business processes currently operate; redesigning them to eliminate wasted/redundant effort and improve efficiency; and implementing the changes to gain competitiveness. Per Sherwood-Smith (1994), BPR's aim is \"seeking to devise new ways of organising tasks, organising people and redesigning IT systems so that the processes support the organisation to realise its goals.\""},{"h4":"10 factors influencing the need to adopt BPR"},{"ol":["poor performance of the old process;","obsolescence of the old process;","breakdown of existing machines/equipment;","threat from competitor performance;","changing trends in customer expectations;","redeployment of insufficient resources;","securing better management;","improving efficiency;","reducing cost; and","reducing waste."]},{"h4":"15 areas commonly adopting BPR"},{"p":"E-learning; e-marketing; e-payment; ATM (Automatic Teller Machine) processes; manufacturing process; virtual meetings; payroll process; claims processes; marketing and sales process; inventory control; customer complaints; employee appraisal; HR recruitment; contract bidding/approval; and online office processes."}]},{"n":"6.2","t":"Objectives of business process re-engineering","b":[{"p":"When BPR is applied, implementation effort focuses on 7 objectives:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Objective","Description"],"rows":[["**Customer focus**","Customer-service-oriented processes aimed at eliminating customer complaints."],["**Speed**","The need to deliver products/services faster."],["**Compression**","Cutting down prolonged processes to cut cost and reduce waste in the value chain."],["**Flexibility**","Changing conditions/competitor activity forcing organisations to adapt new processes/structures."],["**Quality**","Delivering up-to-date product/service quality — an overarching target for visionary organisations."],["**Innovation**","New processes creating a form of competitive advantage."],["**Productivity**","BPR drastically improves effectiveness/efficiency indices in the organisation."]]}}]},{"n":"6.3","t":"Implementation of business process re-engineering","b":[{"p":"BPR is implemented in STAGES, given the drastic/dramatic changes it brings:"},{"steps":["Envision stage — the company reviews existing strategy/business processes, targets processes for improvement, and identifies IT opportunities.","Diagnosis stage — documenting processes/sub-processes in terms of their attributes (activities, resources, communication, roles, IT, cost).","Redesign stage — developing a new process design via process-design alternatives, using brainstorming/creativity techniques.","Reconstruction stage — management-technique changes to ensure smooth migration to the new process responsibilities and human-resource roles.","Evaluation stage — monitoring the new process to determine whether goals are met, and examining total quality programmes."]}]},{"n":"6.4","t":"Benefits of implementing BPR","b":[{"ol":["gives business an appropriate customer-needs-revolving focus;","helps build a strategic view of operational procedures via radical inquiries into how processes can improve;","eliminates unnecessary activities, reducing organisational complexity;","coordinates and integrates several functions immediately;","improves organisational viability/adequacy by eliminating delay and unessential operational/management phases;","reduces the number of checks/controls and reconciliation processes; and","helps overcome short-sighted approaches from excessive functional-boundary concentration."]}]},{"n":"6.5","t":"Limitations of implementing BPR","b":[{"ol":["employee resistance to change, viewing BPR as a job threat;","unsuitability for every business need — depends on size/resource availability, usually benefiting LARGE organisations most;","one department's efficiency may improve at the expense of the OVERALL process;","no immediate resolution — BPR concentrates on long-haul income collaborations that take time to take shape and are hard to measure/gauge;","may require substantial IT investment plus proper planning, strong teamwork, and exceptional implementation;","can replace humans in getting the job done, posing a real job threat;","lack of time to focus on improving the business process;","lack of recognition of the underlying problem;","lack of expertise to implement the new process; and","lack of resources/tools to fix problems."]}]},{"n":"6.6","t":"Summary","b":[{"p":"In this chapter we have explored the meaning of **business process re-engineering** as the process of **recreating a core business process** with the goal of **improving output quality or reducing cost through the introduction of information technology**."},{"p":"**Factors that influence the need for adoption of BPR** in an organisation include **poor performance of the old process**, **obsolescence of the old process**, **breakdown of existing machines and equipment**, and **threat from competitors' performance**."},{"p":"The **objectives** of adopting BPR include **customer focus**, **speed**, **compression of the process** to make it shorter and quicker, **flexibility**, **quality service delivery**, **innovation and creativity** to give competitive advantage, and **productivity**."},{"p":"BPR involves changes in **structure and processes** within the business organisation through automation. Areas in which an organisation can adopt BPR include **e-learning, e-marketing, e-payment, the automatic teller machine, the manufacturing process, virtual meetings, the payroll process and claims processes**."},{"p":"Implementing BPR can be a challenging task, as it needs to proceed through defined phases for it to be successful. The stages are the **envision stage, diagnosis stage, redesign stage, reconstruction stage and evaluation stage**."},{"p":"The **benefits** of implementing BPR are strategic to any organisation, as it makes the organisation competitive in the marketplace amid increasing changes in customer demands and specifications, and threats from competitors. **BPR integrates all the functional processes and compresses them**, so that service delivery can be improved and customers retained."},{"p":"BPR implementation does come with some **limitations** — such as employees' resistance to change, as they consider BPR a threat to their jobs; it may not be suitable for all work processes; it does not give an immediate solution to the problem, as its expected output is in the long term; and it requires sustained investment over time."}]},{"n":"6.7","t":"Review questions, with the study text's answers","b":[{"p":"The chapter closes with **seven multiple choice questions** and **five short answer questions**, with the study text's own printed answers."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review questions","t":"Multiple choice and short answer questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"Multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["Which of the following is **not** true of Business Process Re-engineering (BPR)? **A.** Sometimes BPR is needed to lower costs; **B.** Sometimes BPR is needed to increase quality; **C.** Information technology can be the enabler for radical change; **D.** BPR tends to focus on incremental and gradual improvement; **E.** None of the above.","……… helps in improving performance by learning from the best practices and the processes by which they are achieved. **A.** Business Process Re-engineering; **B.** Strategic Change; **C.** Benchmarking; **D.** Strategic Leadership; **E.** Strategic evaluation.","……… are the desired end results of the redesign process which the management and organisation attempt to realise. **A.** Objectives; **B.** Mission; **C.** Goals; **D.** Vision; **E.** Tactics.","BPR is also called any of the following **EXCEPT** **A.** Business process redesign; **B.** Business transformation; **C.** Business process change management; **D.** Business process management; **E.** None of the above.","One of the following is **NOT** a factor that influences the need for adoption of BPR: **A.** Best performance of the old process; **B.** Obsolescence of the old process; **C.** Breakdown of existing machines and equipment; **D.** Threat from competitors' performance; **E.** Changing trends of customer expectations.","One of these is **NOT** a stage in the implementation of BPR: **A.** Evaluation stage; **B.** Diagnosis stage; **C.** Redesign stage; **D.** Envision stage; **E.** Process stage.","Which of these is **NOT** true of the aim of BPR? **A.** Seeking to devise new ways of organising tasks; **B.** Organising people and resources; **C.** Redesigning IT systems; **D.** Conducting an environmental scan; **E.** None of the above."]},{"h4":"Short answer questions"},{"ol":["Business process re-engineering is the process of ……… a core business process with the goal of improving output quality or reducing cost through the introduction of ………","The stage in the BPR process in which documentation of processes and sub-processes takes place, in terms of process attributes, is called ………","BPR revolves around customer need and focus. **TRUE/FALSE**","The stage at which the new process is monitored to determine if goals are met, and total quality programmes are examined, is called ………","The end beneficiaries of BPR implementation are the ………"]}],"a":[{"h4":"Answers — multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cll","head":["Q","Answer","Why"],"rows":[["1","**D**","BPR is **radical and fundamental**, not incremental and gradual — that is the defining feature at §6.1, and incremental improvement is what BPR is contrasted with."],["2","**C** — Benchmarking","Learning from best practice elsewhere is benchmarking. BPR redesigns a process from the ground up rather than copying another organisation's."],["3","**A** — Objectives","The desired end results of the redesign process, set out at §6.2."],["4","**D** — Business process management","BPR is also called business process **redesign**, business **transformation** and business process **change management** (§6.1). \"Business process management\" is a different, ongoing discipline."],["5","**A** — Best performance of the old process","It is **poor** performance of the old process that drives the need for BPR; good performance removes the need for it."],["6","**E** — Process stage","The five stages are **envision, diagnosis, redesign, reconstruction and evaluation** (§6.3). There is no \"process stage\"."],["7","**D** — Conducting an environmental scan","Environmental scanning is a strategic-planning activity (Chapter 1, §B.4), not an aim of BPR. BPR aims at devising new ways of organising tasks, organising people and resources, and redesigning IT systems."]]}},{"h4":"Answers — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Recreating / redesigning**; **information technology**","**Diagnosis stage**","**TRUE**","**Evaluation stage**","**Customers**"]},{"key":"Questions 2 and 4 of the short answer bank test the **five stages** from both ends, and they are the most reliably examinable thing in this short chapter: **envision** (identify the need and the opportunity), **diagnosis** (document the existing processes and sub-processes in terms of their attributes), **redesign** (develop the new process using brainstorming and creativity techniques), **reconstruction** (manage the migration to new responsibilities and roles), and **evaluation** (monitor whether goals are met, and examine total quality programmes)."},{"note":"Short answer 5's answer — **customers** — is the thread running through the whole chapter. BPR's first objective is customer focus (§6.2), its purpose is to deliver **more value to the customer** (§6.1), and its benefit is that service delivery improves and **customers are retained** (§6.4). Any answer about BPR that does not mention the customer has missed its point."},{"warn":"MCQ 1 and MCQ 7 both offer **\"None of the above\"** as option E alongside four substantive options, and in both cases the answer is one of the four — D. Where a \"none of the above\" option appears, check each substantive option against the text first; it is almost always a distractor here rather than the answer."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"A short, high-yield chapter — know that BPR means FUNDAMENTAL, not incremental, change (a classic exam distinction), the exact 5-stage implementation sequence in order (envision→diagnosis→redesign→reconstruction→evaluation), and the 7 named objectives (customer focus/speed/compression/flexibility/quality/innovation/productivity). The benefits-vs-limitations lists are also easy to test against each other since some limitations directly mirror stated benefits (e.g. it can reduce organisational complexity in one department while creating imbalance elsewhere).","errors":["Describing BPR as focused on incremental, gradual process improvement — this is precisely WRONG; BPR is defined by RADICAL, fundamental redesign, not incremental improvement (the source's own MCQ 1 tests exactly this confusion).","Assuming BPR benefits every organisation equally regardless of size — the source explicitly notes BPR usually benefits LARGE organisations most, given the resource investment (IT, planning, teamwork) required.","Treating the \"envision\" and \"diagnosis\" stages as interchangeable — envision reviews existing STRATEGY and identifies IT opportunities; diagnosis specifically DOCUMENTS existing processes/sub-processes by their attributes (activities, resources, communication, roles, IT, cost) — a distinct, more granular step that comes after."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT true of Business Process Re-engineering (BPR)?","o":["Sometimes BPR is needed to lower costs","Sometimes BPR is needed to increase quality","Information technology can be the enabler for radical change","BPR tends to focus on incremental and gradual improvement","None of the above"],"a":3,"w":"BPR is defined by RADICAL, fundamental redesign of core business processes — NOT incremental/gradual improvement, which is precisely what BPR is meant to transcend.","src":"Chapter 6 Review Questions","sec":"6.1"},{"q":"BPR is also called any of the following EXCEPT","o":["Business process redesign","Business transformation","Business process change management","Business process management","None of the above"],"a":3,"w":"BPR is also called business process redesign, business transformation, or business process change management — \"business process management\" (a distinct, broader discipline) is not one of BPR's alternate names.","src":"Chapter 6 Review Questions","sec":"6.1"},{"q":"One of the following is NOT a factor that influences the need for adoption of BPR:","o":["Best performance of the old process","Obsolescence of the old process","Break down of existing machines and equipment","Threat from competitors' performance","Changing trends of customer expectations"],"a":0,"w":"\"Best performance of the old process\" is the opposite of a BPR driver — it is POOR performance of the old process (not best performance) that drives BPR adoption.","src":"Chapter 6 Review Questions","sec":"6.1"},{"q":"One of these is NOT a stage in the implementation of BPR:","o":["Evaluation stage","Diagnosis stage","Redesign stage","Envision stage","Process stage"],"a":4,"w":"The 5 BPR implementation stages are envision, diagnosis, redesign, reconstruction, and evaluation — \"process stage\" is not one of them.","src":"Chapter 6 Review Questions","sec":"6.3"},{"q":"The stage in the BPR process in which documentation of processes and sub-processes takes place in terms of process attributes (activities, resources, communication, roles, IT and cost) is called:","o":["Envision stage","Diagnosis stage","Redesign stage","Reconstruction stage","Evaluation stage"],"a":1,"w":"The diagnosis stage specifically documents processes and sub-processes in terms of their attributes — activities, resources, communication, roles, IT and cost.","src":"Chapter 6 Review Questions","sec":"6.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the 5 stages of implementing Business Process Re-engineering, in the correct order.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"The 5 stages, in order: (1) **Envision stage** — the company reviews its existing strategy and business processes, targets processes for improvement, and identifies IT opportunities; (2) **Diagnosis stage** — documenting processes/sub-processes in terms of their attributes (activities, resources, communication, roles, IT, cost); (3) **Redesign stage** — developing a new process design via process-design alternatives, using brainstorming/creativity techniques; (4) **Reconstruction stage** — management-technique changes ensuring smooth migration to the new process responsibilities and human-resource roles; and (5) **Evaluation stage** — monitoring the new process to determine whether goals are met, and examining total quality programmes."}],"src":"Chapter 6.3","sec":"6.3"},{"q":"State and briefly explain any 4 limitations of implementing Business Process Re-engineering.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Any 4 of: **employee resistance to change** (BPR viewed as a job threat); **unsuitability for every business** (depends on size/resource availability, usually benefiting large organisations most); **local optimisation at the overall process's expense** (one department's efficiency improving while harming the process as a whole); **no immediate resolution** (BPR concentrates on long-haul benefits that take time to materialise and are hard to measure); **substantial required investment** (IT investment, proper planning, strong teamwork, exceptional implementation); **potential job displacement** (BPR can replace humans in getting work done); and **implementation gaps** (lack of time, problem recognition, expertise, or resources/tools to fix problems)."}],"src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"}]}},{"n":7,"t":"Organisational Effectiveness","brief":"A short chapter on what it means for an organisation to be effective — the degree to which it realises its goals within its environment. Covers the definition, the seven key components of organisational effectiveness, the eight factors that influence it, and the seven strategies for improving it, together with the criteria by which effectiveness is measured and Rensis Likert's three classes of determinant.","outcomes":["Define organisational effectiveness","Explain the key components of organisational effectiveness","Explain the factors influencing organisational effectiveness","Explain the strategies for improving organisational effectiveness"],"secs":[{"n":"7.1","t":"Definition of organisational effectiveness","b":[{"def":{"t":"Organisational effectiveness","d":"the degree to which an organisation realises its goals."}},{"p":"It can be noted that this goal can only be realised effectively by **(a)** information sharing, **(b)** quick and timely decision making, and **(c)** shared awareness of tasks and responsibilities within the organisation."},{"key":"**Effectiveness is the ability of the organisation to exploit the environment in the acquisition of critical resources.**"},{"note":"Hold the two halves of the idea together, because the exam tests them separately. Looking **inward**, effectiveness is about **realising goals** — which is why it is measured against goal attainment and why §7.4 begins with defining the vision and goals clearly. Looking **outward**, it is about **exploiting the environment to acquire critical resources** — which is why the external environment appears among the influencing factors at §7.3 and among the measurement criteria at §7.5."},{"warn":"Do not confuse **effectiveness** with **efficiency**. Effectiveness asks whether the right goals are being achieved; efficiency asks whether resources are being used economically in achieving them. The distinction is drawn fully at §2.10 (measures of managerial performance: productivity, effectiveness and efficiency), and both appear here — efficiency is one *component* of effectiveness (§7.2(5)) and one *criterion* for measuring it (§7.5), not a synonym for it."}]},{"n":"7.2","t":"Components of organisational effectiveness","b":[{"p":"The key components of organisational effectiveness are:"},{"ol":["**Communication** — there should be clear and open communication that breeds strong alignment, collaboration and informed decision making.","**Strong leadership** — the leadership should be visionary, able to motivate employees and activate performance.","**Goal alignment** — organisational goals must align with individual and team objectives so as to drive focused efforts.","**Innovativeness and adaptability** — the team must be innovative and adaptive, to imbibe changes that sustain competitiveness.","**Efficient operation** — processes must be efficiently streamlined and resources optimally utilised, to ensure improved output and efficient waste control.","**Ensure customer satisfaction** — endear your customers by exceeding their expectations. This will enhance customer loyalty and market success.","**Performance measurement** — assess critical performance areas, so as to track progress and identify possible improvement areas."]},{"key":"The seven components divide cleanly into three groups, and grouping them this way reads better in an answer than a flat list. **People and direction** — communication, strong leadership, goal alignment. **Operations** — efficient operation, innovativeness and adaptability. **Outcomes and feedback** — customer satisfaction and performance measurement. The last of these closes the loop: without measurement, none of the other six can be shown to be working."},{"note":"Note that **communication** appears twice in this chapter — as a *component* of effectiveness here, and as a *factor influencing* it at §7.3(6). That is not a duplication in the book so much as a reflection of its double role: it is part of what effectiveness consists of, and also one of the conditions that produce it."}]},{"n":"7.3","t":"Factors influencing organisational effectiveness","b":[{"ol":["**Leadership style** — the leadership style must be effective, to inspire and influence high performance.","**Organisational culture** — the culture cultivated should drive individual and team effectiveness and efficiency.","**Organisational structure** — the structure should engender an appropriate balance of authority and responsibility.","**Technology** — there must be deployment of the appropriate technology to drive operations.","**Human resources** — human resource is a critical element in organisational effectiveness. The right skills and hands must be engaged to drive it.","**Communication** — the organisation must ensure effective communication both within and outside the organisation.","**External organisational elements** — changes in the external environment can influence organisational effectiveness.","**Change management** — the ability of the organisation to manage internal transitions smoothly has implications for its stability and its effectiveness."]},{"key":"Seven of the eight factors are **internal** and within management's control — leadership style, culture, structure, technology, human resources, communication and change management. Only one, **external organisational elements**, is not. That asymmetry is the point: effectiveness is overwhelmingly something an organisation does to itself, which is why §7.4 can offer strategies for improving it at all."},{"note":"Contrast this list with §7.2. The **components** are what effectiveness *looks like* when you observe an organisation; the **factors** are what *causes* it. An examiner asking for \"factors influencing\" wants causes — leadership style, culture, structure — not the observable features, and vice versa. Read the requirement carefully, because the two lists overlap on communication and are otherwise different."}]},{"n":"7.4","t":"Strategies for improving organisational effectiveness","b":[{"steps":["**Clear definition of vision and goals** — the organisation should define its vision and goals clearly, so as to align all efforts towards a common outcome.","**Effective and efficient communication** — ensure open and transparent two-way communication, to fertilise efficient coordination and decision making.","**Engage employees in the planning process** — employee involvement in planning will foster understanding and motivation to help achieve the plan.","**A culture of continuous training and development** — the dividend on investment in training and development is performance improvement, which translates to higher productivity.","**Create objective performance metrics** — set measurable targets, where objective performance can easily be noted and recognised, to drive performance.","**Support a creative, innovative and adaptive work environment** — this will activate a culture of flexibility and innovation to respond to changes in the market place.","**Optimise resource utilisation** — every organisational resource should be judiciously and efficiently utilised."]},{"key":"Each strategy answers one of the components at §7.2, which is why the two lists are almost the same length. Defining vision and goals delivers **goal alignment**; two-way communication delivers **communication**; objective metrics deliver **performance measurement**; the creative environment delivers **innovativeness and adaptability**; and optimising resources delivers **efficient operation**. If asked for strategies and you can only remember the components, work across from one list to the other."},{"note":"Two of the seven are worth singling out as the ones candidates omit. **Engaging employees in the planning process** is a participation strategy — it works because involvement produces commitment, not merely because it produces better plans. And **continuous training and development** is framed by the book as an *investment* whose dividend is measurable productivity, which is the argument to make if a question asks you to justify training expenditure to a sceptical board."}]},{"n":"7.5","t":"Summary","b":[{"p":"Organisational effectiveness is the **degree to which an organisation realises its goals within its given environment**."},{"p":"**Criteria for measuring organisational effectiveness** include: adaptation to the external environment; integration; sense of identity; productivity; goal attainment; cohesion; efficiency; political effectiveness; survival; and control over the environment."},{"p":"**Components of organisational performance** include communication, strong leadership, goal alignment, innovativeness and adaptability to changes in the environment, efficient operation, customer satisfaction, and performance measurement to check for improvement indices."},{"p":"**Factors that influence organisational effectiveness** include leadership style, organisational culture, organisational structure, deployment of appropriate technology, human resources development, effective communication, external organisational elements in the environment, and adaptability to change."},{"p":"**Strategies for improving organisational effectiveness** include having clearly defined vision and goals, effective and efficient communication, engaging employees in the planning process, imbibing the culture of continuous training and development, creating objective performance metrics, supporting a creative, innovative and adaptive work environment, and optimising resource utilisation."},{"warn":"The **criteria for measuring organisational effectiveness** appear in the study text **only here, in its chapter summary** — the body of the chapter (§7.1 to §7.4) never sets them out, even though Review Question 2 asks for them. They are reproduced in full above rather than left in the summary alone, because they are examinable and are not obtainable from any other section. Ten criteria are listed: **adaptation to the external environment, integration, sense of identity, productivity, goal attainment, cohesion, efficiency, political effectiveness, survival, and control over the environment**."},{"note":"Note how the ten criteria pair up with the definition at §7.1. **Goal attainment, productivity, efficiency, integration, cohesion and sense of identity** measure the *inward* half — realising goals. **Adaptation to the external environment, control over the environment, political effectiveness and survival** measure the *outward* half — exploiting the environment to acquire critical resources. Survival is the ultimate criterion: an organisation that does not survive has, by definition, failed to secure the resources it needed."}]},{"n":"7.6","t":"Review questions and short answer questions, with the study text's answers","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with **four review questions** and **three short answer questions**, the latter with its own printed answers. All are reproduced here, with the answers to the review questions supplied from the chapter's own material."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review question 1","t":"What is organisational effectiveness?","open":true,"q":[{"p":"What is organisational effectiveness?"}],"a":[{"p":"Organisational effectiveness describes the **degree to which an organisation realises its goals**. That goal can only be realised effectively by **(a)** information sharing, **(b)** quick and timely decision making, and **(c)** shared awareness of tasks and responsibilities within the organisation. Effectiveness is also the **ability of the organisation to exploit the environment in the acquisition of critical resources**."},{"note":"Give both limbs. A one-line answer naming only goal realisation misses the environmental limb, which is the half the short answer questions below go on to test. *(Source: §7.1.)*"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review question 2","t":"The components that can be used to measure organisational effectiveness","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Highlight the components that can be used to measure organisational effectiveness."}],"a":[{"p":"Taking the chapter's **components** (§7.2) and the **criteria for measurement** given in its summary (§7.5) together:"},{"h4":"Components of organisational effectiveness (§7.2)"},{"ul":["communication;","strong leadership;","goal alignment;","innovativeness and adaptability;","efficient operation;","customer satisfaction; and","performance measurement."]},{"h4":"Criteria for measuring organisational effectiveness (§7.5)"},{"ul":["adaptation to the external environment;","integration;","sense of identity;","productivity;","goal attainment;","cohesion;","efficiency;","political effectiveness;","survival; and","control over the environment."]},{"warn":"The question asks what can be used **to measure** effectiveness, so the criteria list is the stricter answer — and it appears **only in the book's summary**, not in the body of the chapter (see the warning at §7.5). Give the criteria, and add the seven components, since the book's own summary uses the word \"components\" for both sets."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review question 3","t":"Rensis Likert's determinants of organisational effectiveness","open":true,"q":[{"p":"What, according to **Rensis Likert**, are the determinants of organisational effectiveness?"}],"a":[{"warn":"This question asks for material the study text **never covers**. Rensis Likert is not mentioned anywhere in this chapter — not in §7.1 to §7.4, and not in the summary. The answer below is supplied so that the question is not left unanswerable, and is flagged as an addition rather than a transcription."},{"p":"Likert analysed organisational effectiveness in terms of **three classes of variable**, which stand in a causal chain:"},{"ol":["**Causal variables** — the factors management can alter directly: organisational structure, leadership style, policies, controls, and the skills and behaviour of managers;","**Intervening variables** — the internal state of the human organisation that the causal variables act upon: employees' motivation, loyalty, attitudes, perceptions, commitment to goals, and the capacity for communication, decision making and co-ordinated action; and","**End-result variables** — the dependent outcomes by which the organisation is judged: productivity, costs, quality, earnings, growth, and employee turnover and absenteeism."]},{"key":"The point of Likert's scheme is the **time lag** between the three. Changing a causal variable (tightening controls, say) shifts the end-result variables quickly but may damage the intervening variables, so short-term gains in productivity can be bought at the cost of motivation and loyalty — and the end-result variables then deteriorate later. Measuring only end results therefore misleads management about whether its organisation is becoming more or less effective."},{"note":"Likert's four systems of management — **exploitative authoritative, benevolent authoritative, consultative and participative** (System 4, which he regarded as the most effective) — are the related part of his work, and belong with the leadership material of Chapter 5 rather than here."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review question 4","t":"Five strategies to improve organisational effectiveness","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Give and explain any **five (5)** strategies to improve organisational effectiveness."}],"a":[{"p":"Any five of the seven at §7.4:"},{"ol":["**Clear definition of vision and goals** — so as to align all efforts towards a common outcome.","**Effective and efficient communication** — open, transparent and two-way, to support coordination and decision making.","**Engage employees in the planning process** — involvement fosters the understanding and motivation needed to achieve the plan.","**A culture of continuous training and development** — the return on which is performance improvement and higher productivity.","**Create objective performance metrics** — measurable targets against which performance can be recognised and driven.","**Support a creative, innovative and adaptive work environment** — activating flexibility and innovation in response to market change.","**Optimise resource utilisation** — every organisational resource judiciously and efficiently used."]},{"note":"The requirement is **five**, and it says \"**give and explain**\" — so each of the five needs a sentence of explanation, not just a name. Five explained points will score better than all seven merely listed."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Short answer questions","t":"Short answer questions, with the study text's own answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Effectiveness is the ability of the organisation to exploit the ……… in the acquisition of critical resources.","Organisational effectiveness describes the degree to which an organisation realises its ………","Organisations bring resources in from the environment, and those resources are transformed into ……… delivered back into the environment."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Answers to the short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Environment**","**Goals**","**Output**"]},{"key":"The three answers trace the **systems view** of an organisation in sequence: it draws **inputs** (critical resources) from the **environment**, transforms them, and delivers **output** back into that environment — and its effectiveness is the degree to which doing so realises its **goals**. That is the input-transformation-output cycle of systems theory at §2.19.2, applied to effectiveness."},{"warn":"Question 3's premise — that organisations bring resources in from the environment and transform them into output delivered back to it — is **not stated anywhere in this chapter**, although it is the systems model covered in Chapter 2. It is a second instance of the chapter's review questions reaching beyond its own text."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This is one of the shortest chapters in the subject and one of the easiest to under-prepare because of it. The examinable content is four enumerated lists — 7 components, 8 influencing factors, 7 improvement strategies, and the 10 measurement criteria buried in the chapter summary — so the whole chapter is a memorisation exercise with one conceptual distinction running through it: effectiveness (realising the right goals, and securing resources from the environment) is not efficiency (using resources economically). Learn the lists in their groups rather than flat: components divide into people/direction, operations, and outcomes/feedback; influencing factors are 7 internal and 1 external; and each improvement strategy maps onto a component. Note especially that the measurement criteria and Rensis Likert's determinants are both examined by the chapter's own review questions but are absent from its body — the criteria appear only in the summary, and Likert nowhere at all.","errors":["Treating effectiveness and efficiency as the same thing. Effectiveness is the degree to which goals are realised (and resources secured from the environment); efficiency is the economical use of resources in doing so. Efficiency is one component of effectiveness and one criterion for measuring it — not a synonym.","Answering a \"factors influencing\" question with the components list, or vice versa. The components are what effectiveness consists of as observed; the factors are what causes it. They overlap only on communication.","Omitting the measurement criteria because they do not appear in the body of the chapter — they are in the chapter summary (adaptation to the external environment, integration, sense of identity, productivity, goal attainment, cohesion, efficiency, political effectiveness, survival, control over the environment) and Review Question 2 asks for them directly.","Giving only the goal-realisation half of the definition. The chapter states twice that effectiveness is also the ability to exploit the environment in acquiring critical resources, and the short answer questions test precisely that half.","Listing all seven improvement strategies without explaining any, when the requirement says \"give and explain any five\". Marks follow the explanations."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Organisational effectiveness is best described as","o":["the economical use of an organisation's resources","the degree to which an organisation realises its goals","the ratio of output to input in a production process","the organisation's total market share in its industry","the number of employees an organisation is able to retain"],"a":1,"w":"Effectiveness is the degree to which an organisation realises its goals — and, equally, its ability to exploit the environment in acquiring critical resources. The economical use of resources (option A) and the output-to-input ratio (option C) are efficiency and productivity respectively.","src":"Chapter 7.1","sec":"7.1"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the study text as a component of organisational effectiveness?","o":["Communication","Goal alignment","Performance measurement","Organisational structure","Customer satisfaction"],"a":3,"w":"Organisational structure is one of the eight **factors influencing** effectiveness (§7.3), not one of the seven **components** of it (§7.2). The components are communication, strong leadership, goal alignment, innovativeness and adaptability, efficient operation, customer satisfaction, and performance measurement.","src":"Chapter 7.2","sec":"7.2"},{"q":"Of the eight factors influencing organisational effectiveness, how many are internal to the organisation?","o":["Four","Five","Six","Seven","All eight"],"a":3,"w":"Seven are internal and within management's control — leadership style, organisational culture, organisational structure, technology, human resources, communication and change management. Only **external organisational elements** lies outside the organisation.","src":"Chapter 7.3","sec":"7.3"},{"q":"Effectiveness is the ability of the organisation to exploit the ……… in the acquisition of critical resources.","o":["market","environment","workforce","technology","capital structure"],"a":1,"w":"The study text's own short answer question gives the answer as **environment** — the outward-looking half of the definition of effectiveness.","src":"Chapter 7.1","sec":"7.1"},{"q":"Which of the following criteria for measuring organisational effectiveness appears in the study text's chapter summary but nowhere in the body of the chapter?","o":["Goal alignment","Customer satisfaction","Political effectiveness","Performance measurement","Efficient operation"],"a":2,"w":"Political effectiveness is one of the ten measurement criteria given only in the chapter summary (§7.5), alongside adaptation to the external environment, integration, sense of identity, productivity, goal attainment, cohesion, efficiency, survival and control over the environment. The other four options are components from §7.2.","src":"Chapter 7.5","sec":"7.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"Define organisational effectiveness and state the criteria by which it may be measured.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"Organisational effectiveness is the **degree to which an organisation realises its goals** within its given environment. The goal can only be realised effectively through information sharing, quick and timely decision making, and shared awareness of tasks and responsibilities. Effectiveness is also the **ability of the organisation to exploit the environment in the acquisition of critical resources**."},{"p":"The criteria for measuring organisational effectiveness are:"},{"ol":["adaptation to the external environment;","integration;","sense of identity;","productivity;","goal attainment;","cohesion;","efficiency;","political effectiveness;","survival; and","control over the environment."]}],"src":"Chapter 7.1 and 7.5","sec":"7.5"},{"q":"Explain any five factors that influence organisational effectiveness, and any five strategies by which it may be improved.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"Factors influencing organisational effectiveness (any five)"},{"ol":["**Leadership style** — must be effective, to inspire and influence high performance.","**Organisational culture** — should drive individual and team effectiveness and efficiency.","**Organisational structure** — should engender an appropriate balance of authority and responsibility.","**Technology** — appropriate technology must be deployed to drive operations.","**Human resources** — the right skills and hands must be engaged.","**Communication** — effective both within and outside the organisation.","**External organisational elements** — changes in the external environment influence effectiveness.","**Change management** — the ability to manage internal transitions smoothly affects stability and effectiveness."]},{"h4":"Strategies for improving organisational effectiveness (any five)"},{"ol":["clear definition of vision and goals;","effective and efficient two-way communication;","engaging employees in the planning process;","a culture of continuous training and development;","creating objective performance metrics;","supporting a creative, innovative and adaptive work environment; and","optimising resource utilisation."]}],"src":"Chapter 7.3 and 7.4","sec":"7.4"}]}},{"n":8,"t":"Office Practice and Procedure, Health and Safety","brief":"Two major concepts in one chapter. First, **office practice and procedure** — what an office is and does, the closed and open-plan layouts and their trade-offs, the factors in planning a layout and in office landscaping, office machines, furniture, equipment and stationery, organisation and methods (O & M) studies, the design and control of forms, and the application of information technology across the five management functions. Second, **health and safety at work** — its importance, the Nigerian and Ghanaian legislative framework (the National Policy on Occupational Safety and Health 2006, the Factories Act and its subsidiary regulations, the Workmen's Compensation Act and the Employees' Compensation Act 2010), the sixteen common sources of danger at the workplace, and the fourteen prevention and protective measures.","outcomes":["Explain the meaning of an office","Explain the functions of an office","Identify the types of office","Explain office planning and layout","Explain office machines and furniture","Explain organisation and methods","Explain the design and control of forms","Explain information technology application in management","Explain the importance of health and safety at work, and health and safety regulation","Explain sources of danger to health within the workplace","Explain the safety and protection methods in an office setting"],"secs":[{"n":"8.1","t":"Definition of an office","b":[{"def":{"t":"Office","d":"a space where day-to-day work is carried out — a place in an organisation where business, clerical and professional activities take place. It is a place where private or public records are prepared, handled and preserved for future reference, and made available as and when required."}},{"key":"Notice that the definition is built around **records**, not around furniture or a building. An office is defined by the work done in it — preparing, handling and preserving records, and making them available on demand — which is exactly what the nine functions at §8.2 enumerate. That is why the same definition stretches to cover a modern paperless or remote office."}]},{"n":"8.2","t":"Functions of an office","b":[{"p":"The functions of an office are as follows; an office:"},{"ol":["**provides a convenient place where managers carry out their functions**;","**collects information from within the organisation** — examples of such information are letters, memoranda and documents generated from other departments;","**collects information from outside the organisation** — the office receives incoming mail from outside, for example letters, parcels, documents, invoices and telephone calls;","**records information** — the office classifies and records information received from other departments and outsiders in files, computers and other media;","**provides information for others** — office staff provide information to staff and to outsiders who require specific information on the activities of the organisation, such as customers, government and the general public;","**stores information** — the recorded information accumulated in the office needs to be preserved and maintained for future reference. The basic objective of preserving information is to make it available as and when needed by management, without delay;","**helps in organising information** — the information received in the office needs to be organised, processed and presented in useful forms needed for good decision making;","**affords a place for receiving visitors** — the office provides a place where official visitors are received and meetings are arranged. Such visitors include bank officials, government officials, contractors and other business associates; and","**arranging logistics** — the office is a place where logistics such as transport, distribution, storage, warehousing and transactions are arranged, facilitating business activities."]},{"key":"Six of the nine functions form a single **information cycle**, and presenting them that way is far stronger than reciting nine separate items: the office **collects** information (from inside and from outside), **records** it, **organises** it, **stores** it, and **provides** it to those who need it. The remaining three are about the office as a **physical place** — somewhere managers work, somewhere visitors are received, and somewhere logistics are arranged."}]},{"n":"8.3","t":"Types of office","b":[{"p":"There are two types of office; these are **(a)** the closed office and **(b)** the open plan office."},{"def":{"t":"Closed office","d":"an office where individuals, units or departments are allocated separate office rooms."}},{"def":{"t":"Open plan office","d":"an office in which more than one person, unit or department share the same large office space. In this case there may be temporary partitions to create cubicles."}},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type of office","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Closed**","(i) There is **privacy** for confidential discussions. (ii) The occupant can **concentrate**, because there is very little disturbance from other employees. (iii) There is **reduction of noise**.","(i) It is **not economical** — large space may be occupied by one individual. (ii) **Separate facilities** (computers, telephones, etc.) may have to be provided for each office. (iii) It may be **difficult to supervise** staff, because they are in different offices. (iv) It might **slow down the flow** of some activities which require constant interaction among employees."],["**Open plan**","(i) It is **easy to supervise**, since everybody is in view. (ii) It **economises space**. (iii) **Exchange of information** among staff is easy, and this facilitates the flow of work. (iv) Certain **facilities may easily be shared**, e.g. printers. (v) **Lower energy costs**. (vi) **Few communication barriers**. (vii) Could easily be **rearranged**.","(i) Some **senior staff dislike** the idea of being \"dumped\" in the same room with their subordinates. (ii) There is **no privacy** for people who wish to discuss confidential matters. (iii) **Noise** from movements in and out of the office, and from office machines, might disturb some staff."]]}},{"note":"Table 8.1 in the study text. Read down the two columns and the trade-off is exactly symmetrical: every advantage of one is a disadvantage of the other. The closed office buys **privacy, concentration and quiet** at the cost of **space, duplicated facilities, supervision and workflow**; the open plan buys all four back and loses the first three. There is no \"better\" layout — the answer depends on the **business needs** factor at §8.4."}]},{"n":"8.4","t":"Planning an office layout","b":[{"def":{"t":"Office layout","d":"the way the office is arranged to facilitate the flow of work. Office layout deals with the design and décor of an office, taking into account all equipment, supplies, accessories, and designing an arrangement needed for the proper functioning of the office."}},{"p":"The following factors are taken into consideration in planning the layout of an office:"},{"ol":["**Churn rate** — for many organisations, office layouts are subject to frequent changes. The process of change in office layout is known as the **churn rate**, which is expressed as the **percentage of staff moved during the year**.","**Statutory requirement** — legal requirements contained in relevant legislation also affect the planning of office layout. The **Factories Act of Nigeria** and the **Factories, Offices and Shops Act of Ghana** explicitly specify fire prevention arrangements, lighting levels, ventilation, temperature control, etc.","**Business needs** — office layout should provide an environment suitable for the business of the organisation. For example, the layout of a bank will be different from that of an accounting firm.","**Accommodation standards** — organisations often have a policy on the minimum standard of accommodation for each staff grade. Administration staff may work in open plan offices, whereas managers may have individual offices, sized on a seniority basis.","**Space availability** — planning an office layout will also depend on the quality and the types of office space available for use."]},{"h4":"Office landscaping"},{"def":{"t":"Office landscaping","d":"the provision of a beautified office environment that creates a pleasant ambience or scenery in an office, which facilitates work, attracts customers and motivates workers."}},{"p":"Office landscaping can be divided into **(i) ambience**, **(ii) space and functions** and **(iii) signs and symbols**. The following table summarises the considerations and provisions in an office that make it comfortable for workers:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Ambience","Space and functions","Signs and symbols"],"rows":[["Temperature","Office layout","Signage for direction"],["Air quality","Equipment","Artifacts"],["Noise level","Furniture","Awards plaques"],["Music","Parking space","Uniforms"],["Lighting","Convenience (toilet facilities)",""],["Ventilation","Emergency",""],["Neatness and cleanliness","Health and safety",""],["Decorations","",""],["Paintings","",""]]}},{"note":"Table 8.2 in the study text. Note the three-way split, which is what an answer should reproduce: **ambience** is what the office *feels* like (temperature, air, noise, light, cleanliness); **space and functions** is what it *contains and allows* (layout, equipment, furniture, parking, conveniences, emergency provision, health and safety); and **signs and symbols** is what it *communicates* (signage, artifacts, award plaques, uniforms). The third group is the one candidates forget, and it is the one that does the work of projecting identity to visitors."},{"key":"**Churn rate** is the term most likely to be asked for by name: it is the **percentage of staff moved during the year**, and a high churn rate argues for a flexible open plan arrangement that \"could easily be rearranged\" (§8.3) rather than fixed closed offices."}]},{"n":"8.5","t":"Office machines and furniture","b":[{"def":{"t":"Office machines","d":"pieces of equipment that are used in an office to help employees perform their duties more effectively."}},{"p":"Office machines are used because of the following reasons — they:"},{"ol":["aid or **speed up the performance of routine functions**;","**improve the quality of work**;","**save space**;","**simplify the work** of employees;","facilitate **greater accuracy**;","**save time**; and","**increase efficiency**."]},{"note":"The seven reasons reduce to three benefits worth naming in an answer: **speed** (speeding up routine work, saving time), **quality** (improving work quality and accuracy), and **economy** (saving space, simplifying work, increasing efficiency)."}]},{"n":"8.6","t":"Types of office machines and furniture","b":[{"p":"The main types of machines you are likely to find in most offices are:"},{"ol":["**Photocopiers** — a machine used to copy from original documents. The original document may be handwritten, printed or typewritten. Photocopiers are used to prepare extra copies of documents.","**Facsimile (fax) machine** — this machine scans printed or typewritten texts and images and transmits them through telephone lines to a receiving fax machine, which converts the electronic signals back to the original text or image. Suitable for transmission through telephone lines, cables or satellite networks, facsimile machines could store messages and transmit them later, when transmission costs are lower.","**Calculating machine** — used mainly in the office for calculations and complex computations.","**Cash register** — used in the cash office to receive cash and compute cash received from clients and customers. They are also used for computations.","**Paper shredders** — used in shredding unneeded documents, to protect against leakage of contents in the process of disposal.","**Computers** — perhaps the most common equipment in modern offices. Used for data and word processing, document storage, communication, presentation, etc.","**Scanners** — used for scanning images of documents to computers for printing, storage, display or communication via the internet.","**Binding machine** — used for putting sheets of documents, or the contents of a file, together.","**Typewriters** — used for typing documents. Its use is fast becoming obsolete.","**Paper cutter and envelope opener** — paper cutters are used for trimming the edges of documents to make them neater, while envelope openers are used for opening envelopes neatly, to ensure that neither the content nor the envelope itself is damaged.","**Laminating machines** — machines used for bonding waterproof material to documents for preservation purposes.","**Perforators** — used for punching holes into documents, to make attachment to a file neater.","**Stapler** — for holding documents together.","**File cabinets** — for storage of folders and protection of documents from fire outbreak, theft, etc."]},{"h4":"Office furniture"},{"ol":["**Office chairs** — which come either in swivel or stationary form, used for sitting in offices. The design is dependent on several factors, some of which include seniority, size of space and purpose.","**Office tables and desks** — used for sitting, writing, and storage of materials and documents.","**Book cases and shelves** — used for display of books, files, trophies, etc., for ease of access."]},{"h4":"Other office equipment and materials"},{"ul":["close circuit TV;","tape recorder;","telephones/intercom;","television;","file rack/tray;","flip chart; and","pin boards."]},{"h4":"Office stationery"},{"ul":["correcting fluid/Tippex;","eraser;","gum;","ink pads;","note pads/note books;","office pins;","paper clips;","photocopy paper;","sharpener;","staple pins; and","writing materials such as pens, pencils and markers."]},{"note":"The study text draws **four separate lists** here, and a question will usually name one of them: **machines** (14 items), **furniture** (3), **other equipment and materials** (7), and **stationery** (11). Keep them apart — a stapler and a perforator are machines in the book's classification, while staple pins and paper clips are stationery."},{"warn":"A dated entry worth noticing rather than reproducing uncritically: the book still lists the **typewriter**, while noting that \"its use is fast becoming obsolete\", and devotes a long entry to the **fax machine**. Both are superseded in practice by the computer and scanner also on the list. They remain in the syllabus, so know them — but if a question asks what equipment a *modern* office needs, the computer, scanner, photocopier and shredder are the defensible answers."}]},{"n":"8.7","t":"Organisation and Methods (O & M)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Organisation and Methods (O & M)","d":"the techniques used to improve productivity in an organisation — any systematic attempt to increase the productivity of an organisation by improving procedures, methods, systems and organisation structures."}},{"p":"In some organisations a special unit called the **organisation and methods (O & M) unit** is set up to carry out studies and introduce new methods needed to improve productivity."},{"p":"The key objectives of O & M are to:"},{"ol":["**simplify jobs** and make it easier for employees to carry out their tasks;","ensure **efficient flow of information** throughout the organisation;","improve the **monitoring** of the activities of various individuals and departments;","ensure the **efficient utilisation of material, money and labour** resources of the organisation;","**reduce cost of production** by minimising or eliminating waste; and","ensure the **free flow of work** among departments."]},{"h4":"8.7.1  Steps/procedures in an O & M study"},{"p":"An O & M study involves the following basic steps:"},{"steps":["**Selection** of the work or table for the study.","**Recording** of the existing method.","**Examination** of the existing method.","**Developing** the improved method.","**Installation** of the new method.","**Maintenance** of the new method."]},{"key":"The six steps are worth learning as a sequence, because the examiner may ask for them in order: **select, record, examine, develop, install, maintain**. Note the last one — **maintenance** — which candidates routinely omit. An improved method that is not maintained decays back to the old one, which is why installation is not the final step."},{"h4":"8.7.2  Importance of O & M"},{"ol":["it **improves the structure of the organisation** by simplifying procedures and systems;","it could lead to the **elimination of unnecessary procedures** and improve workflow;","it leads to a **more efficient use of materials, labour and office space**;","it **improves coordination** of activities among individuals and departments;","it could lead to **standardisation of procedures and forms**;","it helps to **improve factory and office layout**; and","it helps to **unearth lapses in job performance**, which could be removed through improved training of employees."]},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section and its two sub-sections are printed in the body of the chapter as **\"3.7\", \"3.7.1\" and \"3.7.2\"** — chapter 3's numbers appearing inside chapter 8. The chapter's own contents list places them correctly between §8.6 and §8.8, and they are numbered 8.7, 8.7.1 and 8.7.2 here."}]},{"n":"8.8","t":"Forms","b":[{"def":{"t":"Form","d":"a document with spaces (fields) in which to write or select, for a series of documents with similar contents. A form in a computer allows for conveniently typing in the variable parts (data input). When completed, forms may be a statement, a request, an order, etc."}},{"p":"Examples of forms include ledgers, cheques, tax forms and appraisal forms. Forms may be filled in duplicate, triplicate or more, where the information gathered on them needs to be distributed to several departments within the organisation. This can be done using carbon paper."},{"p":"Generally, forms designed for information gathering are **free from copyright**. Examples of such forms include account books, bank cheques, scorecards, address books, report forms, order forms, diaries, time cards and graph paper. Otherwise, for protection of intellectual property, some specially designed forms could be **subject to copyright** — for example, some forms used for selection tests."},{"h4":"8.8.1  Advantages of forms"},{"ol":["**reduces the need for writing** (while the printing is done in some automatic way);","generally **contains guidelines on how it should be filled**, thus minimising errors;","**uniformity**, for convenience in processing;","simpler tasks such as collecting or distributing data **can be separated in the workflow** from more complex processes; and","information collected on the form **can be analysed later**."]},{"h4":"8.8.2  Design of forms"},{"p":"In the design of forms, the following factors must be considered:"},{"ol":["**Content** — the form must request all relevant information. As much as possible, all irrelevant questions should be avoided.","**Language** — questions contained in the form must be as short as possible and easily understood by those filling them. For example, the way questions are designed in forms for manual labourers should reflect their level of education.","**Purpose** — the purpose of the form will determine its design.","**Ease of processing** — where processing of information is to be done via computers, the design will be such as to make this possible and easy. In some cases, specially designed forms that may require shading with pencils are used."]},{"h4":"8.8.3  Control of forms"},{"p":"Controls exist only for forms that are classified as **security documents**."},{"def":{"t":"Security documents","d":"documents whose contents are restricted to only authorised personnel, due to the nature of the information contained in them or the possibility of their being used to perpetrate fraud or theft. Examples are cheques, receipts and some types of voucher."}},{"p":"Generally, the following control measures are used in handling forms classified as security documents:"},{"ol":["**Storage** — these forms are usually stored in **security safes**. It is advised that such safes are made of cast iron or concrete and placed in a secured office. Such storage facility should be **fire and water resistant**. The keys to such a safe should be kept by the **officer in charge** (cash officer, cashier, etc.) **and** somebody of **sufficient seniority** in the organisation (e.g. the Head of Finance and Accounts).","**Tracking** — in some organisations, **release registers** are used to track the release of receipt booklets and cheque books, for accountability purposes.","**Production** — production of forms like receipts should be **restricted to organisations with sufficient integrity**. Also, some **security features** should be present on such forms, to prevent counterfeiting."]},{"key":"The storage rule is a **dual control**: the keys are held by the officer in charge **and** by a sufficiently senior second person, so that no single individual can reach the security documents alone. That is the same segregation-of-duties principle that runs through internal control generally, applied to blank cheques and receipt books."},{"note":"Note the limiting words at the start of §8.8.3: controls exist **only** for forms classified as security documents. Ordinary information-gathering forms — order forms, appraisal forms, time cards — need good **design** (§8.8.2) but not safe-custody **control**. The test for a security document is whether its contents are restricted, or whether it could be used to perpetrate fraud or theft."}]},{"n":"8.9","t":"Information technology application in management","b":[{"def":{"t":"Information technology","d":"concerned with the use of computer and communication-based technologies to solve a variety of human, organisational, country-wide or global problems. It is technology-based systems that enhance efficiency and effectiveness in the generation and use of information in a variety of strategic, tactical and operational situations."}},{"p":"Information technology has become a veritable tool to achieve efficiency and effectiveness in **all areas of management**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Managerial function","Role of IT"],"rows":[["**Planning**","It aids **scenario planning**; a wealth of information helps **decision making**; **consumer access** to product and service information allows comparisons, with implications for marketing; strategies thus **focus on consumer needs** and the service required."],["**Organising**","Organisations become **change agents**; **new organisational forms** emerge (e.g. outsourcing, online operations); knowledge of work promotes **non-hierarchical structures**."],["**Staffing**","**Specialised technical knowledge** is needed; **greater mobility** of workers in changing jobs; information enhances workers' **upward mobility**; specialised knowledge workers function as **consultants or contractors** in temporary or part-time employment; **outsourcing of human resources management** to specialised firms at home and abroad; use of **older, semi-retired professionals** as consultants; **continued training and retraining** to keep abreast of new technologies; development of **new measurements** for evaluating and rewarding workers."],["**Leading**","Information availability **gives power to knowledge workers and consumers**; knowledge workers demand **higher-order needs satisfaction** (recognition, self-actualisation, responsibility, participation, etc.); **electronic communication technology** aids the framework; **lower communication cost**."],["**Controlling**","**Ease of control**; **detection of deviation from plan**; facilitates **productivity measurement at low cost**; the internet enables **worldwide selling**, and **scanning of the external environment** is made easier and faster."]]}},{"note":"Table 8.3 in the study text, sourced to **Weihrich, Cannice and Koontz, *Management*, 12th edition, McGraw-Hill, pp. 483–485**. The table is organised by the **five management functions** of §2.4 — planning, organising, staffing, leading and controlling — so a question on \"the impact of IT on management\" is best answered function by function in that order rather than as a general list of benefits."},{"key":"The deepest change in the table is in **leading**: information availability **shifts power** to knowledge workers and consumers, and knowledge workers then demand higher-order need satisfaction — recognition, self-actualisation, responsibility, participation. That is a direct link to the motivation theory of Chapter 5: IT does not merely make the manager's existing job easier, it changes what employees want from the job and therefore what leadership has to supply."}]},{"n":"8.10","t":"Health and safety at work","b":[{"p":"Despite the level of development in a country like the United Kingdom, it was estimated that over **500 people are killed at work every year** and several hundred thousand more are injured or suffer ill health (Armstrong, 2006). The equivalent figures for West African countries will be much higher. This underscores the importance of effectively managing health and safety at work."},{"h4":"What is safety and health?"},{"p":"Webster defines:"},{"ol":["**Safety** — freedom from danger, injury or damage; security.","**Health** — physical and mental well-being; freedom from disease, pain or defect; normality of physical and mental functions; soundness."]},{"p":"The **Occupational Safety and Health Act (OSHA)** states its purpose as: *\"To assure as far as possible every working man and woman in the nation safe and healthful working conditions, and to preserve our human resources.\"*"},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section and its first sub-section are printed in the body as **\"3.10\"** and **\"3.10.1\"**, again carrying chapter 3's number. The remaining sub-sections (8.10.2, 8.10.3, 8.10.4) are numbered correctly, and the chapter contents list places all of them in chapter 8."},{"h4":"8.10.1  Importance of health and safety at work"},{"ol":["it is important to achieve the **highest standard of health and safety** at the workplace, because ill-health and injuries result in **loss and damage for the organisation**. Losses could arise from legal fees, fires, compensatory damages, lost production, and lost goodwill from workers, customers and the wider community;","health and safety at the workplace is also important because it **safeguards the health and safety of workers**. Morally, no worker should be made to work where his or her welfare is at risk; and","in most countries there are **statutes on health and safety** issues with which organisations must comply. Failure to do this might attract **government sanctions**."]},{"key":"The three reasons are **economic, moral and legal**, in that order — and an answer that names all three headings scores better than one that lists consequences without classifying them. The economic case (losses from legal fees, fires, compensation, lost production and lost goodwill) is the one a board responds to; the moral case stands independently of cost; and the legal case makes compliance non-optional."},{"h4":"8.10.2  Health and safety regulations and legislation"},{"h4":"National Policy on Occupational Safety and Health (2006)"},{"p":"The National Policy on Occupational Safety and Health, a guide to occupational safety and health in Nigeria, is the government's approach for achieving a national development philosophy of building a **united, self-reliant and egalitarian economy**, through minimising so far as is reasonably practicable the causes and effects of hazards inherent in the working environment — in response to Nigeria's ratification of **Convention 155 on Occupational Safety, Health and Working Environment**. The Policy came into force in **November 2006**."},{"p":"The **goals** of the Policy are to:"},{"ol":["**facilitate improvement of occupational safety and health performance**, by providing the framework for participative occupational safety and health protection of workers — including the most vulnerable groups — in all sectors of economic activity; and","**ensure harmonisation of workers' rights protection with regional and international standards** in a private sector-led economy, thus focusing the role of the competent authority on facilitating an enabling environment and regulating various provisions for securing the safety, health and welfare of workers in Nigerian workplaces."]},{"p":"The **objectives** are:"},{"ol":["to create a **general framework for the improvement of working conditions** and the working environment;","to **prevent accidents and departures from health** arising out of, or in the course of, work; and","to ensure the **provision of occupational safety and health services** to workers in all sectors of economic activity."]},{"p":"The **scope** of the Policy covers both the **formal and informal sectors** of the economy; protection of all categories of workers from undue persecution; effective communication and cooperation amongst all stakeholders; prevention and control of hazards from all forms of occupational exposure; provision of health surveillance and emergency medical services; training in the area of occupational health and safety; and the gathering and storage of information pertaining to occupational safety and health, amongst other areas of focus."},{"h4":"The statutory framework in Nigeria and Ghana"},{"p":"In **Nigeria**, health and safety legislation is found in the **common law**, the **Factories Act Cap 26, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 1990** and the **Workmen's Compensation Act Cap 470, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 1990**. Nigeria is also a member of the **International Labour Organisation (ILO)** and a signatory to **ILO Convention No. 155**, titled the Occupational Safety and Health Convention, 1981."},{"p":"In **Ghana**, apart from the common law, health and safety issues are dealt with in the **Factories, Offices and Shops Act 1970**, the **Labour Act of 2003** and the **Workman Compensation Act**. Like Nigeria, Ghana is also a signatory to **ILO Convention 155 of 1981** on health and safety at work."},{"p":"The **Nigerian Factories Act 1990**, just like the **Factories, Offices and Shops Act 1990 of Ghana**, is primarily designed to **govern, order and regulate industrial activities** generally. Their main objective is to **prevent occupational hazards and diseases** in workplaces. These laws make it **obligatory for employers of labour to take various precautions** to protect their workers, dealing with issues such as cleanliness, ventilation, lighting, sanitation, conveniences and fire prevention. The laws also prescribe how machinery and equipment that could cause injury at work should be handled — by fencing, wearing of helmets, etc. — and prescribe **various penalties for contravention**."},{"h4":"The Factories Act, CAP F1, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 2004"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Subsidiary legislation (regulations)","What the regulations provide for"],"rows":[["**Factories (Wood Working Machinery) Regulations, LN 189 of 1958**","Provides guidance for the conduct of any factory or parts thereof, and any place to which section 46 of the Act applies, in which wood work occurs. It speaks to the provision of equipment and machinery fencing and guards in such establishments."],["**Factories (Notification of Dangerous Occurrences) Regulations, LN 105 of 1961**","Provides guidance for the notification of accidents and dangerous occurrences in accordance with sections 51–52 of the Act, whether or not death or disablement occurs."],["**Docks (Safety of Labour) Regulations, LN 42 of 1958**","Provides guidance for the process of loading, unloading or bunkering any vessel in any dock in a port, and for all machinery or plant used in those processes."],["**Factories (Registration, Fees etc.) Regulations 2007**","Provides for fees payable for the certification and appointment of \"approved persons\" for the purposes of lifting equipment inspection and certification, and for the registration and renewal of the Certificate of Registration of Factory Premises."],["**Docks (Sanitary Accommodation) Regulations, 1958**","Provides for the provision of sanitary conveniences to all docks under the jurisdiction of the Docks (Safety of Labour) Regulations."],["**Declaration of Industrial Diseases Notice, LN 114 of 1956**","Provides for the identification of diseases or illnesses that might be contracted by an employed person by reason of the nature of his employment."],["**First Aid Boxes (Prescribed Standards) Order, LN 188 of 1958**","Gives specifications for the provision of first aid boxes to be provided at factories, in line with the number of employees present."]]}},{"note":"Table 8.4 in the study text."},{"h4":"The Workmen's Compensation Act, and the Employees' Compensation Act 2010"},{"p":"The **Workmen's Compensation Act** — like the **Workman Compensation Act 1923 of Ghana** and the Ghana Labour Act — prescribes various compensation for workers in the event of **injury or death by accident arising out of, or in the course of, the employment** of the workman."},{"p":"The **Employees' Compensation Act, 2010** repeals the Workmen's Compensation Act, Cap. W6, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 2004, and makes comprehensive provision for payment of compensation to employees who **suffer from occupational diseases or sustain injuries arising from accident at the workplace or in the course of employment**."},{"p":"The **objectives** of the Act are to:"},{"ol":["provide for an **open and fair system of guaranteed and adequate compensation** for all employees or their dependants, for any death, injury, disease or disability arising out of or in the course of employment;","provide **rehabilitation** to employees with work-related disabilities as provided in the Act;","establish and maintain a **solvent compensation fund**, managed in the interest of employees and employers;","provide for **fair and adequate assessments for employers**;","provide an **appeal procedure** that is simple, fair and accessible, with minimal delays; and","**combine efforts and resources of relevant stakeholders for the prevention of workplace disabilities**, including the enforcement of occupational safety and health standards."]},{"key":"Keep the two strands of the legislation apart, because an answer that merges them misses half the marks. The **Factories Act** (and Ghana's Factories, Offices and Shops Act) is **preventive** — it regulates how workplaces must be run so that injury does not occur, and penalises contravention. The **Employees' Compensation Act 2010** (replacing the Workmen's Compensation Act) is **compensatory** — it provides what happens after injury, disease or death. Note that objective (f) of the 2010 Act reaches back across the divide into prevention."},{"note":"Both Nigeria and Ghana are signatories to **ILO Convention 155 (1981)** on occupational safety and health, and Nigeria's **National Policy on Occupational Safety and Health (November 2006)** was adopted in response to ratifying it. That international obligation is the thread that explains why the two countries' frameworks resemble each other."},{"h4":"8.10.3  Sources of danger to health and safety at the workplace"},{"p":"Common sources of safety hazard at the workplace are:"},{"ol":["**slippery floors**;","**open cabinets**;","**frayed carpets**;","**poorly lit stairways or offices**;","**unsafe work processes**;","**poor ventilation**;","**careless storage of hazardous items**, such as sharp objects and dangerous chemicals;","**dangerous fumes**;","**wrong use or handling of equipment/machinery** at the workplace;","**manufacturing equipment malfunctioning**, which may be compounded by inadequate maintenance;","**poor factory layout**;","**horseplay** at the workplace;","**inadequate or absence of sewage facility**;","**dirty environment**;","**inadequate safety equipment/gadgets**; and","**failure to use safety equipment, or refusal to adhere to safety procedures** during work."]},{"key":"The sixteen sources fall into three groups, and classifying them makes a far better answer than reciting the list: **the physical environment** (slippery floors, open cabinets, frayed carpets, poor lighting, poor ventilation, dirty environment, inadequate sewage, poor factory layout); **equipment and process** (unsafe work processes, careless storage of hazardous items, dangerous fumes, malfunctioning equipment, inadequate safety gadgets); and **human behaviour** (wrong use of equipment, horseplay, and failure or refusal to use safety equipment and follow procedures). The last group is the one no amount of capital expenditure can fix — which is why item (i) of §8.10.4 makes worker commitment a measure in its own right."},{"h4":"8.10.4  Prevention and protective measures for safety in an office"},{"ol":["**Safety policy** — the first step towards prevention of injury or death at the workplace is to put in place a safety policy. In some developed countries this is required by law. The safety policy is a **statement of commitment of management** to safety and health at work.","**Periodic risk assessment** — the process of **identifying hazards and the risks attached to them**. It is a proactive strategy aimed at tackling every health and safety risk at the workplace, and information obtained from this exercise will help in taking preventive measures.","**Hazard elimination** — this may require the **use of alternatives, design improvement or change of process**.","**Substitution** — for example, replacement of hazardous equipment with a less dangerous one.","**Use of barriers** — removal of dangerous equipment from workers, or of workers from dangerous equipment.","**Use of procedure** — putting in place a step-by-step procedure for carrying out an operation.","**Use of a warning system** — such as instructions, signs and labels. However, these depend on **human response**.","**Use of protective equipment** — such as clothing and gloves.","**Commitment of workers** to adherence to safety procedures and use of safety equipment — no matter how safety-conscious an organisation is, if workers refuse to comply with safety rules, injury or death at the workplace may occur.","hazards can be prevented by **designing and installing safety equipment and protective devices**;","**periodic inspections and checks** to eliminate risks;","**investigating accidents** as they occur, and taking corrective actions;","a **continuous programme of education and training** on safe work habits and methods of preventing accidents; and","**designation of a Safety and Health Officer** — this officer shall be the health and safety champion in the organisation."]},{"key":"Measures (c) to (h) form a deliberate **hierarchy of control**, strongest first, and saying so turns a list into an argument: **eliminate** the hazard; if you cannot, **substitute** something less dangerous; if you cannot, put a **barrier** between worker and hazard; if you cannot, impose a **procedure**; if you cannot, **warn**; and only as the last resort rely on **personal protective equipment**. The order matters because each step down depends more heavily on human behaviour — the book makes the point explicitly of warning systems, which \"depend on human response\"."},{"note":"Two measures bracket the whole list and are the ones to name first in an answer. The **safety policy** is \"the first step\" — a written statement of management commitment, without which nothing else has authority. And the **designation of a Safety and Health Officer** gives the policy an owner. Between them sit assessment, the control hierarchy, inspection, accident investigation and training."}]},{"n":"8.11","t":"Summary","b":[{"p":"In this chapter, two major concepts were discussed, namely **(i)** office practice and procedure, and **(ii)** health and safety."},{"p":"An **office** is defined as a place in an organisation where business, clerical and professional activities take place. We noted that an office, among other functions, provides a convenient place where managers carry out their functions, collects information from within the organisation, and records information. We also looked at the various forms of office layout — **closed and open plan**. Factors to be considered in planning an office layout were also explained; these factors include **churn rate, statutory requirements, business needs, accommodation standards and available space**. Office equipment and furniture such as the facsimile were also mentioned. We discussed **organisation and methods**. We also defined **forms**, the control of forms, and factors to be considered in their design. The chapter also discussed the **application of ICT in management**."},{"p":"Also, the chapter discussed the **importance of health and safety** at the workplace. It was stated that health and safety are important to organisations and workers. We also explained various legislations and regulations governing health and safety issues at the workplace. In **Nigeria**, the Factories Act of 1990 and the Workmen's Compensation Act of 1990, as well as the common law, are part of the laws governing health and safety. In **Ghana**, the Factories, Offices and Shops Act 1970, the common law and the Labour Act of 2003 regulate issues of occupational health and safety. **Sources of danger** to health and safety at the workplace were also discussed. Among other measures, the **design of a safety policy** will set the pace for organisational commitment to health and safety at the workplace. The chapter concluded with the sources of danger to health and safety, as well as the preventive measures to ensure health and safety in the workplace."}]},{"n":"8.12","t":"Review questions, with the study text's own solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with **five multiple choice questions** and **five short answer questions**, and prints its own solutions to both. All are reproduced here, with the official answers and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review questions","t":"Multiple choice questions, with the study text's solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is a type of office layout? **A.** Open Plan; **B.** Matrix Plan; **C.** Diagonal Plan; **D.** Horizontal Plan; **E.** Hybrid Plan.","The following are factors to be considered in planning an office layout **EXCEPT** **A.** Churn Rate; **B.** Statutory Requirement; **C.** Business Needs; **D.** Accommodation; **E.** Management Initiative.","Which of the following is **NOT** a filing system? **A.** Alphabetical filing system; **B.** Open filing system; **C.** Alpha-numeric filing system; **D.** Subject filing system; **E.** Geographical filing system.","Drafting of health and safety policy is the responsibility of ……… **A.** Human Resources Department; **B.** Board of Directors; **C.** Production Department; **D.** Research and Development Department; **E.** Chief Executive Officer.","The following are workplace accident prevention measures **EXCEPT** **A.** Design of safety devices; **B.** Wearing of protective clothing; **C.** Effective performance management; **D.** Health and safety training; **E.** Accident risk assessment."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — multiple choice questions"},{"ol":["**A — Open Plan.** The study text recognises exactly **two** types of office: the **closed** office and the **open plan** office (§8.3). Matrix, diagonal, horizontal and hybrid plans are not among them — \"matrix\" belongs to organisation *structure*, not office layout.","**E — Management Initiative.** The five factors at §8.4 are **churn rate, statutory requirement, business needs, accommodation standards and space availability**. Management initiative is not one of them.","**B — Open filing system.** Alphabetical, alpha-numeric, subject and geographical are recognised filing systems; \"open filing system\" is not. *(See the warning below — this chapter never discusses filing systems.)*","**A — Human Resources Department.** Note the contrast with option E: the safety policy is a statement of **management commitment** (§8.10.4(a)), but the task of **drafting** it falls to the Human Resources function, which owns employee welfare.","**C — Effective performance management.** The prevention and protective measures at §8.10.4 include designing safety devices, protective clothing, health and safety training and risk assessment. Performance management is a human-resource practice, not an accident prevention measure."]},{"warn":"Question 3 asks about **filing systems**, which this chapter does not cover at all — the words \"filing system\" appear nowhere in §8.1 to §8.11. The question reaches outside its own chapter. For completeness, the recognised systems are **alphabetical** (by name), **numerical** (by assigned number), **alpha-numeric** (a combination), **subject** (by topic), **geographical** (by location) and **chronological** (by date) — and \"open filing\" is not among them, which is what makes B the answer."},{"note":"Questions 2 and 5 are both **\"EXCEPT\" questions**, and four of the five options in each are drawn verbatim from the chapter's own lists. The technique is to recall the list, tick off the options that appear on it, and the survivor is the answer — rather than reasoning about whether each option sounds plausible, since \"management initiative\" and \"effective performance management\" both sound entirely plausible."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Short answer questions","t":"Short answer questions, with the study text's solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The process of identifying hazards for the purpose of taking protective and preventive measures is called ………","The statement of commitment of the management to safety at work is known as ………","The use of alternatives, design improvement and/or change of process is referred to as ………","The systematic attempt to increase productivity of an organisation by improving procedures, systems and organisation structure is referred to as ………","……… means freedom from danger, injury or damage; security."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions — short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Risk assessment** *(§8.10.4(b))*","**Health and safety policy** *(§8.10.4(a))*","**Hazard elimination** *(§8.10.4(c))*","**Organisation and Methods (O & M)** *(§8.7)*","**Safety** *(§8.10, Webster's definition)*"]},{"key":"Questions 1 to 3 test three **different** measures from the same list at §8.10.4, and they are easy to confuse. **Risk assessment** is *identifying* hazards and their risks. **Hazard elimination** is *removing* the hazard, by alternatives, design improvement or process change. The **safety policy** is the written *statement of management commitment* that authorises both. Learn them as identify → remove → authorise."},{"note":"Question 4 reaches back to §8.7 and question 5 to Webster's definition at §8.10, so the short answer bank spans the whole chapter rather than only its health and safety half. Note that question 4 quotes the O & M definition almost word for word, which is a good reason to learn that definition verbatim."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is two chapters stitched together, and the examiner usually treats them that way — expect one question on office practice and one on health and safety, rather than one question spanning both. On the office side, the examinable content is almost entirely enumerated lists: 9 functions of an office, 2 office types with their symmetrical advantages and disadvantages, 5 layout factors (learn \"churn rate\" by name and by its definition as the percentage of staff moved in a year), the three-way split of office landscaping, four separate equipment lists, the 6 objectives and 6 steps of an O & M study, and the design and control of forms. On the health and safety side, learn the three reasons it matters (economic, moral, legal), the Nigerian and Ghanaian statutes by name and year, the 16 sources of danger grouped into environment/equipment/behaviour, and above all the 14 prevention measures — within which measures (c) to (h) form a hierarchy of control running from eliminate down to personal protective equipment. Note two book defects: §8.7 and §8.10 are misnumbered as \"3.7\" and \"3.10\" in the body, and the review questions ask about filing systems, which the chapter never covers.","errors":["Naming office types other than closed and open plan. The study text recognises exactly two — a multiple choice question is built on precisely this, with \"matrix\", \"diagonal\", \"horizontal\" and \"hybrid\" as distractors.","Confusing the three closely related safety measures: risk assessment is IDENTIFYING hazards and their risks; hazard elimination is REMOVING the hazard through alternatives, design improvement or process change; and the safety policy is the written STATEMENT OF MANAGEMENT COMMITMENT. All three appear as separate short answer questions.","Listing the 14 prevention measures flat, instead of showing that (c) to (h) are a hierarchy of control — eliminate, substitute, barrier, procedure, warning, protective equipment — in descending order of reliability, because each step relies more on human response.","Mixing up the preventive and the compensatory legislation. The Factories Act regulates how workplaces must be run so injury does not occur; the Employees' Compensation Act 2010 (repealing the Workmen's Compensation Act) provides compensation after injury, disease or death.","Treating staplers and perforators as stationery, or staple pins and paper clips as machines. The study text draws four distinct lists — machines, furniture, other equipment and materials, and stationery — and a question will name one of them.","Forgetting the last step of an O & M study. The six steps are select, record, examine, develop, install and MAINTAIN — an improved method that is not maintained decays back to the method it replaced."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The percentage of staff moved during the year, used as a factor in planning an office layout, is known as the","o":["turnover rate","churn rate","attrition rate","mobility index","accommodation standard"],"a":1,"w":"The churn rate is the process of change in office layout, expressed as the percentage of staff moved during the year. A high churn rate argues for a flexible open plan arrangement, which \"could easily be rearranged\".","src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is an advantage of the CLOSED office rather than the open plan office?","o":["It economises space","It is easy to supervise since everybody is in view","There is privacy for confidential discussions","Certain facilities may easily be shared","There are few communication barriers"],"a":2,"w":"Privacy for confidential discussions, the ability to concentrate, and reduction of noise are the three advantages of the closed office. All four other options are advantages of the open plan office — and the two lists are almost exactly mirror images of each other.","src":"Chapter 8.3","sec":"8.3"},{"q":"In the hierarchy of prevention and protective measures, which of the following is the LEAST reliable because it depends most heavily on human response?","o":["Hazard elimination","Substitution","Use of barriers","Use of a warning system","Change of process"],"a":3,"w":"The study text notes expressly of warning systems — instructions, signs and labels — that \"these depend on human response\". The measures run strongest to weakest: eliminate the hazard, substitute something less dangerous, interpose a barrier, impose a procedure, warn, and only last rely on personal protective equipment.","src":"Chapter 8.10.4","sec":"8.10"},{"q":"Which Act repealed the Workmen's Compensation Act, Cap. W6, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 2004?","o":["The Factories Act, CAP F1, LFN 2004","The National Policy on Occupational Safety and Health 2006","The Employees' Compensation Act, 2010","ILO Convention No. 155 of 1981","The Labour Act of 2003"],"a":2,"w":"The Employees' Compensation Act, 2010 repeals the Workmen's Compensation Act and makes comprehensive provision for compensating employees who suffer occupational disease or sustain injury arising from accident at the workplace or in the course of employment.","src":"Chapter 8.10.2","sec":"8.10"},{"q":"Controls over forms, such as storage in security safes and the use of release registers, apply to","o":["all forms used in the organisation","only forms classified as security documents","only forms that are subject to copyright","only forms filled in duplicate or triplicate","only forms processed by computer"],"a":1,"w":"The study text states that controls exist ONLY for forms classified as security documents — those whose contents are restricted to authorised personnel, or which could be used to perpetrate fraud or theft (cheques, receipts, some vouchers). Ordinary information-gathering forms need good design, not safe custody.","src":"Chapter 8.8.3","sec":"8.8"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between a closed office and an open plan office, and state three advantages and three disadvantages of each.","marks":12,"a":[{"p":"A **closed office** is an office where individuals, units or departments are allocated separate office rooms. An **open plan office** is one in which more than one person, unit or department share the same large office space, possibly with temporary partitions to create cubicles."},{"h4":"Closed office"},{"p":"**Advantages:** privacy for confidential discussions; the occupant can concentrate, as there is little disturbance from other employees; reduction of noise."},{"p":"**Disadvantages:** it is not economical, as large space may be occupied by one individual; separate facilities (computers, telephones) may have to be provided for each office; it may be difficult to supervise staff in different offices; it might slow down activities requiring constant interaction."},{"h4":"Open plan office"},{"p":"**Advantages:** easy to supervise, since everybody is in view; economises space; exchange of information is easy, facilitating workflow; facilities such as printers may be shared; lower energy costs; few communication barriers; easily rearranged."},{"p":"**Disadvantages:** some senior staff dislike being \"dumped\" in the same room as their subordinates; no privacy for confidential discussion; noise from movement and office machines may disturb staff."},{"note":"Note that the two lists are mirror images — every advantage of one is a disadvantage of the other — so there is no universally better layout; the choice turns on the business needs of the organisation."}],"src":"Chapter 8.3","sec":"8.3"},{"q":"Explain the importance of health and safety at work, and state any six sources of danger to health and safety at the workplace.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"Importance of health and safety at work"},{"ol":["**Economic** — ill-health and injuries result in loss and damage for the organisation, through legal fees, fires, compensatory damages, lost production, and lost goodwill from workers, customers and the wider community.","**Moral** — health and safety safeguards the health and safety of workers; morally, no worker should be made to work where his or her welfare is at risk.","**Legal** — in most countries there are statutes on health and safety with which organisations must comply, and failure to do so might attract government sanctions."]},{"h4":"Sources of danger to health and safety (any six)"},{"ul":["slippery floors;","open cabinets;","frayed carpets;","poorly lit stairways or offices;","unsafe work processes;","poor ventilation;","careless storage of hazardous items such as sharp objects and dangerous chemicals;","dangerous fumes;","wrong use or handling of equipment or machinery;","malfunctioning manufacturing equipment, compounded by inadequate maintenance;","poor factory layout;","horseplay at the workplace;","inadequate or absent sewage facility;","dirty environment;","inadequate safety equipment or gadgets; and","failure to use safety equipment, or refusal to adhere to safety procedures."]},{"note":"The sources group into the physical environment, equipment and process, and human behaviour — and the third group is why worker commitment to safety procedures is itself listed as a prevention measure."}],"src":"Chapter 8.10.1 and 8.10.3","sec":"8.10"}]}},{"n":9,"t":"Contemporary Management Issues","brief":"Three current workplace topics, each a lettered section of the study text. **Section A — Mentoring**: its definition and two types (natural and planned), its characteristics and objectives, the four-step mentoring process, the dependence / counter-dependence / inter-dependence phases, the benefits to mentor, mentee and organisation, the demerits, and the roles and responsibilities of both parties. **Section B — Remote work**: working from home against working from anywhere (telecommuting), the four drivers, and the advantages and disadvantages. **Section C — Workplace couple**: workplace couple, work spouse and workplace romance distinguished, with the advantages, disadvantages and best practices.","outcomes":["Define the concept of mentoring, and state its characteristics and objectives","Explain the mentoring process and discuss the phases of mentoring","List the benefits of mentoring to the mentor, the mentee and the organisation, and outline its demerits","Identify the roles and responsibilities of mentors and mentees","Define remote work, and distinguish between working from home and working from anywhere","List the drivers of remote work, and state its advantages and disadvantages","Define workplace couple, workplace spouse and workplace romance","State the advantages and disadvantages of a workplace couple, and outline the best practices for one in an organisation"],"secs":[{"n":"A.1","t":"Definition of mentoring","b":[{"def":{"t":"Mentoring","d":"a professional relationship in which an experienced person (the **mentor**) assists another (the **mentee**) in developing specific skills and knowledge that will enhance the less experienced person's professional and personal growth."}},{"p":"Mentoring is a **long-term relationship** that meets a development need, helps develop full potential, and benefits all partners — mentor, mentee and the organisation. It is a **protected relationship** in which learning and experimentation can occur and potential skills be developed, and in which results can be **measured in terms of competencies gained**."},{"key":"Mentoring is normally a **voluntary, one-on-one relationship** between the mentor and protégé, where **neither is paid** to be involved in the process. It is important at the workplace because it helps employees to do the right thing, by exposing them to senior employees. **Mentoring differs from coaching** because coaching is a **short-term** task and mentoring is a **long-term** one. Mentoring is widely recognised today as an extremely beneficial **career development tool**."},{"h4":"Two types of mentoring"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","How it arises"],"rows":[["**Natural mentoring**","Occurs through friendship, collegiality, teaching, coaching and counselling."],["**Planned mentoring**","Occurs through structured programmes, in which mentors and participants are **selected and matched through formal processes**."]]}},{"note":"The distinction matters for Section A.7. Most of the demerits of mentoring — mandated relationships with no chemistry, perceptions of favouritism, mentor fatigue — are risks of **planned** mentoring, because it is the formal matching process that can pair people badly. Natural mentoring carries the confidentiality and sensitive-information risks but not the matching risk."}]},{"n":"A.2","t":"Characteristics of mentoring","b":[{"p":"From the foregoing definitions, mentoring has the following characteristics:"},{"ol":["mentoring is **long term in nature**;","mentoring **involves two parties** — mentor and mentee — in which the mentor is the more qualified person compared to the mentee;","mentoring is a **professional relationship**;","the process of mentoring **enhances the skills of the mentee**;","mentoring serves as a **career development tool**; and","mentoring leads to **reduced training costs** for an organisation."]}]},{"n":"A.3","t":"Objectives of mentoring","b":[{"p":"The objectives of mentoring include the following:"},{"ol":["providing an **opportunity to young people to share their concerns** and get both moral support and guidance for their development;","establishing a **relationship of trust**;","**modelling behavioural norms** for the young person;","**listening** to the person's concerns and problems;","helping him to **search for alternative solutions** to the problems;","**sharing the mentor's own relevant experiences**;","**responding to his emotional needs**, without making him depend on the mentor; and","developing a **long-lasting, personal and informal relationship**."]},{"key":"Objective (vii) carries the qualification that distinguishes good mentoring from dependency: respond to the mentee's emotional needs **without making him depend on the mentor**. Read it with the multiple choice question in A.10, where \"increase dependence\" is offered as a benefit to the mentee and is wrong. The object of mentoring is to build the mentee's own capacity, not to make the mentor indispensable."}]},{"n":"A.4","t":"The mentoring process","b":[{"p":"The process of mentoring includes the following important steps:"},{"steps":["**Building the relationship** — this is the first and foremost step in the process of mentoring. The mentor–mentee relationship is the first vital aspect of mentoring that needs to be established. **Time and money must be invested** in building a good relationship. The mentor and mentee take their time in getting to know each other; with this, the process of mentoring will be easy.","**Negotiating agreements** — in this step a set of agreements is established, to be implemented and followed during the mentoring relationship. These include: **defining the roles**; **setting schedules** for mentoring sessions; **identifying limitations**; and **identifying mentoring style preferences**.","**Developing the mentee** — this is the **longest step** of the mentoring process. During this stage both the mentor and mentee will **define mentoring goals**, **create a list of mentoring activities** to achieve their goals, and keep **constant communication** with each other.","**Ending the relationship** — the final step. The mentoring process ends with a **celebration of the accomplishments** and an **evaluation of outcomes**. The mentoring relationship must end on a **highly positive note**, rather than closing abruptly."]},{"key":"Two features of the four steps are examinable in their own right. **Step 3 is the longest** — the relationship-building and agreement-setting at the front are preparation, and the development work is the substance. And **step 4 is a planned ending**, not a drift into silence: the relationship closes with celebration and evaluation, which is what allows the results to be \"measured in terms of competencies gained\" as §A.1 requires."},{"warn":"The study text defines \"negotiating\" in step 2 as \"**the action of transferring legal ownership of a document**\" — which is the *negotiable instruments* sense of the word, and has nothing to do with negotiating a mentoring agreement. The definition is an error in the book; the step itself is about **agreeing roles, schedules, limitations and style preferences** between mentor and mentee, as the four bullets that follow it make clear."}]},{"n":"A.5","t":"Phases in the mentoring process","b":[{"p":"There are **two main phases** in the mentoring process — **dependence** and **inter-dependence** — although **counter-dependence** may in some cases be an intermediary phase between the two."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Phase","What happens"],"rows":[["**Dependence**","Admiration for the mentor is followed by **identification** with him, followed by **approval** — getting guidance and checking alternative courses of action."],["**Counter-dependence** *(intermediary, and only in some cases)*","If the protégé experiences the mentor as **overwhelming and overpowering**, counter-dependence may develop before inter-dependence. The protégé may **reject the mentor** and develop his own independence. The search for one's own identity may later lead to appreciation of the mentor's role and relationship, leading on to inter-dependence."],["**Inter-dependence**","Characterised by **trust-building and mutuality**, when the mentor and the protégé may begin to **collaborate** and provide **emotional support to each other**."]]}},{"note":"The study text observes that several well-known people with famous mentors passed through the **counter-dependence** phase, and that some could not progress much beyond it to inter-dependence. That is the practical significance of the phase: it is a normal stage, not a failure, but a relationship that stalls there never reaches the mutual collaboration that is mentoring's most valuable form."},{"key":"Note the direction of travel. In **dependence** the benefit flows one way, from mentor to mentee. In **inter-dependence** it flows both ways — the two collaborate and support each other emotionally. That is why §A.6 can list substantial benefits **for the mentor**, which would make no sense if the relationship stayed in the dependence phase."}]},{"n":"A.6","t":"Benefits of mentoring","b":[{"p":"The benefits of the mentoring process can be observed in three areas — **(i) mentors**, **(ii) mentees** and **(iii) the organisation**."},{"h4":"A.6.1  Benefits for mentors"},{"ol":["the mentoring process **strengthens the mentor's active listening skills**;","mentoring encourages **knowledge sharing**, which increases the **self-worth** of the mentor;","it gives the mentor a **sense of fulfilment and achievement**;","the process helps to enhance the mentor's **relationship-building skills**;","it provides an **added sense of responsibility and purpose** for one's career;","the **ability to explain new and complex ideas**;","**organisational skills**, such as developing meeting plans, information and materials;","**familiarity with new communication methods** and approaches;","**improved facilitation skills**;","the **ability to analyse their own knowledge and behaviour**; and","the **pleasure of giving away their knowledge and experience**."]},{"h4":"A.6.2  Benefits for mentees"},{"ol":["the **skills or competency levels** of mentees are developed by the mentoring process;","mentoring **improves the mentee's confidence** in their ability;","the **communication skills** of the mentee will be enhanced;","the mentee comes to know **how to maintain a professional relationship**; and","the mentee's **network of contacts** is enhanced."]},{"h4":"A.6.3  Benefits for the organisation"},{"ol":["the process of mentoring helps an organisation **achieve its talent development goals**, such as succession planning and strong leadership development;","it **improves employee retention** — keeping an employee for a long period, thereby reducing turnover rates;","**training costs are reduced** by one-to-one interaction;","mentoring **improves talent acquisition efforts** — acquiring skilled human labour to meet organisational needs; and","**new hires become aware of the organisation's career development opportunities**."]},{"key":"A question asking for benefits will usually specify **which party**, and the three lists are genuinely different in character. The **mentor** gains *skills* — listening, relationship building, explaining, facilitating, self-analysis — plus status and satisfaction. The **mentee** gains *competence and confidence*, plus a network. The **organisation** gains on *talent* — development, retention, acquisition, and reduced training cost. Do not offer the mentee's benefits when asked for the mentor's: the fact that the mentor gains at all is the counter-intuitive part, and that is where the marks are."}]},{"n":"A.7","t":"Demerits of mentoring","b":[{"p":"Much of the literature may be one-sided and focus only on the positive aspects of mentoring. However, there are potential drawbacks of some mentoring programmes — for the organisation involved, for the mentor and for the mentee. Some of the demerits of mentoring include:"},{"ol":["the risk of mentors developing **considerable personal and private knowledge** about their mentees, which could lead to **confidentiality breaches or bias**;","**overuse of mentors** can lead to **mentor fatigue**, and can potentially **replicate poor work styles** of a mentor in a mentee;","**lack of clarity about the purpose and expectations** of the mentoring relationship can be detrimental to the process;","the **intimate nature** of mentoring relationships can lead to a **sharing of sensitive information** that could be misused or disclosed;","where mentoring relationships are **mandated or forced**, there may be a **lack of chemistry or a poor fit** between mentor and mentee;","if mentoring is seen as a way to **promote certain individuals or groups**, it can lead to **perceptions of favouritism and exclusion**;","implementing a mentoring programme when there are **few opportunities for advancement** within an organisation — a situation which can lead to **frustration for the mentees**;","allowing a mentoring programme to proceed when there is **not complete organisational commitment** to the programme; and","**gender issues and cases of sexual harassment**."]},{"key":"Several of the demerits are the mirror image of a merit, which is the sharpest way to present them. Mentoring works because the relationship is **close** — so its risks are **confidentiality breach, bias and harassment**. It works because the mentor is **experienced** — so the risk is **replicating the mentor's poor work habits**. It works because it is a **career development tool** — so the risk is **frustration where there is nowhere to be promoted to**, and **perceived favouritism** towards those being mentored."},{"note":"Demerits (vii) and (viii) are both about the **organisation**, not the individuals, and both are preconditions rather than hazards of the relationship: do not run a mentoring programme if there is nowhere for mentees to advance to, and do not run one without full organisational commitment. Those are the two points a question about whether to *introduce* a programme is looking for."},{"warn":"Two typographical defects in the book's list, flagged rather than silently corrected: items (vi) and (viii) both begin with a bare **\"f\"** where \"If\" was intended (\"**f** mentoring is seen as a way to promote…\", \"**f** allowing a mentoring program to proceed…\"). The sense is unaffected."}]},{"n":"A.8","t":"Roles and responsibilities of mentor and mentee","b":[{"h4":"A.8.1  Mentor roles and responsibilities"},{"p":"**1. Identifying roles**"},{"ul":["have a clear understanding of **why he or she wants to be a mentor**; and","mentor with a **realistic assessment** of the mentee's skills and experience."]},{"p":"**2. Communicating expectations**"},{"ul":["have a clear understanding of the **mentor's expectations for the mentee**;","**clearly communicate** those expectations;","**stay flexible** in changing expectations;","**adapt feedback** to the mentee's learning style; and","be **realistic about setting timelines**."]},{"p":"**3. Working together**"},{"ul":["**advise, do not dictate**;","advise on what the mentor **knows**, and **admit the things he or she does not know**;","give **good examples**;","offer **constructive feedback**;","**evaluate progress**; and","be **supportive** to the mentee, and be **reliable**."]},{"p":"**4. Meeting all the goals**"},{"ul":["after mentoring is completed, **follow up on successes**;","provide an **evaluation of the experience**; and","**repeat the mentoring process** with others."]},{"h4":"A.8.2  Mentee roles and responsibilities"},{"p":"**1. Identifying roles**"},{"ul":["have an understanding of **what the mentee wants to be mentored in**; and","**select a mentor** based on his or her goals."]},{"p":"**2. Communicating expectations**"},{"ul":["have a clear understanding of the **mentee's expectations for the mentor**;","**clearly communicate** those expectations;","**stay flexible** in changing expectations;","**inform the mentor** about the learning style the mentee prefers; and","be **realistic about setting timelines**."]},{"p":"**3. Working together**"},{"ul":["**listen and contribute** to the conversation;","understand that **mentors will not have all the answers**;","**accept constructive feedback**;","**evaluate progress**;","**celebrate success**; and","be **reliable**."]},{"p":"**4. Meeting the goals**"},{"ul":["provide **updates to the mentor** after mentoring is completed;","provide an **evaluation of the experience**; and","**say thank you**."]},{"key":"The two lists are deliberately **parallel** — the same four headings in the same order — which is the structure to reproduce in an answer. Within them, note the reciprocity: the mentor **adapts feedback** to the mentee's learning style and the mentee **informs the mentor** what that style is; the mentor **offers** constructive feedback and the mentee **accepts** it; both **evaluate progress** and both must be **reliable**. Mentoring is a two-sided obligation, and the single most quotable instruction in either list is the mentor's: **\"advise, don't dictate\"**."}]},{"n":"A.9","t":"Summary of Section A","b":[{"p":"The section explored the concept of mentoring as a contemporary management issue. Mentoring is defined as a professional relationship in which an experienced person (the mentor) assists another (the mentee) in developing specific skills and knowledge that will enhance the less experienced person's professional and personal growth. From the definition, specific characteristics of mentoring were identified as: it is a **long-term relationship**; it **involves two parties**; it is a **professional relationship**; and it is to **enhance skills and career development**."},{"p":"Benefits of mentoring can be observed for **mentors, mentees and the organisation**, as it is an avenue to develop the skills of younger, inexperienced employees. However, there can be some **dysfunctions** of mentoring, which could include **mismatch of mentor and mentee**, as well as **sexual harassment** in the case of opposite-sex alignment. Roles and responsibilities of mentors and mentees are outlined in the chapter, so that the relationship can be strengthened over time as each party plays his or her roles effectively."}]},{"n":"A.10","t":"Review questions and multiple choice questions — Section A","b":[{"p":"Section A closes with **six review questions** and **ten multiple choice questions**, the latter with the study text's own printed answers."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section A review questions","t":"The six review questions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Define mentoring.","State any **five (5)** characteristics of mentoring.","State any **five (5)** objectives of mentoring.","Give **two (2)** benefits of mentoring to the mentor, the mentee and the organisation.","Mention any **five (5)** demerits of mentoring.","Identify the roles and responsibilities of mentors and mentees."]}],"a":[{"p":"Each of the six is answered directly by a section of this chapter, and the requirement tells you how much to give:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Question","Answer at","What to give"],"rows":[["(i) Define mentoring","**§A.1**","The definition, plus the points that it is long term, voluntary, one-on-one, unpaid, and differs from coaching in being long rather than short term"],["(ii) Five characteristics","**§A.2**","Any 5 of the 6"],["(iii) Five objectives","**§A.3**","Any 5 of the 8"],["(iv) Two benefits to each party","**§A.6.1, §A.6.2, §A.6.3**","**Two from each of the three lists — six in total.** Do not give six benefits from one list"],["(v) Five demerits","**§A.7**","Any 5 of the 9"],["(vi) Roles and responsibilities","**§A.8.1 and §A.8.2**","Both parties, under the four parallel headings: identifying roles, communicating expectations, working together, meeting the goals"]]}},{"note":"Question (iv) is the one most often answered wrongly — it asks for two benefits **to the mentor, the mentee and the organisation**, which is six benefits across three lists, not two benefits in total."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section A multiple choice questions","t":"Ten multiple choice questions, with the study text's own solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["This term can be defined as an act of regarding attentively or watching; mentees do this action weekly. **A.** Observations; **B.** Video Logs; **C.** Meetings; **D.** Emails.","In the mentor programme, you learn about ……… **A.** Yourself; **B.** Others; **C.** New teaching strategies; **D.** All of the above.","……… is a type of listening in which you pay attention to the words, body language and emotions of the person you are talking to. **A.** Double Listening; **B.** Active Listening; **C.** Good Listening; **D.** Pay Attention Listening.","Mentors use this tool to stay updated on assignments and communicate with the Mentor Coach. **A.** Video Logs; **B.** Pencil and Paper; **C.** Email; **D.** Microphone.","What is mentoring? **A.** The act of advising someone with less tenure or experience; **B.** Listening to a new employee's personal troubles; **C.** The act of leading a team of people; **D.** All of the above.","The main benefit to a mentee from working with an experienced specialist mentor is to **A.** Increase dependence; **B.** Improve own performance; **C.** Build trust; **D.** Build resilience.","An organisation is most likely to introduce a mentoring programme in order to ……… **A.** Recognise specific skills; **B.** Close its skills gap; **C.** Increase its training costs; **D.** Develop a promotional process.","Which of the following is the main type of question a mentor would use to seek further clarification? **A.** Open; **B.** Closed; **C.** Leading; **D.** Probing.","A mentor can support a member of staff struggling with the demands of their own role by ……… **A.** Advising a change of duties; **B.** Advising others to be helpful; **C.** Directing to useful resources; **D.** Signposting issues to manager.","The term which describes long-term training which includes a combination of both on-the-job and in-class training is: **A.** Mentorship; **B.** Computer based training; **C.** Vestibule training; **D.** Apprenticeship."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Solutions to the multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**A** — Observations"],["2","**D** — All of the above"],["3","**B** — Active Listening"],["4","**C** — Email"],["5","**A** — The act of advising someone with less tenure or experience"],["6","**C** — Build trust"],["7","**B** — Close its skills gap"],["8","**A** — Open"],["9","**C** — Directing to useful resources"],["10","**A** — Mentorship"]]}},{"key":"Three of the answers are worth reasoning through rather than memorising. **Q3 — active listening** is the attending to words, body language *and* emotions, and it is the first benefit the mentor gains at §A.6.1. **Q7 — closing the skills gap**: note that option C, \"increase its training costs\", is the opposite of the truth, since §A.2 and §A.6.3 both say mentoring *reduces* training costs. **Q10 — mentorship** rather than apprenticeship: both are long term, but apprenticeship is a formal trade qualification, whereas mentorship is the relationship this section describes."},{"warn":"The study text gives **C (build trust)** as the answer to Q6, where the more natural reading of \"the main benefit to a mentee\" points to **B (improve own performance)** — the mentee benefits at §A.6.2 are competence, confidence, communication skills, professional relationships and contacts, which is performance improvement, while trust-building is listed at §A.3 as an *objective* of mentoring rather than a benefit to the mentee. The printed answer is reproduced above because it is the official one; be aware that B is defensible and that the question is loosely drafted. Note in passing that option A, \"increase dependence\", is plainly wrong — §A.3(vii) warns expressly against making the mentee depend on the mentor."},{"note":"Questions 1, 2 and 4 are drawn from a **structured mentoring programme** — weekly observations, video logs, a Mentor Coach, email updates — which this section never describes. They are testing familiarity with planned mentoring (§A.1) in operation rather than anything in the text, and Q8's distinction between open, closed, leading and probing questions belongs to the communication material of Chapter 5."}]}}]},{"n":"B.1","t":"Remote work","b":[{"def":{"t":"Remote work","d":"the practice of working outside of the traditional office, both in a **temporal** (time) and a **spatial** (location) sense (Olson, 1983)."}},{"p":"Previous research has separated the practice into two distinct definitions: **working from home** and **working from anywhere**."},{"note":"Remote work is the one topic in this chapter with a clear historical trigger: the study text's own summary records that it \"became popular **during and after the COVID-19 pandemic**\". The underlying research, though, is much older — Olson's definition dates from **1983** — which is worth knowing if a question asks whether remote work is a new phenomenon."}]},{"n":"B.2","t":"Working from home and working from anywhere","b":[{"h4":"B.2a  Working from home (WFH)"},{"p":"Working from home is defined by Choudhury et al. (2019) as **working within a certain distance from the corporate office (less than 50 miles away)**. This does not necessarily mean the person is working out of their own home, but for most workers that is the case."},{"key":"The important aspect to consider is that working from home provides **temporal flexibility** — meaning the worker chooses their working hours more flexibly. Additionally, it **removes commuting** and **reduces sick days**."},{"h4":"B.2b  Working from anywhere"},{"p":"The difference between working from home and working from anywhere is the **geographical flexibility** of working from anywhere. In addition to choosing their own hours, the worker also **chooses where they work from**, without any constraints and with **very limited physical presence at the office** (a few times per year). This allows workers to select an area with a **lower cost of living**, or one that suits their specific needs (Choudhury et al., 2019). It is also called **telecommuting**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Working from home","Working from anywhere"],"rows":[["Flexibility gained","**Temporal** — chooses working hours","**Temporal and geographical** — chooses hours *and* location"],["Distance from the office","Within a certain distance — **less than 50 miles**","**No constraint**"],["Physical presence at the office","Periodic","**Very limited** — a few times per year"],["Characteristic benefit","Removes commuting; reduces sick days","Allows choice of a **lower cost-of-living** area, or one suiting specific needs"],["Also called","—","**Telecommuting**"]]}},{"key":"The distinction the examiner is testing is **one flexibility against two**. Working from home gives the worker control of **when**; working from anywhere gives control of **when and where**. The 50-mile figure and the term **telecommuting** are both specifically examinable — one multiple choice question asks what telecommuting means, and another asks which principle of job design it is most closely associated with (**autonomy**)."}]},{"n":"B.3","t":"Drivers for remote work","b":[{"p":"According to Staples et al. (2006) there are **four key drivers** for an organisation that needs to carry out work in a virtual world; these include:"},{"ol":["**Availability of information technology (IT)**, which is a key part of a virtual working world. It allows workers to work remotely, allows the leaders to control the organisation, and allows the people within the organisation to communicate with each other.","**Effective communication** between the manager and the employee.","**Experience and training** from remote management or remote work, with the arrangements around it.","**Effective management practices**, which includes the manager's view of what is realistic, so that expectations of meetings, performance and so on can be in line with reality."]},{"key":"Only the **first** driver is technological; the other three are all about **management capability** — communicating, being trained and experienced in remote working, and holding realistic expectations. That is the point worth making in an answer: giving everyone a laptop does not make remote work succeed, because three of the four drivers are managerial rather than technical."}]},{"n":"B.4","t":"Advantages of remote work","b":[{"p":"The following are the advantages of practising remote work:"},{"ol":["**socialisation in families**, with spouses and loved ones;","**cuts down or completely removes the need for commuting**;","**reduction of stress** from traffic congestion and public transport delays, and of the **carbon emissions** of the commute that would otherwise have been undertaken;","it **enhances workers' lives** by reducing the time spent travelling;","there is an **increase in productivity**, with some evidence of **real income increase**; and","**integrating work and non-working life**, and improved social life at home."]}]},{"n":"B.5","t":"Disadvantages of remote work","b":[{"p":"Here are some disadvantages of remote work:"},{"ol":["there are **feelings of loneliness and isolation**;","it **blurs the boundaries** between work life and leisure time;","**increased stress** and the **inability to leave work when the workday ends**;","**missed socialisation at work**; and","**missing out on the \"office grapevine\"** and important information."]},{"key":"Set §B.4 and §B.5 side by side and they are two readings of the **same two facts**. Removing the commute is an advantage (less stress, less travel time, lower emissions) — but being always at the workplace is a disadvantage (inability to stop when the day ends). Being at home increases **family** socialisation — and removes **workplace** socialisation, the grapevine and the information that travels on it. An answer that draws that symmetry is stronger than two unconnected lists."},{"note":"Note what the advantages list claims and the disadvantages list does not deny: **productivity increases**, with some evidence of real income increase. The disadvantages are about the **worker's experience** — loneliness, blurred boundaries, stress, lost socialisation and lost information — rather than about output. That asymmetry is the heart of the current debate about remote work, and is worth stating if a question asks you to advise on adopting it."}]},{"n":"B.6","t":"Summary of Section B","b":[{"p":"The section reviewed the contemporary concept of **remote work** as a management practice. It is a management practice that became popular **during and after the COVID-19 pandemic**. Remote work is the practice of working outside of the traditional office, both in a **temporal** (time) and a **spatial** (location) sense. Remote work can be **working from home (WFH)** or **working from anywhere**."},{"p":"There are key **drivers** of remote work; these include availability of technology, communication between managers and the worker, effective training and experience in remote work, and management expectations of performance. **Advantages** of remote work include removal of the stress of commuting, increased productivity and increased socialisation in the family; whereas the **disadvantages** include loneliness of the worker, missing out on office socialisation, and inability to leave work when the day ends."}]},{"n":"B.7","t":"Review questions and multiple choice questions — Section B","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section B review questions","t":"The five review questions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Define remote work.","Distinguish between working from home and working from anywhere.","List the **four (4)** drivers of remote work.","State any **four (4)** advantages of remote work.","State any **four (4)** disadvantages of remote work."]}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Question","Answer at","What to give"],"rows":[["(i) Define remote work","**§B.1**","Olson's definition — working outside the traditional office in both a temporal and a spatial sense"],["(ii) Distinguish WFH from working from anywhere","**§B.2**","Temporal flexibility only, within 50 miles of the office, against temporal **and** geographical flexibility with almost no office presence — the latter also called telecommuting"],["(iii) Four drivers","**§B.3**","All four — IT availability, effective manager–employee communication, experience and training, and effective management practices. The question asks for four and there are exactly four"],["(iv) Four advantages","**§B.4**","Any 4 of the 6"],["(v) Four disadvantages","**§B.5**","Any 4 of the 5"]]}},{"note":"Question (iii) is the only one in the chapter where the number asked for equals the number available, so nothing may be omitted. Attribute them to **Staples et al. (2006)** if you can — the study text does."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section B multiple choice questions","t":"Five multiple choice questions, with the study text's own answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of these is **not** an advantage of remote work? **A.** Increase in family social life; **B.** Increase in office socialisation; **C.** Increase in worker's productivity; **D.** Increased integration of work and non-work life.","Which of these is **not** related to remote work? **A.** Working from home; **B.** Telecommuting; **C.** Working from anywhere; **D.** Working alone.","The disadvantage of remote work is **A.** Inability to stop work when the work day ends; **B.** Reduced commuting; **C.** Integrating work and family life; **D.** Socialisation in family.","The term \"telecommuting\" in job design refers to: **A.** Job simplification; **B.** Working in an office; **C.** Working from a remote location; **D.** Employee turnover.","The concept of \"telecommuting\" is most closely associated with which principle of job design? **A.** Autonomy; **B.** Variety; **C.** Feedback; **D.** Task significance."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Answers to the multiple choice questions"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**B** — Increase in office socialisation"],["2","**D** — Working alone"],["3","**A** — Inability to stop work when the work day ends"],["4","**C** — Working from a remote location"],["5","**A** — Autonomy"]]}},{"key":"Questions 1 and 3 are the same point from both sides, and both turn on the symmetry at §B.5: remote work **increases family socialisation and removes office socialisation**, and it **removes the commute but makes it hard to stop working**. Question 2's answer is **working alone** — remote work is working away from the office, not working without colleagues, which is precisely why loneliness and lost information are listed as its costs rather than its definition."},{"note":"Question 5's answer, **autonomy**, links this section to job design: telecommuting increases the worker's discretion over **when and where** work is done, which is the autonomy dimension of the job characteristics model — not variety, feedback or task significance, which are unaffected by location."}]}}]},{"n":"C.1","t":"Workplace couple","b":[{"def":{"t":"Workplace couple","d":"two individuals who are **romantically involved and work at the same company**. The term can also refer to a **close, non-romantic relationship** between co-workers, sometimes described as a \"work spouse\" or \"office spouse\"."}}]},{"n":"C.2","t":"Work spouse","b":[{"p":"**Work spouse** is a term mostly used in American English, referring to a co-worker with whom one shares a special relationship, having bonds similar to those of a marriage. Early references suggest that a work spouse may not just be a co-worker, but can also be **someone in a similar field who the individual works closely with from a partnering company**."},{"def":{"t":"Work spouse","d":"a special, **platonic** friendship with a work colleague, characterised by a close emotional bond, high levels of disclosure and support, and mutual trust, honesty, loyalty and respect."}},{"p":"The \"work spouse\" is also referred to as **\"workplace spouse\"**, **\"work wife\"**, **\"office husband\"**, **\"work husband\"** or **\"wusband\"**."},{"key":"The word that does the work in the definition is **platonic**. A work spouse relationship is defined as *not* romantic — which is why §C.1 can describe the workplace couple as covering both the romantic case and the close non-romantic case. Keep the three terms separate: a **workplace couple** is the umbrella term, a **work spouse** is the platonic version, and a **workplace romance** (§C.3) is the romantic or sexual version."}]},{"n":"C.3","t":"Workplace romance","b":[{"p":"**Workplace romance**, or relationships between co-workers, can take various forms — including **romantic, sexual, or combination partnerships**. While some companies may have policies discouraging or even prohibiting workplace relationships — especially **between supervisors and subordinates** — many individuals experience and seek out romantic relationships in the workplace."},{"p":"These relationships can have **both positive and negative consequences**, impacting not only the individuals involved but also the broader **workplace environment and culture**."}]},{"n":"C.4","t":"Advantages of a workplace couple","b":[{"ol":["**Increased job satisfaction** — being in a romantic relationship with a co-worker can make coming to work more enjoyable, due to the emotional support and shared experiences.","**Improved communication** — couples who work together often develop strong communication skills, which can benefit their personal and professional relationships.","**Mutual support** — workplace couples can offer each other emotional support and encouragement, both in their personal and professional lives.","**Shared experiences** — working together can create shared memories and experiences, strengthening the bond between the couple.","**Convenience** — seeing each other at work can save time and make it easier to schedule dates.","**Improved morale and team spirit** — the presence of happy couples can foster a sense of community and camaraderie among employees, boosting morale and team spirit.","**Increased commitment and loyalty** — couples may feel more invested in the company and in the careers of their partners, leading to increased loyalty and commitment.","**Better focus and concentration** — individuals experiencing romantic fulfilment may exhibit improved focus and a happier disposition, leading to increased productivity.","**Voluntary overtime** — couples may be more inclined to help each other with work, even if it goes beyond their job duties, leading to more efficient and productive work hours.","**Improved internal communication** — couples working in different departments can foster closer cooperation by sharing information and insights across departments.","**Increased company knowledge** — couples in different departments may share information about their roles, creating a broader understanding of the company's operations.","**Improved work-life balance** — some studies suggest that workplace relationships can improve work-life balance, as employees may feel more comfortable sharing work-related challenges with their office spouse.","**Enhanced compatibility**, through complementing each other's capabilities and strengths in a work context."]},{"key":"The thirteen advantages fall into two groups, and distinguishing them is what turns a list into an analysis. Items (i) to (v) and (xii) are benefits **to the couple** — satisfaction, communication, support, shared experience, convenience, work-life balance. Items (vi) to (xi) and (xiii) are benefits **to the organisation** — morale, loyalty, focus and productivity, voluntary overtime, cross-departmental communication and company knowledge, and complementary capabilities. A question asking why an employer should tolerate workplace relationships wants the second group."}]},{"n":"C.5","t":"Disadvantages of a workplace couple","b":[{"ol":["**Company policies** — some companies have policies against workplace romances, or may require disclosure of the relationship. If it is against the company's policy, one or both parties could be **disciplined, suspended, demoted or fired**. If one of them reports to the other in the same management chain, **someone will have to transfer** (at minimum).","**Blurred boundaries** — it can be challenging to separate personal and professional relationships, leading to conflicts or favouritism.","**Gossip and rumours** — co-workers may gossip or speculate about the couple's relationship, creating an uncomfortable work environment.","**Conflicts of interest** — workplace couples may face conflicts of interest, such as competing for the same promotions, or having different work priorities.","**Breakup complications** — if the relationship ends, it can be awkward or uncomfortable to continue working together.","**Perceived or actual favouritism** — when one partner is in a position of authority, even if not explicitly displaying bias, there can be an **appearance of favouritism**, leading to resentment from other colleagues. In some cases, breakups can lead to **harassment claims or even workplace violence**.","**Distraction** — workplace romances can be distracting, impacting the focus of both the couple and their colleagues. The focus on personal matters can detract from professional responsibilities, leading to decreased efficiency.","**Strained team dynamics** — workplace relationships can make colleagues feel excluded or uncomfortable.","**Ethical and legal risk** — workplace relationships can raise ethical questions, especially if one partner is in a position of authority or power. They can also increase the risk of **sexual harassment or discrimination claims**."]},{"warn":"The sharpest risk in the list is the **reporting line**. Where one partner reports to the other in the same management chain, the book states flatly that **someone will have to transfer, at minimum** — and that is before any question of policy breach, which can lead to discipline, suspension, demotion or dismissal. Note too that several items are **risks to the organisation rather than the couple**: favouritism and resentment, strained team dynamics, distraction, and the legal exposure of harassment or discrimination claims."},{"note":"Several disadvantages mirror an advantage exactly. **Mutual support** becomes **favouritism**; **improved communication across departments** becomes a **conflict of interest** or the leakage of information; **better focus** from romantic fulfilment becomes **distraction**; and **increased commitment** to a partner's career becomes the appearance of bias. The same closeness produces both columns, which is why §C.6 is about managing it rather than preventing it."}]},{"n":"C.6","t":"Best practices for workplace couple life","b":[{"ol":["**Set boundaries** — establish clear boundaries between personal and professional relationships.","**Maintain professionalism** — treat each other with respect and professionalism in the workplace.","**Communicate openly** — discuss challenges and conflicts openly and honestly.","**Be mindful of company policies** — familiarise yourself with your company's policies on workplace romances.","**Prioritise your relationship** — make time for each other outside of work, to nurture the relationship."]},{"key":"Each best practice answers a specific disadvantage, and pairing them is the best way to present this list: **setting boundaries** answers blurred boundaries; **maintaining professionalism** answers gossip, favouritism and strained team dynamics; **open communication** answers conflict; **awareness of company policy** answers the discipline and reporting-line risk; and **prioritising the relationship outside work** answers the distraction and breakup risks."},{"note":"Note what the list is addressed to: it is advice to the **couple**, not to the employer. The employer's counterpart measure appears in the section's own short answer question — organisations need a **company policy** to regulate workplace relationships between employees. If a question asks what the *organisation* should do, the answer is a disclosure-and-conflict-of-interest policy, with rules on relationships inside a reporting line."}]},{"n":"C.7","t":"Summary of Section C","b":[{"p":"This section discussed the subject of the **workplace couple** as a contemporary management issue. A \"workplace couple\" refers to two individuals who are romantically involved and work at the same company. It can also refer to a **close, non-romantic relationship** between co-workers, sometimes described as a \"work spouse\" or \"office spouse\" — a co-worker with whom one shares a special relationship, having bonds similar to those of a marriage."},{"p":"**Advantages** of workplace couples include increased job satisfaction, improved communication and increased support. **Disadvantages** include gossip and rumours, blurred boundaries and breakup complications."}]},{"n":"C.8","t":"Review questions and MCQ / short answer questions — Section C","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section C review questions","t":"The four review questions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Define the terms **workplace couple, workplace spouse and workplace romance**.","Give any **five (5)** advantages of a workplace couple.","Give any **five (5)** disadvantages of a workplace couple.","Mention any **five (5)** best practices to guide workplace couple relationships in an organisation."]}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Question","Answer at","What to give"],"rows":[["(a) The three terms","**§C.1, §C.2, §C.3**","All three, and keep them distinct: **workplace couple** is the umbrella term covering both romantic and close non-romantic relationships; **work spouse** is the **platonic** version, with a close emotional bond, high disclosure and mutual trust; **workplace romance** is the romantic, sexual or combination version"],["(b) Five advantages","**§C.4**","Any 5 of the 13 — ideally a mix of benefits to the couple and to the organisation"],["(c) Five disadvantages","**§C.5**","Any 5 of the 9 — the reporting-line and policy breach risk is the strongest one to lead with"],["(d) Five best practices","**§C.6**","All five, since there are exactly five"]]}},{"note":"As with Section B's drivers question, part (d) asks for exactly as many items as exist, so nothing can be left out."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Section C MCQ / short answer questions","t":"Short answer and multiple choice questions, with the study text's own answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["……… refers to two individuals who are romantically involved and work at the same company.","Relationships between co-workers which can take romantic and sexual forms are known as ………","Organisations need to have ……… to regulate workplace relationships between employees of the opposite sex.","One of these is **not** a factor that can influence workplace romance: **A.** Distance; **B.** Appearance; **C.** Character; **D.** Proximity.","A failed workplace romantic relationship can result in: **A.** Voluntary overtime; **B.** Increased communication; **C.** Violence; **D.** Moral value.","One of these is **not** an advantage of workplace romance: **A.** Job satisfaction; **B.** Conflict of interest; **C.** Mutual support; **D.** Shared experiences."]}],"a":[{"h4":"Answers"},{"table":{"align":"cl","head":["Q","Answer"],"rows":[["1","**Workplace couple**"],["2","**Workplace romance**"],["3","**Company policy**"],["4","**A** — Distance"],["5","**C** — Violence"],["6","**B** — Conflict of interest"]]}},{"key":"Question 4 turns on the contrast between **distance** and **proximity**. Proximity *promotes* workplace romance — people who work near one another form relationships — so distance, its opposite, is **not** a factor that influences it. Appearance and character are both plainly influences."},{"note":"Question 5's answer, **violence**, comes from §C.5(vi): where one partner is in a position of authority, breakups \"can lead to harassment claims or even workplace violence\". Question 6's answer, **conflict of interest**, is from the disadvantages at §C.5(iv) — the other three options are all genuine advantages from §C.4. And question 3 is the one point in the section addressed to the employer rather than the couple: the organisation's answer to workplace relationships is a **company policy**."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Three unrelated topics in one chapter, each with its own learning objectives, summary and review questions — so expect them to be examined separately, and prepare each as a self-contained section. **Mentoring** is the largest and most examinable: learn the definition (long-term, voluntary, one-on-one, unpaid, and distinguished from coaching by being long rather than short term), the two types (natural and planned), the four-step process with step 3 as the longest, the three phases (dependence, counter-dependence, inter-dependence), and above all the THREE separate benefit lists — a question will specify whether it wants the mentor's, the mentee's or the organisation's. **Remote work** turns on one distinction: working from home gives temporal flexibility within 50 miles of the office, while working from anywhere (telecommuting) gives temporal and geographical flexibility with almost no office presence; note that three of the four drivers are managerial rather than technological, and that the advantages and disadvantages are two readings of the same facts. **Workplace couple** needs the three terms kept apart — the couple is the umbrella term, the work spouse is platonic, the romance is romantic or sexual — and its best practices each answer a specific disadvantage.","errors":["Giving six benefits of mentoring from one list when the question asks for two benefits to the mentor, the mentee AND the organisation — which is six benefits across three different lists. The three lists differ in kind: the mentor gains skills, the mentee gains competence and confidence, the organisation gains on talent development, retention and cost.","Confusing the three workplace relationship terms. A workplace couple is the umbrella term covering both romantic and close non-romantic relationships; a work spouse is expressly PLATONIC; a workplace romance is romantic, sexual or a combination.","Treating remote work as a single thing. Working from home gives temporal flexibility only and keeps the worker within about 50 miles of the office; working from anywhere — telecommuting — adds geographical flexibility and almost removes office presence.","Answering that remote work succeeds or fails on technology. Only one of the four drivers is technological; the other three are effective manager-employee communication, experience and training in remote working, and realistic management expectations.","Omitting the counter-dependence phase of mentoring, or presenting it as a failure. It is an intermediary phase that arises where the protégé experiences the mentor as overpowering, and it normally resolves into inter-dependence.","Listing only the costs of a workplace couple to the couple themselves. Several disadvantages fall on the organisation — perceived favouritism and resentment, strained team dynamics, distraction and reduced efficiency, and the legal risk of harassment or discrimination claims — and these are what an employer's policy exists to address."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Mentoring differs from coaching principally in that","o":["mentoring is paid while coaching is voluntary","mentoring is a long-term task while coaching is a short-term task","mentoring involves a group while coaching is one-on-one","coaching enhances skills while mentoring does not","coaching is a professional relationship while mentoring is a personal one"],"a":1,"w":"The study text states expressly that mentoring differs from coaching because coaching is a short-term task and mentoring is a long-term task. Both are normally one-on-one, and mentoring is specifically described as voluntary and unpaid.","src":"Chapter 9 A.1","sec":"A.1"},{"q":"Which is the LONGEST step in the mentoring process?","o":["Building the relationship","Negotiating agreements","Developing the mentee","Ending the relationship","Evaluating the outcomes"],"a":2,"w":"Developing the mentee is described as the longest step, during which both parties define mentoring goals, create a list of mentoring activities and keep constant communication. The first two steps are preparation, and the fourth is a planned close with celebration and evaluation.","src":"Chapter 9 A.4","sec":"A.4"},{"q":"The phase of mentoring characterised by trust-building and mutuality, in which mentor and protégé collaborate and provide emotional support to each other, is","o":["dependence","counter-dependence","inter-dependence","identification","approval"],"a":2,"w":"Inter-dependence is the phase of trust-building and mutuality. Dependence runs from admiration through identification to approval; counter-dependence is the intermediary phase where the protégé rejects an overpowering mentor.","src":"Chapter 9 A.5","sec":"A.5"},{"q":"Working from home, as defined in the study text, means working within what distance of the corporate office?","o":["Less than 10 miles","Less than 25 miles","Less than 50 miles","Less than 100 miles","There is no distance limit"],"a":2,"w":"Choudhury et al. (2019) define working from home as working within a certain distance from the corporate office — less than 50 miles away. Removing the distance constraint altogether gives working from anywhere, also called telecommuting.","src":"Chapter 9 B.2","sec":"B.2"},{"q":"A \"work spouse\" is best described as","o":["a romantic partner who works at the same company","a special, platonic friendship with a work colleague characterised by a close emotional bond and mutual trust","a supervisor who mentors a subordinate of the opposite sex","a colleague with whom one shares a reporting line","two employees who are married to each other and work in the same department"],"a":1,"w":"The defining word is PLATONIC — a work spouse relationship is expressly not romantic, and is characterised by a close emotional bond, high levels of disclosure and support, and mutual trust, honesty, loyalty and respect. The romantic case is a workplace romance.","src":"Chapter 9 C.2","sec":"C.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Define mentoring, explain the four steps in the mentoring process, and state any four demerits of mentoring.","marks":15,"a":[{"p":"Mentoring is a **professional relationship in which an experienced person (the mentor) assists another (the mentee) in developing specific skills and knowledge that will enhance the less experienced person's professional and personal growth**. It is long term, normally voluntary and one-on-one, with neither party paid to be involved, and it differs from coaching in that coaching is a short-term task while mentoring is a long-term one."},{"h4":"The four steps in the mentoring process"},{"ol":["**Building the relationship** — the first and foremost step; time and money must be invested in it, and the parties take time to get to know each other.","**Negotiating agreements** — establishing a set of agreements for the relationship: defining roles, setting schedules, identifying limitations, and identifying mentoring style preferences.","**Developing the mentee** — the longest step; both parties define mentoring goals, create a list of activities to achieve them, and maintain constant communication.","**Ending the relationship** — the final step, closing with a celebration of accomplishments and an evaluation of outcomes, on a positive note rather than abruptly."]},{"h4":"Demerits of mentoring (any four)"},{"ul":["mentors acquiring considerable private knowledge of mentees, risking confidentiality breaches or bias;","overuse of mentors leading to mentor fatigue, and replication of a mentor's poor work styles;","lack of clarity about the purpose and expectations of the relationship;","sharing of sensitive information that could be misused or disclosed;","mandated or forced relationships with a lack of chemistry or poor fit;","perceptions of favouritism and exclusion;","frustration where there are few opportunities for advancement in the organisation;","proceeding without complete organisational commitment; and","gender issues and cases of sexual harassment."]}],"src":"Chapter 9 A.1, A.4 and A.7","sec":"A.4"},{"q":"Distinguish between working from home and working from anywhere, and state the four drivers of remote work.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"**Remote work** is the practice of working outside the traditional office, both in a temporal (time) and a spatial (location) sense."},{"p":"**Working from home** means working within a certain distance of the corporate office — less than 50 miles away. Its important feature is **temporal flexibility**: the worker chooses their working hours more flexibly. It also removes commuting and reduces sick days."},{"p":"**Working from anywhere** adds **geographical flexibility**. In addition to choosing their own hours, the worker chooses where they work from, without any constraints and with very limited physical presence at the office — a few times per year. This allows workers to select an area with a lower cost of living, or one that suits their specific needs. It is also called **telecommuting**."},{"h4":"The four drivers of remote work (Staples et al., 2006)"},{"ol":["**availability of information technology**, which allows workers to work remotely, leaders to control the organisation, and people within it to communicate;","**effective communication** between the manager and the employee;","**experience and training** in remote management and remote work, and the arrangements around it; and","**effective management practices**, including the manager's view of what is realistic, so that expectations of meetings and performance are in line with reality."]},{"note":"Only the first driver is technological; the other three are managerial, which is why providing the equipment is not by itself enough to make remote work succeed."}],"src":"Chapter 9 B.1, B.2 and B.3","sec":"B.2"}]}},{"n":10,"t":"Entrepreneurship","brief":"The closing chapter of the subject. Covers the concept of entrepreneurship as a system of exploiting market opportunities, who an entrepreneur is and why innovation rather than size is the defining feature, the six functions of an entrepreneur, the six types — innovative, imitating, Fabian, drone, intrapreneur and ultrapreneur — and the sixteen qualities that make up the psychological profile of an entrepreneur.","outcomes":["Explain the concept of entrepreneurship","Define who an entrepreneur is","State the functions of an entrepreneur","Identify and describe the types of entrepreneurs","State the qualities of an entrepreneur"],"secs":[{"n":"10.1","t":"Concept of entrepreneurship","b":[{"def":{"t":"Entrepreneurship","d":"a system of operating business in which opportunities existing within the scope of a market are exploited."}},{"p":"A potential entrepreneur should show the interest to **seek out investment opportunities in the market**, so that they can run the enterprise successfully based on the identifiable opportunities."},{"key":"Entrepreneurship involves the process of **creating, developing and managing a new business venture**, in order to achieve **profit and growth**, by **identifying and capitalising on opportunities in the market**."},{"note":"That second definition is the one to learn verbatim, because it is the one the chapter summary repeats and the one a multiple choice question is built on — the answer there is \"a process involving **innovation, new products or services, and value creation**\", not merely \"setting up a business\" or \"taking a significant risk\". Entrepreneurship is a **process**, and risk-taking alone is not enough to constitute it."}]},{"n":"10.2","t":"Who is an entrepreneur?","b":[{"def":{"t":"Entrepreneur","d":"an individual who, rather than working as an employee, **founds and runs a small business, assuming all the risks and rewards of the venture**. Entrepreneurs who prove to be successful in taking on the risks of a startup are rewarded with **profits, fame and continued growth opportunities**."}},{"p":"An entrepreneur is a business person who can **detect and sense the availability of business opportunities** in any given scenario. He will utilise these opportunities to **create new products by employing new production methods in different markets**. He will also function in different ways by using various resources which will give him profit."},{"key":"Although most entrepreneurial businesses start small, **the owners of such businesses may not be small-scale owners**. They could in fact be big business owners, who first try and test the waters before investing huge resources in the business. **Small business owners dread risk, but successful entrepreneurs are very innovative** and know how to operate profitably in a business environment, even if the risk is very high."},{"p":"**Innovation is the life blood of any kind of entrepreneur** — this is one of the pivotal qualities that helps them gain an advantage over established players in the market. Entrepreneurs are thus defined as *individuals or groups of individuals who carry out entrepreneurship activities that are based on their innovative approaches to solving real-world problems*."},{"warn":"Note the tension the chapter sets up, and resolve it the way the chapter does. The formal definition says an entrepreneur \"founds and runs a **small** business\" — but the text immediately qualifies this: the owner **need not be a small-scale owner**, and the distinguishing feature is not size but **attitude to risk and innovation**. A small business owner who dreads risk is not, on this account, an entrepreneur; a large owner who innovates and bears risk is."},{"note":"Read with the material at §A.12 of Chapter 1 on legal forms of business ownership and §A.7.1 on size-based classification: the **legal form** and the **size** of a business are separate questions from whether its owner is behaving entrepreneurially. That is why the intrapreneur at §10.4(v) can be an **employee** of a large organisation and still be a type of entrepreneur."}]},{"n":"10.3","t":"Functions of an entrepreneur","b":[{"p":"Entrepreneurship has been defined as a function which covers multiple functions, such as:"},{"ol":["**building organisations**;","**providing self-employment**;","**utilisation of available resources**;","**innovation applied to the novel concept**;","**bringing together multiple factors of production** in a tangible manner; and","**identifying and exploiting business opportunities** within the available market."]},{"key":"The six functions describe the entrepreneur as the agent who **combines the factors of production**. Function (v) is the classical economic one — bringing land, labour and capital together — and functions (iii) and (vi) are its practical expression: finding the resources and finding the opportunity. Functions (i) and (ii) are the **social** consequences, building organisations and creating employment, starting with the entrepreneur's own. And function (iv), **innovation**, is what §10.2 calls the life blood of the whole activity."},{"note":"A multiple choice question in this chapter gives the functions as \"to imagine a business idea; to study project feasibility; to set up an enterprise\" — with \"all of the above\" as the answer. Those three are a **practical restatement** of the same functions (identifying the opportunity, assessing it, and building the organisation), and are worth knowing because they phrase the functions as a sequence rather than a list."}]},{"n":"10.4","t":"Types of entrepreneurs","b":[{"p":"Based on their **working relationship with the business environment** they are functioning in, various types of entrepreneurs can be found. The chief categories are these **six types**: **(i)** innovative entrepreneurs, **(ii)** imitating entrepreneurs, **(iii)** Fabian entrepreneurs, **(iv)** drone entrepreneurs, **(v)** intrapreneurs, and **(vi)** ultrapreneurs."},{"h4":"(i)  Innovative entrepreneurs"},{"p":"This type of entrepreneur is **more interested in introducing some new ideas** into the market, the organisation or the nation. They are drawn towards innovations, and **invest a lot of time and wealth in doing research and development**."},{"h4":"(ii)  Imitating entrepreneurs"},{"p":"These are often disparagingly referred to as **\"copy cats\"**. They **observe an existing successful system and replicate it** in a manner where **all the deficiencies of the original business model are addressed and all its efficiencies are retained**. These entrepreneurs help to improve an existing product or production process, and can offer suggestions to enhance the use of better technology."},{"h4":"(iii)  Fabian entrepreneurs"},{"p":"These are entrepreneurs that are **very careful in their approaches and cautious in adopting any changes**. They are **not prone to sudden decisions**, and try to shy away from any innovation or change that does not fit their narrative."},{"h4":"(iv)  Drone entrepreneurs"},{"p":"These are entrepreneurs who **do not like change**. They are considered **\"old school\"**. They want to do business in their own **traditional or orthodox methods** of production and systems. Such people **attach pride and tradition even to outdated methods** of doing business."},{"h4":"(v)  Intrapreneurs"},{"def":{"t":"Intrapreneur","d":"the **employee** who avails himself of an opportunity, develops an idea and takes it to the next level **for the betterment of the organisation**."}},{"p":"**Intrapreneurship** is a phenomenon of **empowering the employees within the organisation**, by valuing their ideas and converting them into a profit-making model for the business. **The organisation would bear the associated risk and loss if the intrapreneur's project failed.** It is the combination of employee responsibilities with his or her entrepreneurship skills that creates an intrapreneur."},{"p":"The top executives in the organisation are encouraged to catch hold of new ideas and then convert them into products through **research and development (R&D) activities within the framework of the organisation**."},{"h4":"(vi)  Ultrapreneurs"},{"def":{"t":"Ultrapreneur","d":"an entrepreneur who **goes beyond typical business success to create a positive impact on their family, friends, community, humanity and country**."}},{"p":"They aim to achieve **financial freedom and lifestyle goals** while also making a **meaningful contribution to society**, often balancing work and personal life. An ultrapreneur is someone who has **exceeded at entrepreneurship** but goes on to have a positive effect on his family, friends, community, humanity and country. He earns enough money to live the lifestyle he wants, doing what he loves, and has ample time to spend it with the people he loves."},{"p":"Ultrapreneurs can be seen as **ultimate elite entrepreneurs** who are focused on **creating a positive impact as well as job creation**. **Mindfulness, and the ability to quiet the mind**, describes an ultrapreneur, compared with an entrepreneur who is comparatively **restless**. Ultrapreneurs **get more done in less time** by taking time out every day to calm the mind."},{"key":"The first four types form a **single spectrum of attitude to change**, and the cleanest way to remember them is in that order: the **innovative** entrepreneur *creates* change; the **imitating** entrepreneur *copies and improves on* change others have made; the **Fabian** entrepreneur is *cautious about* change and adopts it only reluctantly; and the **drone** entrepreneur *refuses* change altogether, taking pride in outdated methods. The last two types are not on that spectrum at all — the **intrapreneur** is defined by *where* he operates (inside an organisation, with the organisation bearing the risk), and the **ultrapreneur** by *what he does with success* (impact beyond the business)."},{"warn":"The distinction between **Fabian** and **drone** is the single most examined point in this chapter, and two of the ten multiple choice questions turn on it. A **Fabian** entrepreneur is **cautious** — slow to adopt change, shying away from innovation that does not fit his narrative, but not refusing it in principle. A **drone** entrepreneur **refuses to change the method of production already introduced** — he is \"old school\" and attaches pride to tradition. Cautious versus refusing."},{"note":"The **intrapreneur** carries a point candidates often miss: the **organisation bears the risk and loss** if the project fails, not the intrapreneur. That is the exact opposite of the entrepreneur at §10.2, who assumes **all the risks and rewards** personally — and it is what makes intrapreneurship an attractive route for an employee with entrepreneurial skills but no appetite for personal exposure."},{"warn":"An inconsistency in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: the body of §10.4 lists the six types as innovative, imitating, Fabian, drone, **intrapreneur** and ultrapreneur — but the **chapter summary** lists them as \"innovators, imitators, drone, fabian **technopreneur**, and ultrapreneur\", substituting a *technopreneur* that appears nowhere in the chapter and dropping the intrapreneur, which the chapter discusses at length. The body's list of six is the one to learn."}]},{"n":"10.5","t":"Qualities of an entrepreneur","b":[{"p":"Entrepreneurs have many of the **personal qualities of leaders**. The psychological profile of entrepreneurs includes the following characteristics:"},{"ol":["**striving for achievements, creativity, innovations**;","**striving for independence** — they are unlikely to be in a subordinate position to anyone. The entrepreneur wants to be autonomous; he needs to control the situation and lead others;","**perspicacity, resourcefulness, creativeness, flexibility, inventiveness**;","**persistence, confidence, courage, stability**;","**optimism** — they believe their ventures will succeed;","**responsibility**;","**ability to take risks**;","**management skills**, and especially **team building abilities**;","**sagacity, good analytical and practical thinking skills**, and the ability to use both creative and analytical thinking;","**reward oriented** — a reward could mean not just material gain, but also being recognised, gaining respect, etc.;","**expansion oriented**;","**goal oriented**;","**enthusiasm**, and the ability to attract and inspire others;","**ability to understand and guide people**;","**decision making skills**; and","**profit oriented** — they see profit as an **indicator of their successes and achievements**."]},{"key":"Sixteen qualities is more than any answer needs, so group them. **Drive** — striving for achievement, persistence, enthusiasm, optimism, expansion and goal orientation. **Autonomy and risk** — striving for independence, courage, responsibility, and the ability to take risks. **Thinking** — creativity, inventiveness, perspicacity, resourcefulness, flexibility, sagacity, and both analytical and creative thinking, feeding decision making. **People** — management and team-building skills, the ability to attract, inspire, understand and guide others. Name a group, then give two or three qualities from it."},{"note":"Two qualities are worth quoting precisely because the book qualifies them. **Reward oriented** does not mean money alone — it expressly includes **recognition and respect**. And **profit oriented** is explained as seeing profit as an **indicator of success and achievement**, not as an end in itself. Both point the same way: the entrepreneur's motivation is achievement, with money as the scorecard."},{"note":"The opening sentence is the link to Chapter 5: entrepreneurs have **many of the personal qualities of leaders**, and items (viii), (xiii) and (xiv) — team building, inspiring others, understanding and guiding people — are leadership qualities rather than business ones. A question asking how entrepreneurship relates to leadership should start there."}]},{"n":"10.6","t":"Summary","b":[{"p":"**Entrepreneurship** involves the process of **creating, developing and managing a new business venture** in order to achieve **profit and growth**, by **identifying and capitalising on opportunities in the market**."},{"p":"An **entrepreneur** is an individual who, rather than working as an employee, **founds and runs a small business, assuming all the risks and rewards of the venture**."},{"p":"There are several **types of entrepreneurs**, namely **innovators, imitators, drone, Fabian, intrapreneur and ultrapreneur**. The **ultrapreneur** is an entrepreneur who has become so successful in his entrepreneurial endeavour that he is making an impact on his family, community and the society at large."},{"warn":"The study text's own summary gives the list of types as \"innovators, imitators, drone, fabian **technopreneur**, and ultrapreneur\". The word **technopreneur** appears nowhere else in the chapter, and the **intrapreneur** — which §10.4(v) discusses in detail — is omitted. The list above restores the intrapreneur and drops the technopreneur, to match the body of the chapter."}]},{"n":"10.7","t":"Review questions, with the study text's own answers","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter — and the subject — with **ten multiple choice questions** and its own printed answer key. All are reproduced here, with the official answers, added explanation, and two answers flagged as wrong."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Review questions","t":"Ten multiple choice questions, with the study text's answer key","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["……… entrepreneurs are cautious to introduce new changes, nor adopt new methods innovated by others. **A.** Technical; **B.** Fabian; **C.** Induced; **D.** Business.","The entrepreneur who does not change the method of production already introduced is: **A.** Drone entrepreneur; **B.** Fabian entrepreneur; **C.** Classical entrepreneur; **D.** None of these.","A person who works within an organisation and has entrepreneurial capabilities is: **A.** Entrepreneur; **B.** Intrapreneur; **C.** Manager; **D.** Ultrapreneur.","Entrepreneurship can best be described as ……… **A.** a process that requires setting up a business; **B.** taking a significant risk in a business context; **C.** having a strong vision; **D.** a process involving innovation, new products or services, and value creation.","Which of the following attitudes is **not** generally associated with successful entrepreneurship? **A.** Competition and co-operation; **B.** Desire to influence others; **C.** Innovation and product improvement; **D.** Status quo in business.","The functions of an entrepreneur include: **A.** To imagine a business idea; **B.** To study project feasibility; **C.** To set up an enterprise; **D.** All of the above.","Which of the following is **not** a characteristic of entrepreneurship? **A.** Managerial training; **B.** Risk taking; **C.** Innovation; **D.** Creative activity.","Which of the following is **inconsistent** in the context of an entrepreneur? **A.** He is owner of the business; **B.** He is a risk taker; **C.** He operates production activities; **D.** He searches out business opportunities.","A/an ……… is an entrepreneur who goes beyond typical business success to create a positive impact on their family, friends and community. **A.** Innovative entrepreneur; **B.** Ultrapreneur; **C.** Drone entrepreneur; **D.** Imitator entrepreneur.","Which of the following might be termed a **disadvantage** faced by the small business? **A.** Greater flexibility; **B.** Greater ability to serve specialised markets; **C.** Extreme personal commitment of the owner; **D.** More personal service."]}],"a":[{"h4":"The study text's printed answer key"},{"table":{"align":"cll","head":["Q","Printed answer","Comment"],"rows":[["1","**B** — Fabian","Correct. Fabian entrepreneurs are cautious in adopting change *(§10.4(iii))*."],["2","**A** — Drone entrepreneur","Correct. The drone refuses to change the method already introduced, taking pride in traditional methods *(§10.4(iv))*."],["3","**B** — Intrapreneur","Correct. The intrapreneur is the **employee** who develops an idea for the betterment of the organisation *(§10.4(v))*."],["4","**D** — a process involving innovation, new products or services, and value creation","Correct, and the best statement of §10.1. Note that options A and B are each only part of it: setting up a business, or taking risk, is not entrepreneurship without innovation and value creation."],["5","**D** — Status quo in business","Correct. Maintaining the status quo is the attitude of the **drone** entrepreneur, not of successful entrepreneurship."],["6","**D** — All of the above","Correct. Imagining the idea, studying feasibility and setting up the enterprise are all entrepreneurial functions *(§10.3)*."],["7","**A** — Managerial training","Correct. Risk taking, innovation and creative activity are all characteristics of entrepreneurship; formal managerial *training* is not — §10.5 lists management **skills** as a quality, but training is not a characteristic of the activity."],["8","**C** — He operates production activities","Correct. The entrepreneur **owns** the business, **bears risk** and **searches out opportunities**; operating production activities is a manager's or operative's role, not what defines the entrepreneur."],["9","**A** — Innovative entrepreneur","**Wrong — see the warning below.**"],["10","**B** — Greater ability to serve specialised markets","**Wrong — see the warning below.**"]]}},{"warn":"**Two answers in the printed key are wrong.** **Question 9** asks which type of entrepreneur \"goes beyond typical business success to create a positive impact on their family, friends and community\". That is the **ultrapreneur**, defined in those exact words at §10.4(vi) — so the answer is **B**, not the printed **A** (innovative entrepreneur). **Question 10** asks for a **disadvantage** faced by the small business. Greater flexibility (A), greater ability to serve specialised markets (B) and more personal service (D) are all **advantages** of being small; the only disadvantage offered is **C — extreme personal commitment of the owner**. So the answer is **C**, not the printed **B**. In both cases the printed key contradicts the chapter's own text."},{"key":"Questions 1 and 2 together are the Fabian/drone distinction, and they are the heart of this chapter's examinable content: **Fabian = cautious** about change; **drone = refuses** change. Questions 7 and 8 are both \"odd one out\" questions resolved by the same insight — the entrepreneur is defined by **ownership, risk and opportunity-seeking**, not by managerial training or by operating the production line."},{"note":"Question 10 reaches outside this chapter: the advantages and disadvantages of the **small business** belong to Chapter 1 (§A.7.1 on size-based classification and §A.12.1 on the sole proprietorship). It is worth noting as the chapter's only question not answerable from its own four pages — and, as flagged above, the printed answer to it is wrong."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"The shortest chapter in the subject and the last — four pages of text plus a ten-question MCQ bank. Almost all of its examinable weight sits in the SIX TYPES at §10.4, and the distinction the examiner returns to is Fabian (cautious about change, slow to adopt) against drone (refuses change, \"old school\", proud of outdated methods) — two of the ten questions turn on exactly that. Know the intrapreneur as the employee whose RISK IS BORNE BY THE ORGANISATION, which is the direct opposite of the entrepreneur at §10.2 who assumes all risks and rewards personally; and know the ultrapreneur word for word, since a question quotes the definition back at you. Beyond the types, learn the §10.1 definition of entrepreneurship as a PROCESS of creating, developing and managing a venture by identifying and capitalising on opportunities (not merely setting up a business or taking risk), the six functions, and a grouped handful of the sixteen qualities. Note the chapter's own defects: the summary substitutes a \"technopreneur\" for the intrapreneur, and TWO of the ten printed answers (questions 9 and 10) contradict the text.","errors":["Confusing Fabian and drone entrepreneurs. The Fabian is CAUTIOUS — slow and reluctant to adopt change; the drone REFUSES change and takes pride in traditional, even outdated, methods. Two of the chapter's ten questions test this one distinction.","Saying the intrapreneur bears the risk of his project. The organisation bears the risk and loss if an intrapreneur's project fails — which is precisely the opposite of the entrepreneur, who assumes all the risks and rewards of the venture personally.","Defining entrepreneurship as \"setting up a business\" or \"taking a risk\". Both are offered as distractors in the chapter's own multiple choice question; the answer is a PROCESS involving innovation, new products or services, and value creation.","Equating entrepreneurship with small business. The study text says expressly that the owners of entrepreneurial businesses may NOT be small-scale owners, and that small business owners dread risk while successful entrepreneurs are innovative and operate profitably even at high risk. The defining features are innovation and risk-bearing, not size.","Listing \"technopreneur\" among the types of entrepreneur. It appears only in the chapter summary, in place of the intrapreneur, and nowhere in the body of the chapter — the six types are innovative, imitating, Fabian, drone, intrapreneur and ultrapreneur.","Reciting all sixteen qualities of an entrepreneur without grouping them. Group by drive, autonomy and risk, thinking, and people skills, then give two or three from each group with a word of explanation."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"An entrepreneur who is very careful in his approach and cautious in adopting any change, shying away from innovation that does not fit his narrative, is a","o":["innovative entrepreneur","imitating entrepreneur","Fabian entrepreneur","drone entrepreneur","intrapreneur"],"a":2,"w":"The Fabian entrepreneur is cautious and not prone to sudden decisions. The drone goes further and refuses change altogether, taking pride in traditional and even outdated methods — that is the distinction the chapter tests most often.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"If an intrapreneur's project fails, who bears the associated risk and loss?","o":["The intrapreneur personally","The organisation","The intrapreneur and the organisation equally","The organisation's shareholders only","An external venture capital investor"],"a":1,"w":"The study text states that the organisation would bear the associated risk and loss if the intrapreneur's project failed. This is the opposite of the entrepreneur at §10.2, who assumes all the risks and rewards of the venture personally.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"Entrepreneurs who observe an existing successful system and replicate it, addressing its deficiencies while retaining its efficiencies, are known as","o":["innovative entrepreneurs","imitating entrepreneurs","Fabian entrepreneurs","ultrapreneurs","drone entrepreneurs"],"a":1,"w":"Imitating entrepreneurs — disparagingly called \"copy cats\" — replicate a successful system while fixing its deficiencies and keeping its efficiencies, and they help improve existing products and processes.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is described in the study text as \"the life blood of any kind of entrepreneur\"?","o":["Capital","Risk-taking","Innovation","Managerial training","Profit"],"a":2,"w":"Innovation is the life blood of any kind of entrepreneur — the pivotal quality that gives an advantage over established players in the market, and the basis of the definition of entrepreneurs as those whose activities rest on innovative approaches to solving real-world problems.","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"In the study text's profile of an entrepreneur, \"profit oriented\" means that the entrepreneur","o":["pursues profit as an end in itself, above all other considerations","sees profit as an indicator of his successes and achievements","distributes profit to employees as an incentive","reinvests all profit in research and development","measures profit only in material terms"],"a":1,"w":"The study text explains \"profit oriented\" as seeing profit as an indicator of success and achievement. The related quality \"reward oriented\" is likewise qualified — reward includes recognition and respect, not just material gain.","src":"Chapter 10.5","sec":"10.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"Define entrepreneurship and an entrepreneur, and explain the six types of entrepreneur.","marks":15,"a":[{"p":"**Entrepreneurship** is a system of operating business in which opportunities existing within the scope of a market are exploited. It involves the process of **creating, developing and managing a new business venture** in order to achieve **profit and growth**, by identifying and capitalising on opportunities in the market."},{"p":"An **entrepreneur** is an individual who, rather than working as an employee, **founds and runs a business, assuming all the risks and rewards of the venture**. He detects and senses business opportunities and uses them to create new products through new production methods. Although most entrepreneurial businesses start small, the owners need not be small-scale owners — the distinguishing features are **innovation and the willingness to bear risk**, innovation being \"the life blood of any kind of entrepreneur\"."},{"h4":"The six types of entrepreneur"},{"ol":["**Innovative entrepreneurs** — interested in introducing new ideas into the market, organisation or nation; drawn to innovation and investing heavily in research and development.","**Imitating entrepreneurs** — \"copy cats\" who observe an existing successful system and replicate it, addressing its deficiencies and retaining its efficiencies, thereby improving existing products and processes.","**Fabian entrepreneurs** — very careful and cautious in adopting change, not prone to sudden decisions, shying away from innovation that does not fit their narrative.","**Drone entrepreneurs** — \"old school\" entrepreneurs who do not like change at all, insisting on traditional or orthodox methods and attaching pride and tradition even to outdated ones.","**Intrapreneurs** — employees who seize an opportunity, develop an idea and take it further for the betterment of the organisation. The **organisation bears the risk and loss** if the project fails.","**Ultrapreneurs** — entrepreneurs who go beyond typical business success to create a positive impact on family, friends, community, humanity and country, achieving financial freedom and lifestyle goals while contributing meaningfully to society."]},{"note":"The first four form a spectrum of attitude to change — create, copy, hesitate, refuse. The last two are classified differently: the intrapreneur by where he operates, and the ultrapreneur by what he does with his success."}],"src":"Chapter 10.1, 10.2 and 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"State the functions of an entrepreneur, and explain any six qualities of an entrepreneur.","marks":12,"a":[{"h4":"Functions of an entrepreneur"},{"ol":["building organisations;","providing self-employment;","utilisation of available resources;","innovation applied to the novel concept;","bringing together multiple factors of production in a tangible manner; and","identifying and exploiting business opportunities within the available market."]},{"h4":"Qualities of an entrepreneur (any six)"},{"ol":["**striving for achievement, creativity and innovation**;","**striving for independence** — wanting autonomy, to control the situation and lead others, rather than be subordinate to anyone;","**resourcefulness, flexibility and inventiveness**;","**persistence, confidence, courage and stability**;","**optimism** — believing the venture will succeed;","**ability to take risks**;","**management skills, and especially team building ability**;","**good analytical and practical thinking**, using both creative and analytical thinking;","**reward orientation** — where reward includes recognition and respect, not just material gain;","**enthusiasm**, and the ability to attract and inspire others;","**decision making skills**; and","**profit orientation** — seeing profit as an indicator of success and achievement."]},{"note":"The study text opens §10.5 by noting that entrepreneurs have many of the personal qualities of leaders, so team building, inspiring others and guiding people are leadership qualities carried into the entrepreneurial profile."}],"src":"Chapter 10.3 and 10.5","sec":"10.5"}]}}]});
